ForgeData.register("gcse-geo",{"GCSE-GEO-HAZ":{"label":"Hazardous Earth","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-01","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"At a destructive (subduction) plate boundary, what typically happens?","options":{"A":"The denser oceanic plate sinks beneath the continental plate","B":"Two continental plates collide and neither sinks, forming fold mountains but no volcanoes","C":"Two plates slide past each other with no vertical movement, causing earthquakes but rarely volcanoes","D":"Two plates move apart, allowing magma to rise and form new crust"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-BOUNDARY","scaffold":"Destructive = one plate (usually denser oceanic crust) subducts beneath the other — friction causes earthquakes and melting can feed volcanoes. B describes a collision boundary, C a conservative boundary, and D a constructive boundary.","reforge":{"stem":"At a conservative (transform) plate boundary, why do volcanoes rarely form even though earthquakes do?","options":{"A":"Volcanoes only form at collision boundaries","B":"Conservative boundaries only exist under the ocean","C":"Plates slide past, so magma has no route up","D":"The plates move too slowly to generate any heat"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-02","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"The 2010 Haiti earthquake (magnitude 7.0) occurred on which type of plate boundary?","options":{"A":"Collision","B":"Conservative","C":"Destructive","D":"Constructive"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-BOUNDARY","scaffold":"Conservative (transform) boundary — shallow focus (~13km) meant intense shaking at the surface despite the \"only\" 7.0 magnitude. Destructive boundaries involve subduction (like Japan's); constructive boundaries produce new crust; collision boundaries crumple two continental plates into mountains.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Haiti's shallow-focus earthquake cause such intense surface shaking despite its moderate 7.0 magnitude?","options":{"A":"The depth of focus has no effect on surface shaking","B":"Haiti has unusually dense rock that amplifies all seismic waves","C":"Shallow earthquakes are always more powerful in magnitude","D":"Shallow focus means less energy is absorbed by rock"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-03","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Japan's 2011 Tohoku earthquake (magnitude 9.0) killed roughly 18,000 people. Haiti's 2010 earthquake (magnitude 7.0) — far less powerful — killed over 100,000. Which best explains this?","options":{"A":"Earthquakes near the Caribbean are always more dangerous than those in the Pacific","B":"Haiti's earthquake released more energy than Japan's","C":"Development level shapes vulnerability — magnitude alone doesn't determine impact.","D":"Japan has fewer people living near the coast"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-MAGNITUDE","scaffold":"Development level shapes vulnerability: building codes, early warning systems and emergency response — the exact contrast this pairing is designed to teach. Magnitude is a direct, logarithmic measure of energy release, so B is factually wrong; Japan actually has one of the world's highest coastal population densities.","reforge":{"stem":"A magnitude 8 earthquake strikes both a high-income and a low-income country simultaneously. Which statement best predicts outcomes?","options":{"A":"The low-income country suffers far more deaths because its buildings are weaker","B":"Both countries suffer similar casualties since the magnitude is identical","C":"The high-income country suffers more because its infrastructure is more complex","D":"Casualties are determined entirely by population density, not development level"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-04","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Mount Pinatubo's 1991 eruption (VEI 6) occurred on which plate boundary, and what was a major global effect?","options":{"A":"A conservative boundary; the eruption had no measurable global effect","B":"A destructive boundary; the eruption caused a mega-tsunami across the Pacific","C":"A constructive boundary; the eruption caused global warming","D":"A destructive boundary; sulfur dioxide cooled global temperatures by 0.5°C"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-VOLCANO","scaffold":"VEI 6, destructive boundary, and a global cooling effect — one eruption, measurable worldwide impact. Pinatubo sits on a destructive (subduction) boundary, not constructive or conservative, and large sulfur dioxide eruptions cause cooling (blocking sunlight), not warming.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Mount Pinatubo's 1991 eruption cause global temperatures to fall rather than rise?","options":{"A":"Volcanic eruptions always cause long-term warming","B":"Sulfur dioxide formed a haze that reflected sunlight","C":"Pinatubo's location near the equator has no effect on climate","D":"The ash cloud trapped heat close to the Earth's surface"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-05","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Iceland's Eyjafjallajökull eruption in 2010 caused huge economic disruption but almost no deaths. Why?","options":{"A":"Its ash cloud grounded European flights for days","B":"It occurred on a destructive boundary similar to Pinatubo","C":"The eruption was extremely explosive (VEI 6+) and destroyed nearby towns","D":"Iceland had no volcanic monitoring in place"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-VOLCANO","scaffold":"Constructive boundary, low death toll, huge economic cost — over 100,000 flights cancelled. Impact isn't only measured in lives lost. Eyjafjallajökull's 2010 eruption was far smaller than Pinatubo's VEI 6 and didn't destroy nearby settlements; Iceland also has extensive volcanic monitoring.","reforge":{"stem":"A volcanic eruption causes almost no deaths but costs an industry over $1 billion. What does this show about measuring hazard impact?","options":{"A":"An eruption with no deaths cannot be considered a hazard","B":"Low death tolls mean an eruption wasn't newsworthy","C":"Impact can be economic and social, not just deaths","D":"Economic impact only matters for earthquakes, not volcanoes"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-06","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"A country builds earthquake-resistant buildings and holds regular evacuation drills. Which part of hazard management is this?","options":{"A":"Monitoring","B":"Protection and planning","C":"Prediction","D":"Modifying the hazard itself"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-RESPONSE","scaffold":"Monitor, predict, protect, plan — four different strategies. Buildings and drills sit under protection and planning. Prediction means forecasting when/where a hazard will strike; monitoring means tracking real-time hazard data; humans can't modify an earthquake or eruption itself.","reforge":{"stem":"Seismologists use satellite data to track ground deformation near a volcano before it erupts. Which part of hazard management is this?","options":{"A":"Protection — reducing the vulnerability of buildings","B":"Planning — preparing an evacuation response","C":"Modification — changing the hazard itself","D":"Monitoring — tracking hazard data for warning signs"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-07","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Hurricane Katrina made landfall as a Category 3 storm, yet caused catastrophic flooding in New Orleans. What was the main direct cause of the flooding?","options":{"A":"A secondary earthquake","B":"Katrina made direct landfall as a Category 5 storm","C":"The levees failed under the storm surge","D":"Extremely high rainfall alone, unrelated to storm surge or levees"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Levee failure under storm surge, not wind alone — New Orleans sits below sea level, making flood defences the critical factor. Katrina peaked at Category 5 over open water but weakened to a strong Category 3 by landfall; hurricanes don't trigger earthquakes.","reforge":{"stem":"New Orleans sits largely below sea level. Why does this make storm surge, rather than wind speed, the greater flooding risk during a hurricane?","options":{"A":"Surge water cannot drain and overwhelms defences","B":"Being below sea level has no effect on flood risk","C":"Wind speed always causes more flooding than storm surge","D":"Storm surge only affects cities above sea level"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-08","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Typhoon Haiyan (2013) had some of the highest landfall wind speeds ever recorded, yet its roughly 6,300 deaths were caused mainly by:","options":{"A":"Wind damage to buildings alone","B":"Cold temperatures following the storm","C":"A secondary earthquake","D":"The storm surge overwhelmed Tacloban."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Storm surge, not wind, was the deadliest element — the same lesson as Katrina, in a lower-income setting with a much higher death toll. Typhoons don't trigger earthquakes, and tropical storms are warm-weather systems, so cold temperatures weren't a factor.","reforge":{"stem":"Two tropical storms have similar wind speeds, but one causes far more deaths. Storm surge height is the key difference. What does this tell us about tropical storm hazards?","options":{"A":"Storm surge only matters for storms in the Pacific","B":"Storm surge, not wind, is often the deadliest element","C":"Wind speed is always the best predictor of deaths","D":"Death toll is unrelated to any physical characteristic of the storm"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the explanation of a destructive plate boundary.","template":"At a destructive boundary, the ___ oceanic plate is subducted beneath the ___ continental plate. Friction and melting at the subduction zone cause both ___ and volcanic activity.","blanks":["denser","lighter","earthquakes"],"bank":["denser","lighter","earthquakes","thinner","heavier","tsunamis"],"tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-BOUNDARY","scaffold":"Destructive boundaries: the denser oceanic plate sinks beneath the lighter continental plate. The friction and melting this causes produces both earthquakes and volcanic activity — not just one or the other.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about the 2010 Haiti earthquake.","template":"Haiti's 2010 earthquake occurred on a ___ boundary, where the Caribbean and North American plates slide past each other. Its ___ focus meant the shaking at the surface was especially intense.","blanks":["conservative","shallow"],"bank":["conservative","shallow","destructive","deep"],"tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-BOUNDARY","scaffold":"Haiti sits on a conservative (transform) boundary. Because the earthquake's focus was shallow, less energy was absorbed by rock before reaching the surface, intensifying the shaking despite a 'moderate' 7.0 magnitude.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the comparison between the Tohoku and Haiti earthquakes.","template":"Japan's Tohoku earthquake was far more ___ than Haiti's, yet caused far fewer deaths, because Japan's greater ___ funded earthquake-resistant construction and an effective early warning system.","blanks":["powerful","wealth"],"bank":["powerful","wealth","weak","population"],"tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-MAGNITUDE","scaffold":"Magnitude is a direct measure of energy release, so Tohoku (9.0) was far more powerful than Haiti (7.0). But wealth and development level — building codes, warning systems, emergency response — determine vulnerability, which is why Haiti's death toll was so much higher.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Mount Pinatubo's 1991 eruption.","template":"Mount Pinatubo erupted on a ___ boundary. The huge volume of ___ dioxide released formed a haze that reflected sunlight, causing global temperatures to ___ by about 0.5°C.","blanks":["destructive","sulfur","fall"],"bank":["destructive","sulfur","fall","constructive","carbon","rise"],"tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-VOLCANO","scaffold":"Pinatubo sits on a destructive boundary. Large sulfur dioxide eruptions form a haze that reflects sunlight back into space, causing global cooling rather than warming.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-FB-05","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Eyjafjallajökull's 2010 eruption.","template":"Eyjafjallajökull sits on a ___ boundary and caused almost no deaths, but its fine ash cloud grounded European flights for days — showing that hazard impact can be ___, not just measured in lives lost.","blanks":["constructive","economic"],"bank":["constructive","economic","destructive","social"],"tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-VOLCANO","scaffold":"Iceland sits on a constructive boundary, producing relatively low-explosivity activity. Despite almost no deaths, the eruption cost the airline industry over $1 billion — proof that impact isn't only measured in deaths.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-FB-06","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about hazard management strategies.","template":"Building earthquake-resistant structures and running evacuation drills are examples of ___ and planning, while using satellite data to track ground deformation before an eruption is an example of ___.","blanks":["protection","monitoring"],"bank":["protection","monitoring","prediction","modification"],"tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-RESPONSE","scaffold":"Hazard management has four strands: monitor, predict, protect, plan. Buildings and drills reduce vulnerability and prepare a response (protection/planning); tracking real-time data like ground deformation is monitoring, not prediction.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-FB-07","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Hurricane Katrina's flooding.","template":"Katrina made landfall as 'only' a Category 3 storm, but New Orleans' ___ failed under the storm surge. Because the city sits below sea level, ___ rather than wind speed was the greater flooding risk.","blanks":["levees","storm surge"],"bank":["levees","storm surge","bridges","wind"],"tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"New Orleans' levees and flood walls failed under the storm surge, flooding roughly 80% of the city. Because the city sits below sea level, seawater pushed inland by the surge had nowhere to drain — making storm surge, not wind, the critical factor.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-FB-08","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Typhoon Haiyan's death toll.","template":"Despite record landfall wind speeds, most of Typhoon Haiyan's roughly 6,300 deaths were caused by the ___, a wall of seawater around 7.5 metres high that overwhelmed low-lying areas like ___.","blanks":["storm surge","Tacloban"],"bank":["storm surge","Tacloban","wind","Manila"],"tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"As with Katrina, storm surge — not wind — was the deadliest element. A roughly 7.5m wall of seawater overwhelmed low-lying coastal areas like Tacloban, in a lower-income setting with a much higher death toll than Katrina.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-09","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Which is the correct definition of the asthenosphere?","options":{"A":"The semi-molten upper mantle layer that flows and carries the plates above","B":"The molten layer of iron and nickel that surrounds the inner core","C":"The thicker, less dense crust that makes up the continents","D":"The solid rock that lies on top of the mantle, broken up into tectonic plates"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Layer order from the surface down: crust → lithosphere (crust + rigid upper mantle) → asthenosphere (semi-molten, flows via convection, carries the plates) → lower mantle → outer core (molten) → inner core (solid). B correctly describes the asthenosphere. C describes continental crust, while D describes the lithosphere — the solid crust and rigid upper mantle broken into tectonic plates.","reforge":{"stem":"Which layer lies directly beneath the lithosphere and allows tectonic plates to move?","options":{"A":"The continental crust, because it is thick and less dense","B":"The inner core, because it is solid iron and nickel","C":"The asthenosphere, which is semi-molten and flows","D":"The outer core, because it surrounds the inner core"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-10","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"A student mixes up 'lithosphere' and 'asthenosphere'. What is the key difference?","options":{"A":"They are two names for exactly the same layer","B":"The rigid lithosphere sits on the flowing asthenosphere","C":"The lithosphere lies beneath the asthenosphere","D":"The asthenosphere is solid and the lithosphere is semi-molten"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Lithosphere = solid, broken into tectonic plates. Asthenosphere = semi-molten, flows due to convection currents, and it's this flow that drags the lithospheric plates above it — the mechanism behind plate movement.","reforge":{"stem":"A student says 'the lithosphere flows and carries the asthenosphere along with it.' What is wrong with this statement?","options":{"A":"Neither layer is capable of movement","B":"The statement is completely correct","C":"The lithosphere is semi-molten, not the asthenosphere","D":"It is backwards — the asthenosphere flows"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-11","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"What is subduction?","options":{"A":"The rise in sea level caused by a tropical cyclone pushing water onto the shore","B":"A process where two plates slide past each other with no vertical movement","C":"One plate is pushed below another and sinks into the asthenosphere","D":"A process where magma rises through the middle of a plate, away from any boundary"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-BOUNDARY","scaffold":"Subduction only happens at destructive (convergent) boundaries, and usually involves the denser oceanic plate sinking beneath continental or older oceanic crust. B describes a conservative boundary, C correctly describes subduction, and D describes a hotspot — a good example of how exam distractors swap in definitions of nearby key terms.","reforge":{"stem":"Subduction always involves which type of crust sinking beneath the other at a destructive boundary?","options":{"A":"The denser oceanic crust sinks beneath","B":"Both plates sink together at equal rates","C":"Subduction can occur at a conservative boundary","D":"Continental crust always sinks beneath oceanic crust"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-12","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"How does a hotspot differ from a normal volcanic plate boundary?","options":{"A":"A hotspot is where two plates move apart","B":"A hotspot is another name for a destructive plate boundary","C":"A hotspot only occurs at conservative boundaries","D":"A hotspot rises through the middle of a plate"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-HOTSPOT","scaffold":"Hawaii is the classic hotspot example — it sits in the middle of the Pacific Plate, nowhere near a boundary. This is why hotspot volcanism catches students out: they expect all volcanoes to sit on boundaries.","reforge":{"stem":"Hawaii's volcanoes sit in the middle of the Pacific Plate, far from any plate boundary. What best explains this?","options":{"A":"A destructive plate boundary running directly under Hawaii","B":"A hotspot — hot magma rising through mid-plate crust","C":"A conservative plate boundary running directly under Hawaii","D":"Hawaii cannot have volcanoes since it is mid-plate"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-13","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"What distinguishes primary effects of a hazard from secondary effects?","options":{"A":"Primary effects are direct; secondary are knock-on","B":"Primary effects only apply to earthquakes; secondary effects only apply to volcanoes","C":"There is no real difference — the terms are interchangeable","D":"Primary effects happen years after the hazard; secondary effects happen immediately"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Example: an earthquake collapsing buildings is a primary effect; a fire caused by a ruptured gas main afterwards is a secondary effect. Don't confuse this pairing with immediate/long-term responses, which is about timing of the response, not the type of effect.","reforge":{"stem":"A collapsed building is a primary effect of an earthquake. Which of these is a SECONDARY effect of the same earthquake?","options":{"A":"The initial collapse of the building","B":"Cracks appearing in walls the moment the earthquake strikes","C":"A fire from a gas main ruptured by the shaking","D":"The ground shaking itself"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-14","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"A student writes 'immediate responses happen straight after a hazard, long-term responses happen much later' but can't give an example of either. Which pair correctly illustrates the distinction?","options":{"A":"Immediate responses are always carried out by NGOs, long-term responses always by governments","B":"Immediate: search and rescue. Long-term: rebuilding to code","C":"Immediate: rebuilding infrastructure over several years. Long-term: emergency shelters provided within hours","D":"Both immediate and long-term responses happen in the same week"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-RESPONSE","scaffold":"Immediate responses = hours to about a week (search and rescue, emergency shelter, first aid). Long-term responses = weeks to years afterwards (rebuilding, improved building codes, economic recovery). B has the timeframes swapped.","reforge":{"stem":"Which pair correctly matches an example to immediate and long-term responses after a hurricane?","options":{"A":"Both examples describe immediate responses","B":"Both examples describe long-term responses","C":"Immediate: rebuilding sea defences over several years. Long-term: emergency shelters within days","D":"Immediate: emergency shelters. Long-term: new sea defences"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-15","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"How should hazard risk be defined?","options":{"A":"A hazard caused by processes on the land, as opposed to the atmosphere","B":"The magnitude of a hazard on the Richter scale","C":"The probability a hazard will harm a population","D":"The total economic cost of a hazard once it has occurred"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-VULNERABILITY","scaffold":"Hazard risk is about the probability of harm to a population, not just the physical size of the event — it depends on the hazard, exposure and vulnerability together. A geological hazard is the physical process itself; risk is the potential for that process to cause harm.","reforge":{"stem":"Two hazards have the same magnitude, but one occurs in a densely populated, poorly-prepared area. What best describes the difference in hazard risk?","options":{"A":"The dense, poorly-prepared area, since risk needs people","B":"Hazard risk is identical in both cases since magnitude is identical","C":"Hazard risk only depends on the Richter scale reading","D":"Hazard risk cannot differ between two hazards of the same magnitude"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-16","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"What is the correct definition of the Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ)?","options":{"A":"The point where the Hadley and Ferrel cells meet at the poles","B":"A belt at 30° north and south where air permanently sinks and no rain falls","C":"A high-pressure belt found only over the Sahara Desert","D":"A low-pressure belt near the equator where winds converge"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"The ITCZ sits roughly over the equator, where the Hadley cells of each hemisphere converge — rising warm, moist air creates low pressure and heavy rainfall, and it's the ITCZ's seasonal shift north and south that drives many tropical wet/dry seasons.","reforge":{"stem":"Which option correctly explains why the ITCZ brings heavy rainfall near the equator?","options":{"A":"Cold, dry air sinks at the equator, creating high pressure and no rainfall","B":"Warm, moist air converges and rises, creating low pressure and rain","C":"The ITCZ is a permanently fixed feature that never shifts throughout the year","D":"The ITCZ only exists over oceans, never over land"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-17","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"A Hadley cell and a Ferrel cell are both part of atmospheric circulation. What is the key difference between them?","options":{"A":"Hadley air rises at the equator; Ferrel air rises at 60°","B":"They are two names for the same atmospheric cell","C":"A Hadley cell only exists in the Southern Hemisphere, a Ferrel cell only in the Northern Hemisphere","D":"A Ferrel cell is found only over oceans, a Hadley cell only over land"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Three-cell model, low to high latitude: Hadley cell (0°–30°) → Ferrel cell (30°–60°) → Polar cell (60°–90°). Each cell's rising-air edge produces low pressure and rain; each sinking-air edge produces high pressure and dry conditions — this is why deserts cluster around 30° north and south.","reforge":{"stem":"In the three-cell model of atmospheric circulation, where does air sink to create the world's major hot desert belts?","options":{"A":"At 60° north and south, where the Ferrel and Polar cells meet","B":"At the equator, where the ITCZ is located","C":"Around 30°, where Hadley and Ferrel air both sink","D":"Only at the poles, at 90° north and south"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-18","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"A tropical storm forms in the Atlantic and moves towards the Caribbean. A very similar storm forms in the western Pacific and moves towards the Philippines. What are these two storms called respectively?","options":{"A":"Cyclone; hurricane","B":"Hurricane; typhoon","C":"Typhoon; hurricane","D":"Hurricane; cyclone"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-FORMATION","scaffold":"Same phenomenon (tropical cyclone), different regional names: hurricane (USA, Latin America, Caribbean), typhoon (India, Japan, Philippines), cyclone (Oceania, Madagascar). Examiners sometimes ask you to name the correct regional term for a given location.","reforge":{"stem":"A storm forms in the western Pacific and moves towards Japan and the Philippines. What is this storm correctly called?","options":{"A":"A hurricane","B":"A tornado","C":"A cyclone","D":"A typhoon"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-19","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Inside a tropical cyclone, what is the difference between the eye and the eyewall?","options":{"A":"The eyewall is at the centre of the storm; the eye surrounds it","B":"The eye has the strongest winds; the eyewall is calm","C":"The eye is calm and low-pressure; the eyewall is violent","D":"They are the same feature, just named differently by different countries"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Counter-intuitive but commonly tested: the very centre (the eye) is the calmest part of the storm, while the ring immediately around it (the eyewall) is the most violent and destructive part.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the most accurate description of conditions inside the eye of a tropical cyclone?","options":{"A":"Extremely low pressure and calm, unlike the eyewall","B":"High pressure and freezing temperatures","C":"The strongest winds and heaviest rainfall in the entire storm","D":"Identical wind speed and rainfall to the eyewall"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-20","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"What is the difference between the greenhouse effect and the enhanced greenhouse effect?","options":{"A":"The enhanced greenhouse effect only happens at the poles","B":"They are identical processes with different names used in different countries","C":"The greenhouse effect is caused by humans; the enhanced greenhouse effect is entirely natural","D":"The enhanced effect is extra warming from human emissions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-GREENHOUSE","scaffold":"Without any greenhouse effect, Earth would be too cold for life — it's natural and essential. The 'enhanced' version is the problem: burning fossil fuels adds extra greenhouse gases, trapping more energy and raising global temperatures faster than natural systems can adjust to.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the enhanced greenhouse effect considered a problem, when the natural greenhouse effect is essential for life on Earth?","options":{"A":"The natural greenhouse effect no longer exists","B":"Extra greenhouse gases trap more energy than natural levels","C":"Both effects are identical and equally beneficial","D":"The enhanced greenhouse effect is entirely natural and unrelated to human activity"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-21","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Which of these is a correct source of evidence for past climate change, and what does it show?","options":{"A":"Ice cores — trapped air and dust record past conditions","B":"Storm surge records — a measure of past tropical cyclone wind speed only","C":"The Richter scale — a logarithmic measure of past earthquake magnitudes","D":"The Coriolis Effect — the effect of Earth's rotation on wind, unrelated to past climate"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-EVIDENCE","scaffold":"Ice cores and tree rings are the two named proxy evidence sources in this glossary. Ice cores trap air bubbles (past atmospheric CO2) and dust layers; tree rings record annual growth conditions (wider rings = warmer/wetter years). B, C and D are unrelated tectonic or atmospheric terms used as distractors.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are ice cores considered a reliable source of evidence for past climate change?","options":{"A":"They measure earthquake magnitude, not climate","B":"They can only be extracted from tropical rainforests","C":"They trap air from the time the ice formed","D":"They only show evidence from the last ten years"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-22","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Milankovitch Cycles is an umbrella term. Which three processes does it cover?","options":{"A":"Subduction, convection currents, and plate boundaries","B":"Eccentricity, obliquity and precession","C":"Primary effects, secondary effects, and long-term responses","D":"The Hadley, Ferrel and Polar cells"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-NATURAL-CAUSE","scaffold":"These three astronomical cycles change how much solar energy reaches Earth and where, over tens of thousands of years, and are a key natural cause of long-term climate change — distinct from the human-driven enhanced greenhouse effect.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of the following is one of the three named Milankovitch Cycles?","options":{"A":"Subduction — a plate sinking beneath another plate","B":"The ITCZ — the zone where global winds converge","C":"The Coriolis Effect — the effect of Earth's rotation on wind","D":"Obliquity — the changing tilt of Earth's axis"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-23","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"A student says earthquakes at conservative boundaries are 'less dangerous' because no volcanoes form there. Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Conservative boundaries cannot produce earthquakes","B":"Conservative boundaries only occur under oceans","C":"Risk depends on depth, density and preparedness","D":"Volcanoes are the only serious tectonic hazard"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-BOUNDARY","scaffold":"Conservative boundaries can produce very damaging shallow earthquakes because plates lock, stress builds, then is released suddenly. Hazard risk is not just the physical event: exposure, vulnerability, building quality and preparedness control the scale of disaster.","reforge":{"stem":"A magnitude 7 earthquake hits a densely populated city with weak building codes on a conservative boundary. Which statement is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Shallow shaking and exposure can still be destructive","B":"It will only create lava flows","C":"It is automatically safer than any constructive boundary","D":"It cannot be serious because there is no volcano"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-24","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why do tropical cyclones usually weaken quickly after moving over land?","options":{"A":"They turn into anticyclones as soon as they cross the coast","B":"The Coriolis effect stops completely over land","C":"Air pressure rises because mountains create earthquakes","D":"They lose warm ocean water and gain land friction"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-FORMATION","scaffold":"Tropical cyclones are powered by heat and moisture from warm seas, normally above about 27°C. Once over land, that energy source is cut off and surface friction disrupts the storm's circulation, so wind speeds usually fall.","reforge":{"stem":"A tropical cyclone crosses a coastline and moves inland. Which change is most likely after several hours?","options":{"A":"It strengthens because land is warmer than the sea","B":"Ocean moisture is cut off and friction increases","C":"It becomes a tectonic hazard","D":"It stops rotating because Earth no longer spins beneath it"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-25","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"A student writes: 'Monitoring means predicting the exact date of an earthquake.' What is the correction?","options":{"A":"Monitoring assesses risk; prediction cannot","B":"Monitoring only means rebuilding after a disaster","C":"Monitoring guarantees that all hazards can be stopped","D":"Monitoring is the same as evacuation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-RESPONSE","scaffold":"Hazard monitoring reduces risk by tracking warning signs and informing preparation, but it does not remove uncertainty. Volcanic eruptions can sometimes be forecast from signs such as gas emissions and ground deformation; earthquakes cannot be predicted to an exact date and time.","reforge":{"stem":"Scientists detect increasing sulphur dioxide emissions and ground swelling around a volcano. What does this monitoring allow them to do?","options":{"A":"Cancel the eruption","B":"Predict the exact minute of an earthquake","C":"Assess rising risk and warn authorities","D":"Prove the volcano is extinct"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-26","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Which factor most clearly explains why climate change can increase tropical cyclone hazard risk?","options":{"A":"Climate change creates new plate boundaries","B":"Warmer oceans give storms more energy","C":"Tropical cyclones only form in cold polar seas","D":"Climate change prevents storm surges from forming"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-FORMATION","scaffold":"Tropical cyclones need warm ocean water. Climate change does not mean every storm becomes stronger, but warmer seas and a warmer atmosphere can increase rainfall intensity and the potential for very intense storms, while sea-level rise worsens storm-surge flooding.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a similar tropical cyclone cause worse coastal flooding in a warmer future climate?","options":{"A":"Earthquakes become more common in the eyewall","B":"The Coriolis effect disappears","C":"Storms no longer need warm ocean water","D":"Higher sea level raises the surge baseline"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-27","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why do many subtropical deserts occur around 30° north and south of the Equator?","options":{"A":"Warm ocean currents remove all water vapour from the atmosphere","B":"Rising air creates permanent low pressure and heavy rainfall","C":"Descending air creates high pressure","D":"Polar air sinks directly over every subtropical landmass"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"In the Hadley cells, air rises near the Equator, moves poleward at altitude and descends around 30° north and south. Descending air creates high pressure, limiting cloud formation and rainfall, which helps explain the global distribution of arid areas.","reforge":{"stem":"A region near 30°S has frequent clear skies and very little rainfall. Which circulation process best explains this?","options":{"A":"Air descends, creating high pressure","B":"Warm air rises continuously over the region","C":"The ITCZ remains fixed above the region all year","D":"A polar cell brings saturated air from the South Pole"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-28","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"What can gases trapped in an ice core reveal about past climate?","options":{"A":"The exact number of earthquakes in every year","B":"The precise population of each ancient settlement","C":"Only the direction of ocean currents","D":"Past air and greenhouse gases"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-EVIDENCE","scaffold":"Air bubbles trapped in ice preserve samples of past atmospheres, while the ice layers provide a time sequence. Scientists can use them alongside other evidence, such as tree rings and historical records, to reconstruct climate change.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are ice cores useful evidence for natural climate change?","options":{"A":"They show the exact future temperature for every year","B":"They provide preserved samples from past atmospheres","C":"They record only modern industrial emissions","D":"They measure the strength of every past ocean current"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-29","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Which human activity is a significant source of methane?","options":{"A":"Livestock and landfill","B":"Using satellites to track storms","C":"Building stone walls on upland slopes","D":"Planting trees in a protected forest"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-GREENHOUSE","scaffold":"Methane is released by sources including livestock digestion, rice cultivation, landfill decomposition and fossil-fuel extraction. It is a greenhouse gas, so human activity can increase the enhanced greenhouse effect through more than carbon dioxide alone.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can expanded cattle farming contribute to global warming?","options":{"A":"Cattle absorb all carbon dioxide","B":"Cattle stop ocean currents moving","C":"Methane from livestock","D":"Cattle block sunlight from Earth"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-30","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why do climate projections for 2100 show a range rather than one certain value?","options":{"A":"Climate models cannot use any observed evidence","B":"Future pathways and model limits","C":"Sea level cannot respond to temperature change","D":"Future climate is controlled only by daily weather"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-PROJECTION","scaffold":"Projections depend on future emissions pathways, the response of ice sheets and oceans, and how models represent feedbacks such as albedo and water vapour. A range communicates uncertainty; it does not mean that climate change evidence is absent.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one reason sea-level projections contain uncertainty?","options":{"A":"Sea level is unrelated to ocean warming","B":"Tide gauges cannot record any present-day change","C":"All projections assume the same future emissions pathway","D":"Future emissions are not known exactly"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-31","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why do tropical cyclones usually form more than about 5° from the Equator?","options":{"A":"High pressure prevents evaporation in all equatorial oceans","B":"Sea temperatures are always below 27°C at the Equator","C":"Coriolis effect is weak at the Equator","D":"The ITCZ never produces rising air near the Equator"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-FORMATION","scaffold":"Tropical cyclones need warm ocean water, rising moist air and rotation supplied by the Coriolis effect. The Coriolis effect is too weak at the Equator, so most cyclones form several degrees north or south before tracking poleward.","reforge":{"stem":"A tropical cyclone begins at 12°N rather than directly on the Equator. Which condition is especially important?","options":{"A":"Sufficient Coriolis rotation","B":"The sea surface must be frozen","C":"Air must sink in the storm centre","D":"The storm must form over a mountain range"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-32","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why does a tropical cyclone often intensify over warm ocean water?","options":{"A":"Friction with warm water removes energy","B":"The Coriolis effect disappears","C":"Cold air sinks into the eye","D":"Condensation releases heat"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-FORMATION","scaffold":"Warm ocean water increases evaporation. Moist air rises, cools and condenses, releasing latent heat that powers further uplift and lowers pressure. This positive feedback can intensify the cyclone while the storm remains over a warm sea.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a tropical cyclone weaken after moving over colder water?","options":{"A":"Cold water always increases the storm's rainfall","B":"Colder water supplies less energy","C":"The eye becomes a high-pressure anticyclone immediately","D":"The storm gains more energy from surface friction"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-33","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why are composite volcanoes often more explosive than shield volcanoes?","options":{"A":"Viscous magma traps gas","B":"They only form at conservative boundaries","C":"Their lava is always hotter and more fluid","D":"They have no ash or pyroclastic material"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-VOLCANO","scaffold":"Composite volcanoes commonly form above subduction zones where viscous, silica-rich magma traps gases. Pressure can build until an explosive eruption occurs. Shield volcanoes usually have more fluid basaltic lava and gentler slopes.","reforge":{"stem":"A volcano has steep sides and erupts ash and pyroclastic flows. Which explanation is most likely?","options":{"A":"It has only low-viscosity basaltic lava","B":"It is a shield volcano at a constructive boundary","C":"Viscous magma traps gases","D":"It has no ash or pyroclastic material"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-34","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Which is a secondary impact of an earthquake?","options":{"A":"Buildings collapsing during the initial shaking","B":"A tsunami after seafloor movement","C":"Roads being damaged by the first seismic waves","D":"People being injured by falling masonry immediately"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Primary impacts happen during the earthquake itself, such as collapsed buildings and damaged roads. A tsunami caused by seafloor displacement is a secondary hazard that follows the initial ground movement and can extend impacts along coasts.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a tsunami be classed as a secondary hazard following an undersea earthquake?","options":{"A":"It is the same event as the first ground shaking","B":"It only affects inland mountain areas","C":"It happens before the earthquake begins","D":"Seabed displacement follows the shaking"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-35","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"What is the main role of the Ferrel cell in global atmospheric circulation?","options":{"A":"It creates permanent equatorial low pressure","B":"It produces every ocean current directly","C":"Mid-latitude air transfer","D":"It prevents weather systems in mid-latitudes"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"The Ferrel cell lies between the Hadley and Polar cells and helps transfer heat through mid-latitude westerlies.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are prevailing winds commonly westerly across the UK?","options":{"A":"Deflected Ferrel-cell airflow","B":"Polar air moves east at the Equator","C":"The Hadley cell reaches the North Pole","D":"Ocean currents reverse Earth's rotation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-36","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"How can a warm ocean current affect a nearby west-facing coast?","options":{"A":"It guarantees desert conditions inland","B":"It stops atmospheric circulation above the sea","C":"It freezes coastal water throughout winter","D":"It raises coastal temperatures and moisture"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-OCEAN","scaffold":"Warm currents transfer heat to the atmosphere, moderating nearby coastal temperatures and often increasing available moisture.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is western Europe milder than some places at the same latitude?","options":{"A":"Mountain ranges create tropical conditions","B":"Warm Atlantic heat transfer","C":"The Polar cell only operates over Asia","D":"Europe receives no cold air in winter"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-37","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why should tree-ring evidence be combined with other climate proxies?","options":{"A":"Several local factors affect growth","B":"Tree rings extend equally far back everywhere","C":"Wide rings always prove higher temperatures","D":"Every tree records global sea level exactly"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-EVIDENCE","scaffold":"Ring width can be affected by temperature, rainfall, disease and competition, so comparison with ice cores and historical evidence improves confidence.","reforge":{"stem":"A tree has unusually narrow rings for five years. What is the safest interpretation?","options":{"A":"Global climate certainly cooled everywhere","B":"The tree's age can no longer be estimated","C":"Less favourable local growth","D":"Atmospheric carbon dioxide fell to zero"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-38","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"How can a major volcanic eruption temporarily cool global climate?","options":{"A":"Lava absorbs heat from every ocean basin","B":"Sulphate aerosols reflect incoming radiation","C":"Volcanoes stop greenhouse gases forming","D":"Ash permanently blocks all incoming sunlight"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-NATURAL-CAUSE","scaffold":"Sulphur-rich gases can form aerosols that reflect solar radiation, producing short-lived cooling despite volcanic carbon emissions.","reforge":{"stem":"Which observation would support volcanic cooling after an eruption?","options":{"A":"All rainfall stops near every volcano","B":"Sea level rises before any eruption occurs","C":"Solar output permanently doubles worldwide","D":"Aerosol-related cooling"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-39","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why does ocean warming contribute to sea-level rise?","options":{"A":"Warm water becomes much denser and sinks","B":"Ocean heating creates new continental crust","C":"Warm seawater expands in volume","D":"Evaporation permanently removes ocean water"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-OCEAN","scaffold":"Thermal expansion occurs as seawater warms; melting land ice adds a separate contribution to sea-level rise.","reforge":{"stem":"Which process raises sea level without adding new water to the oceans?","options":{"A":"Thermal expansion of seawater","B":"Formation of sea ice in winter","C":"Increased snowfall on Antarctica","D":"Melting of a valley glacier"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-40","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why do future climate projections use several emissions pathways?","options":{"A":"Past temperature records cannot be measured","B":"Every model assumes greenhouse gases vanish","C":"Sea level responds only to volcanic activity","D":"Future human choices remain uncertain"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-PROJECTION","scaffold":"Future population, energy use, technology and policy are uncertain, so models test contrasting emissions scenarios rather than one fixed future.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a wider projection range by 2100 mainly indicate?","options":{"A":"All models use identical assumptions","B":"Long-term uncertainty grows","C":"Daily weather cannot be forecast","D":"Observed warming has not occurred"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-41","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Where are the strongest winds usually found in a mature tropical cyclone?","options":{"A":"In the eyewall surrounding the eye","B":"Across every outer rain band equally","C":"At the cold front behind the cyclone","D":"Inside the calm centre of the eye"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The eyewall contains intense uplift, lowest pressure gradients and the strongest winds; the eye itself is relatively calm.","reforge":{"stem":"A satellite image shows a clear eye surrounded by dense cloud. What does the cloud ring mark?","options":{"A":"A cold ocean current beneath the storm","B":"A zone where air only sinks","C":"The eyewall with intense convection","D":"The point where Coriolis becomes zero"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-42","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"What most directly generates a tropical-cyclone storm surge?","options":{"A":"Earthquake movement beneath the seabed","B":"Strong winds push seawater toward the coast","C":"River deposition raises offshore islands","D":"Cold air freezes water across the shoreline"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Onshore winds pile water against the coast, while low atmospheric pressure also allows sea level to rise locally.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a shallow, funnel-shaped bay especially vulnerable to storm surge?","options":{"A":"It removes all wave energy offshore","B":"It causes sea level to fall at high tide","C":"It prevents wind acting on the sea surface","D":"Water is funnelled coastward"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-43","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"How can GIS help monitor a tropical cyclone?","options":{"A":"It prevents the cyclone changing direction","B":"It measures only damage after landfall","C":"It maps changing position and exposure","D":"It replaces all satellite observations"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-RESPONSE","scaffold":"GIS can combine forecast tracks with population, elevation and infrastructure layers to identify places at risk as a storm moves.","reforge":{"stem":"A forecast track shifts east overnight. What should emergency planners update first?","options":{"A":"The mapped evacuation zones at risk","B":"The historical name of the cyclone","C":"The latitude of the Equator","D":"The plate-boundary map of the region"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-44","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Which physical factor can make a coastal community more vulnerable to cyclones?","options":{"A":"A high proportion of trained doctors","B":"Strong building codes across the settlement","C":"Several well-maintained evacuation routes","D":"Low-lying land exposed to storm surge"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-VULNERABILITY","scaffold":"Low elevation and direct coastal exposure increase the likelihood and depth of surge flooding, regardless of national wealth.","reforge":{"stem":"How can mangrove removal increase cyclone vulnerability?","options":{"A":"It strengthens every coastal building","B":"It removes a natural barrier to waves","C":"It makes weather forecasts less accurate","D":"It lowers the local population density"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-45","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why might an evacuation warning fail to prevent cyclone deaths?","options":{"A":"People may lack transport or trust","B":"Warnings automatically strengthen weak houses","C":"Every household owns private transport","D":"Forecasts always show the exact landfall point"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-VULNERABILITY","scaffold":"Evacuation effectiveness depends on communication, trust, mobility, shelter capacity and whether people can afford to leave.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action would most improve an evacuation plan in an informal settlement?","options":{"A":"Close shelters before the storm arrives","B":"Issue warnings only through paid television","C":"Provide transport and accessible shelters","D":"Assume all residents own a car"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-46","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why can tectonic plates move over the asthenosphere?","options":{"A":"The inner core is completely liquid","B":"The asthenosphere can deform slowly","C":"Continental crust is denser than the core","D":"The mantle contains no thermal energy"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The asthenosphere is hot and mechanically weak enough to flow slowly, allowing rigid lithospheric plates to move above it.","reforge":{"stem":"Which description best distinguishes lithosphere from asthenosphere?","options":{"A":"The lithosphere consists only of ocean water","B":"The asthenosphere forms the solid inner core","C":"Both layers are equally rigid and cold","D":"Rigid plates overlie a weaker layer"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-47","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Why do volcanic islands in a hotspot chain often become older with distance from the active volcano?","options":{"A":"The hotspot moves rapidly across a fixed plate","B":"Ocean currents transport volcanoes after eruption","C":"The plate moves over a near-fixed plume","D":"Older islands pull the mantle plume sideways"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-HOTSPOT","scaffold":"A relatively stationary mantle plume forms new volcanoes as the plate moves, leaving extinct and older islands progressively farther away.","reforge":{"stem":"What evidence most strongly supports a moving plate above a hotspot?","options":{"A":"Island ages increase along the chain","B":"Every island erupts at the same time","C":"The chain crosses no oceanic crust","D":"Earthquakes stop beneath the active island"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-48","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"Where are deep-focus earthquakes most likely to occur?","options":{"A":"At a divergent ridge with new crust forming","B":"Along every conservative boundary equally","C":"At a hotspot far from any plate margin","D":"Within a descending subduction slab"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-BOUNDARY","scaffold":"At convergent boundaries, a subducting plate remains brittle at depth and generates earthquakes along the Benioff zone.","reforge":{"stem":"A cross-section shows earthquake foci becoming deeper inland from a trench. What does this indicate?","options":{"A":"Two plates moving apart at a ridge","B":"An oceanic plate is subducting","C":"A mantle plume rising vertically","D":"No plate movement is occurring"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-49","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","stem":"A magnitude 7 earthquake releases roughly how much more energy than a magnitude 6 event?","options":{"A":"About thirty-two times as much energy","B":"About seven times as much energy","C":"Exactly one hundred times as much energy","D":"About twice as much energy"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-MAGNITUDE","scaffold":"Earthquake magnitude is logarithmic: a one-unit increase represents about 32 times more energy release.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a magnitude 8 event far more powerful than a magnitude 7 event?","options":{"A":"All magnitude 8 events have identical impacts","B":"Magnitude increases by one joule at each level","C":"About 32 times more energy","D":"The scale measures only the length of shaking"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-BOUNDARY","stem":"Iceland sits on a constructive boundary and has frequent eruptions but almost no large earthquakes. Japan sits on a destructive boundary and has both. What explains the difference?","options":{"A":"Iceland is too far north in latitude for large earthquakes to occur","B":"Constructive plates pull apart with little friction, so stress stays low","C":"Destructive boundaries have no volcanic activity associated with them","D":"Earthquake magnitude depends on the density of the population living above it"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Boundary type sets the hazard profile. At a constructive boundary plates move apart, magma rises easily and little stress builds, so quakes are shallow and small. At a destructive boundary one plate is forced beneath another; friction locks the plates, stress accumulates and releases as large, deep earthquakes, alongside explosive volcanism.","reforge":{"stem":"A plate boundary produces frequent shallow, low-magnitude earthquakes and gentle basaltic eruptions. Which type is it most likely to be?","options":{"A":"A destructive boundary where oceanic crust is being subducted","B":"A conservative boundary where two plates slide past one another","C":"A collision boundary between two plates of continental crust","D":"A constructive boundary where two plates are moving apart"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-VULNERABILITY","stem":"The 2010 Haiti earthquake (magnitude 7.0) killed over 200,000 people; the 2010 Chile earthquake (magnitude 8.8) killed around 500. What best explains this?","options":{"A":"Chile's earthquake released far less energy than the Haitian one did","B":"Death tolls from earthquakes depend only on the magnitude recorded","C":"Chile had stronger building codes, planning and emergency capacity","D":"Haiti's earthquake happened at night when nobody was in any buildings"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Magnitude is the hazard; deaths are the disaster. Chile's quake released roughly 500 times more energy, but decades of enforced seismic building codes, wealth and a functioning emergency service converted it into a survivable event. Haiti's poverty, unreinforced concrete and weak governance meant a smaller quake destroyed the capital.","reforge":{"stem":"Two countries experience earthquakes of the same magnitude, but one suffers thousands of deaths and the other very few. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Vulnerability and preparedness shape impacts as much as magnitude does","B":"One of the two magnitude readings must simply have been recorded wrongly","C":"Earthquake depth is the only factor that ever influences a death toll","D":"Richer countries always experience earthquakes of a lower magnitude"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-MAGNITUDE","stem":"The moment magnitude scale is logarithmic. Roughly how much more energy does a magnitude 7 earthquake release than a magnitude 6?","options":{"A":"About ten times as much energy is released by the larger event","B":"Exactly the same amount, because the scale measures depth not energy","C":"About one and a half times as much energy as the smaller event","D":"About 32 times as much energy as the magnitude 6 event"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Each whole step on the moment magnitude scale is a tenfold increase in ground-shaking amplitude but roughly a 32-fold increase in energy released. That is why a magnitude 8 is not marginally worse than a magnitude 7 but catastrophically worse, and why comparing raw magnitude numbers without that scaling misleads.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a magnitude 8 earthquake vastly more destructive than a magnitude 6, rather than only slightly worse?","options":{"A":"Because magnitude 8 earthquakes always occur directly beneath large cities","B":"The scale is logarithmic, so each step multiplies the energy released","C":"Because the magnitude scale increases in fixed, equal energy steps","D":"Because deeper earthquakes always cause more damage at the surface"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-HOTSPOT","stem":"Hawaii has active volcanoes but lies thousands of kilometres from any plate boundary. What explains this?","options":{"A":"A hotspot: a rising mantle plume melts through the plate above it","B":"The Pacific plate boundary has recently migrated to run through Hawaii","C":"Volcanoes on islands do not require a magma source beneath them","D":"Hawaii sits directly above a subduction zone that is hidden by the ocean"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"A hotspot is a fixed plume of unusually hot mantle material. The plate slides over it, so the plume burns through the crust and builds a volcano; as the plate moves on, that volcano goes extinct and a new one forms. This produces the Hawaiian island chain, with age increasing away from the active end.","reforge":{"stem":"An island chain becomes progressively older along its length, with only the island at one end still volcanically active. What does this pattern indicate?","options":{"A":"The islands were all formed simultaneously by a single eruption","B":"A destructive plate boundary runs along the length of the whole chain","C":"A plate has moved steadily over a stationary mantle hotspot","D":"The islands are moving independently of the plate they sit on"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-FORMATION","stem":"Tropical cyclones do not form within about 5° of the Equator, even though sea temperatures there are more than warm enough. Why not?","options":{"A":"Sea surface temperatures at the Equator are actually far too low","B":"The Coriolis effect is too weak at the Equator to start a storm spinning","C":"There is never enough moisture in the equatorial atmosphere to form cloud","D":"Air pressure at the Equator is consistently too high for storms to develop"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Cyclone formation needs sea temperatures above about 27°C, low wind shear, and rotation. The Coriolis effect, which deflects moving air and lets a storm spin up, is zero at the Equator and strengthens with latitude. So the belts between roughly 5° and 20° north and south are where all the other conditions and enough spin coincide.","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination of conditions is required for a tropical cyclone to form?","options":{"A":"High air pressure at the surface with rapidly sinking cool air above it","B":"Warm land surfaces, high-latitude location and dry descending air","C":"Cold surface water, strong wind shear and a location right on the Equator","D":"Sea above 27°C, low wind shear and enough Coriolis effect to rotate"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CYCLONE-STRUCTURE","stem":"During a hurricane, conditions suddenly become calm and clear for around 30 minutes before violent winds return from the opposite direction. What has happened?","options":{"A":"The hurricane has weakened permanently and is beginning to dissipate","B":"Two separate hurricanes have passed over the same location in sequence","C":"The eye has passed overhead, and the far eyewall is now arriving","D":"The storm has made landfall and lost contact with its warm ocean source"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"The eye is a column of descending air at the storm's centre: low wind, clear sky, lowest pressure. It is ringed by the eyewall, where the most intense winds and rainfall occur. Because the storm rotates, the winds after the eye arrive from the reverse direction, which catches out people who assume the storm has ended.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are the strongest winds and heaviest rainfall in a tropical cyclone found in the eyewall rather than the eye?","options":{"A":"The eyewall is where air rises most violently, while the eye has sinking air","B":"Rainfall in a cyclone is always heaviest at its centre point of lowest pressure","C":"The eyewall marks the outermost edge of the storm's total cloud cover","D":"The eye contains the fastest rising air anywhere within the whole storm"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-RESPONSE","stem":"After a cyclone, a government invests in mangrove replanting along the coast rather than only rebuilding sea walls. What type of strategy is this, and why choose it?","options":{"A":"An immediate response, because mangroves stop a storm surge within hours","B":"A short-term relief measure, since planting delivers aid to survivors quickly","C":"A prediction strategy, because mangroves forecast where cyclones will land","D":"Long-term mitigation: mangroves absorb surge energy and are cheap to keep"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Responses divide into immediate (search and rescue, shelter, water) and long-term (rebuilding, planning, protection). Mangroves are long-term mitigation and often better value than hard engineering: they dissipate wave and surge energy, trap sediment, self-repair after damage, and support fisheries, whereas a sea wall depreciates and must be funded again.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these is best classified as an immediate response to a tropical cyclone rather than a long-term one?","options":{"A":"Rewriting the national building code to require raised ground floors","B":"Search and rescue teams recovering people from flooded buildings","C":"Replanting coastal mangrove forest along an exposed stretch of shoreline","D":"Constructing a permanent network of storm shelters across the coastal region"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-CIRCULATION","stem":"The world's great hot deserts cluster around 30° north and south. Which part of the global circulation explains this?","options":{"A":"Air descends at 30° in the Hadley cell, warming and holding its moisture","B":"The polar cell brings extremely cold dry air down as far as 30° latitude","C":"Rising air at 30° cools rapidly and releases most of its moisture","D":"Ocean currents at 30° latitude carry warm water away from all coastlines"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"In the Hadley cell, air rises at the Equator, sheds its moisture as tropical rainfall, travels poleward and sinks at about 30°. Descending air compresses and warms, so its capacity to hold water vapour rises and cloud cannot form. Persistent high pressure and clear skies at that latitude give the Sahara, Arabian and Australian deserts.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the Equator receive heavy rainfall almost every day while 30° north is arid?","options":{"A":"The Equator receives no direct solar energy, so water cannot evaporate","B":"Both latitudes have descending air, but the Equator is nearer the ocean","C":"Air rises and cools at the Equator, but descends and warms at 30°","D":"Rainfall depends entirely on distance from the nearest large mountain range"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-EVIDENCE","stem":"Ice cores from Antarctica contain trapped air bubbles. Why are these so valuable as evidence of past climate?","options":{"A":"They record the exact air temperature of every year for the last million years","B":"They give a direct sample of the atmosphere at the time the ice formed","C":"They contain written records left behind by early polar scientific expeditions","D":"They show sea level directly, because ice thickness matches ocean depth exactly"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Snow traps air as it compacts into ice, so each layer seals a genuine sample of the atmosphere. Measuring the carbon dioxide and methane in those bubbles gives a direct record, not a proxy, going back roughly 800,000 years, and the oxygen isotopes in the ice itself indicate temperature. That pairing is what links CO2 to temperature over glacial cycles.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a proxy record of past climate rather than a direct measurement of it?","options":{"A":"A thermometer reading logged at a weather station in 1950","B":"Carbon dioxide concentration measured directly at an observatory today","C":"A satellite measurement of sea surface temperature taken last year","D":"Tree ring widths used to infer growing conditions in past centuries"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-NATURAL-CAUSE","stem":"A student says recent warming must be natural because climate has always changed. What is the strongest geographical response?","options":{"A":"Climate has in fact been completely stable throughout all of Earth's history","B":"Natural causes such as orbital change have never affected the global climate","C":"Natural cycles are real but too slow and too weak to explain the recent rate","D":"Volcanic eruptions are the single cause of every past change in the climate"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Both parts of the argument must be handled. Yes, Milankovitch orbital cycles, solar output and volcanic activity have driven past change, over tens of thousands of years. The warming since about 1850 is roughly ten times faster, coincides with a measured rise in greenhouse gases, and orbital forcing over this period points slightly the other way.","reforge":{"stem":"Milankovitch cycles operate over tens of thousands of years. Why does this matter when assessing warming since 1850?","options":{"A":"They act far too slowly to account for change measured over 170 years","B":"It shows orbital cycles are the fastest climate driver known to scientists","C":"It proves that no natural factor has ever influenced the Earth's climate","D":"It means the temperature record since 1850 cannot be measured reliably"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-VOLCANO","stem":"Nyiragongo in DR Congo produces fast-flowing lava that has reached the city of Goma, while Mount St Helens produced a violent explosive eruption. What causes this difference?","options":{"A":"Volcanoes in Africa are always far less explosive than those in North America","B":"Nyiragongo is a much younger volcano than Mount St Helens is","C":"Explosive eruptions occur only where a volcano lies close to a large city","D":"Magma composition: runny basaltic lava flows, sticky andesitic lava traps gas"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Silica content controls viscosity. Basaltic magma at constructive boundaries and hotspots is low in silica, so gas escapes freely and lava flows fast and far. Andesitic and rhyolitic magma at destructive boundaries is silica-rich and sticky; gas cannot escape, pressure builds, and the eruption is explosive with ash and pyroclastic flows.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do volcanoes at destructive plate boundaries tend to erupt more explosively than those at constructive boundaries?","options":{"A":"They are always located at a much higher altitude above sea level","B":"Their magma is silica-rich and viscous, so pressurised gas cannot escape","C":"They contain no dissolved gas at all within the magma beneath them","D":"They erupt far less often, so more lava is available at each single eruption"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"},{"id":"GCSE-HAZ-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-HAZ","tag":"MC-GG-HAZ-EFFECTS","stem":"Volcanic areas such as the slopes of Mount Merapi in Indonesia remain densely populated despite repeated deadly eruptions. Why do people stay?","options":{"A":"Fertile volcanic soils, geothermal energy and tourism outweigh a rare risk","B":"Nobody living on Merapi is aware that the volcano is currently active","C":"Eruptions on populated volcanoes are always predicted far enough ahead of time","D":"Governments legally require these communities to remain where they are"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Risk is weighed against benefit and against alternatives. Weathered volcanic ash produces exceptionally fertile soil supporting intensive farming; geothermal heat, mining and tourism add income. Set against an eruption every few decades, and with land and family ties elsewhere unavailable, staying is a rational choice rather than ignorance.","reforge":{"stem":"Which benefit best explains why farming communities concentrate on the lower slopes of active volcanoes?","options":{"A":"Volcanic slopes are entirely free from the risk of any future eruption","B":"Steep volcanic slopes make large-scale mechanised farming much easier to do","C":"Weathered volcanic ash produces unusually fertile and productive soil","D":"Land on an active volcano is legally protected from any form of taxation"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-haz"}]},"GCSE-GEO-DEV":{"label":"Development Dynamics","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-DEV-01","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Why do geographers use the Human Development Index (HDI) rather than GDP per capita alone to measure development?","options":{"A":"HDI combines life expectancy, education and income","B":"GDP per capita cannot be measured in developing countries","C":"HDI is easier to calculate than GDP per capita","D":"HDI is measured every year whereas GDP is measured every ten years"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-MEASURING-DEVELOPMENT","scaffold":"Development has many strands — economic, social, demographic, political, environmental — so a single economic indicator gives a partial picture. HDI scores between 0 and 1 using life expectancy (health), education level/literacy (education) and GNI per capita (wealth). A country can have high GDP from oil exports but low life expectancy and literacy; HDI exposes that, GDP per capita hides it.","reforge":{"stem":"A country has a high GDP per capita but a low HDI score. What is the most likely explanation?","options":{"A":"HDI cannot be applied to wealthy countries","B":"The country has miscalculated its GDP","C":"Wealth is concentrated in a small elite","D":"The country must have a very small population"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-02","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"A student writes: 'GDP per capita tells us the quality of life of people in a country.' Why is this a weak claim?","options":{"A":"Quality of life can only be measured by life expectancy","B":"It is an average, so it hides inequality within a country","C":"GDP per capita measures income, and income is irrelevant to quality of life","D":"GDP per capita is never published for developing countries"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-MEASURING-DEVELOPMENT","scaffold":"This is a classic 2-mark exam question. GDP per capita = total output ÷ population. Problems: (1) it's a mean, so extreme wealth at the top drags it up while most live below it; (2) it measures production, not wellbeing — spending on weapons counts the same as spending on hospitals; (3) GDP even rises after a disaster because of rebuilding. Use it alongside social indicators, not instead of them.","reforge":{"stem":"Following a major earthquake, a country's GDP rises the next year because of reconstruction spending. What does this show about GDP as a development measure?","options":{"A":"GDP should be replaced entirely by birth rate","B":"GDP is always calculated incorrectly after disasters","C":"Earthquakes are good for development","D":"Activity rises even when wellbeing falls"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-03","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Which set of characteristics best describes an emerging country?","options":{"A":"No industry, no trade links and a declining population","B":"Very high human development, a dominant service sector and an ageing population","C":"Rapid industrial growth, rising incomes and medium human development","D":"Low human development, subsistence agriculture and very low incomes"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-DEVELOPMENT-CLASSIFICATION","scaffold":"Edexcel B uses three categories: developing (low human development — poor quality of life, inadequate services), emerging (high/medium human development — rapid industrial-led growth, rising incomes, reasonable standard of living for most), and developed (very high human development — modern industries, high incomes). India, China and Brazil are standard emerging examples. B describes a developed country, while C describes an emerging country.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of the following would be the strongest single piece of evidence that a country has moved from 'developing' to 'emerging'?","options":{"A":"Employment shifts from farms to industry","B":"Joining the United Nations","C":"A rise in the number of tourists visiting","D":"An increase in total population"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-04","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Which of the following is a HISTORICAL cause of global inequality?","options":{"A":"Landlocked countries being cut off from seaborne trade","B":"Tropical cyclones repeatedly destroying infrastructure","C":"Corrupt governments failing to invest in healthcare","D":"Colonialism — control and economic exploitation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-HISTORICAL-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Exams often ask you to sort causes into categories. Environmental: climate/hazards, disease, topography and landlocked position. Historical: colonialism and neo-colonialism, trade patterns. Political/economic: governance, corruption, international relations and resource security. Social: healthcare, education, demography and poverty. A is environmental, B is environmental, C is political/economic, and D is historical.","reforge":{"stem":"What distinguishes neo-colonialism from colonialism?","options":{"A":"Neo-colonialism only affects countries that were never colonised","B":"Neo-colonialism uses trade and aid, not direct rule","C":"They are two words for exactly the same process","D":"Neo-colonialism happened first, before colonialism"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-05","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Why does a landlocked location tend to slow a country's economic development?","options":{"A":"It is cut off from seaborne trade routes","B":"Being landlocked means a country has no natural resources","C":"Landlocked countries always have desert climates","D":"Landlocked countries cannot join international organisations"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-LOCATION-BARRIERS","scaffold":"Most world trade moves by sea because container shipping is cheap and bulk. A landlocked country (e.g. Chad) must move goods overland through neighbours first, adding cost, delay and political risk — which discourages TNC investment. Note this is a physical/environmental cause that interacts with political ones: relations with the neighbour controlling your route matter enormously.","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination of physical factors would most severely limit a developing country's ability to grow economically?","options":{"A":"Flat, fertile land close to a navigable river","B":"A long coastline and a temperate climate","C":"Landlocked, mountainous and disease-prone","D":"Large deposits of minerals near a major port"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-06","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Rostow's modernisation theory argues that countries develop by:","options":{"A":"Remaining permanently dependent on wealthier core countries","B":"Five linear stages driven by investment and industry","C":"Relying entirely on aid from non-governmental organisations","D":"Reducing trade with other countries to protect local industry"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-ROSTOW","scaffold":"Rostow (1960): Stage 1 traditional society (subsistence farming, bartering) → Stage 2 pre-conditions for take-off (surpluses traded, infrastructure, shift to manufacturing) → Stage 3 take-off (industrial and regional growth, investment) → Stage 4 drive to maturity (technology, diversification) → Stage 5 high mass consumption (consumer society, dominant service sector). Learn the stage names in order — they're commonly asked directly.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is the strongest criticism of Rostow's modernisation theory?","options":{"A":"It says nothing about industrialisation","B":"It was based on a study of European countries, all of which failed to develop","C":"It has too many stages to be useful","D":"It ignores colonialism and debt as blocks on take-off"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-07","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Frank's dependency theory explains global inequality by arguing that:","options":{"A":"Poverty is caused entirely by physical geography","B":"All countries pass through the same five stages of growth","C":"Core countries add value, keeping the periphery dependent","D":"Development depends only on the size of a country's population"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-DEPENDENCY-THEORY","scaffold":"Frank developed dependency theory in opposition to Rostow. Core (Europe, North America) vs periphery (much of Africa, Latin America). The periphery exports coffee beans, cocoa, sugar and minerals cheaply; the core adds value through processing and sells manufactured goods back. Wealth therefore flows to the core. It's a structural explanation — poverty isn't a starting point you grow out of, it's actively produced by the relationship.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence most challenges Frank's dependency theory?","options":{"A":"China, Brazil and India grew into emerging economies","B":"Some countries in the periphery have large populations","C":"Some core countries have experienced recessions","D":"Coffee prices change from year to year"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-08","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Which of these is a TOP-DOWN development strategy?","options":{"A":"An NGO training local farmers in improved growing techniques","B":"Micro-hydro schemes built and maintained by villagers in rural Nepal","C":"A microfinance loan enabling a woman to start a small business","D":"A hydroelectric power dam funded by a World Bank loan"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-DEVELOPMENT-APPROACHES","scaffold":"Top-down: large-scale, expensive, high technology, decided by governments/IGOs (IMF, World Bank), aimed at economic growth — dams, ports, airports, motorways. Bottom-up: small-scale, local, low-cost, often NGO-led, aimed at social development — wells, clinics, schools, farmer training, intermediate technology, microfinance, Fairtrade. A, B and C are bottom-up examples; D is top-down because the World Bank funds a large infrastructure project.","reforge":{"stem":"A government builds a large HEP dam with an IGO loan. Which criticism is most specific to top-down strategies?","options":{"A":"The project is too small to make any difference","B":"Local people may not benefit and upkeep is costly","C":"It only helps women","D":"It cannot be funded because IGOs never lend money"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-09","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What is the main advantage of intermediate technology as a development strategy?","options":{"A":"It is affordable and matches local needs","B":"It generates the highest possible GDP growth","C":"It replaces the need for any local labour","D":"It requires large loans from the IMF"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-INTERMEDIATE-TECH","scaffold":"Intermediate (appropriate) technology is matched to the skills, wealth and needs of a local community. The Nepal micro-hydro example: villagers build and maintain it, and the electricity lets children study at night and families use phones and the internet. Because there's no dependence on outside engineers or expensive spares, it's sustainable — which is exactly the failure point of many top-down projects.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a bottom-up NGO project be criticised despite meeting local needs effectively?","options":{"A":"It cannot improve health or education","B":"Local people are unable to use the technology","C":"Governments may come to rely on NGOs","D":"It always costs more than a large dam"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-10","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"How does Fairtrade aim to reduce the development gap?","options":{"A":"It removes all tariffs between wealthy countries","B":"It guarantees producers a fairer price","C":"It cancels the debts of the world's poorest countries","D":"It provides emergency food aid after disasters"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-FAIRTRADE","scaffold":"Fairtrade is a bottom-up trade-based strategy: minimum prices, environmentally sustainable farming, and a social premium reinvested locally. The Gumutindo Coffee Cooperative in eastern Uganda is the standard example — over 90% of small coffee farmers joined, and milling the coffee before roasting adds value locally rather than exporting raw beans, which raises farmer income. Note how that directly counters Frank's core–periphery problem.","reforge":{"stem":"Fairtrade cooperatives often process crops locally before export. Why does this matter for development?","options":{"A":"It guarantees the country will reach Rostow's stage 5","B":"It allows the country to stop trading altogether","C":"Processing reduces the weight of the crop so it is cheaper to ship","D":"Processing adds value locally, so income stays"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-11","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"In 2006 the IMF cancelled the debts of 19 of the world's poorest countries. What was the intended development benefit?","options":{"A":"They could join the G20 and stop borrowing entirely","B":"It removed the need for those countries to trade","C":"Debt interest money could go to infrastructure instead","D":"It guaranteed those countries would become developed within ten years"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-DEBT-RELIEF","scaffold":"Many developing countries borrowed heavily in the 1970s–80s and then could not repay because of high interest rates — debt servicing swallowed money that should have funded development. Debt relief frees that money: Uganda used savings to provide safe water for over 2 million people. Evaluation point: the benefit depends on governance — a corrupt government may retain the money rather than invest it.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the key limitation of debt relief as a development strategy?","options":{"A":"Corrupt governments may keep the freed-up money","B":"No country has ever been granted debt relief","C":"Debt relief can only be given to developed countries","D":"Debt relief always increases a country's debt"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-12","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Why do TNCs typically locate manufacturing and data-processing operations in emerging countries?","options":{"A":"TNCs are legally required to locate outside developed countries","B":"Emerging countries have the highest wages in the world","C":"Emerging countries have the strictest environmental regulations","D":"Lower labour costs, big workforces and incentives"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-TNC-FDI","scaffold":"Pull factors for TNCs: cheap, often skilled and English-speaking labour; large domestic markets; special economic zones (SEZs) and export processing zones (EPZs) with low or no tax; restricted union rights and low minimum wages; limited environmental and health-and-safety regulation, which cuts costs. Note that the last two are simultaneously the reason TNC investment is criticised.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is the strongest argument that TNC investment does NOT fully benefit an emerging country?","options":{"A":"TNCs are not allowed to pay taxes","B":"Management jobs stay in the home country","C":"TNC factories always close within one year","D":"TNCs never create any jobs at all"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-13","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What is the 'positive multiplier effect' in development?","options":{"A":"Investment creates jobs, spending and tax revenue","B":"Aid money is divided equally between all regions of a country","C":"Population grows faster than the economy can support","D":"A country's debt increases each year through compound interest"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-MULTIPLIER","scaffold":"The multiplier is the engine behind the 'cycle of wealth'. TNC builds a factory → jobs and wages → workers spend locally → other businesses grow and co-locate → government collects more tax → invests in education, healthcare and infrastructure → the workforce becomes more skilled and attractive to further investment. It's the reason exam answers about development should chain consequences, not just list them.","reforge":{"stem":"A 3-mark question asks you to explain how outsourcing affects economic development. Which answer would score all three marks?","options":{"A":"Outsourcing creates jobs.","B":"Outsourcing is bad for developed countries because they lose jobs.","C":"TNCs invest in infrastructure (1), so other firms co-locate (1), creating more jobs (1)","D":"Outsourcing is when work is sent to another country. It is common in India. Many people work in call centres."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-14","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"According to the demographic transition model (DTM), which stage is characterised by a rapidly FALLING birth rate while death rate continues to fall more slowly?","options":{"A":"Stage 2","B":"Stage 3","C":"Stage 1","D":"Stage 5"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-DTM","scaffold":"Stage 1: high BR, high DR, low total population. Stage 2: DR falls (better diet, healthcare, clean water) while BR stays high — population rises rapidly. Stage 3: BR falls rapidly (contraception, family planning, lower infant mortality, cost of children) while DR falls slowly — growth slows. Stage 4: both low and fluctuating. Stage 5: DR exceeds BR, population declines. Emerging countries such as India and Nigeria sit in stage 3.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the birth rate fall sharply in stage 3 of the DTM?","options":{"A":"Governments make it illegal to have more than one child","B":"The death rate rises so quickly that fewer adults survive to have children","C":"People emigrate, so fewer babies are born abroad","D":"Lower infant mortality, contraception and education"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-15","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"A population pyramid has a very narrow base and a wide top. What does this indicate?","options":{"A":"A youthful population with a high birth rate — DTM stage 2","B":"A country with equal numbers in every age group","C":"An ageing population — typically DTM stage 5","D":"A country experiencing rapid population growth"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-AGEING-POPULATION","scaffold":"Pyramid shapes: concave with a very wide base = stage 2 (Niger, youthful, high BR and DR). Pyramid = stage 3 (Nepal). Column = stage 4 (USA). Pentagon with narrow base = stage 5 (Japan, ageing). When interpreting, check four things: young dependents (base width), working population (middle), old dependents (top), and any male/female imbalance — the UAE's male bulge at 25–39 reflects labour migration.","reforge":{"stem":"What are the main implications for a government of an ageing population?","options":{"A":"Higher pension costs and a shrinking workforce","B":"An increase in the birth rate","C":"Rapidly rising unemployment among young people","D":"A need to build many more primary schools"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-16","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Calculate the dependency ratio for a country with 30 million young dependents, 10 million old dependents and 80 million people of working age.","options":{"A":"40","B":"125","C":"25","D":"50"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-DEPENDENCY-DEMOGRAPHIC-DIVIDEND","scaffold":"Dependency ratio = (young dependents + old dependents) ÷ working population × 100. Here: (30 + 10) ÷ 80 × 100 = 40 ÷ 80 × 100 = 50. This means 50 dependents for every 100 workers. A high ratio means a heavier burden on the working population through taxation. Show the formula in your working — marks are available for method even if the arithmetic slips.","reforge":{"stem":"A country's dependency ratio rises from 48 to 67 over thirty years while its birth rate falls. What is the most likely cause?","options":{"A":"A sudden rise in the number of children","B":"An ageing population, not more children","C":"An increase in the working population","D":"Large-scale immigration of working-age adults"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-DEV","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about categories of development.","template":"A ___ country has low human development, while an ___ country is experiencing rapid growth based on industrial development. The difference in wealth between countries is called the development ___.","blanks":["developing","emerging","gap"],"bank":["developing","emerging","gap","developed","cycle","ratio"],"tag":"MC-DEV-DEVELOPMENT-CLASSIFICATION","scaffold":"The three Edexcel B categories are developing (low human development), emerging (high/medium human development, rapid industrial growth) and developed (very high human development). The difference between them is the development gap.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-DEV","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about the Human Development Index.","template":"HDI combines life ___, education level and ___ per capita to give a score between 0 and ___.","blanks":["expectancy","GNI","1"],"bank":["expectancy","GNI","1","100","mortality","literacy"],"tag":"MC-DEV-MEASURING-DEVELOPMENT","scaffold":"HDI runs from 0 (least developed) to 1 (most developed) and combines a health measure (life expectancy), an education measure (literacy and years of schooling) and a wealth measure (GNI per capita). Because it is composite, it is harder to mislead with than any single indicator.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-DEV","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Rostow's model.","template":"Rostow's model has five stages, beginning with ___ society and ending with high mass ___. The third stage, when rapid industrialisation occurs, is called ___-off.","blanks":["traditional","consumption","take"],"bank":["traditional","consumption","take","modern","production","drive"],"tag":"MC-DEV-ROSTOW","scaffold":"The five stages in order: traditional society, pre-conditions for take-off, take-off, drive to maturity, high mass consumption. Take-off (stage 3) is the pivotal one — countries that cannot raise the capital to reach it, often because of debt repayments, remain stuck.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-DEV","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Frank's dependency theory.","template":"In Frank's model, the ___ produces and sells low-value ___ materials to the ___, which processes them into high-value goods.","blanks":["periphery","raw","core"],"bank":["periphery","raw","core","centre","finished","margin"],"tag":"MC-DEV-DEPENDENCY-THEORY","scaffold":"Frank's core–periphery model: the periphery exports cheap raw materials (coffee, cocoa, sugar, minerals); the core adds value by processing them and sells manufactured goods back at high prices. Wealth accumulates in the core, keeping the periphery dependent.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-FB-05","spec":"GCSE-DEV","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about development strategies.","template":"___-down strategies are usually large, expensive projects funded by IGOs such as the World Bank, while ___-up strategies are small-scale community projects often run by ___.","blanks":["Top","bottom","NGOs"],"bank":["Top","bottom","NGOs","Bottom","top","TNCs"],"tag":"MC-DEV-DEVELOPMENT-APPROACHES","scaffold":"Top-down: dams, ports, motorways — IGO or government funded, economic focus, high technology. Bottom-up: wells, clinics, farmer training, microfinance — NGO-led, social focus, appropriate technology. Watch the capital letter on the first blank.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-FB-06","spec":"GCSE-DEV","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about the demographic transition model.","template":"In stage ___ of the DTM the death rate falls while the birth rate stays high, so population rises rapidly. In stage ___ the death rate exceeds the birth rate and population ___.","blanks":["2","5","declines"],"bank":["2","5","declines","3","4","rises"],"tag":"MC-DEV-DTM","scaffold":"Stage 2 is the rapid-growth stage: better healthcare, diet and clean water cut the death rate but attitudes to family size have not yet changed. Stage 5 is decline: birth rate falls below death rate, as in Japan and South Korea.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-FB-07","spec":"GCSE-DEV","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about the dependency ratio.","template":"Dependency ratio = (young dependents + ___ dependents) ÷ ___ population × ___.","blanks":["old","working","100"],"bank":["old","working","100","total","retired","1000"],"tag":"MC-DEV-DEPENDENCY-DEMOGRAPHIC-DIVIDEND","scaffold":"Learn this formula exactly — it is a guaranteed calculation mark. A high ratio means each worker supports more dependents through taxation. Always show the substitution before the answer.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-FB-08","spec":"GCSE-DEV","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about causes of global inequality.","template":"___ is where a country takes political control of another and exploits it economically. Today, rich countries use 'soft power' through trade, aid and TNCs instead — this is called ___-colonialism.","blanks":["Colonialism","neo"],"bank":["Colonialism","neo","Globalisation","post","Dependency","anti"],"tag":"MC-DEV-HISTORICAL-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Colonialism = direct (hard power) control: military and political rule. Neo-colonialism = indirect (soft power) control: favourable trade terms, aid conditions, IGO influence, TNC brand dominance. Examiners specifically warn against confusing the two.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-17","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What is the Brandt Line?","options":{"A":"A line that divides developed and developing countries on a world map","B":"The boundary between the world's tectonic plates","C":"A line that shows the route taken by Frank's Dependency Model","D":"A line joining every country with a Human Development Index above 0.9"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-BRANDT-LINE","scaffold":"The Brandt Line runs roughly at 30°N, dipping to include Australia and New Zealand in the 'rich north'. It's a simplification — some countries north of the line (e.g. parts of the former USSR) are less developed than some south of it (e.g. Australia) — which is a common evaluation point in exam answers.","reforge":{"stem":"A map divides the world roughly along 30°N into a wealthier 'north' and poorer 'south'. What is this line called, and what is its main limitation?","options":{"A":"The Brandt Line has no known limitations","B":"The equator — it divides the world exactly into developed and developing halves","C":"The Brandt Line — it oversimplifies development","D":"Rostow's line — it perfectly predicts every country's development stage"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-18","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"A student confuses a 'top-down' and a 'bottom-up' development project. What is the correct distinction?","options":{"A":"A top-down project is always smaller than a bottom-up project","B":"Top-down is government or TNC; bottom-up is NGO-led","C":"A bottom-up project is always funded by a national government","D":"They are two names for exactly the same type of scheme"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-DEVELOPMENT-APPROACHES","scaffold":"Top-down = large-scale, government/TNC-led, trickle-down benefits (e.g. a dam project). Bottom-up = small-scale, NGO/charity-led, targets the poorest directly (e.g. a well-building scheme). Exams often ask you to evaluate which is more effective — bottom-up reaches the poorest more reliably but on a smaller scale.","reforge":{"stem":"An NGO runs a small well-building scheme aimed directly at the poorest families in a village. What type of development project is this?","options":{"A":"A government-run national infrastructure scheme","B":"A top-down project","C":"A TNC-led scheme with trickle-down benefits","D":"A bottom-up project"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-19","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What is the key difference between capitalism and communism as economic systems?","options":{"A":"Communism is always run by private companies; capitalism is always run by the state","B":"Capitalism and communism are the same system with different names","C":"Capitalism is private and for profit; communism is communal","D":"Capitalism only exists in developing countries"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-ECONOMIC-SYSTEMS","scaffold":"This links to why some countries develop differently — a capitalist system relies on private investment and market growth to raise living standards, while a communist system aims for equality through state or communal ownership rather than private profit.","reforge":{"stem":"Under a communist economic system, how are goods and the means of production typically owned?","options":{"A":"Communally, often by the state","B":"By no one — production is banned under communism","C":"Exclusively by international TNCs","D":"Entirely by private companies seeking profit"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-20","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Which of the following correctly defines GDP per capita, and how does it differ from plain GDP?","options":{"A":"GDP per capita only measures spending by the government, not private companies","B":"GDP per capita is always higher than total GDP","C":"GDP per capita and GDP are identical measures","D":"GDP divided by population, giving a figure per person"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-MEASURING-DEVELOPMENT","scaffold":"Dividing by population is what makes GDP per capita useful for comparing wealth between countries of very different sizes — a huge total GDP (like India's) can still come with a low GDP per capita if the population is large.","reforge":{"stem":"Country X has the same total GDP as Country Y, but twice the population. How would their GDP per capita compare?","options":{"A":"Their GDP per capita would be identical","B":"Half of Country Y's, for twice the population","C":"GDP per capita is unaffected by population size","D":"Country X's GDP per capita would be double Country Y's"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-21","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What does an indicator of development actually measure?","options":{"A":"Social, economic or environmental data","B":"The exact location of the Brandt Line","C":"The five stages of Rostow's model","D":"Only the total population of a country"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-MEASURING-DEVELOPMENT","scaffold":"An 'indicator' is the general category — any single measure used as a proxy for development. Social indicators (literacy rate, life expectancy), economic indicators (GDP per capita), and environmental indicators (pollution levels) are all types of indicator; HDI is a composite that combines several of them.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these is an environmental indicator of development, as opposed to a social or economic one?","options":{"A":"Literacy rate","B":"Life expectancy","C":"Pollution","D":"GDP per capita"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-22","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"A student defines infant mortality rate as 'how many babies are born each year.' How should this be corrected?","options":{"A":"The student's definition is correct as written","B":"It measures infant deaths, not births","C":"Infant mortality rate measures deaths of people over 65","D":"Infant mortality rate is a measure of literacy, not health"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-HEALTH-INDICATORS","scaffold":"Infant mortality rate is a widely used social/health indicator — a high rate usually points to poor healthcare access, sanitation and nutrition, so it's commonly paired with life expectancy as evidence of a country's level of development.","reforge":{"stem":"A country has a high birth rate but a low infant mortality rate has recently been achieved through better healthcare. What does the infant mortality figure specifically measure?","options":{"A":"The total number of babies born in a year","B":"The average age of first-time parents","C":"The literacy rate of new mothers","D":"The share of under-5s who die"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-23","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What distinguishes economic inequality from social inequality?","options":{"A":"Economic inequality only applies between countries, never within one","B":"Social inequality is always measured using GDP per capita","C":"Economic is wealth gap; social is quality-of-life gap","D":"They are identical measures with different names"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Economic inequality is purely about income/wealth distribution. Social inequality is broader — it includes quality-of-life gaps such as access to healthcare, education and services, not just money.","reforge":{"stem":"A country has low economic inequality (a small gap between richest and poorest 10%) but high social inequality. What does this suggest?","options":{"A":"Income is even, but quality of life still varies","B":"Social inequality only measures income, exactly like economic inequality","C":"Economic and social inequality always rise and fall together","D":"This combination is impossible"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-24","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Which correctly defines informal work, and why is it common in lower income countries?","options":{"A":"A synonym for a top-down development project","B":"Work that is always highly paid and closely regulated by government inspectors","C":"Work carried out exclusively by NGOs","D":"Unregulated, low-paid work where formal jobs are scarce"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-INFORMAL-ECONOMY","scaffold":"Informal work (street vending, casual labour) is common in squatter settlements and rapidly urbanising cities — it provides income where formal employment is limited, but offers no job security, minimum wage or safety protection.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might informal work be common in a rapidly urbanising city with limited formal employment?","options":{"A":"Informal work guarantees a higher income than formal employment","B":"It offers income where formal jobs are scarce","C":"Informal work is always better regulated than formal work","D":"Informal work only exists in wealthy developed countries"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-25","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What is a squatter settlement?","options":{"A":"Temporary housing, often on unfavourable land","B":"A large-scale government housing scheme built to a high safety standard","C":"An area where only wealthy families live","D":"A rural farming settlement with no urban connection"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-SQUATTER-SETTLEMENTS","scaffold":"Squatter settlements form where rural-urban migrants can't afford formal housing — they're often built on steep slopes, floodplains or waste ground precisely because that land is unwanted and unregulated, which increases residents' exposure to hazards.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are squatter settlements often built on steep slopes, floodplains or other unfavourable land?","options":{"A":"Squatter settlements are only found in rural areas, never in cities","B":"Governments deliberately build squatter settlements as planned developments","C":"Low-income families cannot afford formal housing","D":"This land is always the safest and most desirable in a city"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-26","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"A student says 'the poverty line is the same figure in every country.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"The poverty line is a fixed international figure that never changes","B":"What counts as adequate income varies by country","C":"The poverty line measures literacy, not income","D":"The poverty line only applies to developed countries"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-POVERTY-LINE","scaffold":"Poverty lines can be set nationally (relative to a country's own cost of living) or internationally (e.g. the World Bank's extreme poverty line), which is why comparing poverty rates between countries needs care over which measure is being used.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can't a single global poverty line be applied fairly to every country?","options":{"A":"Poverty is impossible to measure in any country","B":"Every country has exactly the same cost of living","C":"Poverty lines are only ever set by international organisations, never nationally","D":"The cost of living differs from place to place"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-27","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What is governance, and why does it matter for development?","options":{"A":"The physical shape and relief of a country's land","B":"Another term for GDP per capita","C":"The management of a place or group of people","D":"A measure of a country's total rainfall"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-GOVERNANCE","scaffold":"Governance covers how decisions are made and enforced — good governance (transparent institutions, low corruption) tends to attract investment and direct aid effectively, while poor governance (see: corruption) can trap a country in low development despite aid or resources.","reforge":{"stem":"A country has valuable natural resources and receives significant international aid, yet remains poorly developed. Which factor is most likely to explain this?","options":{"A":"Weak or corrupt governance misdirecting resources","B":"Natural resources always guarantee high development","C":"The country must have no indicators of development at all","D":"International aid always guarantees improved governance"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-28","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"How does corruption act as a barrier to development?","options":{"A":"Corruption only affects developed countries","B":"Corruption always increases a country's GDP per capita","C":"Corruption is a physical, not a political, cause of inequality","D":"It diverts funds away from public services"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-GOVERNANCE","scaffold":"Corruption sits in the political/economic category of causes of inequality alongside trade barriers and debt — money that should fund public services (schools, hospitals, roads) is instead diverted for private or political gain, slowing development even where a country has decent resources.","reforge":{"stem":"How does corruption most directly slow a country's development, even where resources or aid are available?","options":{"A":"Corruption always increases a country's GDP per capita immediately","B":"Funds are diverted before reaching public services","C":"Corruption only affects a country's topography","D":"Corruption has no measurable effect on development"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-29","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What is topography, and how can it act as a barrier to development?","options":{"A":"The shape and relief of the land","B":"A synonym for climate change","C":"A measure of a country's literacy rate","D":"The formal name for a country's government system"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-LOCATION-BARRIERS","scaffold":"Topography is a physical/environmental cause of inequality, alongside climate and being landlocked. Mountainous terrain (e.g. much of Nepal or Bolivia) raises the cost of roads, railways and electricity grids, which can isolate communities from trade and services.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a mountainous, landlocked country find it harder to attract TNC investment than a coastal country with flat terrain?","options":{"A":"Topography has no effect on a country's ability to trade or attract investment","B":"Mountainous countries always have better transport links than coastal ones","C":"Difficult terrain raises the cost of infrastructure","D":"TNCs only ever invest in landlocked countries"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-30","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"A student defines a 'Non-Governmental Organisation (NGO)' as 'a private company that makes money for shareholders.' What's wrong with this definition?","options":{"A":"NGOs are always run directly by national governments","B":"An NGO is non-profit; a private company seeks profit","C":"The definition is correct — NGOs are simply another type of private company","D":"NGOs only operate in developed countries"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-NGO","scaffold":"The defining features of an NGO are independence from government and a non-profit purpose — this is why NGOs are typically behind bottom-up development projects, which prioritise reaching the poorest rather than generating a financial return.","reforge":{"stem":"An organisation operating in a country has no government affiliation and does not aim to make a profit. What type of organisation is this most likely to be?","options":{"A":"A private, profit-driven company","B":"A national government department","C":"A TNC seeking to maximise shareholder profit","D":"An NGO, such as a charity"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-31","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Why is using a single development indicator risky when comparing countries?","options":{"A":"Indicators are only used for weather forecasting","B":"All indicators measure exactly the same thing","C":"One indicator can hide other dimensions","D":"GDP per capita includes literacy, life expectancy and inequality automatically"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-INDICATORS","scaffold":"Development is multi-dimensional. GDP per capita measures average income, but not distribution, health, education, freedom or sustainability. Strong answers compare indicators and explain what each includes and leaves out.","reforge":{"stem":"Country A has high GDP per capita but low literacy and high inequality. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Economic data can hide social weaknesses","B":"Literacy is not linked to development","C":"It must be fully developed","D":"HDI would be useless because GDP is high"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-32","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"A student says a youthful population is always a development problem. What is the best evaluation?","options":{"A":"It always causes immediate economic collapse","B":"It only matters in developed countries","C":"It means death rates must be rising","D":"It can pressure schools and services"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-YOUTHFUL-POP","scaffold":"A youthful population raises dependency and service pressure in the short term. But if young people receive education, healthcare and employment, they can become a large productive workforce — a demographic dividend. Evaluation depends on investment and job creation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which condition is most important if a youthful population is to support development?","options":{"A":"No schooling so wages stay low","B":"Education, health and jobs","C":"A ban on migration to cities","D":"Keeping all work informal"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-33","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"How can debt relief help development in a low-income country?","options":{"A":"Money moves from debt to public services","B":"It immediately makes every household rich","C":"It prevents trade from happening","D":"It always removes the need for tax revenue"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-DEBT-RELIEF","scaffold":"Debt relief can reduce the burden of repayments, allowing public spending on services and infrastructure. But it is not automatically successful: benefits depend on governance, corruption levels, conditions attached and whether money reaches development priorities.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might debt relief fail to reduce poverty even if repayments fall?","options":{"A":"Because debt relief bans education spending","B":"Because all countries have identical governance","C":"Freed money may be lost to corruption","D":"Because infrastructure never affects poverty"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-34","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Which option best distinguishes aid from foreign direct investment (FDI)?","options":{"A":"Aid and FDI are exactly the same","B":"Aid is support; FDI is company investment","C":"Aid can only be given by TNCs","D":"FDI is always charity with no profit motive"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-AID-VS-FDI","scaffold":"Aid is intended to support development or emergency relief, though it may have conditions. FDI is investment into production, services or assets in another country, usually with a profit motive. Both can support development, but their aims and trade-offs differ.","reforge":{"stem":"A car company builds a factory in an emerging country to produce vehicles for profit. What is this?","options":{"A":"Fairtrade certification of goods","B":"Emergency humanitarian aid","C":"Debt relief from lenders","D":"Foreign direct investment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-35","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What does the Gini coefficient help geographers measure?","options":{"A":"The number of births per woman","B":"The annual rainfall of a country","C":"Inequality in income or wealth","D":"The percentage of workers in farming"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"The Gini coefficient is an inequality measure. A higher value indicates a less equal distribution, while a lower value indicates greater equality. It complements measures such as GDP per capita and HDI rather than replacing them.","reforge":{"stem":"A country has a much higher Gini coefficient than a neighbouring country. What does this suggest?","options":{"A":"Less equal income distribution","B":"The country must have lower rainfall","C":"Every household has the same income","D":"The country has no poverty"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-36","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Which demographic pattern is most associated with a highly developed country?","options":{"A":"High infant mortality and a very youthful population","B":"A wide population pyramid base caused by high birth rates","C":"High fertility and low access to healthcare","D":"Low mortality and an ageing population"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-DEMOGRAPHIC-PATTERN","scaffold":"Developed countries generally have lower infant and maternal mortality, longer life expectancy and lower fertility. Their population structures often show a narrower base and a larger older population, although individual countries vary.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a low infant mortality rate be used as evidence of development?","options":{"A":"It proves all households have identical incomes","B":"Better healthcare and sanitation reduce deaths","C":"It measures only the size of the manufacturing sector","D":"It means the country has no elderly people"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-37","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"How can colonialism contribute to present-day global inequality?","options":{"A":"Colonial extraction left unequal patterns","B":"It prevents every country from using its natural resources","C":"It guarantees equal trade between all countries","D":"It only changes a country's climate"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-HISTORICAL-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Colonial rule often organised territories around exporting raw materials and serving external interests. Long-term effects can include unequal infrastructure, borders and trade relationships, although contemporary inequality also has political, environmental and economic causes.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example best illustrates a possible long-term economic legacy of colonialism?","options":{"A":"All former colonies have identical development levels","B":"Colonial history has no link to present trade patterns","C":"Raw-material exports can persist","D":"A country develops only because it has a large desert"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-38","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Which feature is most typical of a bottom-up development strategy?","options":{"A":"A national dam with no local consultation","B":"A locally led small project","C":"A bank-funded capital-city transport scheme","D":"A TNC choosing the site by its own priorities"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-DEVELOPMENT-APPROACHES","scaffold":"Bottom-up strategies are usually small-scale, locally controlled and designed around community needs. Top-down strategies are more likely to be large-scale, externally funded and planned by governments or organisations above the local level.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a bottom-up water project be more successful in a remote village?","options":{"A":"It always uses the most expensive technology available","B":"It avoids any local participation","C":"It is designed only for a national capital","D":"Local needs guide maintenance"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-39","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Why have some countries benefited more than others from globalisation?","options":{"A":"Only non-exporters attract investment","B":"All countries are affected equally","C":"Infrastructure and skills","D":"Countries benefit by ceasing trade"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-GLOBALISATION","scaffold":"Globalisation can bring trade, FDI, technology and employment, but benefits vary. Countries with reliable infrastructure, educated workers, stable institutions and market access may attract higher-value investment, while others remain dependent on low-value commodities.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor could help an emerging country gain more from globalisation?","options":{"A":"Infrastructure and skills help","B":"Removing all environmental regulation without replacement","C":"Depending on one unprocessed export only","D":"Blocking all foreign trade"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-40","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Which is an environmental consequence of rapid economic development?","options":{"A":"Economic growth automatically removes all pollution","B":"Manufacturing has no effect on water quality","C":"Development always reduces energy demand","D":"Industry can raise pollution"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-ENVIRONMENTAL-COSTS","scaffold":"Rapid development can raise incomes and services but also increase resource use, industrial emissions, traffic and waste. The balance depends on technology, regulation, energy sources and how benefits and costs are distributed.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can rapid industrial growth create a development dilemma?","options":{"A":"It prevents any change to living standards","B":"Growth can raise jobs and pollution","C":"It guarantees that every region benefits equally","D":"It removes the need for environmental management"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-41","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What does a proportional flow-line map show particularly well?","options":{"A":"Trade or migration flows","B":"The height of land along a cross-section","C":"The exact age of every person in a country","D":"The average rainfall inside one small field"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-FLOW-LINE-MAPS","scaffold":"Proportional flow lines use arrows whose width represents the size of a movement between places. They can show trade, migration or investment flows, although a key and careful interpretation are needed.","reforge":{"stem":"On a flow-line map, a much thicker arrow from Country X to Country Y most likely represents:","options":{"A":"A lower-value flow in the opposite direction","B":"No movement between the countries","C":"A larger flow between them","D":"A higher altitude in Country Y"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-42","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"How can foreign investment create both benefits and costs for an emerging country?","options":{"A":"It always removes local employment","B":"Jobs and technology, but leakage and pollution","C":"It only occurs in countries with no natural resources","D":"It has no effect on government revenue or development"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-TNC-FDI","scaffold":"FDI by TNCs can provide employment, infrastructure, skills and export earnings. Possible costs include profit leakage, low wages, environmental damage, dependence on external decisions and tax concessions that reduce public revenue.","reforge":{"stem":"Which judgement is most balanced when evaluating a TNC factory in an emerging country?","options":{"A":"The factory is automatically beneficial because it creates jobs","B":"The factory is automatically harmful because it is foreign-owned","C":"The factory can affect only the company's shareholders","D":"Wages, tax, local links and environmental controls matter"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-43","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Why can HDI give a different development ranking from GDP per capita?","options":{"A":"It ignores life expectancy in every country","B":"It measures only the value of exported goods","C":"Income, health and education","D":"It ranks countries solely by population size"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-MEASURING-DEVELOPMENT","scaffold":"HDI combines income, education and life expectancy, so countries with similar incomes can rank differently on broader human outcomes.","reforge":{"stem":"Two countries have equal income per person but different HDI scores. What best explains the gap?","options":{"A":"Education and life expectancy differ","B":"One country has a larger land area","C":"They must have identical public services","D":"HDI measures only annual rainfall"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-44","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What does it suggest if the richest income quintile receives most national income?","options":{"A":"Income is distributed almost equally","B":"The country must have a low total GDP","C":"Every person in the country is wealthy","D":"There is substantial income inequality"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Income quintiles divide the population into five equal groups; a very large top-quintile share indicates unequal distribution.","reforge":{"stem":"Which pattern indicates the most equal income distribution?","options":{"A":"The top fifth receives nearly all income","B":"Each quintile receives a similar share","C":"The bottom fifth receives no recorded income","D":"One quintile contains half the population"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-45","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What is one limitation of an index of perceived political corruption?","options":{"A":"Perceptions may differ from actual corruption","B":"It gives identical scores in every survey","C":"It directly measures every hidden transaction","D":"It can only be used for weather forecasting"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-GOVERNANCE","scaffold":"Corruption is difficult to observe directly, so perception indices depend on survey evidence and should be interpreted alongside other political measures.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should a corruption ranking be used cautiously?","options":{"A":"Its methods and perceptions may vary","B":"It records every offence with certainty","C":"Political governance cannot be compared","D":"A low score proves corruption is absent"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-46","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Why is maternal mortality commonly used as a development indicator?","options":{"A":"It measures the size of the service sector","B":"Maternal healthcare and wellbeing","C":"It shows the total value of foreign investment","D":"It records average household electricity use"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-HEALTH-INDICATORS","scaffold":"Maternal mortality is shaped by healthcare, nutrition, transport, education and inequality, making it a useful social indicator.","reforge":{"stem":"A country reduces maternal deaths sharply. What development change is most likely?","options":{"A":"A decline in every form of employment","B":"A shift from urban to subsistence living","C":"A fall in all international trade","D":"Improved access to skilled healthcare"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-47","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"How can mountainous terrain contribute to regional inequality?","options":{"A":"It guarantees high agricultural yields everywhere","B":"It makes all regions equally accessible","C":"Isolation from markets and services","D":"It prevents any minerals forming underground"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-LOCATION-BARRIERS","scaffold":"Steep relief can raise transport and infrastructure costs, limiting access to employment, healthcare and education.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a landlocked country face higher trade costs?","options":{"A":"Goods depend on routes through neighbours","B":"Its population must be evenly distributed","C":"It has direct access to several ocean ports","D":"Landlocked states cannot export any products"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-48","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What is neo-colonialism?","options":{"A":"The complete end of external influence after independence","B":"A policy that removes all international investment","C":"Equal control of trade by every participating country","D":"Continued external influence through economic power"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-HISTORICAL-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Neo-colonialism describes indirect control through trade, debt, investment or political influence after formal colonial rule has ended.","reforge":{"stem":"Which situation best illustrates neo-colonial influence?","options":{"A":"A village chooses its own crop rotation","B":"Debt shapes national policy","C":"Two regions share identical rainfall totals","D":"A country closes every international connection"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-49","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"In Rostow's model, what characterises the 'take-off' stage?","options":{"A":"Industrial investment and growth accelerate","B":"All economic growth permanently stops","C":"Traditional subsistence activity remains unchanged","D":"The service sector employs every worker"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-ROSTOW","scaffold":"Take-off involves rising investment, industrialisation and self-sustaining economic growth, although the model is criticised as overly linear.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a common criticism of Rostow's development model?","options":{"A":"It contains no stages of economic change","B":"It focuses entirely on environmental limits","C":"It assumes countries follow a Western path","D":"It argues trade always causes dependency"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-50","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"How does dependency theory explain persistent underdevelopment?","options":{"A":"Poor countries lack any natural resources","B":"Core economies extract peripheral value","C":"All trade benefits countries in equal measure","D":"Climate is the only influence on development"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-DEPENDENCY-THEORY","scaffold":"Frank argued that unequal trade and investment transfer wealth from peripheral countries to developed core economies.","reforge":{"stem":"Which trade pattern most supports dependency theory?","options":{"A":"Trade removes the need for foreign currency","B":"All countries export goods of equal value","C":"Peripheral countries control every global TNC","D":"Cheap raw exports; costly imports"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-51","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Why can intermediate technology suit a bottom-up development project?","options":{"A":"It replaces community knowledge with automation","B":"It requires costly imported experts indefinitely","C":"It is affordable and maintainable locally","D":"It operates only at a national megaproject scale"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-INTERMEDIATE-TECH","scaffold":"Intermediate technology is designed around local skills, resources and maintenance capacity rather than maximum technical complexity.","reforge":{"stem":"Which water project best represents intermediate technology?","options":{"A":"A repairable community hand pump","B":"A dam requiring permanent foreign engineers","C":"A nationwide desalination grid run abroad","D":"A satellite network with imported components"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-52","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"What is one risk of an IGO-funded infrastructure megaproject?","options":{"A":"Large projects can never increase connectivity","B":"Construction creates no employment at all","C":"Infrastructure has no effect on national debt","D":"Loan repayments may burden public finances"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-MEGAPROJECTS","scaffold":"Large infrastructure can improve power or transport but may create debt, displacement and unequal benefits if costs are poorly managed.","reforge":{"stem":"What should be included in a balanced assessment of an IGO-funded dam?","options":{"A":"The assumption that environmental costs are zero","B":"Power gains versus debt and displacement","C":"A claim that all affected people will benefit","D":"Only the dam's maximum electricity output"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-53","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"How can a new TNC factory create a local multiplier effect?","options":{"A":"Workers and suppliers spend income locally","B":"All profits remain outside the host country","C":"It removes income from every local household","D":"The factory prevents supporting firms opening"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-DEV-MULTIPLIER","scaffold":"Wages and purchases from local suppliers can generate further demand, jobs and tax revenue beyond the original investment.","reforge":{"stem":"Which evidence would show a strong local multiplier effect?","options":{"A":"Most inputs are imported and profits leave","B":"The factory employs only temporary foreign staff","C":"Local suppliers and shops gain sales","D":"Nearby businesses close as spending falls"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-54","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Why have some countries gained less from digital globalisation?","options":{"A":"Internet access is equal in every country","B":"Weak networks and skills","C":"Geography no longer affects connectivity","D":"Digital services require no skilled workers"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-DEV-DIGITAL-DIVIDE","scaffold":"Reliable communications, electricity and digital skills shape whether firms and workers can join higher-value global networks.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy would most directly reduce a digital divide?","options":{"A":"Ban all electronic communication with firms","B":"Reduce electricity reliability in rural areas","C":"Concentrate networks in one wealthy district","D":"Broadband and technical education"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-55","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"A population pyramid has a narrow base and a wide upper section. What does it suggest?","options":{"A":"Very high infant mortality with few elderly","B":"Rapid population growth and high fertility","C":"Low fertility and an ageing population","D":"Recent immigration of children only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-DEV-AGEING-POPULATION","scaffold":"A narrow base indicates fewer recent births, while larger older cohorts suggest low mortality and population ageing.","reforge":{"stem":"Which challenge is most associated with an ageing population?","options":{"A":"Greater pressure on pensions and care","B":"Demand for primary schools rises fastest","C":"The dependent child population always doubles","D":"Life expectancy must fall below infancy"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-56","spec":"GCSE-DEV","stem":"Why can tied aid provide less benefit than its headline value suggests?","options":{"A":"Recipients control every purchasing decision","B":"Aid never funds infrastructure or services","C":"The donor receives no economic advantage","D":"Purchases may be restricted to donor firms"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-DEV-TIED-AID","scaffold":"Tied aid can require recipients to buy goods or expertise from the donor, reducing local choice and sometimes raising costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which feature would make aid more responsive to local priorities?","options":{"A":"Repayment terms are kept confidential","B":"Community-led spending decisions","C":"All equipment must come from one donor","D":"Projects ignore local maintenance skills"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-MEASURING-DEVELOPMENT","stem":"Equatorial Guinea has a high GDP per capita from oil, but low life expectancy and poor schooling. What does this reveal about GDP per capita as a measure?","options":{"A":"It is an average that says nothing about how wealth is distributed","B":"It measures health and education outcomes rather than economic output","C":"It is unreliable because oil exports cannot be valued in any currency","D":"It always understates the true level of development of a small country"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"GDP per capita divides total output by population. It captures the size of the economy but not who receives the income, nor whether it converts into health, schooling or safety. Where oil revenue is captured by a small elite, the mean can look like a developed country while most people's lives do not.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the Human Development Index often preferred to GDP per capita alone?","options":{"A":"HDI is measured yearly whereas GDP figures are only collected once","B":"HDI ignores income entirely and so avoids all measurement problems","C":"HDI combines income with life expectancy and education outcomes","D":"HDI is calculated by each individual country using its own chosen method"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-DTM","stem":"A country's birth rate is falling steeply while its death rate is already low. Which stage of the Demographic Transition Model is it in?","options":{"A":"Stage 1, with both birth and death rates high and fluctuating","B":"Stage 3, with birth rates falling and natural increase slowing","C":"Stage 2, with death rates falling fast and population growing rapidly","D":"Stage 5, with births below deaths and total population now declining"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Stage 2 is the rapid-growth stage: deaths fall through sanitation and medicine while births stay high. Stage 3 is when birth rates fall too, driven by female education, contraception, urbanisation and lower infant mortality, so the growth rate slows even though the population is still rising.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change is most responsible for the fall in birth rates that marks Stage 3 of the DTM?","options":{"A":"A sudden rise in the death rate reducing the population of adults","B":"An increase in the amount of farmland available to each rural household","C":"The arrival of new diseases which reduce the number of children born","D":"Improved female education and access to contraception in urban areas"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-ROSTOW","stem":"Rostow's model suggests all countries pass through the same five stages towards mass consumption. What is the strongest criticism of this?","options":{"A":"Rostow's model contains only two stages rather than the five described","B":"It assumes that economic growth is undesirable at every single stage of development","C":"It ignores colonial history, debt and trade rules that constrain some countries","D":"It was written before any country had reached high mass consumption"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Rostow modelled development as an internal sequence any country can climb given investment. Dependency theorists reply that the sequence was available to countries that industrialised first, and that colonial extraction, debt burdens and terms of trade actively hold others back, so the path is not equally open.","reforge":{"stem":"How does dependency theory differ most fundamentally from Rostow's model?","options":{"A":"It treats underdevelopment as caused by relationships between countries","B":"It argues countries develop faster if they take on far more external debt","C":"It claims every country passes through exactly the same five fixed stages","D":"It states that industrialisation has never occurred in any country at all"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-TNC-FDI","stem":"A TNC opens a factory in an emerging country. Which outcome is a genuine disadvantage for the host country rather than a benefit?","options":{"A":"Local workers gain training and skills that transfer to other employers","B":"Local suppliers win contracts and expand their own workforces","C":"Tax revenue from the factory funds new roads and schools in the region","D":"Much of the profit is repatriated to the TNC's country of origin"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Evaluating FDI means weighing both sides. Jobs, skills, infrastructure and supplier contracts are real gains. Against them: profits leave the country, wages may stay low, tax holidays reduce revenue, and the TNC can relocate. A good answer names the leakage of profit specifically rather than saying TNCs are simply bad.","reforge":{"stem":"What does 'profit repatriation' mean in the context of a transnational corporation?","options":{"A":"Workers being sent back to their home country when a factory closes","B":"Profits earned locally being transferred back to the TNC's home country","C":"A government taking full ownership of a foreign-owned factory by force","D":"Profits being reinvested entirely within the region where they were earned"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-INTERMEDIATE-TECH","stem":"An NGO installs hand-operated water pumps that villagers can repair with locally available parts, rather than a diesel-powered borehole system. Why?","options":{"A":"Simple, locally maintainable technology keeps working after the NGO leaves","B":"Hand pumps deliver a far greater volume of water than any borehole system","C":"Diesel systems are prohibited by international law in low income countries","D":"Villagers are unable to learn how to operate more complex mechanical equipment"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Intermediate or appropriate technology is matched to local skills, spare parts and income. A diesel pump outperforms a hand pump until the fuel budget runs out or a part fails and no one can source it. Sustainability here means the scheme still functions in five years, which favours the simpler design.","reforge":{"stem":"Which feature best identifies a development project as using intermediate technology?","options":{"A":"It relies on imported specialist components replaced by foreign engineers","B":"It is the most technically advanced solution currently available anywhere","C":"It can be built, operated and repaired using local skills and materials","D":"It requires a continuous supply of imported fuel to keep running each day"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-MULTIPLIER","stem":"A new port creates 500 direct jobs, but local shops, housing and transport firms also expand. What is this knock-on process called?","options":{"A":"The demographic dividend arising from a youthful national population","B":"The multiplier effect, where initial investment generates further activity","C":"Deindustrialisation affecting the older manufacturing areas around the port","D":"The dependency ratio rising as more people find work in the local area"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"The multiplier works through spending. Wages from the port are spent locally, so shops hire, landlords build and hauliers expand, and those workers spend in turn. It is why governments justify one large project by its wider regional effect, and why the same logic in reverse deepens decline when a major employer closes.","reforge":{"stem":"A large regional employer closes and unemployment rises well beyond its own former workforce. What explains the wider effect?","options":{"A":"The dependency ratio in the region has been deliberately reduced","B":"An intermediate technology scheme has replaced the closed employer","C":"The demographic transition has now moved the whole region into its next stage","D":"The multiplier operating in reverse as lost wages cut local spending"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-TIED-AID","stem":"A donor country gives aid on condition that the recipient buys construction equipment from the donor's own firms. What is this called, and why is it criticised?","options":{"A":"Multilateral aid, criticised because too many countries are involved","B":"Emergency aid, criticised for arriving too slowly after a sudden disaster","C":"Tied aid, criticised because much of the value flows back to the donor","D":"Debt relief, criticised because it cancels loans that were repayable"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Tied aid comes with purchasing conditions. The recipient gets the project but cannot shop for the cheapest supplier, so a share of the money returns to the donor economy and the real value delivered is lower. It can also lock in equipment the recipient cannot maintain or afford to run.","reforge":{"stem":"Which type of aid is distributed through an international organisation such as the World Bank rather than directly between two governments?","options":{"A":"Multilateral aid pooled from many donors and allocated by an agency","B":"Bilateral aid agreed between one donor government and one recipient","C":"Tied aid conditional on purchasing goods from the donor's own firms","D":"Short-term emergency aid delivered immediately after a natural disaster"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-DEPENDENCY-DEMOGRAPHIC-DIVIDEND","stem":"A country's birth rate has fallen but its large youth cohort is now entering working age. Why is this called a demographic dividend?","options":{"A":"The number of dependants per worker has risen sharply across the country","B":"Its population pyramid has become a perfect triangle with a wide base","C":"The country's total population has begun to fall for the very first time","D":"A high share of working-age people supports relatively few dependants"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The dividend is a window, not a permanent state. Falling birth rates shrink the child dependent group before the same cohort ages into retirement, so for a few decades an unusually large share of the population is of working age. It only pays off if there are jobs and schooling for them, otherwise it becomes youth unemployment.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the demographic dividend described as a temporary opportunity rather than a permanent advantage?","options":{"A":"Because birth rates always rise again immediately after they have fallen","B":"The large working-age cohort eventually ages into retirement dependency","C":"Because the working-age population never actually spends any of its income","D":"Because dependency ratios are fixed and cannot change over time at all"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-BRANDT-LINE","stem":"Geographers now use the Brandt Line far less than in the 1980s. What is the main reason?","options":{"A":"Rapid growth in Asia means the North-South split no longer fits the map","B":"The countries the line separated have physically moved on the Earth's surface","C":"Development is no longer measured or compared between countries at all","D":"The line was drawn by an organisation that has since been formally dissolved"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"The Brandt Line captured a real 1980 pattern: an industrialised North and a poorer South. Since then South Korea, Singapore, China and the Gulf states have transformed, parts of the former Soviet bloc have not, and inequality within countries now often exceeds that between them. A single line hides all of that.","reforge":{"stem":"Which fact most directly undermines the usefulness of a simple North-South division of the world?","options":{"A":"Every country in the global South has an identical level of development","B":"No country's level of development has changed at all since 1980","C":"Several southern countries now have higher HDI than some northern ones","D":"The Equator and the Brandt Line follow exactly the same route on a map"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-INEQUALITY","stem":"A country's HDI rises steadily, yet the gap between its richest and poorest regions widens. What does this show?","options":{"A":"HDI must have been calculated incorrectly for that particular country","B":"National averages can improve while internal inequality gets worse","C":"Rising HDI always guarantees that regional gaps will close over time","D":"Regional inequality within a single country cannot be measured by geographers"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"A national indicator is a mean. Core regions with ports, capitals and investment can pull the average up fast while peripheral rural regions stagnate, so the country develops and the internal gap grows at the same time. This is why Edexcel asks for evidence at more than one scale.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do geographers examine development at regional as well as national scale?","options":{"A":"Because regional data is always more accurate than national data is","B":"Because national governments are not permitted to publish national figures","C":"Because development only ever varies between countries, not within them","D":"Because national figures are averages that can conceal sharp internal gaps"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-LOCATION-BARRIERS","stem":"Landlocked countries such as Chad and Bolivia tend to have lower incomes than their coastal neighbours. What is the main geographical reason?","options":{"A":"Landlocked countries always have populations too small to trade at all","B":"Landlocked countries receive no rainfall and therefore cannot farm the land","C":"Trade must cross another country, adding cost, delay and political risk","D":"International law forbids landlocked countries from exporting any goods abroad"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Access to a port shapes trade cost. A landlocked exporter pays for road or rail haulage plus border formalities, and depends on a neighbour's infrastructure and goodwill. Transport costs can add tens of percent to the price of exports, which is enough to make some industries uncompetitive before they start.","reforge":{"stem":"Which physical factor most directly raises the cost of trade for a landlocked country?","options":{"A":"Having no coastline, so all exports must travel overland through neighbours","B":"Having a climate that is significantly cooler than that of coastal nations","C":"Having a smaller total land area than every one of its immediate neighbours","D":"Having no mountains along the borders that it shares with other countries"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"},{"id":"GCSE-DEV-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-DEV","tag":"MC-DEV-DEBT-RELIEF","stem":"A low income country spends more on servicing external debt each year than on healthcare. Why do campaigners argue for debt relief?","options":{"A":"Cancelling debt automatically removes the risk of any future borrowing","B":"Debt repayments are illegal under the rules of the World Trade Organisation","C":"Debt relief guarantees that a country's economy will grow every year after","D":"Money spent on repayments could instead fund health, schooling and water"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Debt servicing is an opportunity cost. Every pound repaid on a loan, sometimes one taken by a previous government decades earlier, is a pound unavailable for clinics or classrooms. The counter-argument is that relief without governance reform can simply enable more borrowing, which is why schemes attach conditions.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main counter-argument against cancelling the debts of low income countries?","options":{"A":"Cancellation would reduce the amount those countries spend on healthcare","B":"Relief without reform may encourage further unsustainable borrowing","C":"Debt cancellation is technically impossible for any lender to carry out","D":"Countries receiving relief are then obliged to leave all trade agreements"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dev"}]},"GCSE-GEO-INDIA":{"label":"India — Emerging Country Case Study","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-IND-01","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"What is the most accurate description of India's global economic significance?","options":{"A":"The world's fifth largest economy, ahead of the UK","B":"It is the world's largest economy and has been since 1990","C":"It has the smallest economy of the BRICS countries and is shrinking","D":"Its economy is entirely based on agriculture"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-GLOBAL-INFLUENCE","scaffold":"Key India stats worth memorising: seventh largest country by area; second largest population (1.3+ billion); fifth largest economy (overtook the UK, Sept 2022); GDP around US$3.18 trillion (2021) with GDP per capita only about US$2,256; roughly 500% GDP growth since 1990; annual growth around 8.7%. The gap between huge total GDP and low GDP per capita is the single most useful contrast in this case study.","reforge":{"stem":"India has the world's fifth largest total GDP but a GDP per capita of only around US$2,256. What does this contrast show?","options":{"A":"Its GDP figure must have been calculated incorrectly","B":"India is a developed country by every measure","C":"A huge population divides that wealth thinly","D":"GDP per capita is always higher than GDP"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-02","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How has the structure of India's economy changed since 1990?","options":{"A":"Agriculture now contributes over 75% of GDP","B":"Services now lead, but farming employs most","C":"Manufacturing has disappeared entirely","D":"It has shifted from services to agriculture"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-ECONOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The classic exam trap here is the split between GDP contribution and employment. Services now dominate GDP, but agriculture remains the largest single employer — falling from around 75% of workers to just over 50% by 2010 and about 43% by 2019. So a huge share of the workforce is still in the low-productivity sector, which is exactly why average incomes lag behind headline GDP growth.","reforge":{"stem":"Agriculture employs around 43% of India's workers but contributes a far smaller share of GDP. What does this imply?","options":{"A":"Agricultural workers are the highest paid in India","B":"Agriculture is growing faster than any other sector","C":"India imports no food","D":"Farm incomes lag services, deepening rural poverty"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-03","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why are TNCs attracted to invest in India?","options":{"A":"India bans foreign investment in manufacturing","B":"India has the highest labour costs in Asia","C":"A large, low-cost, English-speaking workforce","D":"India has a very small domestic market"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-IND-INVESTMENT-POLICY","scaffold":"India's pull factors: skilled, well-educated, low-cost, largely English-speaking labour (crucial for call centres and IT outsourcing); economic liberalisation since 1991 (government took less control over what industries produce); reduced import tariffs; special economic zones with tax reductions. Evidence: FDI inflows of $45 billion in 2021, Google's $1bn investment in Bharti Airtel (2022), Facebook's $5.7bn in Reliance Jio (2020).","reforge":{"stem":"Which government action since 1991 has most directly encouraged foreign investment in India?","options":{"A":"Economic liberalisation from 1991","B":"Banning the export of manufactured goods","C":"Nationalising all foreign-owned companies","D":"Raising taxes on all foreign companies"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-04","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"A student writes: 'India receives aid, so it must be a poor developing country.' Why is this misleading?","options":{"A":"India has never received any aid","B":"Aid has no connection to a country's level of development","C":"Only developed countries can receive aid","D":"India now gives aid as well as receiving it"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEVELOPMENT-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"India received US$4,240 million of Official Development Assistance in 2017 (over 50% from Japan), targeted at its poorest states such as Madhya Pradesh — but it is simultaneously an aid donor to other countries. It also changed policy in 2003 to refuse tied aid except from a few partners including the UK, USA and Germany. Being both recipient and donor is a hallmark of an emerging country.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did India decide in 2003 to stop accepting most tied aid?","options":{"A":"India no longer needed any external funding at all","B":"The money must be spent with the donor country","C":"Tied aid is illegal under international law","D":"Tied aid must be repaid with very high interest"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-05","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Which pair of figures best illustrates the regional inequality between India's urban core and rural periphery?","options":{"A":"US$7,500 in Delhi against US$630 in Bihar","B":"Delhi has a higher fertility rate than Bihar","C":"Delhi and Bihar both have a literacy rate of 86%","D":"Bihar has a higher life expectancy than Delhi"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEVELOPMENT-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Delhi (urban core) vs Bihar (rural periphery): life expectancy 72.9 vs 68.7 years; literacy 86.21% vs 69.83%; GDP per capita US$7,500 vs US$630; death rate 4.1 vs 5.4 per 1,000; fertility 1.6 vs 3.0. Learn one figure from each row — a 4-mark 'explain two socio-economic differences between regions' question needs both the difference AND the reason.","reforge":{"stem":"Why has Bihar remained poorer than Delhi?","options":{"A":"Bihar has no agricultural land at all","B":"Bihar refuses to trade with the rest of India","C":"Floods, weak infrastructure and out-migration hold it back","D":"Bihar has a much larger population than the rest of India combined"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-06","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Roughly what proportion of India's population lived in urban areas in 2021, and how fast is this changing?","options":{"A":"About 80%, and falling","B":"About 34%, rising 2% a year","C":"About 60%, and falling rapidly","D":"About 5%, and static"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-URBANISATION","scaffold":"34% urban in 2021 = 475 million people, growing about 2% per year, with roughly 20 million people migrating from rural to urban areas annually. The significance: India is still majority rural, so it has decades of urbanisation ahead — which is why housing, water supply and air quality pressures in cities are projected to worsen, not ease.","reforge":{"stem":"About 20 million Indians migrate from rural to urban areas each year. Which combination of push and pull factors best explains this?","options":{"A":"Push: government orders. Pull: compulsory rural resettlement","B":"Push: overcrowded cities. Pull: empty countryside","C":"Push: high urban wages. Pull: subsistence farming opportunities","D":"Push: floods and low farm pay. Pull: city jobs and wages"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-07","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How has India's demographic profile changed as its economy has grown?","options":{"A":"Life expectancy has fallen while fertility has risen","B":"There has been no measurable demographic change since 1960","C":"Life expectancy rose from 45 in 1960 to 70 in 2021","D":"Birth and death rates have both risen sharply"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEMOGRAPHIC-CHANGE","scaffold":"Economic development → demographic change. Life expectancy 45 (1960) → 70 (2021), driven by better diets, healthcare availability and water quality. Fertility falling because women are more educated, more women work, contraception is more available, and family planning education has spread. This is textbook DTM stage 3 — link the two topics in your answers.","reforge":{"stem":"India's population pyramid has changed shape between 1960 and 2022. Which change would you expect to see?","options":{"A":"A narrowing base and a widening middle and top","B":"No change in shape, only in total size","C":"A complete inversion, with more over-80s than under-10s","D":"A widening base as birth rates rise"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-08","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Which is a clear SOCIAL benefit of India's economic development?","options":{"A":"An increase in car ownership in New Delhi","B":"An increase in the number of landfill sites","C":"A rise in carbon dioxide emissions","D":"Better education for women"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEMOGRAPHIC-CHANGE","scaffold":"Sort impacts carefully — exam questions specify social, economic or environmental. A, B and C are environmental impacts, while D is a social benefit: contraception and career opportunities can improve women's autonomy and gender equality. India still has uneven access and a gender gap, so the benefit is real but not universal.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a social COST of India's rapid economic development?","options":{"A":"Improved access to family planning","B":"Growth of squatter settlements","C":"Increased literacy rates among women","D":"Rising average incomes"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-09","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why does rural-to-urban migration create problems for India's rural areas?","options":{"A":"Younger workers leave, so rural areas age","B":"It raises rural wages above urban wages","C":"It causes rural areas to become overcrowded","D":"It increases the number of schools needed in villages"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-POPULATION-MOBILITY","scaffold":"This is a favourite 4-mark 'impacts on different age groups' question. Chain it: rural–urban migration (1) → an ageing population left in rural areas with no-one to care for them and few economically active workers (1). Add the other side: children in rural areas are more likely to work rather than attend school, and rural investment is far lower than urban.","reforge":{"stem":"A 4-mark question asks for two negative impacts of rapid development on different age groups. Which answer is structured correctly?","options":{"A":"Air pollution is a problem in New Delhi because there are lots of cars and factories and it is very bad.","B":"Development is bad for everyone in India.","C":"Air pollution (1) harms health (1); too few urban jobs (1) leave migrants unemployed (1)","D":"Old people and young people are both affected by development in different ways."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-10","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"What are the main causes of India's severe urban air pollution?","options":{"A":"Almost entirely from imported goods","B":"Vehicles, factories and stubble burning","C":"Solely the burning of stubble by farmers","D":"Only volcanic activity"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-AIR-POLLUTION","scaffold":"Multi-causal — a good answer names several sources. New Delhi alone has over 10 million vehicles and 3,000–4,000 factories; over 100 million people nationally rely on biomass for cooking. Scale evidence: air pollution runs at 4× WHO guidelines, and 21 of the world's 30 most polluted cities were in India in 2019.","reforge":{"stem":"What are the human effects of India's air pollution?","options":{"A":"There are no measurable health effects","B":"It only affects people over 65","C":"It reduces crop yields but has no effect on human health","D":"Life expectancy is about 5 years lower"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-11","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Approximately what proportion of India's sewage is treated, and what is the consequence?","options":{"A":"About 95% is treated, so water pollution is minimal","B":"All sewage is treated but rivers are polluted by volcanic ash","C":"Only about 30% is treated before it reaches rivers","D":"None is treated, but this has no effect on water quality"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-IND-WATER-WASTE","scaffold":"Water pollution causes: only 30% of sewage treated; industrial waste water carrying mercury, lead and arsenic; heavy pesticide use including DDT, banned elsewhere. Consequences: the Indus and Ganges are among the ten most polluted rivers in the world, 33% of remaining rivers moderately or severely polluted, ~70% of surface water unfit to drink. Effects: aquatic life damaged, liver and kidney disease, gastric cancers, birth defects, lower crop yields.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is India's continued use of pesticides such as DDT an environmental concern?","options":{"A":"It builds up in food chains","B":"DDT only affects the air, not water","C":"DDT improves water quality","D":"DDT is too expensive for farmers to use"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-12","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"What is the main environmental consequence of India sending around 90% of its waste to landfill?","options":{"A":"A reduction in greenhouse gas emissions","B":"Improved groundwater quality","C":"A fall in the total volume of waste produced","D":"Methane, toxic leachate and landfill fires"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-IND-WATER-WASTE","scaffold":"India has over 3,000 landfills and generates around 2.01 billion tonnes of waste annually, ~90% landfilled. Ghazipur in Delhi is 65 m high and caught fire in March 2022, worsening air pollution — a useful example because it links land pollution to air pollution. India is also the third largest CO₂ emitter and fourth largest methane emitter, with over 60% of methane from agriculture.","reforge":{"stem":"India is the world's fourth largest emitter of methane, with over 60% coming from agriculture. Why does this create a development dilemma?","options":{"A":"Methane emissions have no climate effect","B":"Cutting emissions constrains farming and energy","C":"India could simply stop all farming without consequence","D":"Only developed countries are asked to reduce emissions"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-13","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Which evidence best demonstrates India's growing geopolitical influence?","options":{"A":"Membership of BRICS, the G20 and the World Bank","B":"Its refusal to trade with the EU or USA","C":"Its withdrawal from all international organisations","D":"Its declining population"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-GLOBAL-INFLUENCE","scaffold":"Geopolitical influence: growing role in ASEAN; membership of BRICS, G20, UN Security Council, World Bank, IMF, WTO, Asian Development Bank; the IMF attributes about 15% of global growth to India. Trade relationships: the EU is India's third largest trading partner (exports €46.2bn, imports €41.9bn — roughly balanced); with the USA India exports more (US$49.7bn) than it imports (US$26.6bn).","reforge":{"stem":"How does the 1947 partition of India and Pakistan still affect India's development today?","options":{"A":"It has had no effect since 1947","B":"It removed all border disputes in the region","C":"A million deaths and lasting tension","D":"It made India a developed country"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-14","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Which is a COST to India of its changing international relations?","options":{"A":"India becoming able to provide aid to other countries","B":"Low tax deals for TNCs cut government revenue","C":"Increased trade with countries around the world","D":"Wages rising and improving average quality of life"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-INVESTMENT-POLICY","scaffold":"Costs include pressure to cut greenhouse gas emissions, low taxes for TNCs reducing government revenue, reliance on external firms, weak regulation and environmental damage. Benefits include growth funding healthcare, education and infrastructure, wider trade, rising wages, India becoming an aid donor and improved national security. B is a cost because low tax rates attract TNCs but reduce government revenue; C and D are benefits.","reforge":{"stem":"India's increased defence spending is listed as both a cost and a benefit. Why?","options":{"A":"Because it has no effect either way","B":"Because defence spending only affects developed countries","C":"Because defence spending is not really a form of spending","D":"It aids security but diverts money from public services"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-15","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"India is both a recipient and a source of foreign direct investment. Which fact demonstrates the outward flow?","options":{"A":"India received $45 billion of FDI inflows in 2021","B":"Singapore provides 27% of investment into India","C":"Indian firms invested $17.24 billion abroad","D":"Google invested US$1 billion in Bharti Airtel"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-IND-INVESTMENT-IMPACTS","scaffold":"Inflows into India include $45bn in 2021, mainly from Singapore (27%), the USA (18%), Mauritius (16%) and the Netherlands (8%), targeting automobiles, electrical machinery and textiles. Outflows from India include $17.24bn invested abroad, including Indian firms investing in the UK. A and B describe inflows; C is an outflow from India; D is a separate corporate-investment example, so read the direction carefully.","reforge":{"stem":"What does India's position as a major outward investor suggest about Frank's dependency theory?","options":{"A":"A former periphery now invests in core economies","B":"It shows India is still purely a raw material exporter","C":"It proves core countries no longer exist","D":"It confirms the theory exactly as Frank described it"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-16","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How has improved education contributed to India's economic development?","options":{"A":"Only private education exists in India","B":"Education has been reduced to lower government spending","C":"Education has no connection to economic development","D":"Free primary education since 2009 raises skills"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-IND-SKILLS-DEVELOPMENT","scaffold":"Primary education became free in 2009; the 2020 National Education Policy aimed to improve equality and overall standards. Literacy is around 77%. The chain to learn: education → skills → employability → higher wages → more consumer spending and tax revenue → more investment in services and infrastructure → multiplier effect. It also links to falling fertility, since educated women have fewer children.","reforge":{"stem":"Improving female education in India affects both the economy and demography. How?","options":{"A":"It raises fertility rates and lowers incomes","B":"It raises incomes and also lowers fertility rates","C":"It affects the economy but has no demographic effect","D":"It reduces the workforce because women stop working"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-IND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about India's location and significance.","template":"India is located in Asia between the Arabian Sea and the Bay of ___. It is the ___ largest country in the world by area and has the ___ largest population.","blanks":["Bengal","seventh","second"],"bank":["Bengal","seventh","second","Oman","first","third"],"tag":"MC-GG-IND-GEOGRAPHICAL-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Site and situation: a peninsula with water on three sides, north of the equator, bordering six countries (Bangladesh, Nepal, Myanmar, Pakistan, China, Bhutan). Seventh largest by area, second largest by population. Its position between the Middle East and South East Asia gives access to both customers and labour.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-IND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about India's economy.","template":"In 2021 India's GDP was about US$3.18 ___, but GDP per ___ was only about US$2,256. Since 1990 GDP has grown by around ___%.","blanks":["trillion","capita","500"],"bank":["trillion","capita","500","billion","head","50"],"tag":"MC-GG-IND-SCALE-MEASURES","scaffold":"The tension in this case study: enormous total output, modest income per person, because 1.3 billion people share it. Annual growth is around 8.7%, and India overtook the UK to become the fifth largest economy in September 2022.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-IND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about India's economic sectors.","template":"India's GDP is now dominated by the ___ sector, but ___ remains the largest employer, falling from about 75% of workers to around ___% by 2019.","blanks":["service","agriculture","43"],"bank":["service","agriculture","43","primary","manufacturing","75"],"tag":"MC-GG-IND-ECONOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Separate GDP contribution from employment — this is where marks are lost. Services dominate output; agriculture still employs the largest share of people (75% → just over 50% by 2010 → about 43% by 2019). Low agricultural productivity is a key cause of rural poverty in states like Bihar.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-IND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about regional inequality in India.","template":"Delhi, the urban core, has a GDP per capita of about US$7,500 and literacy of 86.21%, while ___, in the rural periphery, has GDP per capita of just US$___ and a much higher ___ rate of 3.0.","blanks":["Bihar","630","fertility"],"bank":["Bihar","630","fertility","Mumbai","6300","death"],"tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEVELOPMENT-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Delhi vs Bihar is the core–periphery contrast you must be able to quote. Life expectancy 72.9 vs 68.7; literacy 86.21% vs 69.83%; GDP per capita US$7,500 vs US$630; death rate 4.1 vs 5.4; fertility 1.6 vs 3.0. Bihar's recurring floods, poor infrastructure and out-migration explain the gap.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-FB-05","spec":"GCSE-IND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about urbanisation in India.","template":"In 2021 about ___% of India's population lived in urban areas — around 475 million people. Urbanisation is rising by about ___% a year, with roughly ___ million people migrating from rural to urban areas annually.","blanks":["34","2","20"],"bank":["34","2","20","64","12","5"],"tag":"MC-GG-IND-URBANISATION","scaffold":"India is still majority rural at 34% urban, which is why its urbanisation pressures are projected to intensify for decades. 475 million urban residents, growing ~2% per year, fed by roughly 20 million rural–urban migrants annually.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-FB-06","spec":"GCSE-IND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about demographic change in India.","template":"Life expectancy in India rose from ___ years in 1960 to ___ years in 2021, while ___ rates are falling as more women are educated and contraception is more available.","blanks":["45","70","fertility"],"bank":["45","70","fertility","55","80","death"],"tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEMOGRAPHIC-CHANGE","scaffold":"Economic development drives demographic change: better diets, healthcare and water quality raised life expectancy from 45 to 70; education, female employment, contraception and family planning are lowering fertility. This places India in stage 3 of the DTM.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-FB-07","spec":"GCSE-IND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about pollution in India.","template":"Only about ___% of India's sewage is treated, leaving roughly ___% of surface water unfit for human consumption. Air pollution is around ___ times the WHO recommended guidelines.","blanks":["30","70","4"],"bank":["30","70","4","90","15","2"],"tag":"MC-GG-IND-WATER-WASTE","scaffold":"Water: 30% of sewage treated, ~70% of surface water unfit to drink, the Indus and Ganges among the world's ten most polluted rivers. Air: 4× WHO guidelines, 21 of the world's 30 most polluted cities in 2019, over 1.5 million deaths in 2019, life expectancy about 5 years lower on average.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-FB-08","spec":"GCSE-IND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about India's international position.","template":"India is a member of ___, a group that also includes Brazil, Russia, China and South Africa. According to the ___, India is responsible for about 15% of global growth. India is now both a recipient and a ___ of aid.","blanks":["BRICS","IMF","donor"],"bank":["BRICS","IMF","donor","ASEAN","WTO","borrower"],"tag":"MC-GG-IND-GLOBAL-INFLUENCE","scaffold":"India's memberships: UN (and Security Council), IMF, World Bank, WTO, Asian Development Bank, G20, BRICS, with growing influence in ASEAN. Being simultaneously an aid recipient (US$4,240m ODA in 2017) and an aid donor is a defining feature of an emerging country.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-17","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why has India's growth in IT and business services been important for economic development?","options":{"A":"Skilled jobs, investment and higher export earnings","B":"It has made agriculture disappear completely","C":"It proves manufacturing is no longer part of India's economy","D":"It only benefits foreign tourists"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-SKILLS-DEVELOPMENT","scaffold":"India's service sector, including IT and business process outsourcing, has generated skilled employment, export income and global links. It also supports multiplier effects, although benefits are uneven and many workers remain in lower-paid informal sectors.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is Bangalore often used as evidence of India's changing economy?","options":{"A":"It is a subsistence farming region only","B":"It proves India has no rural poverty","C":"A shift towards higher-value services","D":"It shows that FDI has stopped entering India"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-18","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"A student says India's economic growth has benefited everyone equally. Which evidence challenges this?","options":{"A":"All states have identical income and infrastructure","B":"Sharp inequality between richer and poorer states","C":"No one has moved to cities","D":"India has no informal employment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEVELOPMENT-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Emerging-country growth is uneven. India's major cities and some states have gained strongly from services, industry and FDI, while many rural areas still face poorer access to sanitation, healthcare, education and secure employment.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should an answer about India's development include regional inequality?","options":{"A":"Because every region has exactly the same development level","B":"Because inequality only exists between countries, never within them","C":"Because India's development is entirely environmental","D":"National averages hide the urban-rural gap"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-19","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How can TNC investment create both benefits and costs for India?","options":{"A":"It only creates costs and never jobs","B":"It only creates benefits and has no trade-offs","C":"Jobs and technology, but profits may leave","D":"It is the same as debt relief"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-IND-INVESTMENT-IMPACTS","scaffold":"TNCs can bring FDI, jobs, skills, infrastructure and tax revenue. Evaluation matters: some profits leave the country, wages may be low, working conditions can be poor, and factories or offices can increase pressure on water, energy and transport.","reforge":{"stem":"A foreign company opens a factory in India. Which is the most balanced judgement?","options":{"A":"It depends on wages, tax and conditions","B":"It is automatically perfect development","C":"It has no effect on local workers","D":"It is automatically harmful"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-20","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why is rapid urban growth in India linked to both opportunity and challenge?","options":{"A":"Cities only create problems and never jobs","B":"Urban growth means rural areas instantly become richer","C":"Urban growth has no connection to infrastructure","D":"Jobs and services, but pressure on housing"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-IND-URBANISATION","scaffold":"Urban growth is a core feature of India's development. It can concentrate investment, education and employment, but if infrastructure lags behind population growth it creates congestion, pollution, informal housing and pressure on services.","reforge":{"stem":"Which answer best evaluates rural-to-urban migration in India?","options":{"A":"It has no impact on either cities or rural areas","B":"Better access to work, but urban strain","C":"It only happens because cities force people to leave","D":"It removes all inequality immediately"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-21","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why is GDP per capita more useful than total GDP when comparing average income between countries?","options":{"A":"It divides output by population","B":"It measures only the informal economy","C":"It shows how income is distributed exactly","D":"It removes the need for any other development indicator"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-SCALE-MEASURES","scaffold":"GDP per capita is total GDP divided by population, so it gives an average output or income figure that allows countries of different population sizes to be compared. It does not show inequality or every aspect of quality of life.","reforge":{"stem":"India has a very large total GDP but a much lower GDP per capita than many developed countries. Why?","options":{"A":"Total GDP is calculated from rainfall","B":"GDP per capita always ignores population","C":"A large population shares total output","D":"India has no economic activity"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-22","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Which evidence would best show regional inequality within India?","options":{"A":"A single national GDP figure","B":"Rural incomes and services lag","C":"India's total land area","D":"The date of India's independence"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEVELOPMENT-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"National averages can hide differences within a country. Comparing income, infrastructure, healthcare, education or sanitation between richer urban cores and poorer rural regions gives evidence of regional inequality.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a national average hide development problems in India?","options":{"A":"National averages include no numerical data","B":"Averages measure only the climate","C":"All Indian regions have identical access to services","D":"Urban and rural gaps are large"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-23","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How has rapid economic development contributed to urbanisation in India?","options":{"A":"It has removed all rural employment","B":"It has stopped people moving between regions","C":"City jobs attract migrants","D":"It has reduced the need for housing in cities"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-IND-URBANISATION","scaffold":"Economic change creates urban employment in manufacturing, services and construction. Rural push factors such as low farm incomes or flooding combine with urban pull factors such as jobs, wages and services to increase rural-to-urban migration.","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination best explains rural-to-urban migration in India?","options":{"A":"Rural pushes meet urban jobs","B":"Urban lack of services and rural factory jobs","C":"Rural high wages and urban drought","D":"No economic difference between rural and urban areas"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-24","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Which is an environmental consequence of India's rapid economic development?","options":{"A":"Industrial growth leaves air quality unchanged","B":"Economic growth protects all farmland","C":"Urban expansion removes waste production","D":"Growth raises air and water pollution"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-IND-AIR-POLLUTION","scaffold":"India's growth has brought jobs, incomes and infrastructure but also serious environmental pressures. Industrial emissions, vehicle pollution, untreated sewage, landfill and greenhouse gases affect people at local, regional and global scales.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can development improve living standards while harming the environment?","options":{"A":"Environmental impacts occur only in developed countries","B":"Jobs can bring pollution","C":"Pollution cannot affect human health","D":"Higher incomes always mean less waste"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-25","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How has India's changing international role affected its geopolitical influence?","options":{"A":"Greater influence in global forums","B":"It has left all international organisations","C":"It no longer trades with the EU or USA","D":"Influence depends only on population"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-GLOBAL-INFLUENCE","scaffold":"As India's economy has grown, its influence through organisations such as the G20, BRICS and the WTO, as well as regional and global trade relationships, has increased. This has not removed every conflict or inequality.","reforge":{"stem":"Which development is evidence of India's increased international influence?","options":{"A":"The closure of every Indian company abroad","B":"A complete end to trade with major economies","C":"G20 and BRICS participation","D":"A fall in all international relationships"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-26","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Which is a possible cost of TNC investment in India?","options":{"A":"TNCs can never create employment","B":"Profit leakage and tax concessions","C":"TNCs only build schools in rural villages","D":"Foreign investment prevents all economic growth"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-INVESTMENT-IMPACTS","scaffold":"TNCs can bring capital, jobs, skills and export income, but profits may be repatriated, local firms may struggle, and low taxes or weak regulation may limit the benefits for public services and the environment.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might India offer tax incentives to TNCs despite a possible loss of tax revenue?","options":{"A":"To ensure no foreign company invests","B":"To reduce all employment in cities","C":"To prevent technology transfer","D":"Investment can create jobs"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-27","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"What could a proportional flow-line map show about India's international relationships?","options":{"A":"The age structure of one city","B":"The depth of the Ganges at each point","C":"Trade and investment flows","D":"The exact height of India's mountains"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-IND-GEOGRAPHICAL-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"A proportional flow-line map uses arrows with widths that represent the size of flows. In India's case, it could show trade, FDI or migration links with regions such as the EU, USA or neighbouring countries.","reforge":{"stem":"A wide arrow from India to the USA on a trade map would indicate:","options":{"A":"A larger flow of trade","B":"No trade connection at all","C":"A small flow of goods or services","D":"High rainfall in the USA"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-28","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why might geographers compare a region's value with the national mean?","options":{"A":"To calculate the country's total population","B":"To decide whether every household is identical","C":"To measure the length of a coastline","D":"Above or below the national mean"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-IND-SCALE-MEASURES","scaffold":"Comparing a core or periphery region with the national mean helps reveal spatial inequality. A difference from the mean can be calculated for indicators such as income, literacy, life expectancy or access to services.","reforge":{"stem":"A rural state has an income 30% below India's national mean. What does this comparison help demonstrate?","options":{"A":"The state has no economic activity","B":"Below-average regional income","C":"The national mean is always the lowest value","D":"Every state has the same income"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-29","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Which option describes India's situation rather than its site?","options":{"A":"Beside major Indian Ocean trade routes","B":"Much of the north contains fertile plains","C":"It occupies a large South Asian peninsula","D":"The Himalayas form a high mountain barrier"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-GEOGRAPHICAL-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Situation describes location relative to other places and connections; site describes physical characteristics of the place itself.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is India's Indian Ocean position economically significant?","options":{"A":"It isolates India from maritime trade","B":"It determines every regional rainfall total","C":"It connects ports to global shipping routes","D":"It prevents trade with East Asia"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-30","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How could India's youthful population create a demographic dividend?","options":{"A":"Every young person automatically gains a job","B":"Employment and skills can raise output","C":"The elderly population immediately disappears","D":"A larger workforce always lowers productivity"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-POPULATION-MOBILITY","scaffold":"A large working-age population can support growth if education, healthcare and enough productive jobs are available.","reforge":{"stem":"When might a youthful population become an economic challenge instead?","options":{"A":"When dependency ratios fall sustainably","B":"When all workers gain advanced training","C":"When firms invest in productive employment","D":"Insufficient jobs for workforce growth"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-31","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"What does a declining primary-sector share and rising tertiary share indicate?","options":{"A":"Agriculture now employs every urban worker","B":"The economy is becoming less diversified","C":"Economic structure is shifting toward services","D":"Manufacturing and services have both disappeared"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-IND-ECONOMIC-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Development commonly changes the relative importance of sectors as productivity rises and services expand.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can sector shares change even when farm output rises?","options":{"A":"Other sectors may grow more quickly","B":"Sector data measure only export destinations","C":"Agriculture must be producing less food","D":"Economic sectors cannot change together"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-32","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why has India attracted outsourced information-technology services?","options":{"A":"Time zones prevent contact with overseas clients","B":"English-language skills are unavailable","C":"Telecommunications are unnecessary for outsourcing","D":"Skilled labour can serve global firms remotely"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-IND-SKILLS-DEVELOPMENT","scaffold":"A large skilled workforce, English use, telecommunications and time-zone advantages have supported India's service exports.","reforge":{"stem":"What technological change most enabled international service outsourcing?","options":{"A":"Reduced access to higher education","B":"Fast cross-border digital links","C":"The closure of international airports","D":"A return to subsistence agriculture"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-33","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How can a special economic zone encourage foreign direct investment?","options":{"A":"Infrastructure and investment incentives","B":"It requires every firm to remain state-owned","C":"It bans firms from exporting their output","D":"It prevents transport links reaching factories"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-INVESTMENT-POLICY","scaffold":"SEZs may offer reliable infrastructure, streamlined regulation and tax incentives to attract domestic and foreign firms.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one possible cost of generous SEZ incentives?","options":{"A":"Every investor leaves before production begins","B":"Industrial areas cannot create any employment","C":"Tax revenue may be reduced initially","D":"Exports become illegal within the zone"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-34","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How can investment in education support India's development?","options":{"A":"Education lowers worker productivity in every sector","B":"Skills can attract higher-value industries","C":"It guarantees equal incomes across all states","D":"It prevents movement into higher-value employment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-SKILLS-DEVELOPMENT","scaffold":"Education develops human capital, enabling workers and firms to move into more productive manufacturing and service activities.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome would best show successful human-capital investment?","options":{"A":"Fewer workers able to use technology","B":"A permanent fall in school attendance","C":"Greater dependence on unprocessed exports","D":"Skilled jobs and higher productivity"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-35","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why has rural-urban migration contributed to India's regional differences?","options":{"A":"Migration makes every state's income identical","B":"All migrants move to the least connected districts","C":"Growth centres attract workers and investment","D":"Cities lose services whenever migrants arrive"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-IND-POPULATION-MOBILITY","scaffold":"Major cities and industrial corridors attract labour and capital, while some rural or peripheral regions receive less investment.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one possible benefit of migration for a rural origin area?","options":{"A":"Remittances support rural households","B":"Every working-age adult permanently leaves","C":"Urban growth stops in destination cities","D":"Local incomes must fall to zero"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-36","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why may rapid economic growth affect women and men differently?","options":{"A":"Growth automatically removes gender inequality","B":"Only men can work in service industries","C":"Education has no link to employment access","D":"Opportunities depend on norms and skills"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEMOGRAPHIC-CHANGE","scaffold":"Benefits depend on education, location, social expectations, safety and access to formal work, so growth is not shared evenly.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change would most widen women's access to formal employment?","options":{"A":"Removing childcare from every workplace","B":"Safer transport and improved education","C":"Restricting access to digital skills","D":"Reducing legal protection at work"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-37","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How can severe urban air pollution reduce the benefits of economic growth?","options":{"A":"Ill health lowers wellbeing and productivity","B":"Air quality affects only uninhabited areas","C":"Pollution raises life expectancy automatically","D":"Industrial emissions cannot enter cities"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-AIR-POLLUTION","scaffold":"Respiratory illness creates healthcare costs, missed work and reduced quality of life, offsetting some economic gains.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy tackles both transport emissions and congestion?","options":{"A":"Subsidise the most polluting private vehicles","B":"Remove all bus routes from city centres","C":"Expand reliable low-emission public transport","D":"Move every workplace beyond the urban fringe"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-38","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why can untreated industrial wastewater create impacts beyond a factory site?","options":{"A":"Rivers prevent pollutants moving downstream","B":"Pollutants spread through river systems","C":"Industrial chemicals disappear on contact with water","D":"Wastewater improves drinking-water quality"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-WATER-WASTE","scaffold":"Contaminants can move downstream, harming ecosystems, agriculture, fishing and communities using the water.","reforge":{"stem":"Which evidence best shows a downstream industrial impact?","options":{"A":"The river becomes steeper near its source","B":"Rainfall rises across the whole river basin","C":"Factories use less electricity during weekends","D":"Poorer water quality below the discharge"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-39","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"How has economic growth increased India's geopolitical influence?","options":{"A":"Economic growth prevents regional diplomacy","B":"India has withdrawn from every international forum","C":"Its market and economy strengthen its global voice","D":"Global influence depends only on land area"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-IND-GLOBAL-INFLUENCE","scaffold":"A larger economy, strategic location and expanding trade give India greater weight in regional and international organisations.","reforge":{"stem":"Which development would most increase a country's geopolitical leverage?","options":{"A":"Greater economic and strategic importance","B":"Complete isolation from international trade","C":"A permanent ban on diplomatic relations","D":"A decline in all regional connections"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-40","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Why can closer trade relations with the USA or EU create debate within India?","options":{"A":"Trade agreements have no domestic effects","B":"Every industry gains by exactly the same amount","C":"Foreign competition cannot affect local firms","D":"Benefits and costs differ among groups"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-IND-INVESTMENT-IMPACTS","scaffold":"Exporters and consumers may gain, while some domestic producers or workers face stronger competition and adjustment costs.","reforge":{"stem":"What makes an evaluation of a trade agreement balanced?","options":{"A":"Ignore employment and environmental effects","B":"Exporter gains versus exposed-sector costs","C":"Discuss only the total value of national exports","D":"Assume every household receives equal benefits"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-41","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"Three state incomes are 80, 100 and 120. How far is 80 below the mean?","options":{"A":"20 income units","B":"30 income units","C":"10 income units","D":"40 income units"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-IND-SCALE-MEASURES","scaffold":"The mean is 100, so the first state's value is 20 units below it.","reforge":{"stem":"A district value is 45 and the regional mean is 60. What is the difference from the mean?","options":{"A":"5 below the mean","B":"10 below the mean","C":"15 below the mean","D":"25 below the mean"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-42","spec":"GCSE-IND","stem":"On a proportional flow-line map, what does a wide arrow entering India show?","options":{"A":"The height of land along the border","B":"A relatively large inward flow","C":"The age structure of the destination","D":"The direction of prevailing winds"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-IND-GEOGRAPHICAL-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Flow-line width represents magnitude, while the arrow direction shows movement such as imports, investment or migration.","reforge":{"stem":"Two export arrows point to different markets. How should their values be compared?","options":{"A":"Assume the longer arrow is always larger","B":"Count the countries crossed by each arrow","C":"Measure only the arrow's compass direction","D":"Use their widths with the map key"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-ECONOMIC-STRUCTURE","stem":"India's services sector now contributes over half of GDP, but agriculture still employs a far larger share of workers. What does this mismatch show?","options":{"A":"Agriculture contributes more to India's GDP than services currently do","B":"India's service sector employs more people than any other sector does","C":"Agricultural productivity per worker is low compared with services","D":"Every Indian worker now earns roughly the same income as every other"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Compare the two shares directly. If agriculture employs around 40% of workers but generates under 20% of output, each farm worker is producing far less value than each service worker. That gap is the engine of rural-urban migration and of rural poverty, and it is why productivity, not just employment, matters.","reforge":{"stem":"A sector employs 45% of a country's workforce but produces only 15% of its GDP. What does this indicate about that sector?","options":{"A":"Output per worker in that sector is low relative to the rest of the economy","B":"It is the most productive sector in the whole of the national economy","C":"The sector must be growing faster than every other sector in that same country","D":"The country has no other economic sectors of any significance at all"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-INVESTMENT-POLICY","stem":"India's 1991 economic reforms cut tariffs and opened many sectors to foreign investment. What was the intended effect?","options":{"A":"To reduce the total volume of goods India traded with other countries","B":"To end all manufacturing activity within India's own domestic economy","C":"To close the Indian economy off entirely from the influence of the world market","D":"To attract foreign capital and expose Indian firms to global competition"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Before 1991 India followed import substitution: high tariffs, licensing, limited foreign ownership. The reforms reversed this to attract FDI, technology and management skills, and to force domestic firms to compete. Growth accelerated sharply; the debate is over who gained, since rural and informal workers benefited far less than urban graduates.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main criticism made of India's post-1991 liberalisation?","options":{"A":"It prevented any foreign company from operating inside India at all","B":"Its gains went disproportionately to urban, educated and skilled workers","C":"It returned the Indian economy to a policy of strict import substitution","D":"It caused India's overall rate of economic growth to fall considerably"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEVELOPMENT-INEQUALITY","stem":"Maharashtra and Bihar are both Indian states, but Maharashtra's income per head is several times higher. Which factor best explains this core-periphery contrast?","options":{"A":"Maharashtra has coastal ports, Mumbai's finance sector and heavy investment","B":"Bihar has a substantially smaller total land area than Maharashtra has","C":"Bihar receives no rainfall at all and therefore cannot support any farming","D":"Every Indian state received exactly the same investment after the 1991 reforms"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Core regions accumulate advantage. Maharashtra's coastal location gave it ports, which attracted industry, which attracted finance and skilled migrants, and each stage made the next easier. Landlocked Bihar, with weaker infrastructure and governance, captured little of the post-1991 investment, so the gap widened as national growth accelerated.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does investment in an emerging country often concentrate in regions that are already wealthy?","options":{"A":"Government policy in every emerging country forbids rural investment","B":"Poorer regions always charge investors much higher rates of local tax","C":"Existing infrastructure, ports and skilled labour attract further investment","D":"Investors are legally required to choose the wealthiest region that is available"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-URBANISATION","stem":"India's urban population is growing rapidly, yet India remains only around one third urban. What does this suggest about future urbanisation?","options":{"A":"India's urban population has already reached its maximum possible size","B":"Large-scale urban growth is still ahead, with major pressure on services","C":"Urbanisation in India has effectively finished and will now slow to zero","D":"India will become less urban over the coming decades as cities shrink"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"A low urban share plus rapid growth means the transition is early, not complete. Hundreds of millions more people are expected to move to Indian cities, which turns questions about housing, water, sanitation and transport from current problems into far larger future ones. That is the basis of most evaluation questions on this topic.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a country that is only one third urban but urbanising fast face particular planning pressure?","options":{"A":"Because urbanisation stops automatically once it passes one third of people","B":"Because its cities will begin to lose population within a few years","C":"Because a low urban share means there is no rural population remaining","D":"Because most of the urban growth is still to come and must be housed"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-AIR-POLLUTION","stem":"Delhi's air quality worsens sharply each winter. Alongside vehicle and industrial emissions, what seasonal factor makes it worse?","options":{"A":"Monsoon rainfall in winter traps pollution close to the ground surface","B":"Winter winds are unusually strong and drive pollution back into the city","C":"Crop residue burning in nearby states, combined with cool, still winter air","D":"Delhi's factories all operate exclusively during the winter months each year"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Two things coincide. Farmers in Punjab and Haryana burn stubble to clear fields quickly between harvests, adding a large smoke load. At the same time cool, stable winter air forms a temperature inversion that caps the city, so pollution accumulates instead of dispersing. Neither cause alone produces the worst episodes.","reforge":{"stem":"How does a temperature inversion worsen urban air pollution?","options":{"A":"A layer of warmer air above traps cooler polluted air near the ground","B":"It removes all sources of emission from within the affected urban area","C":"It increases the wind speed and so drives pollutants into the city centre","D":"It causes heavy rainfall that washes pollutants back down onto the streets"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-WATER-WASTE","stem":"Groundwater levels in Punjab have fallen steeply since the Green Revolution. What is the main cause?","options":{"A":"Rainfall across Punjab has fallen to almost nothing since around 1970","B":"Farmers in Punjab have stopped growing any irrigated crops at all in recent decades","C":"Groundwater in Punjab is being exported in bulk to neighbouring countries","D":"Subsidised electricity makes pumping irrigation water effectively free to use"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The Green Revolution raised yields using high-yield rice and wheat that need far more water than the region's rainfall supplies. Because state electricity for pumps is subsidised, the marginal cost of extracting another litre is near zero, so extraction has run well above recharge for decades. It is a policy-driven resource problem, not a rainfall one.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can subsidising the electricity used by irrigation pumps lead to groundwater depletion?","options":{"A":"Subsidies reduce the total area of farmland that can be irrigated at all","B":"When extraction is nearly free, water is used well beyond the recharge rate","C":"Subsidies force farmers to switch to crops that need much less water","D":"Electricity subsidies increase the rate at which aquifers naturally recharge"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-POPULATION-MOBILITY","stem":"Millions of Indians work seasonally in cities and return to their villages for the harvest. What is this pattern called?","options":{"A":"Circular migration, where workers move repeatedly between village and city","B":"Counter-urbanisation of long-standing residents out of the very largest cities","C":"Permanent international migration between two different sovereign states","D":"Forced displacement caused by a sudden natural hazard event or conflict"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Circular migration is repeated and temporary. It lets a household spread risk across two economies: urban wages during the slack farming season, family labour at harvest. It also means official urban population figures understate how many people actually depend on the city, which complicates planning for housing and services.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does circular migration make urban population figures harder to use for planning?","options":{"A":"Because circular migrants are counted twice in every national census","B":"Because circular migration only involves people moving between countries","C":"Many people using city services are not permanently resident or recorded","D":"Because the total national population changes each time someone migrates"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-SKILLS-DEVELOPMENT","stem":"Bengaluru's IT sector grew rapidly from the 1990s. Which combination of factors best explains its location there?","options":{"A":"Its coastal port location plus large reserves of coal nearby for power","B":"English-speaking graduates, engineering universities and a time-zone advantage","C":"Its extremely low population density and the complete absence of any local industry","D":"Its position on a major plate boundary and abundant geothermal energy"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"IT services need people and connectivity rather than raw materials. Bengaluru had established engineering institutions, a large English-speaking graduate pool, a pleasant upland climate and government research investment; the time difference with the USA allowed overnight working. Agglomeration then took over as firms clustered near each other.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do technology firms tend to cluster in the same city rather than spreading evenly?","options":{"A":"Clustering reduces the number of graduates each individual firm must employ","B":"Governments legally require all technology firms to share one location","C":"Technology firms need to be as far from their competitors as possible","D":"Clustering gives access to a shared skilled labour pool and suppliers"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-DEMOGRAPHIC-CHANGE","stem":"India's fertility rate has fallen to around replacement level, yet its population is still growing. Why?","options":{"A":"India's death rate has risen sharply over the last two decades of records","B":"Fertility rates have no relationship with the size of a country's population","C":"Population momentum: a large young cohort is still reaching childbearing age","D":"Immigration into India from other countries now exceeds emigration from it"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Momentum is a lag effect. Even at two children per woman, a population with an unusually large cohort of young adults produces far more births than deaths for several decades, because there are simply many more potential parents than there are elderly people. Growth slows long after fertility falls.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a population continue growing for decades after fertility falls to replacement level?","options":{"A":"A large young cohort means many more potential parents than elderly people","B":"Because the death rate always falls at exactly the same time as fertility","C":"Because replacement-level fertility means an average of four children","D":"Because population size is entirely determined by rates of international migration"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-GLOBAL-INFLUENCE","stem":"India is a founding member of the Non-Aligned Movement and now a member of the G20 and BRICS. What does this pattern show?","options":{"A":"India has withdrawn from every international organisation it once joined","B":"India has no economic relationships at all with any country outside of South Asia","C":"India's foreign policy is decided entirely by the World Trade Organisation","D":"India pursues influence through many groupings rather than one single alliance"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"India's geopolitical strategy is deliberately plural: non-alignment during the Cold War, and now simultaneous membership of BRICS, the G20 and the Quad. That lets it bargain with several blocs at once rather than depending on any of them, and it is the basis for describing India as an emerging power rather than a client state.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence best supports the claim that India's global influence is growing?","options":{"A":"India's total land area has increased steadily over the past two decades","B":"Its membership of the G20 and rising share of world trade and investment","C":"India has reduced the number of countries it exports manufactured goods to","D":"India's population has fallen sharply relative to that of its neighbours"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-INVESTMENT-IMPACTS","stem":"A large dam project in India generates power and irrigation but displaces tens of thousands of villagers. How should this best be evaluated?","options":{"A":"By weighing national benefits against local losses and who bears each","B":"Only by the total quantity of electricity the completed scheme generates","C":"Only by the financial cost of building the dam and its associated reservoir","D":"By the number of people employed on the site during its construction"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Megaprojects redistribute. The electricity and irrigation are national gains; the flooded land, lost livelihoods and often inadequate compensation fall on a specific local, frequently indigenous, population. A strong evaluation names both, identifies who gains and who loses, and reaches a judgement rather than listing effects.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are large dam schemes often described as having uneven costs and benefits?","options":{"A":"Because dams provide no measurable benefit to anyone in the country","B":"Because the costs and the benefits both fall on exactly the same small group","C":"Benefits are spread nationally while the costs fall on displaced communities","D":"Because dams are always built in regions where nobody currently lives"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"},{"id":"GCSE-IND-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-IND","tag":"MC-GG-IND-SCALE-MEASURES","stem":"A choropleth map shows Indian states shaded by literacy rate. What is its main limitation?","options":{"A":"Choropleth maps are unable to display any statistical data whatsoever","B":"It shows one value per state and hides all variation within each state","C":"It can only ever be used to display physical rather than human geography","D":"It requires that every state on the map is shaded in exactly the same colour"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"A choropleth assigns one shade per area unit, so it implies uniformity that does not exist: Maharashtra's single figure averages Mumbai with its poorest rural districts. It is excellent for showing broad regional patterns and misleading if used to make claims about any individual place inside a shaded unit.","reforge":{"stem":"Which technique would best show the size of migration flows between Indian states?","options":{"A":"A choropleth map shading each state by its total resident population","B":"A pie chart showing each state's percentage share of national land area","C":"A scatter graph plotting each state's literacy rate against its rainfall","D":"A proportional flow-line map with arrow widths scaled to the flow size"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ind"}]},"GCSE-GEO-URB":{"label":"Challenges of an Urbanising World","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-URB-01","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Mumbai has grown from around 3 million people in 1950 to over 20 million in its metropolitan area today, making it a megacity. A student says this growth is purely from births in the city itself. What else is driving it?","options":{"A":"Rural-urban migration towards Mumbai's jobs","B":"International migration from other countries is the only cause","C":"Nothing else — natural increase alone explains all megacity growth","D":"Mumbai's growth is caused entirely by counter-urbanisation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-GROWTH","scaffold":"Megacity growth (a city with over 10 million people) is driven by both natural increase (births minus deaths) AND rural-urban migration. In Mumbai, and most rapidly urbanising cities in lower and middle income countries, migration is often the dominant factor — people are pulled by the promise of jobs, education and services, and pushed from rural areas by poverty, drought, or lack of opportunity.","reforge":{"stem":"A rapidly growing city in a lower income country sees its population double in 15 years, with most of the growth made up of young adults arriving from rural provinces rather than being born in the city. What best explains this?","options":{"A":"Counter-urbanisation","B":"International migration only","C":"Rural-urban migration","D":"Natural increase from a falling death rate alone"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-02","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Migrants moving to Mumbai are often 'pushed' from rural areas by poverty and drought, and 'pulled' by the promise of jobs and services. A student says push and pull factors always come true for migrants. Why might this be wrong for many arriving in Mumbai?","options":{"A":"Every migrant to Mumbai finds a well-paid formal job immediately","B":"Many find neither formal work nor affordable housing","C":"Mumbai has no housing shortage, so all migrants are easily housed","D":"Push and pull factors don't apply to internal (rural-urban) migration"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MIGRATION-REALITY","scaffold":"Push factors (rural poverty, drought, lack of land/jobs) and pull factors (perceived jobs, education, healthcare) explain why people move, but the reality often falls short: Mumbai's formal job market and housing supply can't absorb the scale of migration, so many migrants find only informal work and end up in slums like Dharavi rather than the opportunities they expected.","reforge":{"stem":"A migrant moves to a megacity expecting factory work and a proper house, but instead finds only casual, informal work and has to build a shelter from scrap materials in an unplanned settlement. What does this show about push-pull migration theory?","options":{"A":"Informal settlements only exist because of natural disasters","B":"Push and pull factors guarantee a better outcome for every migrant","C":"This migrant must have moved for climate reasons only","D":"The pull factors rarely match the reality on arrival"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-03","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Dharavi, one of Asia's largest slums, sits in the middle of Mumbai and is home to around 1 million people in roughly 2.1 km². A student calls it 'just an area of poverty with nothing going on.' Why is this an oversimplification?","options":{"A":"Dharavi is a wealthy, formally planned district of Mumbai","B":"Dharavi has no economic activity of any kind","C":"A thriving informal economy alongside poor sanitation","D":"Everyone in Dharavi is unemployed"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URB-INFORMAL-ECONOMY","scaffold":"Dharavi is often stereotyped as pure deprivation, but it's also a major centre of small-scale, informal industry (recycling, leather, pottery, garments) that contributes hugely to Mumbai's economy — while still facing real challenges: extreme population density, poor sanitation, limited access to clean water, and insecure land tenure. Both things are true at once.","reforge":{"stem":"A visitor to Dharavi is surprised to find thousands of small workshops recycling plastic and producing leather goods for export, generating significant income, despite the area's poor infrastructure and sanitation. What does this suggest about informal settlements?","options":{"A":"They combine deprivation with real economic activity","B":"Poor infrastructure always means no economic activity exists","C":"Informal settlements never have any economic value","D":"Informal settlements are identical to formally planned industrial zones"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-04","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Rapid urban growth in Mumbai has caused challenges including traffic congestion, air and water pollution, and a severe housing shortage. A student says these problems only affect informal settlements like Dharavi. Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Traffic congestion in Mumbai only occurs inside Dharavi","B":"Air pollution in Mumbai comes only from informal settlements","C":"Wealthy areas of Mumbai are completely unaffected by any city-wide infrastructure problems","D":"Growth has outpaced infrastructure across the whole city"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URB-CITYWIDE-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Rapid, often unplanned urban growth strains city-wide systems, not just informal areas: Mumbai's roads, public transport, water supply, and waste management all face pressure from a population that has grown far faster than formal infrastructure could keep pace with, so congestion and pollution affect richer and poorer districts alike, even if the severity of housing/sanitation problems is worse in slums.","reforge":{"stem":"A rapidly growing city's commute times triple over two decades, and its air quality index regularly reaches 'hazardous' levels city-wide, not just in its poorest districts. What does this suggest about the nature of these urban challenges?","options":{"A":"Only slum residents ever experience traffic congestion","B":"City-wide infrastructure cannot keep pace with growth","C":"Rich areas of a city are structurally immune to congestion","D":"Air pollution in cities is always confined to informal settlements"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-05","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"The Dharavi Redevelopment Project proposes demolishing much of the settlement and rehousing residents in high-rise flats, partly funded by selling some of the reclaimed land to private developers. A student says all Dharavi residents support this. Why is this a key controversy?","options":{"A":"Residents fear losing livelihoods and community networks","B":"Every single resident of Dharavi is fully in favour of demolition with no concerns","C":"Redevelopment schemes never affect where people can run a business from","D":"The scheme has no effect on informal businesses operating in Dharavi"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URB-REDEVELOPMENT-LIVELIHOODS","scaffold":"Top-down redevelopment schemes like this one are controversial: while they promise improved housing, critics point out that high-rise flats don't easily accommodate ground-floor workshops that Dharavi's informal economy depends on, some residents may not qualify for rehousing (needing proof of long-term residency), and community/social networks built up over decades can be broken up — so support is far from unanimous.","reforge":{"stem":"A city government plans to demolish an informal settlement and move residents into apartment blocks, funding the project by selling part of the site to developers. Local workshop owners protest that flats have no space for their ground-floor businesses. What does this illustrate about top-down slum redevelopment schemes?","options":{"A":"Workshop owners in informal settlements never object to redevelopment","B":"Selling land to developers has no bearing on how a scheme is received","C":"They can displace the informal work residents rely on","D":"Redevelopment always benefits every resident equally with no downsides"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-06","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Sites and services schemes provide migrants with a legal plot of land, basic infrastructure (water, electricity, sanitation), and let residents build their own homes gradually. A student says this is identical to the Dharavi Redevelopment Project's approach. What's the key difference?","options":{"A":"There is no meaningful difference between the two approaches","B":"It works with residents' own building, not demolition","C":"Sites and services schemes provide no infrastructure at all","D":"Both schemes demolish existing homes and force residents into high-rise flats"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URB-BOTTOM-UP-SERVICES","scaffold":"Sites and services is a bottom-up strategy: it gives residents secure land tenure and basic services, then lets them build and improve homes themselves over time at low cost — contrasting with top-down schemes like Dharavi's redevelopment plan, which demolish existing settlements and replace them with formally built high-rise housing, often disrupting existing communities and informal economies.","reforge":{"stem":"A city scheme gives informal settlement residents legal ownership of a small plot plus a water connection and basic drainage, then leaves residents to build and upgrade their own housing over several years. What type of urban management strategy is this?","options":{"A":"Counter-urbanisation policy","B":"A top-down demolition and high-rise redevelopment scheme","C":"A green belt policy","D":"A bottom-up sites and services scheme"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-URB","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Mumbai's growth.","template":"Mumbai's growth into a ___ (over 10 million people) is driven by both natural increase and ___-___ migration, as people move from rural areas in search of jobs.","blanks":["megacity","rural","urban"],"bank":["megacity","rural","urban","counter","international","city"],"tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-GROWTH","scaffold":"A megacity has over 10 million people. Mumbai's growth combines natural increase with large-scale rural-urban migration, as people are pushed from the countryside and pulled by the promise of jobs and services in the city.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-URB","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about push and pull factors.","template":"Migrants to Mumbai are ___ from rural areas by poverty and drought, and ___ by the promise of jobs — but many end up in ___ work and informal settlements rather than the opportunities they expected.","blanks":["pushed","pulled","informal"],"bank":["pushed","pulled","informal","formal","counter-urbanised","regenerated"],"tag":"MC-GG-URB-MIGRATION-REALITY","scaffold":"Push factors (rural poverty, drought) and pull factors (perceived jobs and services) drive migration, but the reality often falls short — many migrants find only informal, insecure work rather than the formal jobs they hoped for.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-URB","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Dharavi's economy.","template":"Dharavi has a large ___ economy — including ___, leather goods and textiles — generating an estimated $1 billion a year, despite facing serious challenges like overcrowding and poor ___.","blanks":["informal","recycling","sanitation"],"bank":["informal","recycling","sanitation","formal","tourism","transport"],"tag":"MC-GG-URB-INFORMAL-ECONOMY","scaffold":"Dharavi combines a thriving informal economy (recycling, leather, textiles, pottery) worth an estimated $1 billion a year with real challenges — extreme density and poor sanitation — both true at once.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-URB","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about urban challenges in Mumbai.","template":"Rapid urban growth strains ___-wide infrastructure in Mumbai, causing traffic ___ and pollution that affect wealthier formal areas as well as ___ settlements.","blanks":["city","congestion","informal"],"bank":["city","congestion","informal","rural","regeneration","sustainable"],"tag":"MC-GG-URB-CITYWIDE-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Mumbai's rapid growth has outpaced formal infrastructure city-wide, so congestion and pollution affect richer and poorer districts alike, even though housing and sanitation problems are worse in informal settlements.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-FB-05","spec":"GCSE-URB","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about the Dharavi Redevelopment Project.","template":"The Dharavi Redevelopment Project proposes ___ much of the settlement and rehousing residents in ___-rise flats, but is controversial because ___ workshops don't easily fit into flats.","blanks":["demolishing","high","informal"],"bank":["demolishing","high","informal","building","low","formal"],"tag":"MC-GG-URB-REDEVELOPMENT-LIVELIHOODS","scaffold":"The scheme's top-down demolition-and-rebuild approach is controversial partly because ground-floor informal workshops, central to Dharavi's economy, don't fit easily into high-rise flats — a major source of resident and business opposition.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-FB-06","spec":"GCSE-URB","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about sites and services schemes.","template":"Sites and services is a ___-up approach that gives residents a legal plot and basic ___ (water, electricity, sanitation), letting them build their own homes ___ over time.","blanks":["bottom","infrastructure","gradually"],"bank":["bottom","infrastructure","gradually","top","instantly","tourism"],"tag":"MC-GG-URB-BOTTOM-UP-SERVICES","scaffold":"Sites and services schemes work bottom-up: secure land tenure plus basic infrastructure, with residents building and upgrading housing themselves over time — a contrast to top-down demolition-and-rebuild schemes.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-07","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Mumbai is described as having both formal and informal employment. Which example best illustrates informal employment?","options":{"A":"A street vendor with no contract or regular pay","B":"A government-employed railway engineer with a contract and pension","C":"A university professor employed by the state","D":"A bank manager in a registered office"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URB-INFORMAL-ECONOMY","scaffold":"Informal employment is common in rapidly urbanising cities: work is often small-scale, unregistered, insecure and outside tax or labour protections. It can provide vital income, but workers are vulnerable because rights, safety and pay are not guaranteed.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is Dharavi's informal economy important despite its problems?","options":{"A":"It provides no employment at all","B":"It is run entirely by foreign governments","C":"It creates income but offers no security","D":"It only employs tourists"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-08","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"A student says Mumbai's traffic congestion is only an environmental problem. Why is this incomplete?","options":{"A":"Congestion is beneficial because it always increases income","B":"Air pollution, lost work time and poorer quality of life","C":"Congestion only affects rural areas","D":"Congestion has no link to air pollution"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URB-CITYWIDE-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Urban problems are multi-dimensional. Traffic congestion increases emissions and air pollution, but it also wastes time, raises transport costs, delays deliveries and can reduce quality of life for residents and commuters.","reforge":{"stem":"Which answer best evaluates a new metro line in Mumbai?","options":{"A":"It only matters if it makes the city look modern","B":"It cannot affect employment access","C":"It will solve all urban problems immediately","D":"It can cut congestion, but cost matters"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-09","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Why can rapid urbanisation lead to water and sanitation problems in Mumbai?","options":{"A":"Cities do not need water infrastructure","B":"Sanitation problems only happen when birth rates fall","C":"Growth outpaces piped water, sewers and waste systems","D":"Informal settlements always receive infrastructure before formal housing"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URB-CITYWIDE-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Rapid urbanisation becomes a challenge when infrastructure cannot keep up with population growth. Informal settlements may lack reliable piped water, toilets and drainage, raising health risks and increasing pressure on already stretched city services.","reforge":{"stem":"A settlement grows quickly before sewers and piped water are installed. Which consequence is most likely?","options":{"A":"More waterborne disease and strain on shared taps","B":"Improved public health automatically","C":"Immediate disappearance of informal work","D":"No change because infrastructure is unrelated to population"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-10","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Which criticism of top-down urban redevelopment is strongest in the Dharavi case?","options":{"A":"It gives every resident a bigger home, a better job and a share of profits","B":"It is identical to self-help housing","C":"It never changes land values","D":"It can ignore livelihoods and community networks"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URB-REDEVELOPMENT-LIVELIHOODS","scaffold":"Top-down redevelopment can improve housing and infrastructure, but in Dharavi the controversy is whether high-rise rehousing protects the dense networks of small workshops, street-level businesses and community support that make the settlement function.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might some Dharavi residents oppose high-rise rehousing even if flats have better sanitation?","options":{"A":"They prefer having no legal security","B":"They lose workshop space, customers and neighbours","C":"They are unaffected by any redevelopment","D":"High-rise flats are always cheaper to run than informal homes"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-11","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"What has been a major global trend in urbanisation since 1980?","options":{"A":"Urban share is rising","B":"Every region urbanised equally","C":"Cities have disappeared","D":"Urbanisation has stopped"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-GROWTH","scaffold":"The global urban share has risen since 1980, although rates differ between regions. Many developing and emerging countries are urbanising rapidly, while some developed countries have a slower or more mature urban transition.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can urbanisation rates differ between global regions?","options":{"A":"Urbanisation is controlled only by latitude","B":"Every country has identical migration patterns","C":"Countries urbanise at different stages","D":"Rural-to-urban migration never occurs"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-12","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"What is a megacity?","options":{"A":"A country's tallest buildings","B":"Over 10 million people","C":"Only formal employment","D":"A capital with no slums"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Edexcel defines a megacity as an urban area with a population of over 10 million. Megacities can have very different levels of wealth, functions, infrastructure and quality of life.","reforge":{"stem":"Which option correctly uses the term megacity?","options":{"A":"Every capital city is automatically a megacity","B":"A megacity can only be located in a developed country","C":"A megacity has no rural population nearby","D":"At least 10 million residents"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-13","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"What does urban primacy describe?","options":{"A":"A city's number of parks","B":"Movement to a village","C":"One city dominates","D":"The order of construction"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Urban primacy occurs when one city dominates a country's population, economy or political influence compared with other cities. It can attract investment and services but may increase regional inequality.","reforge":{"stem":"A country has one city that is far larger and more economically powerful than all other cities. This is evidence of:","options":{"A":"Urban primacy","B":"De-industrialisation","C":"Counter-urbanisation","D":"A rural settlement hierarchy"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-14","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Which is an example of informal employment in a rapidly growing city?","options":{"A":"A worker with a permanent contract in a registered bank","B":"A civil servant working in a government office","C":"An employee in a formal factory with legal protections","D":"An unregistered street trader"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URB-INFORMAL-ECONOMY","scaffold":"Informal employment is often unregistered and lacks secure contracts, legal protection or regular benefits. It can provide vital livelihoods when formal jobs are insufficient, but conditions may be insecure and poorly paid.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can informal employment be important in a developing or emerging city?","options":{"A":"It guarantees a pension and paid leave for everyone","B":"Informal work supplies income","C":"It removes the need for urban services","D":"It only exists inside large multinational companies"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-15","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"What is counter-urbanisation?","options":{"A":"Movement from urban areas to rural areas","B":"Movement from rural areas to the city centre","C":"Growth of a city's CBD through new offices","D":"The replacement of formal jobs by informal jobs"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URB-ACCESSIBILITY-LANDUSE","scaffold":"Counter-urbanisation is the movement of people from urban areas to rural areas, often because of high house prices, congestion or a preference for a quieter environment. It differs from urbanisation, which is movement towards urban areas.","reforge":{"stem":"A family leaves a crowded city for a cheaper village within commuting distance. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Rural depopulation caused by drought","B":"Urbanisation","C":"Counter-urbanisation","D":"Urban primacy"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-16","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Why is accessibility an important influence on urban land use?","options":{"A":"Accessibility determines the colour of every building","B":"Accessible land attracts businesses","C":"Accessible land is always the cheapest land","D":"Accessibility prevents all residential development"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URB-ACCESSIBILITY-LANDUSE","scaffold":"Accessible sites near roads, railways or central locations are attractive to businesses and services because workers, customers and deliveries can reach them more easily. High accessibility can also make land expensive.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are offices often found in accessible central locations?","options":{"A":"Central sites are always free from congestion","B":"Office workers do not need transport","C":"Planning rules require every office to be rural","D":"Businesses need workers and transport"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-17","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Which comparison would provide the strongest evidence that quality of life varies within a megacity?","options":{"A":"The city has one average population figure","B":"Every district has the same road network","C":"Wealthy services versus poor housing","D":"The city has more than one type of building"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URB-QUALITY-OF-LIFE","scaffold":"Quality of life can vary within one megacity because income, housing, services, employment and environmental conditions differ between areas. A comparison should use evidence from contrasting neighbourhoods rather than only a city-wide average.","reforge":{"stem":"Which pair of indicators would best compare quality of life between two city neighbourhoods?","options":{"A":"Water access and income","B":"Distance from the Equator and annual rainfall","C":"Building colour and road names","D":"The number of countries in the city"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-18","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Which approach is most likely to improve a megacity settlement sustainably?","options":{"A":"Remove all residents without offering alternative housing","B":"Build a distant high-rise estate without transport links","C":"Ignore informal businesses when planning redevelopment","D":"Upgrade services with residents"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URB-REDEVELOPMENT-LIVELIHOODS","scaffold":"Sustainable strategies should improve quality of life while recognising residents' livelihoods and reducing environmental pressure. In-situ upgrading can provide services and safer housing without automatically removing communities or destroying local economic networks.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can resident participation improve an urban-upgrading project?","options":{"A":"It guarantees that no government funding is needed","B":"Residents shape sustainable upgrades","C":"It prevents any change to housing conditions","D":"It means infrastructure standards can be ignored"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-19","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Why is the urban population growing fastest in many emerging regions?","options":{"A":"Migration and natural increase combine","B":"All developed cities are becoming rural","C":"Urban jobs have disappeared from emerging economies","D":"Population growth occurs only in villages"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-GROWTH","scaffold":"Rural-urban migration and natural increase within youthful city populations can operate together.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a city's population keep growing after migration slows?","options":{"A":"Every resident moves back to the countryside","B":"Urban housing becomes permanently empty","C":"Births may continue to exceed deaths","D":"Natural increase applies only to rural areas"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-20","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Why can capital-city functions reinforce the growth of a dominant city?","options":{"A":"They distribute institutions evenly among regions","B":"They concentrate decisions and investment","C":"They reduce demand for high-level services","D":"They prevent national and international migration"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Government, finance and corporate headquarters attract skilled workers, infrastructure and further investment to an already dominant city.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one national cost when investment is concentrated in a dominant city?","options":{"A":"Every smaller city gains equal employment","B":"Regional inequality automatically disappears","C":"Political influence becomes evenly distributed","D":"Other regions may receive less investment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-21","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"How does natural increase contribute to megacity growth?","options":{"A":"International migrants replace every existing resident","B":"Death rates rise above birth rates","C":"Births exceed deaths within the city","D":"The urban boundary contracts each year"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-GROWTH","scaffold":"Natural increase is population growth from births exceeding deaths among the existing urban population.","reforge":{"stem":"Which data are needed to calculate a city's natural increase?","options":{"A":"Birth and death totals","B":"Migration and commuter flows","C":"Road length and traffic speed","D":"Land values and building heights"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-22","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Why can international migration increase a city's global connectivity?","options":{"A":"Migrants remove every overseas link","B":"Migration prevents cultural exchange","C":"Cities become independent of remittances","D":"Stronger transnational networks"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MIGRATION-REALITY","scaffold":"Migrant networks can strengthen flows of finance, knowledge, culture and business between origin and destination places.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of a transnational urban link?","options":{"A":"A local bus using a different route","B":"Overseas remittance flows","C":"A shop changing its opening hours","D":"A household moving within one suburb"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-23","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"What distinguishes informal employment from formal employment?","options":{"A":"It often lacks contracts and legal protection","B":"Informal work always pays higher wages","C":"Formal work never contributes to taxation","D":"Informal workers cannot provide useful services"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URB-INFORMAL-ECONOMY","scaffold":"Informal employment operates outside full regulation and may lack contracts, security, benefits and safe conditions.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can informal work still be an urban opportunity?","options":{"A":"It guarantees pensions for every worker","B":"It removes all risk of exploitation","C":"Income where formal jobs are scarce","D":"It prevents workers developing any skills"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-24","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"What is suburbanisation?","options":{"A":"Movement from villages into the CBD","B":"Population and development shift outward","C":"Loss of all housing from the city edge","D":"Growth caused only by international migration"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URB-ACCESSIBILITY-LANDUSE","scaffold":"Suburbanisation moves residents and development from central areas toward lower-density outer districts.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change most clearly indicates suburbanisation?","options":{"A":"Rural residents move into central apartments","B":"CBD land values rise after office construction","C":"Factories close beside an inner-city canal","D":"New housing expands around the urban edge"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-25","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"How can deindustrialisation change an inner-city area?","options":{"A":"It increases heavy manufacturing employment","B":"Industrial land automatically becomes farmland","C":"Factory closure, dereliction and job loss","D":"It immediately removes all social inequality"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URB-URBAN-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The decline of manufacturing can leave vacant land, unemployment and population loss before regeneration occurs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which strategy most directly reuses derelict industrial land?","options":{"A":"Remediate and redevelop the brownfield site","B":"Leave contaminated land without treatment","C":"Build further factories on untouched farmland","D":"Move the urban boundary around the site"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-26","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Why are central urban land values often high?","options":{"A":"Central sites have unlimited vacant land","B":"Planning rules never apply in city centres","C":"Accessibility has no effect on demand","D":"Many users compete for accessible sites"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URB-ACCESSIBILITY-LANDUSE","scaffold":"CBD accessibility attracts offices, retail and services, while limited space increases competition and bid rent.","reforge":{"stem":"Which land use can usually afford the highest central rent?","options":{"A":"Extensive low-value grazing land","B":"High-turnover commerce","C":"A large reservoir and floodplain","D":"A remote subsistence farm"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-27","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"How can satellite imagery help identify an industrial land-use zone?","options":{"A":"Large roofs and transport yards are visible","B":"It records every worker's occupation","C":"It proves all factories are currently operating","D":"It measures household income directly"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URB-URBAN-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Industrial zones may show large buildings, yards, warehouses and links to major roads or railways.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a limitation of identifying land use from one satellite image?","options":{"A":"Images cannot show any building shape","B":"Roads are never visible from above","C":"Function needs ground evidence","D":"Satellite data always show future land use"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-28","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Why did Mumbai's coastal situation support its growth?","options":{"A":"The coast prevented all international contact","B":"A port connected trade and investment","C":"Mountains supplied unlimited building land","D":"Its islands made overland travel unnecessary"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URB-URBAN-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Mumbai's west-coast port and national connections supported trade, industry, migration and global-city functions.","reforge":{"stem":"Which feature describes Mumbai's site?","options":{"A":"Its rail connections to inland states","B":"Its trade links with overseas markets","C":"Its role in India's financial system","D":"Low-lying coast and former islands"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-29","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Which function is most characteristic of a megacity CBD?","options":{"A":"Low-density subsistence farming","B":"Permanent storage of untreated waste","C":"Offices, retail and finance","D":"Large-scale extraction of mineral ores"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URB-ACCESSIBILITY-LANDUSE","scaffold":"The CBD concentrates high-value commercial functions where accessibility and customer flows are greatest.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are buildings often taller in a CBD?","options":{"A":"High land values encourage intensive use","B":"Central land has no commercial demand","C":"Tall buildings reduce every transport cost","D":"Planning always requires identical heights"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-30","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Why do insecure property rights reduce quality of life?","options":{"A":"They guarantee investment in every dwelling","B":"Residents can always obtain low-cost mortgages","C":"Governments must provide permanent services","D":"Households risk eviction and underinvestment"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URB-QUALITY-OF-LIFE","scaffold":"Without secure tenure, households may be displaced and are less able to invest in homes or gain formal service connections.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy would most directly improve tenure security?","options":{"A":"Ban residents from improving structures","B":"Legal recognition of occupancy","C":"Demolish homes without consulting residents","D":"Remove addresses from every settlement"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-31","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Why can inadequate waste collection create a flood hazard?","options":{"A":"Blocked drains increase surface flooding","B":"Waste increases the capacity of every drain","C":"Uncollected waste prevents intense rainfall","D":"Floodwater cannot carry contaminants"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URB-CITYWIDE-CHALLENGES","scaffold":"Waste can block drainage channels, while contaminated floodwater spreads disease through dense settlements.","reforge":{"stem":"Which linked strategy addresses both waste and health?","options":{"A":"Close all community collection points","B":"Store refuse permanently in drainage channels","C":"Regular collection with safe disposal","D":"Remove access to clean water supplies"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-32","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"What is one advantage of a city-wide mass-transit strategy?","options":{"A":"It benefits only households owning two cars","B":"It can reduce congestion for many users","C":"It requires no public investment or planning","D":"It eliminates every journey immediately"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URB-QUALITY-OF-LIFE","scaffold":"High-capacity public transport can improve access and reduce road congestion, though affordability and coverage affect who benefits.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one limitation of a new metro line?","options":{"A":"Stations make employment less accessible","B":"It cannot carry more than one passenger","C":"Rail transport always raises road traffic","D":"High costs and possible displacement"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-33","spec":"GCSE-URB","stem":"Why can a community-managed toilet scheme improve sustainability?","options":{"A":"It relies entirely on distant decision-makers","B":"Community schemes cannot improve health","C":"Local participation supports maintenance","D":"It removes the need for clean water"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URB-BOTTOM-UP-SERVICES","scaffold":"Local involvement can align facilities with needs, build ownership and create systems for maintenance and user fees.","reforge":{"stem":"Which evidence best shows a sanitation project is sustainable?","options":{"A":"Safe facilities remain maintained and used","B":"Residents were not consulted before construction","C":"Facilities close when external staff leave","D":"Waste is discharged into nearby watercourses"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-GROWTH","stem":"Lagos and Tokyo are both megacities, but Lagos is growing at over 3% a year while Tokyo's population is now falling slightly. What best explains this contrast?","options":{"A":"Megacities in richer countries always grow faster than those in poorer ones","B":"Tokyo stopped being counted as an urban area by the UN during the 2010s","C":"Lagos has a lower birth rate than Tokyo in almost all of its districts","D":"Lagos is early in its urban transition; Tokyo's is already mature"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Urbanisation follows a curve. Emerging and developing countries such as Nigeria sit on the steep part: high natural increase plus heavy rural-urban migration. Developed countries such as Japan urbanised decades ago, so their urban share is already high and growth flattens or reverses, driven by ageing and low birth rates rather than migration.","reforge":{"stem":"A country is already 92% urban and its cities are growing by under 0.2% a year. What stage of the urbanisation curve is it on?","options":{"A":"The steep, rapid-growth stage typical of an emerging economy","B":"A mature, late stage with most people already living in towns and cities","C":"The earliest stage, before any significant urban growth has begun","D":"It has left the curve because counter-urbanisation has stopped completely"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MIGRATION-REALITY","stem":"A student writes that rural-urban migrants to Mumbai are 'forced' to move by push factors alone. Why does this misread the decision most migrants make?","options":{"A":"Migrants weigh rural pressure against a perceived urban opportunity","B":"Push factors are the only influence on any migration decision made","C":"Migrants hold complete information and know exactly what awaits them","D":"Rural poverty has no bearing at all on whether a household decides to move"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Push and pull operate together. A household in rural Maharashtra facing failed monsoon rains is under real pressure, but it still selects a destination, often following relatives already in the city. Calling the move 'forced' erases that decision-making, and it hides why migration flows target particular cities rather than spreading evenly.","reforge":{"stem":"Migrants from one rural district overwhelmingly move to the same distant city, where relatives already live, rather than to the nearest large town. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Migration is essentially random and cannot be explained by geographers","B":"Push factors can operate without any destination being chosen at all","C":"Migrants use social networks and information when choosing where to go","D":"Distance is the only factor that determines where any given migrant goes"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-INFORMAL-ECONOMY","stem":"Dharavi's recycling workshops process a large share of Mumbai's plastic waste. A student argues this proves the informal sector needs no regulation. What is the strongest counter-argument?","options":{"A":"The informal sector produces nothing of value to the wider city economy","B":"Useful work can still be unsafe, unprotected and without any legal contract","C":"Regulating recycling would raise the amount of waste Mumbai sends to landfill","D":"Recycling in Mumbai is already fully regulated and inspected across the city"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Two things are true at once. The informal sector supplies real services the formal city depends on, and it does so with no legal protection, no sick pay and frequent exposure to toxic materials. Good answers hold both: the value of the work is an argument for improving conditions, not for leaving them alone.","reforge":{"stem":"An informal waste-picking network saves a city millions in disposal costs, but its workers have no protective equipment and no legal status. What does this best illustrate?","options":{"A":"Informal work is economically useless to the city taken as a whole","B":"Legal protection and economic usefulness are entirely unrelated questions","C":"Cities with large informal sectors send more waste to landfill than others","D":"Valuable work can still leave the people doing it wholly unprotected"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-CITYWIDE-CHALLENGES","stem":"Mumbai floods severely during the monsoon. Rainfall totals have not changed dramatically, but flood damage has risen sharply since 1990. What best explains the increase?","options":{"A":"Monsoon rainfall across western India has roughly doubled since 1990","B":"New drainage built across the city has increased the overall flood risk","C":"Building over mangroves and floodplain has replaced absorbent surfaces","D":"Flooding is caused only by rainfall, so damage must track rainfall exactly"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Flood risk is a function of hazard and exposure. Mumbai's Mithi River floodplain and coastal mangroves were natural storage; land reclamation and unplanned building have sealed those surfaces, so the same rain now produces faster, higher surface run-off, and it lands on far more people and property than it did in 1990.","reforge":{"stem":"Two storms of identical rainfall hit the same city 30 years apart, and the second causes far more damage. What is the most likely reason?","options":{"A":"Urban growth has increased impermeable surfaces and people exposed","B":"The second storm must have carried more rain than the official records show","C":"Rivers gain the ability to absorb more water as a city grows around them","D":"Flood damage is unrelated to the way land surfaces have been changed"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-REDEVELOPMENT-LIVELIHOODS","stem":"A top-down scheme rehouses slum residents in high-rise flats 20 km from the city centre. Within two years many have sold up and returned to informal housing nearby. Why?","options":{"A":"Informal settlements always provide larger homes than any formal flat does","B":"Former slum residents cannot legally occupy newly built high-rise flats","C":"The residents actively preferred to have no piped water or electricity","D":"The move cut them off from the central jobs their income depended on"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Housing quality is only one variable. Livelihoods in the informal economy depend on proximity: a street trader, a domestic worker or a workshop owner must reach customers daily and cheaply. Relocation that improves the building but destroys the commute can leave a household poorer overall, which is why in-situ upgrading is often preferred.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do planners increasingly favour upgrading an informal settlement where it stands rather than relocating its residents elsewhere?","options":{"A":"Because relocation housing is always built worse than self-built housing","B":"It keeps the location, social networks and livelihoods people depend on","C":"Because upgrading in place requires no government funding of any kind","D":"Because relocating residents of informal settlements is illegal in most states"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-BOTTOM-UP-SERVICES","stem":"A charity works with residents of an informal settlement to install shared toilet blocks that the community then manages and maintains itself. What makes this a bottom-up strategy?","options":{"A":"Local people identify the need and control the scheme; the charity supports","B":"It is funded entirely from the national government's infrastructure budget","C":"It uses the most technically advanced sanitation system currently available","D":"It replaces the settlement with new housing built to a single national standard"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Bottom-up refers to the direction of control, not the size of the budget or the technology used. Residents define the priority, hold the maintenance responsibility and shape the design. That tends to produce facilities people actually use and repair, which is where many large top-down sanitation projects have failed.","reforge":{"stem":"Which feature would most clearly mark an urban improvement project as top-down rather than bottom-up?","options":{"A":"Residents elect a committee that decides where the facilities are sited","B":"The scheme uses locally sourced materials and local labour all the way through","C":"A national agency designs and imposes it with no local consultation","D":"A charity trains residents to maintain the facilities they asked for themselves"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-STRUCTURE","stem":"India has several very large cities, while Peru's capital Lima holds roughly a third of the national population. Which term describes Peru's pattern?","options":{"A":"Counter-urbanisation throughout the national settlement system","B":"Urban primacy, where one city dominates the urban hierarchy","C":"Suburbanisation around the outer edge of every city in the country","D":"Deindustrialisation across the country's older manufacturing regions"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Urban primacy is measured against the rest of the hierarchy, not by absolute size. Lima is not among the world's biggest cities, but it dwarfs Peru's second city, so it is strongly primate. Mumbai is far larger in absolute terms, yet India also has Delhi, Kolkata and Bengaluru, so India's hierarchy is much less primate.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a city be strongly primate without being one of the largest cities in the world?","options":{"A":"Primacy is decided by a city's land area rather than by its population","B":"Primacy applies only to cities with more than 10 million residents","C":"Primacy describes people moving out of a city into rural areas nearby","D":"Primacy measures dominance over a country's other cities, not raw size"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-ACCESSIBILITY-LANDUSE","stem":"Land values in many cities fall with distance from the centre, but rise again sharply at motorway junctions on the edge. What explains the second peak?","options":{"A":"Land right at the urban edge is always cheaper than land anywhere else in the city","B":"Edge-of-city land has no transport links, so demand for it collapses","C":"Junctions are accessible for lorries, so retail and distribution bid land up","D":"Building heights are legally restricted only within the central area"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Accessibility, not distance, drives land value. The classic bid-rent curve assumes the centre is the only accessible point. Once motorways exist, edge junctions become accessible in a different way: cheap, large sites plus lorry access is exactly what a distribution warehouse or retail park needs, so demand and price spike locally.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do large distribution warehouses locate at motorway junctions rather than in the city centre?","options":{"A":"They need large cheap sites with fast road access, unlike the centre","B":"Central sites have no customers or workers within any reasonable reach","C":"Planning law prevents all commercial use of centrally located urban land","D":"Warehouses gain no operational advantage from being near a transport route"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-QUALITY-OF-LIFE","stem":"A megacity's average GDP per head is high, yet a third of its residents lack piped water. What does this show about using city-wide averages?","options":{"A":"Access to water cannot be measured at district level by geographers","B":"GDP per head is always a reliable measure of quality of life in a city","C":"A high average figure proves that no district in the city is deprived","D":"An average can hide extreme inequality between districts of one city"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Averages compress. A city with a wealthy financial district and extensive informal settlements can post a respectable mean while a large minority lack basic services. This is why fieldwork, and the teacher heatmap, compare contrasting neighbourhoods rather than quoting one city-wide figure.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best reveal inequality within a single city?","options":{"A":"The city's total population as recorded at the most recent census","B":"Water access and income compared across two contrasting districts","C":"The city's mean GDP per head measured over the last ten years","D":"The number of separate countries the city currently trades with"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-URBAN-STRUCTURE","stem":"Former dockland warehouses in a UK city are converted into expensive flats and the original low-income residents are gradually priced out. What is this process called?","options":{"A":"Gentrification, where reinvestment raises values and displaces residents","B":"Counter-urbanisation moving people from the inner city out into surrounding villages","C":"Suburbanisation of employment towards the outer urban fringe","D":"Urban primacy operating within the national settlement hierarchy"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Gentrification is a change in who lives somewhere, driven by reinvestment. It improves the building stock and the local tax base while displacing the people the improvement was not aimed at. Judging it means weighing regeneration against displacement, which is exactly what an 8-mark evaluation rewards.","reforge":{"stem":"A run-down inner-city area gains cafes, renovated housing and rising rents, and its long-standing residents move away. What is the main geographical criticism of this change?","options":{"A":"It always lowers the physical quality of the local housing stock","B":"It causes the total population of the whole city to fall immediately","C":"Benefits go to incomers while the original community is displaced","D":"It prevents any further investment from reaching the inner city at all"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-CITYWIDE-CHALLENGES","stem":"A city builds a new six-lane ring road to cut congestion. Within five years traffic levels and journey times are back to what they were. What does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Road building has no measurable effect on how people choose to travel","B":"Extra road capacity attracts extra journeys until it fills again","C":"Urban congestion is caused entirely by growth in the resident population","D":"The new road must have been built to a lower standard than was specified"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Extra capacity lowers the cost of driving, so more people drive, journeys lengthen and some switch away from public transport. The new capacity fills. This is why sustainable transport strategies pair road schemes with public transport, congestion charging or cycle provision rather than relying on tarmac alone.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do many cities now combine road schemes with public transport investment and congestion charging?","options":{"A":"Because congestion charging is required of them by international treaty","B":"Because the number of cars in use is unaffected by public transport quality","C":"Because road building costs more than every other available option","D":"Road capacity alone draws more traffic until congestion returns"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"},{"id":"GCSE-URB-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-URB","tag":"MC-GG-URB-MEGACITY-GROWTH","stem":"World urban population passed 50% in around 2007. A student concludes that every country crossed that threshold at the same time. Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"The 50% figure covers only countries in Europe and North America","B":"No individual country has ever been more than half urban at any point","C":"It is a global average: the UK passed 50% in the 1850s, Niger has not yet","D":"Urban population cannot be measured at a national level with any real accuracy at all"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"A global milestone is an aggregate of very different national curves. Britain urbanised with industrialisation in the nineteenth century; much of sub-Saharan Africa is urbanising now, and far faster. Quoting the 2007 figure is fine, but the marks come from recognising the spread hidden inside it.","reforge":{"stem":"Two countries are both 60% urban today, but one reached that level in 1900 and the other in 2015. What does this difference suggest?","options":{"A":"Countries urbanise at different times and speeds as they develop","B":"The two countries must have near-identical economies and populations","C":"Urban share can only ever increase at one single fixed global rate","D":"One of these two figures has certainly been recorded incorrectly"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urb"}]},"GCSE-GEO-UKLAND":{"label":"UK's Evolving Physical Landscape","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-01","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"On the Holderness Coast, waves attack a section of soft boulder clay cliff by compressing air into cracks until the rock shatters. What process is this?","options":{"A":"Hydraulic action","B":"Abrasion by sand and pebbles","C":"Solution of soluble rock","D":"Attrition between rocks"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-EROSION-PROCESS","scaffold":"Hydraulic action is the sheer force of water and compressed air breaking rock apart — no rock-on-rock contact needed, which is why it's so effective on Holderness's soft, unconsolidated boulder clay. Abrasion needs the waves to be carrying material to throw at the cliff; attrition happens between loose rock fragments, not to the cliff face itself; solution only affects soluble rock like limestone or chalk, not boulder clay.","reforge":{"stem":"Further along the Holderness coast, waves carrying sand and shingle scrape against the cliff face, wearing it away like sandpaper. What process is this?","options":{"A":"Solution — rock dissolving","B":"Attrition — pebbles wearing each other down","C":"Abrasion — carried material grinds the cliff","D":"Hydraulic action — compressed air shattering rock"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-02","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"A headland and bay coastline forms where alternating bands of resistant rock (like chalk) and less resistant rock (like clay) meet the sea. What causes the headlands to form?","options":{"A":"Headlands are always made of the same rock type as the bays next to them","B":"Softer rock erodes faster, leaving a headland","C":"Longshore drift deposits sand only in front of resistant rock","D":"The resistant rock is eroded faster, leaving low-lying bays behind"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Differential erosion: softer rock (clay) erodes quickly, carving out a bay, while harder rock (chalk or limestone) resists erosion and juts out as a headland. It's the rate of erosion, not the rock forming faster, that creates the shape — and headlands and bays are made of different rock types by definition.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do headlands, once formed, typically erode more slowly than the bays beside them?","options":{"A":"Bays receive no wave energy at all","B":"Headlands are protected from waves entirely","C":"Headlands and bays erode at exactly the same rate","D":"They are made of more resistant rock"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-03","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"A student describes a headland's erosional sequence as: 'crack, then arch, then stack, then stump.' What has been missed out?","options":{"A":"A spit, which forms between the crack and the arch","B":"A stack always forms before the cave","C":"A cave, widened from a crack in the rock","D":"Nothing — the sequence is complete as stated"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-STACK-SEQUENCE","scaffold":"The correct sequence is crack → cave → arch → stack → stump. Waves widen a weakness into a cave; continued erosion breaks through the headland to form an arch; the arch collapses, isolating a stack; the stack erodes down to a stump. A spit is a depositional feature, not part of this erosional sequence.","reforge":{"stem":"An arch's roof eventually collapses because it can no longer support its own weight, isolating a tall column of rock from the headland. What landform has just been created?","options":{"A":"A stack","B":"A stump","C":"A spit","D":"A cave"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-04","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Waves approach a beach at an angle, but the backwash always returns straight down the beach under gravity. What landform does this longshore drift process build over time at a river mouth or bend in the coastline?","options":{"A":"A stack","B":"A bar","C":"A headland","D":"A spit"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-LONGSHORE","scaffold":"Longshore drift moves material along the coast in a zigzag (swash at an angle, backwash straight down the beach). Where the coastline changes direction — like a river mouth — the material keeps being deposited in the same direction, building out a spit, sometimes with a curved/hooked end where wave refraction bends it. Headlands and stacks are erosional features; a wave-cut platform is the flat rock left behind at the base of an eroding cliff.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a spit often develop a hooked or curved end?","options":{"A":"River currents always create a 90-degree bend","B":"A change in wind direction curves the end","C":"Spits are always straight and never curve","D":"The hook is caused by erosion, not deposition"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-05","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"At Mappleton on the Holderness Coast, rock groynes and a rock revetment were built to protect the village and the coast road. A student says: 'This solved erosion for the whole Holderness coast.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Beaches further along are starved of sediment","B":"Holderness has no erosion problem elsewhere, so protecting one village was unnecessary","C":"Hard engineering has no effect on longshore drift","D":"Groynes and revetments stop all erosion everywhere on Holderness"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-MANAGE","scaffold":"Groynes trap sediment moving by longshore drift to protect one stretch of coast — but that starves beaches further along, which lose their natural buffer and erode faster. This is exactly why Holderness (one of Europe's fastest-eroding coastlines, ~2m/year on average) still has undefended sections like Cowden losing land rapidly despite Mappleton's defences.","reforge":{"stem":"A village protects its coastline with groynes. A neighbouring, undefended village further down the coast reports erosion rates increasing afterwards. What's the most likely explanation?","options":{"A":"Hard engineering always reduces erosion everywhere, with no local trade-offs","B":"Groynes increase wave energy along the whole coastline","C":"They trapped sediment bound for the next beach","D":"The two villages' erosion rates are unrelated to each other"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-06","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"A river's long profile runs from source to mouth. A student says: 'Erosion is the dominant process everywhere along a river.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"A river's energy stays exactly the same from source to mouth","B":"Vertical erosion upstream, deposition downstream","C":"Erosion and deposition never both occur on the same river","D":"Deposition dominates everywhere along a river"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-LONG-PROFILE","scaffold":"A river's energy and gradient are highest near the source, favouring vertical erosion (cutting down, forming V-shaped valleys/waterfalls). Further downstream, gradient flattens, energy falls, and the river deposits the sediment it's carrying, building features like floodplains. It's a gradual shift, not an on/off switch, and both processes can occur at once in different parts of the channel.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do meanders (bends) typically develop in a river's middle to lower course rather than close to the source?","options":{"A":"Meanders form randomly with no link to gradient or energy","B":"Meanders only form where a river is at its steepest","C":"Rivers only erode in their upper course and never downstream","D":"Lower gradient lets lateral erosion take over"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-07","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"On the outside bend of a meander, the river is deep and erosion dominates; on the inside bend, the river is shallow and deposition dominates. A student says this is just random. What actually causes it?","options":{"A":"Erosion only ever happens on the inside of a bend","B":"Meanders have no consistent pattern of erosion or deposition","C":"Helicoidal flow erodes the outside bend","D":"The river bed is naturally deeper on the outside bend with no link to current or flow"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-MEANDER","scaffold":"Helicoidal (corkscrew) flow explains the asymmetry: faster current and greater erosive power hits the outside bend (undercutting it into a river cliff), while slower-moving water on the inside bend deposits sediment as a gently sloping slip-off slope. Over time this exaggerates the bend until it can be cut off, forming an oxbow lake.","reforge":{"stem":"Over many years, a meander's neck becomes so narrow that the river cuts straight across it during a flood, leaving the old bend isolated. What landform has just formed?","options":{"A":"An oxbow lake","B":"A braided channel","C":"A waterfall step","D":"A meander scar"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-08","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"In July 2007, Tewkesbury (at the confluence of the Severn and Avon) flooded severely, cutting the town off and flooding thousands of homes. A student says this was caused purely by 'too much rain.' What else made it worse?","options":{"A":"Rainfall was the only factor and location made no difference","B":"The floods were caused entirely by a dam failure upstream","C":"Tewkesbury has no rivers nearby, so flooding must have come from the sea","D":"Saturated ground and a confluence overwhelmed the town"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-FLOOD-RISK","scaffold":"2007 was one of the wettest summers on record — but Tewkesbury's flood risk is compounded by its confluence location, where the Severn and Avon's floodwaters combine, and by antecedent (prior) rainfall leaving the ground saturated with nowhere for new rainfall to infiltrate, forcing more of it into rapid surface runoff.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a settlement located at the confluence of two rivers face a greater flood risk than one on a single river of similar size?","options":{"A":"Two rivers meeting always cancel out each other's flood risk","B":"Both rivers peak at the same point, raising discharge","C":"Confluences always have lower rainfall than other locations","D":"Confluence location has no bearing on flood risk"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-27","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"A stretch of coast has a wide beach at low tide but a narrow beach after a winter storm. Which explanation best fits this change?","options":{"A":"Storm waves can move beach sediment offshore, narrowing the beach temporarily","B":"The beach must have changed from a depositional landform into an erosional landform","C":"Longshore drift stops whenever wave energy increases during a storm","D":"Beach width is controlled only by the geology of the cliff behind it, not by wave conditions"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-LONGSHORE","scaffold":"High-energy storm waves have greater erosive power and can move sand and shingle offshore into bars or along the coast. Some sediment may return during calmer conditions, so a narrow post-storm beach does not necessarily mean permanent cliff retreat.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the most likely effect of calmer, constructive waves after a storm has removed beach material?","options":{"A":"They increase cliff undercutting by concentrating all energy at the cliff base","B":"They dissolve all beach sediment before it can be moved","C":"They move sediment up the beach, rebuilding the berm","D":"They stop sediment movement because swash is absent"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-28","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Why can urbanisation increase flood risk during heavy rainfall?","options":{"A":"Urban surfaces always absorb more rainfall than woodland soils","B":"Concrete and tarmac reduce infiltration, sending water rapidly into drains","C":"Buildings lower river discharge by storing all rainfall permanently","D":"Urban areas prevent precipitation from reaching the drainage basin during storms"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-FLOOD-RISK","scaffold":"Impermeable surfaces reduce infiltration and interception. Water reaches drains and the river quickly, shortening lag time and increasing peak discharge. The exact response also depends on rainfall intensity, basin size and drainage design.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change would most likely lengthen a drainage basin's lag time?","options":{"A":"Replacing grass with more tarmac","B":"Straightening every tributary channel","C":"Removing wetlands from the floodplain","D":"Woodland and permeable surfaces"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-29","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"A floodplain is useful for farming but can be risky for new housing. Which planning response best manages this conflict?","options":{"A":"Build all new homes on the lowest part of the floodplain","B":"Remove every riverbank so water can spread into urban areas","C":"Zoning keeps vulnerable buildings off the floodplain","D":"Allow development wherever land prices are lowest"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-FLOODPLAIN-USE","scaffold":"Floodplain zoning uses flood-risk maps to guide land use. Highly vulnerable development is restricted in areas with frequent or severe flooding, while parks, grazing or playing fields may be more appropriate. It reduces exposure rather than pretending the hazard can be eliminated.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a playing field be considered more suitable than a hospital on a high-risk floodplain?","options":{"A":"A playing field can tolerate occasional flooding","B":"Hospitals never need road access during floods","C":"Playing fields prevent all floodwater from entering the river","D":"Hospitals are always built below sea level"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-30","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Which combination best explains why a river may have greater discharge downstream?","options":{"A":"The channel becomes narrower and rainfall disappears","B":"Discharge rises only because bedload becomes smaller","C":"The river loses all groundwater as it approaches the mouth","D":"Tributaries add water and the basin collects runoff"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-LONG-PROFILE","scaffold":"Downstream, tributaries join the main channel and the river drains an increasingly large catchment, so total discharge generally increases. Local abstraction, evaporation or infiltration can interrupt the pattern, so this is a general trend rather than an unbreakable rule.","reforge":{"stem":"Which observation would be evidence that a downstream discharge trend has been interrupted by human activity?","options":{"A":"A tributary joins the river","B":"A reservoir holds water back","C":"The catchment becomes larger","D":"The river reaches a wider floodplain"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about coastal erosion processes on Holderness.","template":"___ ___ is the sheer force of water (and compressed air) breaking rock apart, while ___ is material carried by waves grinding against the cliff like sandpaper.","blanks":["Hydraulic","action","abrasion"],"bank":["Hydraulic","action","abrasion","attrition","solution","weathering"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKL-EROSION-PROCESS","scaffold":"Hydraulic action is water and compressed air shattering rock with no rock-on-rock contact needed. Abrasion is different — it needs the waves to be carrying sand/pebbles to throw at the cliff, sandpapering it away.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about headland and bay formation.","template":"Headlands and bays form through ___ erosion: ___ resistant rock like clay erodes quickly to form a bay, while more ___ rock like chalk juts out as a headland.","blanks":["differential","less","resistant"],"bank":["differential","less","resistant","uniform","more","soluble"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Differential erosion means different rock types erode at different rates when exposed to the same wave energy — the softer, less resistant rock (clay) is worn back into a bay while the harder, more resistant rock (chalk) survives as a headland.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the erosional sequence that forms a stack.","template":"The sequence runs: crack → ___ → ___ → stack → ___.","blanks":["cave","arch","stump"],"bank":["cave","arch","stump","spit","platform","bay"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKL-STACK-SEQUENCE","scaffold":"Crack → cave → arch → stack → stump. Hydraulic action and abrasion widen a crack into a cave; continued erosion breaks through to form an arch; the arch's roof collapses, isolating a stack; the stack erodes down to a stump.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about spit formation.","template":"___ ___ moves material along the coast in a zigzag pattern. Where the coastline changes direction, such as at a river mouth, material builds out into a ___.","blanks":["Longshore","drift","spit"],"bank":["Longshore","drift","spit","stack","headland","platform"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKL-LONGSHORE","scaffold":"Longshore drift: swash carries material up the beach at an angle (driven by prevailing wind), backwash drags it straight back down under gravity — the net zigzag movement builds a spit wherever the coastline suddenly changes direction.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-05","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about hard engineering at Mappleton on the Holderness Coast.","template":"Rock groynes at Mappleton trap sediment moved by longshore drift, protecting the village — but this ___ beaches further along the coast of sediment, meaning ___ actually speeds up in those undefended areas.","blanks":["starves","erosion"],"bank":["starves","erosion","supplies","deposition"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-MANAGE","scaffold":"Groynes trap sediment travelling by longshore drift to protect one stretch of coast, but that starves beaches further down the coast of the sediment they'd otherwise receive — those undefended sections, like Cowden, then erode faster than before.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-06","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about a river's long profile.","template":"In the upper course, high energy and steep gradient favour ___ erosion. Further downstream, gradient and energy fall, and ___ becomes dominant as the river drops its sediment.","blanks":["vertical","deposition"],"bank":["vertical","deposition","lateral","erosion"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKL-LONG-PROFILE","scaffold":"Vertical erosion (cutting downwards, forming steep V-shaped valleys and waterfalls) dominates where gradient and energy are highest, near the source. As the river flows towards its mouth, falling energy means it increasingly deposits sediment instead of eroding.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-07","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about meander formation.","template":"___ flow is a corkscrew-like current that erodes the ___ bend of a meander, undercutting it into a river cliff, while depositing sediment as a slip-off slope on the ___ bend.","blanks":["Helicoidal","outside","inside"],"bank":["Helicoidal","outside","inside","vertical","upper","lower"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKL-MEANDER","scaffold":"Helicoidal (corkscrew) flow moves faster on the outside of a bend, eroding it into a steep river cliff, then spirals across the channel to deposit sediment more slowly on the inside bend as a gently sloping slip-off slope — the asymmetry that drives a meander to grow.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-FB-08","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about the July 2007 Tewkesbury floods.","template":"Tewkesbury sits at the ___ of the Severn and Avon, so floodwater from both rivers arrived together. The ground was already ___ from a very wet preceding month, increasing surface ___.","blanks":["confluence","saturated","runoff"],"bank":["confluence","saturated","runoff","source","dry","infiltration"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKL-FLOOD-RISK","scaffold":"Tewkesbury's confluence location meant floodwater from both the Severn and Avon combined at the same point. Ground already saturated from a very wet preceding month couldn't absorb (infiltrate) new rainfall, forcing more of it into rapid surface runoff and worsening the flood.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-09","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"A student mixes up abrasion and attrition. What is the correct distinction?","options":{"A":"Abrasion sandpapers the bed; attrition rounds the rocks","B":"Abrasion only happens in rivers, attrition only happens at the coast","C":"Attrition wears away the cliff face directly; abrasion only affects the rocks being transported","D":"Abrasion and attrition are two names for the same erosional process"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-EROSION-PROCESS","scaffold":"Both are erosion processes but act on different things: abrasion is material scraping against the landform itself (the cliff or riverbed), while attrition is transported rocks scraping against each other, reducing their own size and making them rounder — this is why beach pebbles are smoother than upstream river rocks.","reforge":{"stem":"Sea spray flings loose sand and shingle at a cliff face, gradually wearing it away. Which erosion process is this?","options":{"A":"Attrition — rocks wear each other down as they collide","B":"Hydraulic action — air compressed into cracks breaks the rock apart","C":"Abrasion — loose material sandpapers the cliff surface","D":"Solution — acid in the water dissolves the rock"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-10","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"What is hydraulic action, and where does it occur?","options":{"A":"The dissolving of chemical compounds in a rock by weak acid in rainwater","B":"Air compressed into cracks breaking the rock apart","C":"The rolling of large boulders along a riverbed","D":"The transportation of sediment along a beach by the prevailing wind"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-EROSION-PROCESS","scaffold":"Hydraulic action is purely a mechanical, force-based process — air trapped in cracks is compressed by incoming waves and then releases suddenly, widening the crack. It's distinct from corrasion (material flung at the cliff) and solution (chemical dissolving), which are the other coastal erosion processes.","reforge":{"stem":"During a storm, waves compress air into cracks in a cliff face, and the sudden release widens the crack. Which process is this?","options":{"A":"Attrition between rocks","B":"Longshore drift","C":"Traction along the bed","D":"Hydraulic action"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-11","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"A student can't tell freeze-thaw weathering apart from mechanical erosion like hydraulic action. What is the key difference?","options":{"A":"Freeze-thaw only happens at the coast; hydraulic action only happens on mountains","B":"They are identical processes with different names","C":"Freeze-thaw is weathering in place; hydraulic action erodes","D":"Freeze-thaw requires no water at all"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-SUBAERIAL","scaffold":"Weathering breaks rock down where it is, with no transport involved (freeze-thaw, biological, chemical weathering are all sub-aerial — they happen 'in the open air'). Erosion involves a moving agent (water, wind, ice) wearing away and carrying material off. Freeze-thaw often weakens a cliff first, making it more vulnerable to erosion afterwards.","reforge":{"stem":"A cliff face is weakened over winter by water repeatedly freezing and expanding in its cracks, before waves finally erode the loosened rock in spring. Which two processes does this describe, in order?","options":{"A":"Freeze-thaw weathering first, followed by erosion","B":"Erosion first, followed by freeze-thaw weathering","C":"Only weathering is involved — no erosion takes place","D":"Only erosion is involved — no weathering takes place"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-12","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"A coastline has bands of resistant and less resistant rock running perpendicular (at a right angle) to the shore. What type of coast is this, and what landform does it typically produce?","options":{"A":"A discordant coast, which always prevents any erosion from occurring","B":"A concordant coast, where rock bands run perpendicular to the shore","C":"A concordant coast, which produces a straight, unbroken cliff line with no headlands or bays","D":"A discordant coast of alternating headlands and bays"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Discordant = perpendicular bands = headland-and-bay coastline (e.g. Swanage). Concordant = parallel bands = a smoother, straighter coastline because the same resistant rock band runs continuously along the front, protecting what's behind it (e.g. Lulworth Cove's cove formed where the outer resistant band was breached).","reforge":{"stem":"A coastline has bands of the same resistant rock running parallel to the shore, protecting the land behind it. What type of coast is this, and what does it typically produce?","options":{"A":"A discordant coast, which always produces headlands and bays","B":"A concordant coast, which tends to produce a smoother, straighter coastline","C":"A discordant coast, which produces a smooth, unbroken cliff line","D":"A concordant coast, which always prevents any coastal landforms from forming"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-13","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"What is the difference between hard engineering and soft engineering as approaches to coastal management?","options":{"A":"Hard uses concrete structures; soft works with nature","B":"Hard engineering only protects rivers; soft engineering only protects coasts","C":"They are the same strategy carried out at different times of year","D":"Soft engineering always costs more than hard engineering"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-MANAGE","scaffold":"Hard engineering gives strong, immediate protection but is expensive, unsightly and can increase erosion elsewhere along the coast (starving beaches downdrift of sediment). Soft engineering works with natural sediment processes and is often cheaper long-term but offers less immediate certainty.","reforge":{"stem":"A coastal town chooses beach nourishment and managed retreat instead of a sea wall. What type of approach is this?","options":{"A":"Neither approach counts as coastal management","B":"This combination is not possible under coastal management strategies","C":"Soft engineering — natural, sustainable approaches","D":"Hard engineering — using artificial concrete structures"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-14","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"How does a groyne reduce coastal erosion?","options":{"A":"It only works by dissolving the salt in incoming waves","B":"A wall across the shore that traps drifting sediment","C":"It removes sediment from the beach to lower the risk of flooding","D":"It is a wall built parallel to the shore that redirects wave energy away from the cliff"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-MANAGE","scaffold":"Don't confuse a groyne (perpendicular to the shore, traps sediment moving along the beach) with a sea wall (parallel to the shore, deflects wave energy directly). A wider trapped beach means waves lose energy over a longer distance before reaching the cliff base.","reforge":{"stem":"A sea wall is built parallel to the shore rather than perpendicular like a groyne. How does it protect the coast?","options":{"A":"It traps sediment moved by longshore drift, exactly like a groyne","B":"It has no effect on wave energy","C":"It only works by removing sand from the beach entirely","D":"It deflects wave energy rather than trapping sediment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-15","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"In a river, what is the difference between traction, saltation and suspension as methods of transportation?","options":{"A":"Suspension is the rolling of boulders; traction is the floating of fine sediment","B":"All three terms describe exactly the same process of transportation","C":"Traction rolls, saltation bounces, suspension floats","D":"Traction only occurs in the upper course; saltation and suspension only occur in the lower course"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-RIVER-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"The four transportation processes scale with sediment size and river energy: traction (largest, rolled along the bed), saltation (medium, bounced along the bed), suspension (small, carried within the flow), and solution (dissolved chemicals, carried invisibly in the water).","reforge":{"stem":"Large boulders are too heavy to be carried by the river and instead roll along the riverbed. Which transportation process is this?","options":{"A":"Traction","B":"Solution","C":"Saltation","D":"Suspension"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-16","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"What is a river's floodplain, and why is floodplain zoning used to manage it?","options":{"A":"Floodplain zoning is a hard-engineering technique used exclusively at the coast","B":"A floodplain is another name for a river's drainage basin","C":"A floodplain is a landform found only in the upper course, where the river erodes rather than floods","D":"The valley floor takes overspill; zoning limits building"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-FLOODPLAIN-USE","scaffold":"Floodplains naturally flood — that's their function, spreading excess discharge over a wide area and reducing flood height downstream. Floodplain zoning is a management response: restricting housing and infrastructure on the areas most likely to flood, rather than trying to stop the river flooding at all.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a floodplain naturally flood, and why is this actually useful?","options":{"A":"Floodplains never flood under any circumstances","B":"The valley floor spreads overspill from a full channel","C":"Floodplains exist purely to be built on with housing","D":"Floodplains are found only in the upper course of a river"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-17","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"What does a storm hydrograph show, and what is the 'lag time'?","options":{"A":"It plots discharge over time; lag time is the delay","B":"A storm hydrograph only measures rainfall, not discharge","C":"A storm hydrograph shows a river's long profile from source to mouth","D":"The lag time is the total volume of water in a drainage basin"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-HYDROGRAPH","scaffold":"A short lag time (steep rising limb) means a 'flashy' hydrograph — water reaches the channel quickly, raising flood risk. This happens where infiltration is low (impermeable rock, urbanised or saturated ground) so more rainfall becomes rapid surface runoff instead of soaking in.","reforge":{"stem":"A river has a long lag time and a gently rising limb on its storm hydrograph. What does this suggest about the drainage basin?","options":{"A":"The drainage basin is heavily urbanised with impermeable surfaces everywhere","B":"The basin has just experienced a period of saturation with no infiltration at all","C":"High infiltration means rain soaks in, not runs off","D":"Lag time has no relationship to infiltration or flood risk"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-18","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"A student defines a permeable rock as 'a type of igneous rock.' Why is this definition wrong?","options":{"A":"Permeability only applies to rivers, never to rock","B":"Whether water can pass through the rock, not rock type","C":"Permeable means the same thing as metamorphic","D":"The definition is correct — only igneous rocks can be permeable"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-GEOLOGY","scaffold":"Permeable/impermeable is a property that cuts across the three rock types (igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic). Chalk (sedimentary) is permeable; clay (sedimentary) is often impermeable — so students shouldn't assume rock type alone tells you permeability.","reforge":{"stem":"Chalk is a sedimentary rock that allows water to pass through it easily. What property does this describe?","options":{"A":"Chalk is impermeable","B":"Permeability only applies to igneous rock","C":"Chalk is metamorphic","D":"Chalk is permeable"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-31","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Which landform is most strongly associated with glacial erosion in the UK?","options":{"A":"A constructive-wave beach","B":"A longshore-drift spit","C":"U-shaped valley","D":"A lower-course levee"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-GLACIAL","scaffold":"Glacial erosion widens and deepens a V-shaped river valley, producing a broad U-shaped valley with steep sides. The other options are mainly coastal or fluvial depositional features.","reforge":{"stem":"A broad valley has steep sides and a flat floor. Which process most likely shaped it?","options":{"A":"Glacial erosion","B":"Longshore drift","C":"River-mouth deposition","D":"Constructive waves"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-32","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Why can granite form upland landscapes in the UK?","options":{"A":"It is always deposited by rivers in floodplains","B":"It is a soft sediment deposited by longshore drift","C":"It dissolves faster than most surrounding rocks","D":"It is relatively resistant to erosion"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-GEOLOGY","scaffold":"Granite is an intrusive igneous rock that is generally resistant to erosion. Where surrounding rocks are worn away more quickly, granite can remain as higher relief or upland terrain.","reforge":{"stem":"A granite area stands above nearby weaker rocks. What best explains this relief?","options":{"A":"Granite is made from beach sediment","B":"Granite resists erosion","C":"Granite is always found in lowland basins","D":"Granite is transported downstream by traction"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-33","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"A cliff retreats from 120 m to 90 m in 10 years. What is the mean erosion rate?","options":{"A":"3 m per year","B":"30 m per year","C":"12 m per year","D":"0.3 m per year"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-EROSION-PROCESS","scaffold":"Mean erosion rate is total retreat divided by time: (120 − 90) ÷ 10 = 30 ÷ 10 = 3 metres per year. The calculation uses change in distance, not the final distance alone.","reforge":{"stem":"A cliff retreats from 100 m to 80 m in 5 years. What is the mean rate of retreat?","options":{"A":"20 m per year","B":"5 m per year","C":"4 m per year","D":"0.4 m per year"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-34","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"What does lag time on a storm hydrograph measure?","options":{"A":"The total rainfall in a storm","B":"Rain peak to discharge peak","C":"The length of the river channel","D":"The highest discharge recorded"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-HYDROGRAPH","scaffold":"Lag time is the interval between peak rainfall and peak discharge. A short lag time means the river responds quickly; a longer lag gives more time for warning and can reduce the immediate flood peak.","reforge":{"stem":"On a storm hydrograph, what does a long lag time show?","options":{"A":"Rainfall has stopped before the storm began","B":"The river has no drainage basin","C":"Discharge is always at its maximum","D":"Peak follows rainfall later"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-35","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"How can a groyne reduce erosion on part of a beach?","options":{"A":"It makes the entire coast resistant to weathering","B":"It increases the force of every destructive wave","C":"It traps sediment moving alongshore","D":"It stops all water entering the drainage basin"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-MANAGE","scaffold":"Groynes interrupt longshore drift and build a wider beach on their up-drift side. The extra sediment absorbs wave energy, although the down-drift coastline may receive less sediment.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a wider beach form beside a groyne?","options":{"A":"It traps sediment","B":"The groyne removes all waves","C":"The beach becomes a floodplain","D":"The rock stops all weathering"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-36","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Why is floodplain zoning used?","options":{"A":"To encourage new housing on the highest-risk land","B":"To prevent all farming beside rivers","C":"To make every river channel deeper","D":"To keep high-risk land from new housing"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-FLOODPLAIN-USE","scaffold":"Floodplain zoning limits development in areas likely to flood. It reduces exposure of people and property while allowing some compatible uses, such as recreation or carefully managed agriculture.","reforge":{"stem":"Which planning decision best reduces flood exposure?","options":{"A":"Building more homes on the floodplain","B":"Restricting development in high-risk zones","C":"Removing all wetlands from the river valley","D":"Paving the whole floodplain for car parks"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-37","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"What is a likely feature of strategic realignment on a coast?","options":{"A":"Selected land is allowed to flood and form new habitats","B":"Groynes are placed at regular intervals along the whole coast","C":"A new sea wall protects every metre of shoreline","D":"Beach sediment is removed to expose the cliff base"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-MANAGE","scaffold":"Strategic realignment allows the sea to move inland in selected locations, often creating salt marsh or mudflat that absorbs wave energy. It can be more sustainable but may require land and property to be surrendered.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can strategic realignment reduce long-term flood risk?","options":{"A":"It forces all waves to break offshore","B":"It removes the need to understand coastal processes","C":"New wetlands can absorb wave energy","D":"It makes every coastal community equally protected"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-38","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Which river transport process moves large pebbles by rolling them along the bed?","options":{"A":"Suspension","B":"Traction","C":"Solution","D":"Saltation"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-RIVER-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Traction is the rolling of large particles along the river bed. Saltation involves smaller particles bouncing, suspension carries fine sediment within the water and solution carries dissolved material.","reforge":{"stem":"A heavy cobble rolls along the bed of a river. Which process is this?","options":{"A":"Suspension of silt","B":"Saltation of sand","C":"Solution","D":"Traction"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-39","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Which feature is most likely to be formed by glacial deposition?","options":{"A":"A narrow gorge below a waterfall","B":"A corrie with a steep back wall","C":"A drumlin made of deposited till","D":"A wave-cut notch at a cliff base"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-GLACIAL","scaffold":"Drumlins are streamlined hills of till deposited and shaped beneath moving ice.","reforge":{"stem":"What evidence would suggest a valley was once glaciated?","options":{"A":"U-shaped valley with moraines","B":"A narrow V-shaped valley with spurs","C":"A floodplain crossed by meanders","D":"A beach backed by sand dunes"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-40","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"How is slate classified?","options":{"A":"A sedimentary rock formed from shells","B":"An igneous rock cooled below ground","C":"An unconsolidated deposit left by rivers","D":"Heat-and-pressure altered rock"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-GEOLOGY","scaffold":"Slate forms when shale is altered by heat and pressure and is common in resistant upland landscapes.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are slate landscapes often associated with upland relief?","options":{"A":"Slate dissolves instantly in rainwater","B":"Slate resists erosion","C":"Slate forms only on modern beaches","D":"Slate prevents any slope movement"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-41","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Why can limestone drainage basins have low surface runoff?","options":{"A":"Infiltration through fractures","B":"Limestone is impermeable and saturated","C":"Rainfall evaporates before reaching the rock","D":"Limestone prevents water entering joints"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-GEOLOGY","scaffold":"Carboniferous limestone is permeable through joints and bedding planes, encouraging infiltration and underground drainage.","reforge":{"stem":"Which feature is most likely in a limestone area?","options":{"A":"A clay floodplain with no infiltration","B":"A granite tor formed by block weathering","C":"A stream entering a swallow hole","D":"A volcanic cone above a hotspot"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-42","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"How does freeze-thaw weathering break rock?","options":{"A":"Salt dissolves every mineral in the rock","B":"Water freezes in cracks and expands","C":"Plant roots melt the rock surface","D":"Rainwater deposits new layers of clay"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-SUBAERIAL","scaffold":"Repeated freezing and thawing widens cracks until angular fragments detach.","reforge":{"stem":"Where is freeze-thaw weathering most effective?","options":{"A":"Where temperatures remain above 25°C","B":"Where rock has no cracks or joints","C":"Where rainfall never reaches the ground","D":"Repeated crossing of freezing point"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-43","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Why can prolonged rainfall trigger mass movement on a slope?","options":{"A":"Wet soil becomes lighter and more stable","B":"Water removes the pull of gravity","C":"More weight and less friction","D":"Saturation always increases rock strength"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-SUBAERIAL","scaffold":"Water increases pore pressure and mass while lowering resistance along a slip plane.","reforge":{"stem":"Which slope is most vulnerable to rotational slumping?","options":{"A":"A saturated cliff made of weak clay","B":"A dry granite plateau with no joints","C":"A flat chalk surface far inland","D":"A beach during a calm low tide"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-44","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"How can a cove form on a concordant coastline?","options":{"A":"Longshore drift seals every opening with sand","B":"Alternating rocks meet the coast at right angles","C":"A river cuts through only resistant granite","D":"Soft rock is exposed after hard rock is breached"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"A narrow breach through resistant outer rock allows waves to erode softer rock behind, widening a cove.","reforge":{"stem":"Which structure defines a concordant coastline?","options":{"A":"Rock bands run parallel to the shore","B":"Rock bands perpendicular to shore","C":"Only one rock type occurs inland","D":"Faults are absent from the coastline"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-45","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"What is the usual sequence as waves exploit a joint in a headland?","options":{"A":"Cave, arch, stack, stump","B":"Stack, cave, arch, stump","C":"Arch, cave, stump, stack","D":"Stump, stack, cave, arch"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-STACK-SEQUENCE","scaffold":"Hydraulic action and abrasion enlarge a crack into a cave, then an arch; roof collapse leaves a stack and later a stump.","reforge":{"stem":"What most directly causes an arch to become a stack?","options":{"A":"Deposition fills the opening with beach material","B":"Longshore drift moves the arch inland","C":"The weakened roof collapses","D":"The headland becomes harder over time"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-46","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Why is erosion often concentrated on coastal headlands?","options":{"A":"Constructive waves remove cliff material fastest","B":"Wave refraction focuses energy there","C":"Headlands are always made of soft clay","D":"Bays receive all incoming wave energy"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Wave fronts bend in shallow water, concentrating energy on projecting headlands and dispersing it within bays.","reforge":{"stem":"How does wave refraction encourage deposition in a bay?","options":{"A":"All waves become destructive at once","B":"It makes the bay rock less resistant","C":"It prevents sediment entering the bay","D":"Wave energy spreads and becomes lower"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-47","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"How does an offshore bar form?","options":{"A":"A stack collapses across a river mouth","B":"Cliff retreat leaves a rocky platform","C":"A spit extends across a bay","D":"A glacier deposits till parallel to shore"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-LONGSHORE","scaffold":"A spit may continue across a bay where no strong river current breaks it, forming a bar and often trapping a lagoon.","reforge":{"stem":"What feature commonly forms behind a coastal bar?","options":{"A":"A lagoon in sheltered water","B":"A waterfall and plunge pool","C":"A glacial ribbon lake","D":"A tectonic rift valley"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-48","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Which is a sub-aerial process affecting a sea cliff?","options":{"A":"Hydraulic action by breaking waves","B":"Traction moving shingle along the seabed","C":"Wave refraction around a headland","D":"Atmospheric weathering of cliff rock"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-SUBAERIAL","scaffold":"Sub-aerial processes act from the atmosphere and slopes, including weathering, runoff and mass movement.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can sub-aerial weathering accelerate marine erosion?","options":{"A":"It makes incoming waves constructive","B":"It weakens rock before waves attack","C":"It transports sediment only offshore","D":"It raises the cliff above wave reach"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-49","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"How does saturated ground before a storm affect a river hydrograph?","options":{"A":"It increases runoff and shortens lag time","B":"It increases infiltration capacity greatly","C":"It prevents rainfall reaching the channel","D":"It lowers discharge throughout the event"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-HYDROGRAPH","scaffold":"Antecedent saturation leaves little storage capacity, so rainfall reaches the channel rapidly.","reforge":{"stem":"Which hydrograph is most likely after several wet weeks?","options":{"A":"No change in discharge after rainfall","B":"A long lag with a very low peak","C":"A steep rising limb and high peak","D":"A falling limb before rain begins"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-50","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Why does an elongated drainage basin often have a longer lag time than a circular one?","options":{"A":"Water reaches the channel from all points together","B":"Runoff arrives over a wider period","C":"Elongated basins always have impermeable rock","D":"Basin shape prevents tributaries forming"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-HYDROGRAPH","scaffold":"Distances to the outlet vary more in an elongated basin, spreading tributary inputs over time.","reforge":{"stem":"Which basin shape is most likely to produce a sharp peak discharge?","options":{"A":"A long narrow basin with sparse tributaries","B":"An elongated basin with permeable soils","C":"A basin without any channel network","D":"A compact circular drainage basin"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-51","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"Why are natural levees highest beside a river channel?","options":{"A":"The finest sediment settles first near the bank","B":"Floodwater speeds up across the floodplain","C":"Coarser load is deposited as water spills out","D":"River erosion cuts ridges into the floodplain"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-MEANDER","scaffold":"When floodwater leaves the channel it loses velocity; heavier sediment is deposited first beside the banks.","reforge":{"stem":"What happens to sediment size farther across a floodplain during a flood?","options":{"A":"Deposits generally become finer","B":"Deposits become progressively coarser","C":"No sediment leaves the channel","D":"Only dissolved load is deposited beside banks"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-52","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","stem":"How can river restoration reduce downstream flood risk?","options":{"A":"It straightens and deepens the entire river","B":"It builds housing across the washland","C":"It removes vegetation from every bank","D":"Restored floodplain storage"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-FLOOD-RISK","scaffold":"Restoring meanders, wetlands and floodplain storage slows flow and temporarily stores water.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one trade-off of floodplain restoration?","options":{"A":"It guarantees no future flood can occur","B":"Some land is allowed to flood more often","C":"It increases channel velocity everywhere","D":"It requires concrete walls along all banks"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-WAVE-TYPE","stem":"A beach is built up over a calm summer and cut back during winter storms. What difference between wave types explains this?","options":{"A":"Constructive waves have a stronger swash; destructive ones a stronger backwash","B":"Constructive waves occur in winter, and destructive waves only ever in the summer","C":"Destructive waves carry far more sand onto the beach than constructive waves","D":"Wave type has no influence on whether a beach gains or loses any material"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"The balance of swash and backwash decides whether sediment stays. Constructive waves are low and long, so a powerful swash carries material up the beach and a weak backwash leaves it there. Destructive waves are tall and steep with a short swash and a strong backwash that drags sediment offshore, so the beach is scoured.","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination describes a destructive wave?","options":{"A":"Low height, long wavelength, strong swash and a weak backwash","B":"Tall and steep, with a strong swash that builds the beach profile up","C":"Tall and steep, with a weak swash and a much stronger backwash","D":"Low height and long wavelength, with almost no swash or backwash at all"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-WAVE-TYPE","stem":"Wave energy reaching a UK beach depends partly on fetch. What does fetch mean?","options":{"A":"The depth of the water measured directly offshore from the beach","B":"The distance of open water a wind blows across before reaching the coast","C":"The height a wave reaches at the moment it finally breaks on the shore","D":"The total number of waves that break on a beach in any given single hour"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"The longer the fetch, the more time and distance the wind has to transfer energy into the water, so the larger the waves arriving. This is why the Atlantic-facing coasts of Cornwall receive far more powerful waves than sheltered stretches of the North Sea coast, and why fetch is part of explaining where erosion is fastest.","reforge":{"stem":"Two UK beaches experience the same wind speed, but one receives much larger waves. What is the most likely reason?","options":{"A":"One beach is composed of sand while the other is made of shingle","B":"The two beaches are at slightly different heights above mean sea level","C":"One beach happens to face north while the other one faces to the south","D":"That beach has a much longer fetch of open water facing the wind"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-UPPER-COURSE","stem":"A waterfall in the upper course has a deep plunge pool and a steep gorge downstream of it. How did the gorge form?","options":{"A":"The gorge was cut by a glacier long before the waterfall existed","B":"Deposition of large boulders gradually built up the steep gorge walls","C":"The waterfall retreated upstream, leaving the gorge it cut behind it","D":"The gorge formed first and the waterfall later flowed into its lower end"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Hard rock overlies softer rock. The softer rock is undercut, leaving an overhang that eventually collapses, and the waterfall shifts a short distance upstream. Repeat that over thousands of years and the retreating waterfall leaves a steep-sided gorge marking every position it once occupied.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a waterfall retreat upstream over time?","options":{"A":"Softer rock beneath is undercut until the hard overhang collapses","B":"Because deposition at the base slowly pushes the whole waterfall back","C":"Because the plunge pool fills completely with sediment and blocks it","D":"Because the river's discharge falls steadily as the years go by"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-UPPER-COURSE","stem":"An upper-course valley is narrow and steep-sided, with interlocking spurs. Which process dominates here?","options":{"A":"Glacial erosion widening the valley into a broad U-shaped cross-profile","B":"Lateral erosion swinging the channel sideways across a wide flat floor","C":"Deposition of fine silt building up the valley floor after each flood","D":"Vertical erosion cutting downwards, with weathering loosening the sides"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Near the source the river is steep and turbulent but carries a coarse, angular load, so its energy goes into cutting downwards. The valley sides are then loosened by weathering and mass movement, giving a narrow V-shape. The river lacks the power to cut sideways, so it winds around obstacles, leaving spurs that interlock.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the key difference between a V-shaped valley and a U-shaped valley in the UK uplands?","options":{"A":"A V-shaped valley is always found closer to the mouth of the river","B":"A U-shaped valley was cut by a glacier widening a former river valley","C":"A U-shaped valley is produced entirely by deposition rather than erosion","D":"A V-shaped valley forms only where the underlying rock is limestone"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-MEANDER","stem":"A curved lake sits on a floodplain, separated from the river channel beside it. How did it form?","options":{"A":"A meander neck was cut through in a flood and the old bend was sealed off","B":"A glacier scooped out a curved hollow that later filled with rainwater","C":"The river deposited a curved levee that trapped water permanently behind it","D":"Groundwater rose to the surface along a curved line of permeable rock"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Erosion on the outside of two adjacent bends narrows the neck of land between them. In a high-discharge flood the river breaks through, taking the shorter, steeper route. Flow through the old bend slows, so deposition seals both ends and leaves an oxbow lake that gradually silts up.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does an oxbow lake become cut off from the river rather than staying connected to it?","options":{"A":"The lake is lifted above the river as the floodplain rises over time","B":"Farmers deliberately dam both ends of the old bend in order to create a reservoir","C":"Flow through the old bend slows, so deposition seals off both of its ends","D":"The river changes direction entirely and flows away from the old bend"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-LONGSHORE","stem":"A spit extends across a river mouth and its far end curves landwards, with salt marsh behind it. What explains the curved end?","options":{"A":"The river's current pushes the end of the spit back towards the land","B":"Occasional changes in the prevailing wind direction curve the end round","C":"Salt marsh vegetation physically drags the end of the spit round as it grows","D":"Spits always form in a curve because of the rotation of the Earth itself"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Longshore drift moves sediment along the coast and deposits it where the coastline changes direction, building a spit into open water. A secondary wind direction periodically pushes the exposed end landwards, producing recurved hooks. Behind the spit the water is sheltered, so silt settles and salt marsh develops.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does salt marsh develop behind a spit rather than in front of it?","options":{"A":"Wave energy behind a spit is much higher, which brings in more sediment","B":"Salt marsh plants can only grow on the seaward side of any sandy spit","C":"The water behind a spit is deeper, which suits marsh vegetation better","D":"The sheltered water behind the spit is calm enough for silt to settle"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-MANAGE","stem":"A council chooses beach nourishment over building a new sea wall. What is the main trade-off it has accepted?","options":{"A":"Nourishment is far more expensive to install than a sea wall would be","B":"Nourishment permanently ends all erosion along that stretch of coastline","C":"It is cheaper and looks natural, but must be repeated as sand washes away","D":"Nourishment protects the cliff but removes the beach that tourists come for"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Soft engineering works with natural processes rather than against them. Nourishment costs far less than a sea wall, keeps the beach for tourism and absorbs wave energy. The cost is recurring: the added sand is itself eroded, so it needs topping up every few years, and the sediment has to be dredged from somewhere else.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is the clearest example of soft engineering on a coastline?","options":{"A":"Replanting marram grass to stabilise dunes behind a sandy beach","B":"A rock armour revetment placed at the base of an eroding cliff","C":"A line of timber groynes built out across the beach at intervals","D":"A concrete sea wall with a curved recurved face at its top"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-COAST-MANAGE","stem":"On part of the Holderness Coast the shoreline management plan is 'no active intervention', while Mappleton is defended. Why defend some stretches and not others?","options":{"A":"Undefended stretches have no erosion occurring along them at present","B":"Defences can only ever be built where the cliff is made of resistant hard rock","C":"Stretches are chosen at random because erosion rates cannot be predicted","D":"Defences are placed where the value at risk justifies the cost of protection"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Coastal management is a cost-benefit judgement. Mappleton's road and homes justified rock groynes and a revetment; long stretches of farmland do not, because the cost of defending them exceeds the value of the land lost. That is why holding the line everywhere was abandoned, and it is what makes the policy contested by those living on undefended stretches.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main criticism made by landowners on an undefended stretch of coast next to a defended one?","options":{"A":"Defended stretches attract more storms towards the neighbouring coastline","B":"Defences upcoast starve their beach of sediment, so erosion speeds up","C":"Building defences anywhere raises sea level along the whole coastline","D":"Undefended land becomes more valuable, which raises the tax paid on it"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-SUBAERIAL","stem":"Holderness cliffs of boulder clay slump after heavy rain, forming a curved scar and a lobe of mud at the base. Is this erosion?","options":{"A":"No — it is mass movement, where saturated material slides down under gravity","B":"Yes — slumping is a marine erosion process driven by wave action","C":"Yes — the rain itself erodes the whole cliff face by dissolving the clay away","D":"No — it is chemical weathering breaking the clay down where it sits"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Waves erode the cliff foot and steepen it, but the slump itself is mass movement: rain saturates the permeable clay, adds weight and reduces friction, so a block rotates and slides. The two work together, which is why Holderness retreats fastest after wet winters, and why a question can ask for both marine and sub-aerial processes.","reforge":{"stem":"How do sub-aerial processes and marine erosion combine to make a cliff retreat?","options":{"A":"Sub-aerial processes act only after all of the marine erosion has fully stopped","B":"Both processes are simply different names for exactly the same thing","C":"Waves undercut the base while weathering and mass movement weaken the face","D":"Marine erosion protects the cliff from the sub-aerial processes above it"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-GEOLOGY","stem":"Two neighbouring drainage basins receive identical rainfall, but the one on impermeable clay floods far more often. Why?","options":{"A":"Clay basins always receive more intense rainfall than any other basin","B":"Water cannot infiltrate clay, so it runs off the surface into the river fast","C":"Impermeable rock stores all the water underground and releases it all at once","D":"Clay dissolves in rainwater, which adds extra volume to the river channel"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Permeability decides the route water takes. Through permeable chalk or limestone, water infiltrates and travels slowly underground, arriving at the channel over days. On impermeable clay or granite it stays on the surface, so it reaches the river within hours: a shorter lag time and a higher, sharper peak discharge.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do chalk and limestone areas often have few surface streams?","options":{"A":"Rainfall in these areas evaporates before it can ever reach the ground","B":"Chalk and limestone are too hard for any river channel to cut into them","C":"These rocks are impermeable, so all the water is held on the surface","D":"Water infiltrates through joints and travels underground instead"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-HYDROGRAPH","stem":"Two hydrographs from the same storm show one basin with a lag time of 4 hours and a high sharp peak, the other 14 hours with a low broad peak. Which is more likely to be urbanised?","options":{"A":"The 14-hour basin, because urban drainage always slows water down","B":"Neither, because urbanisation has no effect at all on a storm hydrograph","C":"The 4-hour basin, because impermeable surfaces and drains deliver water fast","D":"The 14-hour basin, because cities store the water in reservoirs before release"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Urbanisation replaces soil and vegetation with tarmac and roofs, and adds drains that carry water straight to the channel. Infiltration and interception both fall, so water arrives quickly and together: lag time shortens and the peak rises. A flashy hydrograph is the signature of an urban, impermeable or steep basin.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change would lengthen a basin's lag time rather than shorten it?","options":{"A":"Planting woodland across the upper part of the drainage basin","B":"Extending the storm drain network to reach more of the built-up area","C":"A prolonged wet spell that leaves the basin's soils fully saturated","D":"Paving over front gardens and driveways across a large housing estate"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"},{"id":"GCSE-UKLAND-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-UKLAND","tag":"MC-GG-UKL-LONG-PROFILE","stem":"Moving downstream, a river's gradient falls but its discharge and velocity rise. A student calls this a contradiction. Why is it not?","options":{"A":"Velocity downstream is in fact lower than it is in the steep upper course","B":"Gradient is the only factor that has any influence on a river's velocity","C":"Discharge falls downstream as water is lost, which offsets the gradient","D":"Tributaries add water and the channel becomes smoother, reducing friction"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Steepness is not the whole story. In the upper course a shallow channel choked with large angular boulders creates enormous friction, so despite the gradient the water is slow. Downstream, tributaries raise discharge and the channel is deeper, wider and smoother, so proportionally less water touches the bed and velocity rises.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a river's load become smaller and rounder towards the mouth?","options":{"A":"The river deposits its smallest particles first, leaving rounded ones behind","B":"Attrition wears fragments down as they collide and are carried along","C":"Rounded pebbles are carried into the river by tributaries near the mouth","D":"Chemical weathering dissolves the corners of every pebble in the channel"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukland"}]},"GCSE-GEO-UKHUMAN":{"label":"UK's Evolving Human Landscape","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-01","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"London's Docklands, once derelict after the Port of London's container trade collapsed, was transformed into Canary Wharf's offices, flats, and the Jubilee Line Extension. A student says this just means 'new buildings were put up.' What term better describes this process?","options":{"A":"Urban regeneration — reversing decline in a run-down area","B":"Deindustrialisation — the decline of manufacturing industry","C":"Counter-urbanisation — people moving out of cities into the countryside","D":"Suburbanisation — outward growth of housing at the city edge"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-REGENERATION","scaffold":"Urban regeneration is a planned, long-term attempt to reverse decline in a run-down urban area — improving the economy, environment, and social conditions, not just erecting buildings. London's Docklands fell derelict as container shipping moved to deep-water ports like Tilbury, then the London Docklands Development Corporation drove investment into Canary Wharf's offices, new housing, and transport links (DLR, Jubilee Line Extension). Counter-urbanisation and suburbanisation describe population movement out of cities; deindustrialisation is the decline itself, not the response to it.","reforge":{"stem":"Bristol's Harbourside was redeveloped from disused docks into housing, museums, and leisure space, bringing jobs and investment back to the area. What is this an example of?","options":{"A":"Urban sprawl","B":"Rural depopulation","C":"Urban regeneration","D":"Counter-urbanisation"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-02","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Through the 1960s-80s, London's docks and East End manufacturing lost huge numbers of jobs as container shipping and cheaper overseas production took over. What is this process called, and what did it cause before regeneration?","options":{"A":"Counter-urbanisation — people leaving cities for rural areas","B":"Deindustrialisation — manufacturing decline causing job loss","C":"Immigration — international migrants arriving in cities","D":"Reindustrialisation — the growth of new manufacturing industry"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-STRUCTURAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"Deindustrialisation is the long-term decline of manufacturing/traditional industry, driven by cheaper overseas labour, automation, and changing trade patterns (here, larger container ships needing deep-water ports further downriver). It left London's Docklands derelict and unemployed for years — exactly the problem the Docklands regeneration scheme was designed to fix.","reforge":{"stem":"A former coal-mining town in South Yorkshire lost its main employer in the 1980s-90s as UK coal mining declined sharply. What is the general term for this loss of traditional industry?","options":{"A":"Counter-urbanisation","B":"Urbanisation","C":"Regeneration","D":"Deindustrialisation"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-03","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"London's population includes large numbers of people born outside the UK, drawn by job opportunities, universities, and existing migrant communities. A student says international migration only affects London's population size. What else does it change?","options":{"A":"Nothing else — migration only ever changes population totals","B":"It makes London's population entirely homogeneous","C":"Its social and cultural character becomes more diverse","D":"It only affects the age structure, not culture"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-CULTURAL-MIGRATION","scaffold":"International migration is a major driver of London's dynamic character: it doesn't just add to the population count, it reshapes the city's ethnic and cultural make-up, creating diverse neighbourhoods (e.g. Brick Lane's Bangladeshi community, Southall's Punjabi community) with distinct shops, places of worship, and cuisines — part of what makes London a 'world city'.","reforge":{"stem":"Areas of London such as Brick Lane have a strong South Asian character, with specific cuisines, shops, and mosques reflecting the heritage of migrant communities who settled there. This is best explained as an effect of what process?","options":{"A":"International migration","B":"Rural depopulation of villages","C":"Deindustrialisation of the docks","D":"Counter-urbanisation to the countryside"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-04","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Cornwall has seen house prices rise sharply as retirees and second-home buyers move in, while young local people are increasingly priced out and some villages feel 'empty' outside the summer season. A student says rural areas never change. What's actually happening here?","options":{"A":"Deindustrialisation — the decline of manufacturing","B":"Urban regeneration — the renewal of derelict city-centre areas","C":"Urbanisation — the growth of the whole country's urban population","D":"Rural landscape change driven by counter-urbanisation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-RURAL-HOUSING","scaffold":"Rural landscapes change too, not just cities: counter-urbanisation (people moving from cities to attractive rural/coastal areas, often to retire) combined with high demand for second homes has pushed Cornish house prices far above what local wages can support, squeezing out younger residents and, in some villages, leaving houses empty for much of the year — a major and controversial issue in Cornwall's 'second homes crisis'.","reforge":{"stem":"A coastal village in Cornwall sees average house prices rise sharply, its primary school threatened with closure due to falling pupil numbers, and a large share of homes standing empty outside peak summer weeks. Which process best explains this pattern?","options":{"A":"Deindustrialisation of local industry","B":"Counter-urbanisation and second homes","C":"Urban sprawl into the green belt","D":"International migration into the village"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-05","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"The Eden Project was built inside a disused china clay pit near St Austell, Cornwall, using giant biomes to house plants from around the world and attract over a million visitors a year. A student says this is 'just a tourist attraction.' What wider planning goal does it demonstrate?","options":{"A":"Sustainable rural regeneration of a derelict site","B":"Urban sprawl — the unplanned outward spread of a city","C":"Deindustrialisation — reducing manufacturing industry","D":"Counter-urbanisation — people moving from cities to the countryside"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-REGENERATION","scaffold":"The Eden Project reclaimed a derelict, environmentally scarred china clay pit and turned it into a major visitor attraction, creating thousands of local jobs and diversifying Cornwall's economy away from its declining primary industries — while its design emphasises renewable energy, recycled materials and environmental education. It's a rural regeneration project, not simply a tourist site.","reforge":{"stem":"A disused quarry is transformed into an eco-attraction with renewable energy, education centres on biodiversity, and hundreds of new local jobs in a struggling rural economy. What does this best represent?","options":{"A":"Urban sprawl at the city edge","B":"Deindustrialisation of the quarry","C":"Sustainable rural regeneration","D":"Counter-urbanisation of workers"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-06","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Cornwall's tin mining and fishing industries have declined sharply since the mid-20th century, while tourism now dominates the economy. A student says this makes Cornwall's economy more secure than before. Why is this often untrue?","options":{"A":"Tourism has had no effect on Cornwall's economy at all","B":"Tourism is seasonal, so many jobs are low-paid and part-year","C":"Tin mining and fishing have grown rather than declined in Cornwall","D":"Tourism jobs are guaranteed to be permanent, well-paid, and available all year round"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-STRUCTURAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"Cornwall's shift from primary industries (tin mining, fishing) to a tourism-dominated economy hasn't simply replaced one stable industry with another: tourism is strongly seasonal, so many jobs are part-time, low-paid, and concentrated in summer, leaving many Cornish workers facing unemployment or underemployment for much of the year and the wider economy vulnerable to downturns in visitor numbers.","reforge":{"stem":"A rural economy that once depended on fishing now relies mainly on summer tourism. Local workers report struggling to find paid work between October and March each year. What does this reveal about economies that shift heavily towards tourism?","options":{"A":"Seasonal employment patterns don't apply to tourism-dependent economies","B":"The decline of fishing has no bearing on the wider local economy","C":"Tourism-based economies are always more stable than industries like fishing","D":"They face seasonal unemployment outside peak months"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about London's Docklands.","template":"___ ___ is long-term investment to reverse the economic and environmental decline of a run-down area, as seen at London's Docklands where derelict wharves became the offices and flats of ___.","blanks":["Urban","regeneration","Canary Wharf"],"bank":["Urban","regeneration","Canary Wharf","sprawl","counter-urbanisation","green belt"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKH-REGENERATION","scaffold":"Urban regeneration reverses decline through planned, long-term investment — London's derelict Docklands were transformed into the offices, housing and transport links of Canary Wharf.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about deindustrialisation.","template":"___ is the long-term decline of traditional industry, caused by factors such as cheaper overseas competition and changing technology, which left London's Docklands ___ and unemployed before ___ schemes were introduced.","blanks":["Deindustrialisation","derelict","regeneration"],"bank":["Deindustrialisation","derelict","regeneration","urbanisation","booming","migration"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKH-STRUCTURAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"Deindustrialisation — docks and factories closing as container shipping moved elsewhere and manufacturing shifted overseas — left London's Docklands derelict and unemployed, creating the need for regeneration.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about migration and London's character.","template":"International ___ has shaped London's social and cultural character, creating ethnically ___ areas such as Brick Lane, with distinctive shops, food and places of ___.","blanks":["migration","diverse","worship"],"bank":["migration","diverse","worship","homogeneous","regeneration","sprawl"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKH-CULTURAL-MIGRATION","scaffold":"International migration doesn't just change population size — it reshapes culture and identity, creating diverse, multicultural neighbourhoods like Brick Lane with their own shops, cuisines and religious buildings.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about rural change in Cornwall.","template":"___ ___ and rising demand for ___ homes have pushed up house prices across Cornwall, pricing out many young local residents.","blanks":["Counter","urbanisation","second"],"bank":["Counter","urbanisation","second","de","industrialisation","first"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKH-RURAL-HOUSING","scaffold":"Counter-urbanisation — people, often retirees, moving from cities into rural/coastal Cornwall — combined with high demand for second homes has changed Cornwall's housing market, raising prices well beyond what local wages can support.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-05","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about the Eden Project.","template":"The Eden Project reclaimed a disused china clay ___ near St Austell, creating a ___ rural regeneration scheme that brought thousands of ___ jobs to the area.","blanks":["pit","sustainable","local"],"bank":["pit","sustainable","local","quarry","unsustainable","national"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKH-REGENERATION","scaffold":"The Eden Project turned a derelict, scarred china clay pit into a major eco-attraction, diversifying Cornwall's economy with thousands of local jobs while keeping a strong environmental focus — a model of sustainable rural regeneration.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-FB-06","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Cornwall's economy.","template":"As tin mining and fishing declined, Cornwall's economy shifted towards ___, but this industry is highly ___, leaving many workers facing ___ for much of the year.","blanks":["tourism","seasonal","unemployment"],"bank":["tourism","seasonal","unemployment","manufacturing","stable","overemployment"],"tag":"MC-GG-UKH-STRUCTURAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"Cornwall's shift from primary industries to tourism hasn't guaranteed economic security: tourism is strongly seasonal, so many jobs are part-time and concentrated in summer, leaving workers facing unemployment or underemployment for much of the rest of the year.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-07","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"A student mixes up immigration and migration. What is the correct distinction?","options":{"A":"Immigration crosses a national border; migration can be internal","B":"Immigration and migration are two words for exactly the same process","C":"Migration only refers to movement between countries; immigration only refers to movement within a country","D":"Immigration is always temporary, migration is always permanent"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-MIGRATION-TERMS","scaffold":"International movement = immigration (into a country) or emigration (out of a country). Internal movement = migration (e.g. London to Cornwall). Net migration then measures the balance of immigration minus emigration for a country.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK resident moves permanently from Manchester to Cornwall. Which term correctly describes this movement?","options":{"A":"Immigration, since it crosses an international border","B":"Net migration, since it only measures one person","C":"Migration, since it stays within one country","D":"Emigration, since it leaves the UK entirely"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-08","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What is net migration?","options":{"A":"The total number of people who have ever migrated into a country","B":"The difference between immigration and emigration","C":"The percentage of a country's population that was born abroad","D":"A measure of how many people move within a country each year"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-MIGRATION-TERMS","scaffold":"Net migration is a balance figure: (immigration) minus (emigration). Positive net migration means more people are arriving than leaving; this is one of the key drivers of the UK's growing and increasingly multicultural population alongside natural increase.","reforge":{"stem":"A country records 50,000 people arriving to live there and 30,000 people leaving in the same year. What is the net migration figure?","options":{"A":"-20,000 — more people left than arrived","B":"80,000 — the total of both figures added together","C":"Net migration cannot be calculated from this information","D":"+20,000 — more people arrived than left"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-09","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"How does a Trans-National Company (TNC) differ from ordinary Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)?","options":{"A":"A TNC only operates within its home country","B":"A TNC and FDI are identical terms describing the same thing","C":"A TNC operates abroad; FDI is the investment itself","D":"FDI can only be carried out by governments, never by companies"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-INVESTMENT","scaffold":"A TNC is the organisation (e.g. a car manufacturer with factories on several continents); FDI is the financial mechanism it uses to set up or invest in those overseas operations. TNC investment is a major driver of globalisation.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK-based supermarket chain opens and operates stores across several other countries. What type of organisation does this make it, and what did it likely use to fund this expansion abroad?","options":{"A":"A Trans-National Company, funded through FDI","B":"An enterprise zone, funded through privatisation","C":"A trade bloc, funded through government grants","D":"An NGO, funded entirely through charitable donations"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-10","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What does the 'shrinking world' effect describe, and what causes it?","options":{"A":"The declining global population","B":"The process of a country's economy shrinking due to deindustrialisation","C":"A measurable reduction in the Earth's physical land area","D":"The world feeling smaller as transport and contact speed up"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-CONNECTIVITY","scaffold":"'Shrinking world' is about perceived, not physical, distance — jet travel, container shipping and instant digital communication mean places that once took weeks to reach are now hours away, which is a major driver behind globalisation and increased migration and trade.","reforge":{"stem":"Cheaper long-haul flights and instant global communication mean a business call can now be made across the world in seconds rather than weeks by letter. What geographical concept does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Segregation — groups of people becoming isolated from wider society","B":"The shrinking world, driven by transport and communication","C":"Urbanisation — more people living in cities","D":"Deindustrialisation — the decline of manufacturing"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-11","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"A student defines an enterprise zone as 'any area with a lot of businesses.' How should this be corrected?","options":{"A":"A zone where government incentives attract businesses","B":"The student's definition is already correct","C":"An enterprise zone only applies to farming and agriculture","D":"An enterprise zone is a region where all business activity is banned"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-ECONOMIC-POLICY","scaffold":"Enterprise zones are a deliberate government regeneration tool, not just a description of where businesses happen to cluster — they're used to attract investment into areas that have lost industry, such as former docklands or coalfield towns.","reforge":{"stem":"A former industrial area is designated by the government with lower tax rates and new offices to attract businesses back. What is this designation called?","options":{"A":"A rural-urban continuum","B":"A trade bloc","C":"An enterprise zone","D":"A multicultural event"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-12","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Which correctly orders the four types of industry from raw materials to finished service, and gives an accurate example of each?","options":{"A":"Quaternary → Tertiary → Secondary → Primary, with the examples reversed","B":"Primary (farming) → Secondary (factory) → Tertiary (teaching) → Quaternary (design)","C":"Primary and secondary industry both refer to manufacturing, just at different scales","D":"Tertiary industry refers to raw material extraction such as mining"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-SECTOR-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Primary = extracting raw materials (farming, mining). Secondary = manufacturing (turning raw materials into products). Tertiary = providing a service to people (teaching, retail). Quaternary = knowledge/technology-based work (research, design, IT). The UK's economy has shifted heavily from primary/secondary towards tertiary/quaternary over the last century — this is a key idea across the whole topic.","reforge":{"stem":"A product designer working on new technology, and a factory worker assembling a product, belong to which two industry sectors respectively?","options":{"A":"Secondary and quaternary","B":"Primary and tertiary","C":"Tertiary and primary","D":"Quaternary and secondary"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-13","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What is privatisation, and how does it differ from a government grant?","options":{"A":"Privatisation means a government takes over a private company","B":"A grant always leads to privatisation of the organisation receiving it","C":"Privatisation hands a service to a firm; a grant is state money","D":"Privatisation and a grant are the same government policy"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-ECONOMIC-POLICY","scaffold":"Privatisation shifts ownership of a service from public to private hands (e.g. rail, water). A grant is government money flowing the other way, into a business or organisation, often to support development goals such as research or regeneration — don't confuse the direction of each.","reforge":{"stem":"A government sells off a previously state-run rail service to a private company. What is this process called?","options":{"A":"Privatisation","B":"A grant","C":"Nationalisation","D":"Enterprise zoning"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-14","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What is the rural-urban continuum, and what is it used to show?","options":{"A":"A measure of a country's total population density","B":"The boundary that legally separates urban and rural land use","C":"A physical road connecting a city centre to the nearest rural village","D":"A line from city centre to countryside showing gradual change"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-POPULATION-PATTERNS","scaffold":"The continuum is a conceptual tool, not a real line — it helps geographers describe how characteristics like population density, house prices, age structure and services change gradually, rather than switching abruptly, as you move from city centre to countryside.","reforge":{"stem":"Population density, house prices and access to services tend to change gradually rather than suddenly as you travel from a city centre out into the surrounding countryside. What concept does this describe?","options":{"A":"A fixed legal boundary between rural and urban land","B":"The rural-urban continuum","C":"Economic inequality","D":"Net migration"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-15","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"A student says 'segregation and multiculturalism mean the same thing.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Segregation isolates a group; multiculturalism mixes cultures","B":"The student is correct — they are interchangeable terms","C":"Segregation only refers to religious groups, multiculturalism only to ethnic groups","D":"Multiculturalism means a population has only one ethnic group"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-CULTURAL-MIGRATION","scaffold":"Segregation describes separation and isolation between groups; multicultural events (like Chinese New Year or Notting Hill Carnival) represent integration and shared celebration of diverse cultures within the same community — exam answers about a place's cultural character often need you to distinguish evidence of one from the other.","reforge":{"stem":"A neighbourhood hosts an annual festival celebrating a culture originally from another country, drawing residents of many backgrounds together. What does this best represent, in contrast to segregation?","options":{"A":"Segregation — since a specific culture is being highlighted","B":"Hate crime — since it focuses on one culture's traditions","C":"A multicultural event celebrating cultural mixing","D":"Net migration — since the event involves migrants"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-16","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What does the Commonwealth refer to, and how is it linked to UK migration patterns?","options":{"A":"A trade bloc that only the UK and USA belong to","B":"Former British colonies with lasting UK ties","C":"A term describing only the UK's domestic regions","D":"An alternative name for the European Union"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-MIGRATION-TERMS","scaffold":"The Commonwealth's shared colonial history explains why post-war UK immigration (e.g. the Windrush generation from the Caribbean) often came from former colonies — historical ties, language and existing community networks make migration between these countries more likely.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do patterns of UK immigration often link back to former British colonies?","options":{"A":"The Commonwealth only includes European countries","B":"Commonwealth countries are legally barred from migrating to the UK","C":"The Commonwealth is unrelated to migration patterns","D":"Shared Commonwealth history, language and community ties"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-17","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Why did the Jubilee Line Extension support regeneration in London's Docklands?","options":{"A":"It removed the need for any new housing or services","B":"It moved every Docklands resident into the countryside","C":"It improved access to jobs and investment","D":"It caused container shipping to return to the old docks"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-REGENERATION","scaffold":"Better transport can create a multiplier effect: it connects residents to employment, attracts firms and encourages further investment in housing and services. The Jubilee Line Extension was one part of Docklands regeneration, alongside the DLR, Canary Wharf development and environmental improvement.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome would provide evidence of a transport-led multiplier effect in Docklands?","options":{"A":"Improved access encourages offices to locate nearby","B":"A new station opens but no businesses, jobs or residents follow","C":"All transport investment reduces land values","D":"The railway operates without connecting to any other part of London"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-18","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Why can tourism make Cornwall's economy vulnerable despite creating jobs?","options":{"A":"Tourism guarantees stable employment in every season","B":"Tourism removes the need for transport and internet links","C":"Tourism has no relationship with house prices or land use","D":"Seasonal demand can produce insecure, low-paid work"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-RURAL-ECONOMY","scaffold":"Tourism diversifies Cornwall beyond declining mining and fishing, but it can create dependence on seasonal and discretionary spending. Jobs may be temporary, homes may become second residences, and bad weather or recession can reduce demand.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy would best reduce Cornwall's dependence on seasonal tourism?","options":{"A":"Convert all local housing into holiday lets","B":"Develop year-round sectors such as renewable energy","C":"Close transport links outside the summer season","D":"Reduce the range of employment available to local residents"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-19","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"How can rural-urban migration affect the population structure of both places?","options":{"A":"It removes younger adults from rural areas, ageing them","B":"It always increases the proportion of working-age people in rural areas","C":"It changes population size but never age structure","D":"It causes identical population changes in origin and destination areas"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-POPULATION-PATTERNS","scaffold":"Migrants are often young adults, so origin areas may experience ageing and a smaller working-age population, while destinations gain workers and consumers. Migration can reshape both population size and age structure.","reforge":{"stem":"A rural district loses many young adults to cities. Which consequence is most plausible if the pattern continues?","options":{"A":"A younger workforce, since young adults all stay","B":"A guaranteed increase in local school enrolment","C":"More elderly residents and pressure on services","D":"No demographic change because only population totals matter"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-20","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What is one likely consequence when house prices rise quickly in a popular rural area?","options":{"A":"Local workers always become more able to buy homes","B":"Second homes and retirement demand cut affordable supply","C":"Rural services automatically expand because prices rise","D":"Population pressure disappears as villages become cheaper"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-RURAL-HOUSING","scaffold":"Counter-urbanisation, retirement migration and second-home ownership can increase demand and prices. Existing owners may gain, but local young adults and lower-income workers can be priced out, while seasonal occupancy can weaken year-round demand for services.","reforge":{"stem":"Which finding would best support the claim that rising second-home ownership is affecting a rural community?","options":{"A":"House prices fall as visitor numbers increase","B":"Every village shop reports longer opening hours all year","C":"More local young adults can afford to buy their first home","D":"More homes stand empty while local rents and prices rise"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-21","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What does a population pyramid with a very broad base usually indicate?","options":{"A":"No migration into the area","B":"An ageing population","C":"High birth rate","D":"Mostly working-age adults"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-POPULATION-PATTERNS","scaffold":"A broad base represents many children and usually suggests a high birth rate or youthful population. A narrow base is more typical of an ageing population with lower fertility.","reforge":{"stem":"A district's population pyramid has many children but few older people. What is most likely?","options":{"A":"A youthful population","B":"A very low birth rate","C":"No schools are needed","D":"The district has no working-age residents"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-22","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Why is population density often higher in an urban core than in a rural area?","options":{"A":"Rural areas always have taller buildings","B":"Agriculture requires every household to live centrally","C":"Urban cores have no transport connections","D":"Jobs and services attract people"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-POPULATION-PATTERNS","scaffold":"Urban cores concentrate employment, education, healthcare, shops and transport. These opportunities attract residents and support denser housing than is usually found in rural areas.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor commonly attracts people towards an urban core?","options":{"A":"Fewer services and longer journeys","B":"Employment and transport links","C":"A complete absence of housing pressure","D":"Less access to education and healthcare"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-23","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Which activity belongs to the quaternary sector?","options":{"A":"Scientific research","B":"Car manufacturing","C":"Coal mining","D":"Running a restaurant"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-SECTOR-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The quaternary sector involves information, knowledge and research, such as scientific development or data services. Primary extracts resources, secondary manufactures and tertiary provides services.","reforge":{"stem":"A university research laboratory is part of which economic sector?","options":{"A":"Primary","B":"Secondary","C":"Quaternary","D":"Agricultural"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-24","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What is foreign direct investment (FDI)?","options":{"A":"Money sent by tourists abroad","B":"Investment by an overseas firm","C":"Trade in second-hand goods","D":"A census of overseas residents"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-INVESTMENT","scaffold":"FDI occurs when a company or investor from one country invests in productive activity in another, such as opening a factory, office or distribution centre. It can create jobs but may also increase external influence.","reforge":{"stem":"A Japanese company builds and operates a factory in the UK. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Domestic migration","B":"Deindustrialisation","C":"A government subsidy","D":"Foreign investment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-25","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What is the main purpose of an enterprise zone?","options":{"A":"It replaces every local service with farming","B":"It prevents all firms from locating there","C":"Incentives attract businesses","D":"It guarantees that no land-use conflict occurs"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-ECONOMIC-POLICY","scaffold":"Enterprise zones use incentives such as reduced taxes, simpler planning or improved infrastructure to attract firms and jobs. Their benefits may be uneven and they can move activity from nearby areas.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a government create an enterprise zone in a declining area?","options":{"A":"Attract investment","B":"To stop new businesses opening","C":"To reduce transport connections","D":"To remove all public services"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-26","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Which is an example of rural diversification?","options":{"A":"Closing every farm shop in a village","B":"Moving a city CBD into a national park","C":"Replacing all rural employment with mining","D":"A farm adding visitor accommodation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-RURAL-ECONOMY","scaffold":"Rural diversification adds new income streams alongside traditional farming, such as farm shops, accommodation, leisure or tourism. It can support employment but may increase traffic or environmental pressure.","reforge":{"stem":"How might a farm diversify its income?","options":{"A":"By ending all visitor activity","B":"By adding a farm café or holiday lets","C":"By reducing the range of local services","D":"By moving its fields into the city centre"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-27","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What is one likely effect of counter-urbanisation on a popular rural area?","options":{"A":"Housing demand can raise local prices","B":"Urban employment disappears immediately","C":"Housing becomes cheaper for every local resident","D":"The rural area loses all transport links"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-RURAL-HOUSING","scaffold":"People moving from cities to accessible rural areas can increase demand for homes and services. This may raise prices and create affordability problems for younger residents, even while bringing spending and new skills.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might counter-urbanisation disadvantage some young people in a rural village?","options":{"A":"It removes all local employment","B":"It empties homes all year","C":"Higher housing demand","D":"It stops new services opening"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-28","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Which judgement best summarises a possible TNC impact on the UK?","options":{"A":"TNCs only affect local weather","B":"Jobs and capital, but profit leakage","C":"TNCs cannot change regional economies","D":"TNCs always remove all UK jobs"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-INVESTMENT","scaffold":"TNCs may bring investment, employment, technology and links to global markets. However, profits may leave the UK, decisions may be made elsewhere and local firms may face stronger competition.","reforge":{"stem":"Which option gives a balanced view of foreign investment in a UK region?","options":{"A":"It is automatically beneficial in every community","B":"It is automatically harmful because the firm is foreign","C":"It affects only the company's overseas headquarters","D":"It can create jobs while profits leave the region"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-29","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"How can international migration alter the UK's age structure?","options":{"A":"Migrants are always older than retirees","B":"Migration changes ethnicity but never age","C":"More working-age residents","D":"Every migrant settles in the same region"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-POPULATION-PATTERNS","scaffold":"Many migrants are working age, affecting labour supply, dependency ratios and demand for housing and services.","reforge":{"stem":"Which service may face greater demand after an influx of young families?","options":{"A":"Schools and maternity care","B":"Motorway freight terminals","C":"Residential care homes only","D":"Remote forestry plantations"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-30","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Why has migration increased cultural diversity in UK cities?","options":{"A":"Migrant communities remove existing cultures","B":"Immigration prevents international connections","C":"Policies make every city identical","D":"People bring languages, faiths and traditions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-CULTURAL-MIGRATION","scaffold":"Migration adds cultural practices, businesses and social networks, with patterns varying among cities and neighbourhoods.","reforge":{"stem":"Which urban change best indicates greater cultural diversity?","options":{"A":"The closure of every international food shop","B":"More diverse services and businesses","C":"Identical age structures in all districts","D":"A complete end to overseas migration"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-31","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Why has employment in UK primary industries declined?","options":{"A":"Mechanisation and import competition","B":"Extraction and farming now require more workers","C":"The UK no longer consumes raw materials","D":"Primary activities occur only in city centres"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-STRUCTURAL-CHANGE","scaffold":"Technology raises output per worker, while imports and resource depletion can reduce domestic employment.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can farm output rise while agricultural employment falls?","options":{"A":"Workers become less productive each year","B":"All farms switch to hand tools","C":"Machinery increases labour productivity","D":"Food production moves into the CBD"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-32","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What best characterises quaternary-sector employment?","options":{"A":"Manual extraction of coal and stone","B":"Knowledge and research work","C":"Assembly-line production of consumer goods","D":"Retail services requiring no information"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-SECTOR-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The quaternary sector includes research, information technology, consultancy and other knowledge-based activities.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do major cities attract quaternary firms?","options":{"A":"Digital businesses avoid high-connectivity locations","B":"Knowledge firms need to be far from universities","C":"Research work depends on nearby farmland","D":"Skilled labour and specialist networks cluster there"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-33","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"How can privatisation encourage foreign investment in the UK?","options":{"A":"It prevents private firms buying assets","B":"It guarantees all profits stay in the UK","C":"Overseas firms can acquire or operate services","D":"Privatisation removes competition from markets"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-CONNECTIVITY","scaffold":"Selling state assets or opening services to competition can create opportunities for overseas investors and TNCs.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one concern about foreign ownership of infrastructure?","options":{"A":"Decisions and profits may leave the UK","B":"It ends all access to international capital","C":"It makes regulation legally impossible","D":"It always lowers every household bill"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-34","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Which Census comparison could reveal inner-city inequality?","options":{"A":"Rock type and annual precipitation","B":"River discharge and channel width","C":"Wind direction and coastal erosion","D":"Employment, health and housing"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-URBAN-CHANGE","scaffold":"Small-area Census data allow social and economic indicators to be compared across urban neighbourhoods.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should several indicators be used to assess inequality?","options":{"A":"All neighbourhood data show the same pattern","B":"Deprivation has multiple dimensions","C":"Income alone measures every aspect of life","D":"Health and housing cannot be compared"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-35","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Why do retail parks often locate near the urban fringe?","options":{"A":"Space and major-road access","B":"Land is costlier and roads are inaccessible","C":"Customers cannot reach fringe locations by car","D":"Planning encourages all shops into the CBD"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-URBAN-CHANGE","scaffold":"Fringe locations offer space for large stores and parking near ring roads, often at lower land cost than city centres.","reforge":{"stem":"How can out-of-town retail affect a traditional centre?","options":{"A":"It guarantees higher footfall on every high street","B":"It prevents any change in land values","C":"Trade may be drawn away from central shops","D":"It moves the CBD to a rural national park"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-36","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"How has e-commerce contributed to high-street change?","options":{"A":"Online retail requires more central shop floors","B":"Some spending shifts away from stores","C":"Delivery systems eliminate distribution centres","D":"Every consumer stops visiting physical shops"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-URBAN-CHANGE","scaffold":"Online purchasing can reduce demand for some retail premises while increasing warehousing and delivery activity.","reforge":{"stem":"Which land use may expand because of online shopping?","options":{"A":"Heavy industry in every pedestrian precinct","B":"Reservoir storage beneath high streets","C":"Subsistence farming inside shopping centres","D":"Warehousing near strategic transport routes"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-37","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What is studentification?","options":{"A":"Older households move into isolated farms","B":"Industry replaces every residential property","C":"Student-led neighbourhood change","D":"A city population becomes uniformly elderly"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-URBAN-CHANGE","scaffold":"Large student populations can change housing tenure, rents, services, noise and seasonal occupancy.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change is commonly linked to studentification?","options":{"A":"More houses converted to shared rentals","B":"A fall in all demand for rental housing","C":"Permanent closure of local leisure services","D":"The disappearance of short-term residents"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-38","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"How can accessible green space improve urban quality of life?","options":{"A":"It increases pollution and heat exposure","B":"It removes opportunities for exercise","C":"It guarantees equal income distribution","D":"It supports recreation and cooling"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-PLACE-ACCESS","scaffold":"Parks provide recreation, habitats, shade and cooling, though benefits depend on access, safety and maintenance.","reforge":{"stem":"Which evidence best tests whether green-space access is unequal?","options":{"A":"Count trees in one affluent park only","B":"Map access against deprivation","C":"Use national GDP without neighbourhood data","D":"Record weather at a distant rural station"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-39","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"What is one benefit of energy-efficient affordable housing?","options":{"A":"Lower energy use can reduce household bills","B":"It raises heat loss through walls and roofs","C":"It guarantees property prices never change","D":"Efficient homes require no initial investment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-RURAL-ECONOMY","scaffold":"Insulation and efficient systems can improve comfort and reduce energy costs, especially for lower-income households.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might an affordable-housing scheme still face opposition?","options":{"A":"It removes all demand for local services","B":"It can only be built on industrial land","C":"Residents may object to density or location","D":"Affordable homes always increase commuting distance"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-40","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"How does daily commuting show city-rural interdependence?","options":{"A":"Cities produce every food and raw material locally","B":"Workers and income flow between connected places","C":"Rural residents never use urban employment","D":"Commuting prevents transport congestion"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-CONNECTIVITY","scaffold":"Accessible rural areas supply labour and homes, while cities provide jobs and specialist services.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one rural cost of heavy out-commuting?","options":{"A":"Every local wage is spent within the village","B":"Urban employers lose access to labour","C":"Public transport automatically becomes cheaper","D":"Road congestion and dormitory settlements"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-41","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Why can rural service provision decline?","options":{"A":"Rural areas always have the youngest population","B":"Distance has no effect on service access","C":"Sparse populations raise provision costs","D":"Every village supports a major hospital"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-PLACE-ACCESS","scaffold":"Small, dispersed populations and ageing structures can make schools, buses, shops and healthcare expensive to maintain.","reforge":{"stem":"Which group is especially affected by losing a rural bus route?","options":{"A":"People unable to drive to services","B":"Businesses using only international air freight","C":"Commuters living beside a city metro","D":"Households with several private vehicles"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-42","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","stem":"Why can the Index of Multiple Deprivation identify hidden rural need?","options":{"A":"It measures only national average income","B":"It excludes access to services","C":"It ranks places solely by population density","D":"Multiple deprivation dimensions"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-PLACE-ACCESS","scaffold":"IMD includes income, employment, health, education, crime, housing access and environment; poor service access may matter in otherwise attractive rural areas.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a picturesque village still contain deprivation?","options":{"A":"High environmental quality removes housing costs","B":"Low incomes and poor service access","C":"Tourism guarantees high wages for all households","D":"Rural deprivation can only occur in cities"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-URBAN-CHANGE","stem":"A green belt surrounds many UK cities. A student says its purpose is to give city residents somewhere pleasant to visit. Why is this incomplete?","options":{"A":"Its main purpose is preventing urban sprawl and stopping towns merging","B":"Green belt land is owned by the public and is entirely open for recreation everywhere","C":"Green belts exist only to protect rare wildlife habitats from any development","D":"Green belt land must legally be left completely unfarmed and unused at all times"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"A green belt is a planning restriction, not a park. Its purposes are to check unrestricted sprawl, prevent neighbouring towns merging, safeguard countryside and encourage building on derelict urban land instead. Much of it is private farmland with no public access, which is why the recreation answer only captures a side effect.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the strongest argument made by those who want green belt land released for housing?","options":{"A":"Green belt land contains no farmland or wildlife of any value whatsoever","B":"Building on green belt land would immediately end all commuting into cities","C":"Restricting land supply near cities pushes house prices beyond local incomes","D":"Green belts were designed to be removed entirely after a fixed period of time"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-URBAN-CHANGE","stem":"A council must choose between a brownfield and a greenfield site for new housing. What is the main disadvantage of the brownfield option?","options":{"A":"Brownfield sites are always located far from any existing public transport","B":"Previous industrial use may mean contaminated ground that is costly to clear","C":"Building on brownfield land increases pressure on countryside at the urban fringe","D":"Brownfield sites have no existing services such as water, power or drainage"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Brownfield is previously developed land inside the built-up area. It reuses existing infrastructure and takes pressure off the countryside, which is why planning policy favours it. The cost is real though: demolition, contamination from former industry, and awkward site shapes make it more expensive per home than a clear greenfield site.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does UK planning policy generally favour brownfield sites over greenfield ones?","options":{"A":"Brownfield sites are always considerably cheaper to build houses on","B":"Greenfield sites are legally protected everywhere in the UK from development","C":"Greenfield development is the only way to provide any affordable housing","D":"It reuses derelict urban land and reduces pressure on the countryside"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-STRUCTURAL-CHANGE","stem":"Average incomes and life expectancy are generally higher in the South East than in parts of the North East. What is the main historical reason?","options":{"A":"The North East has a much colder climate, which directly lowers all of its local incomes","B":"The North East has always had a far smaller population than the South East","C":"Heavy industry declined in the North while services and finance grew in the South","D":"Government has never invested any money in northern regions at any point"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"The North-South divide is a legacy of structural change. Coal, steel, shipbuilding and textiles employed whole northern towns, and their closure from the 1970s removed the economic base. Growth since then has concentrated in London and the South East around finance, services and quaternary work, so the gap widened rather than closed.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy is most directly aimed at reducing the UK's North-South divide?","options":{"A":"Investing in transport and enterprise zones in northern city regions","B":"Encouraging further expansion of financial services within central London","C":"Restricting the number of people permitted to live in northern England","D":"Increasing the rate of tax paid by every household living in the North"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-RURAL-HOUSING","stem":"In some Cornish villages more than a third of homes are second homes or holiday lets. Beyond high prices, what is the main effect on the community?","options":{"A":"Second-home owners increase demand for local schools throughout the year","B":"Holiday lets guarantee permanent full-time employment for every single local resident","C":"Second homes have no measurable effect on how village services are used at all","D":"Off-season the village empties, so shops, buses and schools lose year-round users"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Second homes remove permanent residents from the population. Shops, pubs, bus routes and primary schools need year-round customers and pupils to survive, so a village that is busy in August and empty in February loses the services that make it liveable. That in turn pushes more young families out, which is the spiral local housing policies try to break.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do some Cornish parishes now require new-build homes to be for permanent residents only?","options":{"A":"To increase the total number of tourists visiting the parish each summer","B":"To keep housing available for local people and sustain year-round services","C":"Because national law forbids anyone from owning more than one single house","D":"To reduce the amount of council tax that second-home owners have to pay"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-RURAL-ECONOMY","stem":"Cornwall has invested heavily in superfast broadband across rural areas. How does this support economic diversification?","options":{"A":"It lets people run businesses and work remotely, adding year-round income","B":"It guarantees that every resident of Cornwall will find well-paid seasonal work","C":"Broadband removes the need for Cornwall to have any transport links at all","D":"Broadband directly reopens the county's former tin mines and fishing fleets"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Cornwall's problem is dependence on seasonal, low-wage tourism and declining primary industry. Connectivity lets knowledge-based businesses operate from a peripheral location and allows remote workers to live there year-round, which spreads income across the calendar instead of concentrating it in the summer. It is diversification, not replacement.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is economic diversification particularly important for a rural area dependent on tourism?","options":{"A":"Diversification removes the need to attract any visitors to the area again","B":"Tourism is the only sector that can ever operate in a rural area anywhere","C":"It reduces vulnerability to a bad season, poor weather or changing tastes","D":"Diversifying always produces higher wages than tourism does in every case"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-PLACE-ACCESS","stem":"A city centre draws shoppers from a 40 km radius, while a village shop serves only its own parish. Which term describes this difference?","options":{"A":"Population density measured across the surrounding administrative region","B":"Sphere of influence — the area a settlement's services actually draw people from","C":"The rural-urban continuum, running all the way from the remotest countryside to the core","D":"Net migration between the city centre and the villages that surround it"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Sphere of influence grows with the range of services offered. A village shop sells low-order convenience goods people will not travel for; a city centre offers high-order comparison goods, hospitals and universities that justify a long journey. This is why settlement hierarchy and sphere of influence are two sides of the same idea.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a large hospital have a bigger sphere of influence than a village post office?","options":{"A":"Hospitals are always built in the exact geographical centre of a region","B":"Post offices are legally restricted to serving one single parish at a time","C":"Village services cost more money to use, so fewer people travel to them","D":"People will travel much further for a specialised, high-order service"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-POPULATION-PATTERNS","stem":"A Cornish parish has a median age of 54 against a UK median of 40. What is the most likely combination of causes?","options":{"A":"A very high birth rate combined with sustained migration of young families in","B":"An unusually high death rate among the older residents of the parish itself","C":"Young adults leaving for work and study while retired people move in","D":"The parish has recently been redrawn to include a large new housing estate"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Two flows work in the same direction. Young adults leave rural areas for universities and urban jobs, and retirees move in for the environment and lower housing costs relative to cities. Neither alone would age the population as sharply; together they hollow out the middle of the pyramid and raise demand for health and social care.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main service pressure created by an ageing rural population?","options":{"A":"Rising demand for healthcare and social care, often far from the nearest hospital","B":"Sharply rising demand for new primary school places across the parish","C":"A rapid increase in the number of people who are looking for full-time work locally","D":"Increased need for university places within the immediate local area"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-CONNECTIVITY","stem":"Crossrail cut journey times across London and property values rose sharply near its new stations before it even opened. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Journey times matter to businesses but never influence any residential choice","B":"Property values in London are determined entirely by the age of the housing","C":"Rail investment has no effect on where people choose to live or work","D":"Transport investment reshapes accessibility, and land values follow it"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Accessibility is what land value responds to. A station that puts central London within thirty minutes changes what a location is worth for housing and offices alike, and the market prices that in as soon as the scheme is certain. It is the same mechanism as the Jubilee Line Extension in Docklands, at a larger scale.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is improved transport connectivity often described as a driver of regeneration rather than just a convenience?","options":{"A":"Because new railway lines are always built through the poorest districts","B":"Because better access attracts investment, businesses and residents to an area","C":"Because faster journeys reduce the total population of the whole of the city region","D":"Because it removes the need for any other investment in a run-down area"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-POPULATION-PATTERNS","stem":"London's population has a notably large share of people aged 20 to 35 compared with the UK average. What best explains this?","options":{"A":"Internal migration of young adults arriving for work, study and opportunity","B":"People aged over 60 are not permitted to live within the Greater London area","C":"Life expectancy in London is far lower than it is elsewhere in the country","D":"London has a much higher birth rate than every other region of the UK"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Internal migration is selective by age. Young adults are the most mobile group, moving to London for universities, graduate jobs and the range of opportunity a large labour market offers. Many later leave when they have children and want space, which is why London's pyramid bulges in the twenties and thins again in the forties.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do many people leave London in their thirties and forties despite moving there earlier?","options":{"A":"People are required to leave the capital once they have started a family","B":"Employment opportunities in London disappear entirely after the age of thirty","C":"Housing costs and space push families out towards commuter towns","D":"Transport connections from London to other regions become unavailable"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-STRUCTURAL-CHANGE","stem":"A former industrial town replaces lost factory jobs with distribution warehouse and call-centre work. A student calls this a complete recovery. Why is that too simple?","options":{"A":"Warehouse and call-centre jobs cannot legally be created in former industrial towns","B":"Replacement service jobs are often lower paid, part-time or on insecure contracts","C":"The new jobs employ exactly the same number of people as the factories did","D":"Service-sector employment always pays considerably more than manufacturing work"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Counting jobs is not the same as counting job quality. Manufacturing work was often unionised, full-time and skilled, with wages that supported a household. Much of what replaced it is lower paid and less secure, so a town can restore its employment rate while household incomes and stability stay below what they were.","reforge":{"stem":"Which evidence would best test whether a town has genuinely recovered from deindustrialisation?","options":{"A":"The total number of business premises currently standing in the town","B":"The number of people who describe the town as having recovered","C":"The number of new shops that have opened up in the town centre this year","D":"Median household income and job security, not just the employment rate"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-INVESTMENT","stem":"A Japanese car manufacturer opens a UK plant employing 4,000 people. Which is the strongest reason to treat this as a mixed outcome for the UK?","options":{"A":"Foreign firms are simply not permitted to employ any UK workers in their own factories","B":"The plant will certainly employ more people than any UK-owned firm could","C":"The plant brings jobs and skills, but decisions and profits sit outside the UK","D":"Foreign direct investment has no measurable effect on UK employment levels"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"FDI delivers real gains: jobs, training, supply-chain contracts and export earnings. The vulnerability is control. A decision made at headquarters abroad, responding to costs or trade rules elsewhere, can close the plant regardless of local performance, and the profit is repatriated rather than reinvested locally by default.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a region that depends heavily on one large foreign-owned employer considered vulnerable?","options":{"A":"A decision made abroad can remove the region's main employer at short notice","B":"Large employers always pay lower wages than small local firms do","C":"Such regions are barred from attracting any further outside investment","D":"Foreign-owned firms are legally obliged to close after a fixed number of years"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"},{"id":"GCSE-UKHUMAN-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-UKHUMAN","tag":"MC-GG-UKH-CULTURAL-MIGRATION","stem":"Migrants make up a disproportionate share of NHS and construction staff in London. What does this best illustrate?","options":{"A":"Migration to the UK is driven entirely by the availability of public services","B":"Migrants only ever work in the public sector rather than in private businesses","C":"Every sector of the London economy has an identical proportion of migrant workers in it","D":"Migration fills specific labour shortages that the resident workforce leaves unmet"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Labour migration responds to demand. Where a sector cannot recruit enough workers domestically, whether through training bottlenecks, pay or an ageing workforce, migration fills the gap. That is why the effect is concentrated in particular sectors rather than spread evenly, and why restricting it has sector-specific consequences.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does an ageing UK population strengthen the economic argument for inward migration?","options":{"A":"Migration causes the total UK population to fall back to its earlier level","B":"Migrants of working age help support a growing retired dependent population","C":"An ageing population reduces demand for healthcare and social care workers","D":"Older residents are legally required to be replaced by new arrivals each year"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ukhuman"}]},"GCSE-GEO-ENQUIRY":{"label":"Geographical Enquiry & Fieldwork Skills","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-01","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A student's fieldwork hypothesis reads: 'The river is different at different points.' Why is this a poor hypothesis?","options":{"A":"It states no direction of change","B":"Hypotheses are not allowed in GCSE fieldwork","C":"It should instead be written as a question","D":"It is too short to be a hypothesis"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-ENQ-HYPOTHESIS-DESIGN","scaffold":"A hypothesis must be clear, directional and measurable — a statement that can be tested and shown to be true or false, e.g. 'River discharge increases with distance from the source' or 'Environmental quality increases with distance from the new road.' 'The river is different' names no variable and no direction, so it can never be tested. Aims and hypotheses are often built from an established theory or model, such as Bradshaw's model for rivers.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these is correctly written as a testable geographical hypothesis?","options":{"A":"Environmental quality is a useful thing to measure","B":"The city centre and the edge of the city are not the same","C":"Environmental quality improves with distance from the CBD","D":"Some parts of the urban area are nicer than others"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-02","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"Why might a fieldwork enquiry use a null hypothesis alongside its main hypothesis?","options":{"A":"It replaces the need for a conclusion","B":"It removes bias from interpretation","C":"It is only used when the equipment fails","D":"It guarantees the results will be reliable"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-ENQ-NULL-HYPOTHESIS","scaffold":"A null hypothesis is the opposite statement to the hypothesis, e.g. hypothesis: 'Environmental quality improves with distance from the CBD'; null hypothesis: 'Environmental quality does not improve with distance from the CBD.' Testing both prevents a student from unconsciously ignoring evidence that doesn't fit — if the hypothesis can't be proved, the null hypothesis must be accepted instead.","reforge":{"stem":"A student only tests their hypothesis and ignores the null hypothesis. What is the risk?","options":{"A":"The sample size automatically becomes too small","B":"The fieldwork becomes illegal under exam board rules","C":"There is no risk — null hypotheses are optional decoration","D":"They may read ambiguous data in their own favour"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-03","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"Which is an example of PRIMARY data in a fieldwork enquiry?","options":{"A":"Census population data downloaded from the ONS website","B":"An old newspaper article about a flood event","C":"Width and depth measured by the student that day","D":"Historical weather records from the Met Office"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-ENQ-PRIMARY-DATA","scaffold":"Primary data is collected first-hand by the student: questionnaires, river measurements, photographs, interviews. Secondary data is collected by someone else and reused: census data, weather records, old photographs, maps, newspaper articles. Primary data is reliable and specific to the enquiry but time-consuming; secondary data is quick and free but not specific to the enquiry and its quality can't be controlled.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main limitation of relying on secondary data in a fieldwork enquiry?","options":{"A":"It may be dated and collected for another aim","B":"It is illegal to use in GCSE coursework","C":"It is always more expensive than primary data","D":"It cannot be presented on a graph"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-04","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A geography class wants to sample environmental quality at sites every 200 metres along a transect. Which sampling method are they using?","options":{"A":"Random sampling — sites selected using random number generation","B":"Opportunistic sampling — sites selected based on whatever is accessible","C":"Stratified sampling — sites selected to represent subsets of the population","D":"Systematic sampling — sites selected at regular intervals"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-ENQ-SYSTEMATIC-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Systematic = regular intervals (e.g. every 200m or every tenth person) — quick and covers the whole area, but not every site has an equal chance of selection, which can introduce bias. Random = numbered grid squares chosen via random number generator — least biased, but may miss whole areas or land on inaccessible sites. Stratified = proportional to known subsets, e.g. if 10% of the population is over 65, 10% of questionnaire respondents should be too.","reforge":{"stem":"A researcher wants their questionnaire sample to include the same proportion of over-65s as the wider population. Which sampling method are they using?","options":{"A":"Opportunistic sampling","B":"Stratified sampling","C":"Random sampling","D":"Systematic sampling"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-05","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"Why might a student use opportunistic sampling instead of their planned sampling strategy?","options":{"A":"Use the nearest accessible point to the planned site","B":"Opportunistic sampling always gives more accurate results","C":"It removes the need for a risk assessment","D":"It is required by the exam board for all fieldwork"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-ENQ-OPPORTUNISTIC-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Opportunistic sampling is a practical compromise, not a preferred method — used when the planned site (from systematic, random or stratified sampling) cannot be reached, e.g. private land, dangerous terrain, restricted access. The rule is to stay as close as possible to the originally selected site so the sample remains as representative as it can be.","reforge":{"stem":"A river sample site selected by random sampling turns out to be on private land with no public access. What is the best response?","options":{"A":"Trespass to reach the exact site","B":"Select a completely different river","C":"Sample the nearest accessible point","D":"Abandon the enquiry entirely"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-06","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A student conducts an environmental quality survey on their own using a bipolar scale from -3 to +3. What is the main weakness of this method?","options":{"A":"Bipolar scales cannot be used for environmental quality","B":"It is subjective — one person's judgement decides","C":"It produces qualitative data that cannot be compared between sites","D":"It requires expensive specialist equipment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-ENQ-ENVIRONMENTAL-QUALITY-SCALE","scaffold":"Environmental Quality Surveys (EQS) use a sliding or bipolar scale (e.g. -3 to +3) to judge features like litter, graffiti, and green space — they produce quantitative data, but the score reflects one person's opinion, making them subjective. This is reduced by completing the survey in small groups to reach a consensus, or by combining scores from multiple students and using the mode.","reforge":{"stem":"How can the subjectivity of an environmental quality survey best be reduced?","options":{"A":"Only survey sites during the day","B":"Use a wider bipolar scale, e.g. -10 to +10","C":"Replace the survey with a single photograph","D":"Score in groups, or take the mode"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-07","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A student wants to show the relationship between river width and discharge collected at eight sample sites. Which graph is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"A choropleth map, since it shows spatial variation by shading","B":"A rose diagram, since it uses compass direction","C":"A scattergraph, which shows a two-variable relationship","D":"A pie chart, since it shows proportions of a whole"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-ENQ-SCATTER-CORRELATION","scaffold":"Scattergraphs plot two variables against each other, with points left unconnected — a best-fit line reveals the relationship, and outliers/anomalies stand out clearly. Pie charts show proportions of a whole; rose diagrams plot directional data like wind or noise; choropleth maps show spatial variation using shading bands. Match graph type to the type of comparison being made — that's the recurring exam skill.","reforge":{"stem":"Which limitation applies specifically to scattergraphs?","options":{"A":"They only show two variables and cannot be labelled","B":"They can only be used with qualitative data","C":"They cannot show anomalies or outliers","D":"They must always show a perfect positive correlation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-08","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A choropleth map is used to show environmental quality scores across zones of a town. What is its main limitation?","options":{"A":"It cannot display more than two categories of data","B":"It only works for numerical data with negative values","C":"It cannot be drawn using GIS software","D":"It implies an abrupt change at each zone boundary"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-ENQ-CHOROPLETH","scaffold":"Choropleth maps shade areas according to a value range using one colour in different intensities — good for showing a clear overall spatial pattern, but the sharp boundary between two shades implies a sudden change that doesn't exist on the ground, and variation within a single shaded zone is hidden. Proportional symbol maps avoid this by placing a scaled symbol at each exact point instead.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a proportional symbols map be preferred over a choropleth map for showing traffic counts at exact sample sites?","options":{"A":"It can display continuous surfaces better than any other method","B":"It shows data at the exact point, without false zones","C":"It is quicker to construct than a choropleth map","D":"It removes the need for a scale or key"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-09","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"In a data set of environmental quality scores: 2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5, 5, 8, what is the mode?","options":{"A":"5","B":"8","C":"4","D":"3"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-ENQ-CENTRAL-TENDENCY","scaffold":"Mode = the value that occurs most often. Here 5 appears three times, more than any other value. Mean = sum ÷ count = 35 ÷ 8 = 4.375. Median = middle value when ranked = average of the 4th and 5th values = (4+5)/2 = 4.5. Range = highest − lowest = 8 − 2 = 6. Learn to calculate all three measures of central tendency plus range, since exam questions often give a small raw data set and ask for one specific measure.","reforge":{"stem":"Using the same data set (2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5, 5, 8), what is the range?","options":{"A":"3","B":"8","C":"6","D":"5"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-10","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A student finds one river velocity reading is far lower than all the others recorded nearby with similar gradients. What should they do with this anomaly?","options":{"A":"Replace it with the mean of the other readings without comment","B":"Identify it, describe it and try to explain the cause","C":"Automatically delete it from the data set before graphing","D":"Ignore it since anomalies never affect conclusions"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-ENQ-ANOMALIES","scaffold":"Anomalies are results that don't fit the expected pattern or trend. Good practice is to identify them, describe how they differ, and suggest an explanation — equipment fault, human error in reading/recording, an unusual local feature (e.g. an obstruction affecting flow), or a genuine but unexpected geographical process. Simply deleting inconvenient data undermines the validity of the whole enquiry.","reforge":{"stem":"A river velocity anomaly is later found to be caused by a fallen branch obstructing flow at that exact spot. How should this be treated in the conclusion?","options":{"A":"As evidence that all velocity data is unreliable","B":"As proof that the hypothesis is entirely wrong","C":"As data that should never have been collected","D":"An anomaly from a temporary obstruction"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-11","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"How should what an 'evaluation' of a fieldwork enquiry should include be understood here?","options":{"A":"A description of what the weather was like on the day of the fieldwork","B":"A simple restatement of the results without any comment on accuracy","C":"Method limits, reliability, and how to improve it","D":"A list of all the equipment used with no comment on its effectiveness"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-ENQ-EVALUATION-CONCLUSION","scaffold":"Evaluation ≠ conclusion. Conclusion returns to the hypothesis and states whether it was supported. Evaluation critically examines the enquiry itself: were sample sites accessible, was the sample size big enough, was the time available enough, were the questions/methods appropriate, was there human or equipment error, did the weather or unforeseen issues (roadworks, unusual river flow) affect results — and what would you change if repeating it.","reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'My hypothesis was supported because velocity increased downstream.' Is this a conclusion or an evaluation?","options":{"A":"A conclusion — it returns to the hypothesis","B":"Both, since conclusions and evaluations are the same thing","C":"An evaluation — it discusses the reliability of the methods used","D":"Neither — it is a restatement of the aim"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-12","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A student used a float to measure river velocity but suggests a flow meter would have been better. Why?","options":{"A":"Flow meters remove the need for repeat readings","B":"A float always overestimates velocity","C":"A float can only be used in still water","D":"A flow meter avoids reaction-time error"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-ENQ-FLOW-METER","scaffold":"This is a standard 'suggest an improvement' exam answer. Using a float: measure a set distance (e.g. 10m), time how long the float takes with a stopwatch, repeat three times per position for a mean. Human reaction time in starting/stopping the stopwatch introduces error. A flow meter placed at least 3cm below the surface gives a direct velocity reading, removing that source of error — though it is more expensive and needs training to use correctly.","reforge":{"stem":"Which improvement to a river fieldwork enquiry would most directly increase the reliability of the conclusions?","options":{"A":"Removing the health and safety risk assessment","B":"Increase the sample size and repeat readings","C":"Only measuring the river at a single site","D":"Reducing the number of sites"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about hypotheses.","template":"A good hypothesis must be clear, ___ and ___, so that it can be tested against the data collected. A ___ hypothesis is the opposite statement, used to reduce bias.","blanks":["directional","measurable","null"],"bank":["directional","measurable","null","random","vague","secondary"],"tag":"MC-ENQ-HYPOTHESIS-DESIGN","scaffold":"'The river is different' fails all three tests. 'Discharge increases with distance downstream' passes: it names a direction (increases) and a measurable variable (discharge). Testing the null hypothesis alongside it guards against selectively interpreting ambiguous results.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about data types.","template":"Data collected first-hand by the student is called ___ data, while data collected by someone else and reused, such as census figures, is called ___ data.","blanks":["primary","secondary"],"bank":["primary","secondary","qualitative","quantitative","random","stratified"],"tag":"MC-ENQ-PRIMARY-DATA","scaffold":"Primary: questionnaires, river measurements, photographs taken by the student. Secondary: census data, Met Office weather records, old maps and newspaper articles. Primary is specific and controlled but time-consuming; secondary is quick and free but not tailored to the enquiry.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about sampling methods.","template":"___ sampling selects sites at regular intervals, ___ sampling gives every site an equal chance of selection, and ___ sampling ensures subsets of the population are represented proportionally.","blanks":["Systematic","random","stratified"],"bank":["Systematic","random","stratified","opportunistic","biased","secondary"],"tag":"MC-ENQ-SYSTEMATIC-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Systematic = fixed intervals, quick, covers the whole area, but not fully unbiased. Random = numbered grid squares via random number generator, least biased overall. Stratified = proportional representation of known subsets, e.g. age groups matching the census.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about measures of central tendency.","template":"The ___ is calculated by adding all values and dividing by the number of values. The ___ is the middle value when data is ranked in order. The ___ is the most frequently occurring value.","blanks":["mean","median","mode"],"bank":["mean","median","mode","range","interquartile","anomaly"],"tag":"MC-ENQ-CENTRAL-TENDENCY","scaffold":"Mean = total ÷ count. Median = middle value once ranked (average the two middle values if there is an even number of data points). Mode = most frequent value. Range = highest − lowest — a measure of dispersion (spread), not central tendency.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-FB-05","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about fieldwork evaluation.","template":"Evaluation should identify problems with the data collection ___, suggest ___ that could have been made, and assess how ___ the conclusions were.","blanks":["methods","improvements","reliable"],"bank":["methods","improvements","reliable","hypotheses","graphs","aims"],"tag":"MC-ENQ-EVALUATION-CONCLUSION","scaffold":"Evaluation covers: accessibility of sites, sample size, duration of data collection, appropriateness of methods/questions, equipment issues, human error, and unforeseen problems on the day. It then suggests concrete improvements — larger sample, more measurements, better equipment, repeat visits — before judging overall reliability.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-18","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A student surveys only people entering a shopping centre to investigate residents' views of urban regeneration. What is the main sampling problem?","options":{"A":"Shopping-centre surveys always produce too many numerical answers","B":"The sample may over-represent shoppers and exclude non-users, creating bias","C":"A questionnaire cannot investigate people's views","D":"The sample is automatically stratified because everyone enters through the same doors at similar times"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-ENQ-SAMPLING-BIAS","scaffold":"Sampling at one shopping centre is convenient, but it may exclude non-shoppers, older residents and people who avoid the area. The results may not represent the target population. Sampling several locations and times would improve coverage.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change would most improve the representativeness of this urban survey?","options":{"A":"Ask only the first five people who pass the entrance","B":"Use one respondent's answer as the community average","C":"Remove all questions about regeneration","D":"Sample several locations, times and resident groups"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-19","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A student records traffic counts at 08:00 on one weekday and concludes that the road is always congested. Which evaluation is strongest?","options":{"A":"The conclusion is fully reliable because 08:00 is a precise time","B":"Traffic counts cannot be used in geographical enquiry","C":"One count cannot represent daily or weekly variation","D":"The student should remove all numerical data from the investigation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-ENQ-EVALUATION-CONCLUSION","scaffold":"A single time-point can capture a morning peak but cannot establish that congestion is constant. Traffic varies by day, weather, holidays, incidents and school terms. Repeating counts at different times and on more than one day would test whether the pattern is typical.","reforge":{"stem":"Which evidence would best test whether a morning traffic peak is typical?","options":{"A":"Repeat counts on several weekdays and off-peak","B":"Count vehicles once at midnight","C":"Use a photograph of the road with no time recorded","D":"Ask one driver whether the road is usually busy"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-20","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A choropleth map shades areas according to average income. Which design choice is most important for interpreting it fairly?","options":{"A":"Use as many decorative colours as possible","B":"Use one identical shade for every area to avoid bias","C":"Leave out the map title because the pattern should speak for itself","D":"Choose sensible class intervals and a clear key"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-ENQ-CHOROPLETH","scaffold":"A choropleth's pattern depends heavily on its classification. Sensible, clearly labelled class intervals and a readable key allow the reader to interpret differences. Arbitrary intervals can exaggerate or hide spatial patterns.","reforge":{"stem":"What could happen if a map uses very wide income classes for some values and very narrow classes for others without explanation?","options":{"A":"The map becomes automatically more accurate because it has fewer labels","B":"It can exaggerate some differences and hide others","C":"Class intervals have no effect on a choropleth pattern","D":"Every area must appear in a separate colour"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-21","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"A fieldwork group finds that two variables have a strong correlation. What conclusion is scientifically safest?","options":{"A":"It suggests a relationship, but may be coincidence","B":"The variables are unrelated because correlation is never useful","C":"The first variable definitely caused the second","D":"The data can be accepted without checking sample size or anomalies"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-ENQ-SCATTER-CORRELATION","scaffold":"Correlation shows that two variables change together; it does not by itself prove causation. A good conclusion describes the relationship, considers anomalies and sample size, and identifies other possible explanations.","reforge":{"stem":"Which additional step would make a correlation-based conclusion more convincing?","options":{"A":"Change the axis labels until the correlation looks stronger","B":"Delete results that do not fit the expected pattern","C":"Check sample size, anomalies and a plausible cause","D":"Treat the correlation as proof without considering other variables"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-22","spec":"GCSE-ENQUIRY","stem":"Which sampling method gives every member of a population an equal chance of selection?","options":{"A":"Systematic sampling","B":"Random sampling","C":"Convenience sampling","D":"Stratified sampling"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-ENQ-RANDOM-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Random sampling uses a method such as random numbers so each member has an equal chance of selection. Systematic and stratified methods can also be representative but use additional structure.","reforge":{"stem":"A researcher uses random numbers to choose households from a complete list. Which method is this?","options":{"A":"Systematic sampling","B":"Opportunistic sampling","C":"Quota sampling","D":"Random sampling"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-23","spec":"GCSE-ENQUIRY","stem":"What is a bar chart particularly useful for showing?","options":{"A":"A continuous relationship between two measurements","B":"The exact location of points using coordinates","C":"Comparisons between categories","D":"The cumulative total of a data set"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-ENQ-BAR-CHARTS","scaffold":"Bar charts compare separate categories, with gaps between bars. Line graphs are more suitable for continuous change, while scatter graphs show relationships between two variables.","reforge":{"stem":"A class wants to compare mean EQS scores at four separate sites. Which display is suitable?","options":{"A":"A bar chart","B":"A scatter graph","C":"A flow-line map","D":"A triangular graph"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-24","spec":"GCSE-ENQUIRY","stem":"Why can percentage change be useful when comparing fieldwork results?","options":{"A":"It removes all sampling error","B":"It proves that one variable caused another","C":"It replaces the need to record original values","D":"Compare relative change"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-ENQ-PERCENTAGE-CHANGE","scaffold":"Percentage change puts a change into context by comparing it with the starting value. This allows results with different scales to be compared, although the original values should still be reported.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might percentage change be preferred to raw change when comparing two sites?","options":{"A":"It makes all results identical","B":"Compare different starts","C":"It removes the need for a hypothesis","D":"It guarantees a reliable conclusion"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-25","spec":"GCSE-ENQUIRY","stem":"What does standard deviation describe?","options":{"A":"The spread of results around the mean","B":"The middle result in an ordered set","C":"The difference between the highest and lowest result","D":"The most common result"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-ENQ-VARIABILITY","scaffold":"Standard deviation measures how spread out results are around the mean. A smaller value means readings are more tightly clustered; a larger value means greater variation.","reforge":{"stem":"A data set has a large standard deviation. What does this suggest?","options":{"A":"Every reading is identical","B":"The median must equal the mean","C":"Spread around mean","D":"The sample contains no variation"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-26","spec":"GCSE-ENQUIRY","stem":"Why should fieldwork measurements be repeated?","options":{"A":"To prove the hypothesis","B":"To improve reliability","C":"To replace sampling","D":"To force results to the mean"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-ENQ-REPEAT-MEASUREMENTS","scaffold":"Repeating measurements helps identify anomalies and gives a more reliable average. It does not guarantee accuracy or prove a hypothesis, especially if the method itself is biased.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a main benefit of taking several velocity readings at one site?","options":{"A":"The site no longer needs to be described","B":"The equipment cannot produce errors","C":"The hypothesis becomes automatically true","D":"The mean is more reliable"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-27","spec":"GCSE-ENQUIRY","stem":"What does validity mean in fieldwork?","options":{"A":"The data was collected quickly","B":"The results contain a large sample","C":"Measures the intended variable","D":"The graph has a title and key"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-ENQ-VALIDITY","scaffold":"A valid method measures the intended variable. For example, a flow meter is more valid than a float for measuring mean river velocity because it is designed for that purpose.","reforge":{"stem":"A questionnaire asks about transport but only offers answers about housing. Which quality is weakest?","options":{"A":"Validity","B":"Reliability","C":"Sample size","D":"Precision"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-28","spec":"GCSE-ENQUIRY","stem":"Why is a scatter graph useful in geographical enquiry?","options":{"A":"It orders survey questions","B":"It locates land uses","C":"It totals each category","D":"Shows relationships"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-ENQ-SCATTER-CORRELATION","scaffold":"A scatter graph plots paired measurements for two variables and can show positive, negative or no correlation. Correlation alone does not prove causation.","reforge":{"stem":"A group compares river width with discharge at several sites. Which graph best shows the relationship?","options":{"A":"A pie chart","B":"Scatter graph","C":"A bar chart","D":"A choropleth map"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-29","spec":"GCSE-ENQUIRY","stem":"What is the purpose of a line of best fit on a scatter graph?","options":{"A":"To show the overall trend","B":"To calculate the sample size","C":"To replace the original data","D":"To show every reading exactly"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-ENQ-SCATTER-CORRELATION","scaffold":"A line of best fit summarises the general pattern in paired data. It should not be forced through every point and does not prove that one variable causes the other.","reforge":{"stem":"A line of best fit slopes upwards. What does it indicate?","options":{"A":"Identical values","B":"No relationship","C":"Positive trend","D":"A biased sample"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-30","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"Which is primary data in a geographical enquiry?","options":{"A":"A national Census table downloaded online","B":"Measurements collected by the students","C":"A historical map published by an archive","D":"Rainfall normals from the Met Office"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-ENQ-PRIMARY-DATA","scaffold":"Primary data are collected first-hand for the investigation; secondary data were collected previously by another source.","reforge":{"stem":"Which source is secondary data?","options":{"A":"A field sketch drawn at the site","B":"Today's responses to a class questionnaire","C":"A student's beach-width measurement","D":"An Environment Agency flood-risk map"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-31","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"When is systematic sampling most appropriate?","options":{"A":"When every site must be chosen by chance","B":"When researchers select preferred results","C":"Regular-interval measurements","D":"When only convenient sites are available"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-ENQ-SYSTEMATIC-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Systematic sampling follows a consistent interval, such as every 10 metres or every fifth person.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one risk of systematic sampling?","options":{"A":"A repeating pattern may bias the sample","B":"It can never be repeated by another group","C":"It produces only qualitative information","D":"Researchers cannot record locations"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-32","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"Why use stratified sampling in a questionnaire?","options":{"A":"It removes every source of response bias","B":"It guarantees a very large sample","C":"It selects only the easiest respondents","D":"Representation of key groups"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-ENQ-STRATIFIED-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Stratification ensures relevant groups are included in proportions or numbers suited to the enquiry.","reforge":{"stem":"Which sample is stratified by age?","options":{"A":"Interview the first ten people seen","B":"Select respondents from each age band","C":"Choose names using a random generator","D":"Question only people near a college"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-33","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"What is a bipolar environmental-quality scale?","options":{"A":"A rating between opposite descriptions","B":"A survey using only yes or no answers","C":"A map showing two different settlements","D":"A measurement with no ordered values"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-ENQ-ENVIRONMENTAL-QUALITY-SCALE","scaffold":"A bipolar scale places observations between contrasting endpoints, such as very clean and very littered.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should every group use the same bipolar criteria?","options":{"A":"It removes the need for judgement","B":"It ensures every site receives the same score","C":"It improves consistency between observers","D":"It converts opinions into national Census data"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-34","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"What is the purpose of a pilot study?","options":{"A":"To replace the full investigation permanently","B":"To test and improve the method","C":"To guarantee the hypothesis is correct","D":"To remove all need for risk assessment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-ENQ-PILOT-STUDY","scaffold":"A small trial can reveal unclear questions, unsuitable intervals, equipment problems or missing recording categories.","reforge":{"stem":"A pilot questionnaire confuses several respondents. What should happen next?","options":{"A":"Keep the wording so results remain unclear","B":"Discard the whole enquiry immediately","C":"Tell respondents which answer to select","D":"Revise the questions before full sampling"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-35","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"What does the interquartile range measure?","options":{"A":"The difference between maximum and minimum only","B":"The most frequent value in a dataset","C":"The spread of the middle half of data","D":"The total of all values divided by frequency"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-ENQ-VARIABILITY","scaffold":"IQR equals upper quartile minus lower quartile and is less affected by extreme values than the full range.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might IQR be preferred when data contain outliers?","options":{"A":"Extreme values have less influence on it","B":"It always equals the arithmetic mean","C":"It identifies the cause of every anomaly","D":"It converts qualitative data into maps"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-36","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"When is the median especially useful?","options":{"A":"When categories have no meaningful order","B":"When the highest value is required","C":"When every value must be added together","D":"When extreme values distort the mean"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-ENQ-CENTRAL-TENDENCY","scaffold":"The median is the middle ordered value and is robust when a distribution is skewed or contains outliers.","reforge":{"stem":"House prices include one exceptionally expensive property. Which average is least distorted?","options":{"A":"The range of all property prices","B":"The median house price","C":"The maximum observed price","D":"The arithmetic mean"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-37","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"When is a choropleth map appropriate?","options":{"A":"Shading areas by a standardised rate","B":"Plotting change continuously through time","C":"Displaying exact routes between settlements","D":"Showing individual point locations without areas"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-ENQ-CHOROPLETH","scaffold":"Choropleths compare areas using rates or densities; raw totals can mislead where area or population differs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which dataset best suits a choropleth map?","options":{"A":"Locations of individual bus stops","B":"Daily temperature through one month","C":"Population density by local authority","D":"Migration flows between two countries"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-38","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"What is a key requirement for proportional-symbol maps?","options":{"A":"Every symbol must be identical in size","B":"Symbol size must follow a clear scale","C":"Symbols should hide the mapped locations","D":"The largest value uses the smallest symbol"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-ENQ-PROPORTIONAL-SYMBOLS","scaffold":"Symbol area or size represents magnitude, so a legend is essential for accurate comparison.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one limitation of large proportional symbols?","options":{"A":"They remove the need for a base map","B":"They always indicate population density","C":"They cannot represent quantitative values","D":"They may overlap and obscure places"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-39","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"Why should totals be standardised before comparing areas with different populations?","options":{"A":"Standardising proves one factor caused the pattern","B":"Raw totals already account for population size","C":"Rates allow a fairer comparison","D":"Every area must be given the same total"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-ENQ-STANDARDISATION","scaffold":"Converting totals to rates or percentages controls for different population sizes and makes area comparisons more meaningful.","reforge":{"stem":"Two wards have different populations. How should crime totals be compared most fairly?","options":{"A":"Calculate a crime rate for each ward","B":"Use the larger raw total as the higher risk","C":"Remove the population figures from the data","D":"Assume both wards have equal populations"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-40","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"What should a student do with an anomalous fieldwork result?","options":{"A":"Delete it without recording any reason","B":"Use it as proof the method was perfect","C":"Change it to match the expected trend","D":"Check it and consider possible causes"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-ENQ-ANOMALIES","scaffold":"An anomaly may reveal error or genuine local variation; it should be checked, retained transparently and discussed.","reforge":{"stem":"A repeated measurement remains unusually high. What is the best interpretation?","options":{"A":"It must be a recording mistake","B":"Genuine local variation","C":"It should be replaced by the mean","D":"The entire enquiry becomes invalid"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-41","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"What distinguishes reliability from validity?","options":{"A":"Consistency versus relevance","B":"Validity means results can be repeated exactly","C":"The terms have identical meanings","D":"Reliable data always prove causation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-ENQ-RELIABILITY-VALIDITY","scaffold":"Repeated similar results show reliability, while validity asks whether the method actually measures the intended concept.","reforge":{"stem":"A noise survey gives similar readings but is used to measure housing quality. What is the main concern?","options":{"A":"The data must be qualitative","B":"The readings cannot be reliable","C":"The measure may lack validity","D":"The sample is necessarily random"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-42","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"Why use triangulation in an enquiry?","options":{"A":"It reduces evidence to one preferred source","B":"Different methods can corroborate findings","C":"It ensures all data are quantitative","D":"It prevents conclusions changing"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-ENQ-TRIANGULATION","scaffold":"Combining observations, measurements, questionnaires and secondary sources can strengthen confidence when patterns agree.","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination best triangulates urban quality of life?","options":{"A":"Three identical photographs from one point","B":"Only one resident interview","C":"A national map with no local evidence","D":"EQS, questionnaires and Census data"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-43","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"Which practice makes a questionnaire more ethical?","options":{"A":"Collect names when they are unnecessary","B":"Pressure people to answer every item","C":"Gain consent and protect anonymity","D":"Hide the purpose of the investigation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-ENQ-ETHICS","scaffold":"Participants should understand the task, choose whether to take part and have unnecessary personal information protected.","reforge":{"stem":"What should a student do if a respondent declines?","options":{"A":"Thank them and do not pressure them","B":"Record invented answers on their behalf","C":"Photograph them without permission","D":"Ask for private details before leaving"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"},{"id":"GCSE-ENQ-44","spec":"GCSE-ENQ","stem":"What makes a fieldwork conclusion well evidenced?","options":{"A":"It repeats the hypothesis without results","B":"It describes equipment instead of patterns","C":"It ignores findings that disagree","D":"It links the claim to analysed data"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-ENQ-CONCLUSION-EVIDENCE","scaffold":"A strong conclusion answers the enquiry using specific processed results and acknowledges anomalies or limitations.","reforge":{"stem":"Which conclusion is strongest?","options":{"A":"The hypothesis is true because expected","B":"Four of five sites support it","C":"No result needs to be quoted in a conclusion","D":"The sites looked different to us"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-enq"}]},"GCSE-GEO-RIVERFIELD":{"label":"River Fieldwork — River Darent","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-RVF-01","spec":"GCSE-RVF","stem":"A class investigates the River Darent at three sites: Westerham (near the source), Park Farm, and Lullingstone Roman Villa (further downstream). Which hypothesis links this to Bradshaw's model?","options":{"A":"Width and discharge increase downstream","B":"Channel width decreases with distance downstream","C":"Discharge is unrelated to distance from the source","D":"Velocity is identical at all three sites"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-BRADSHAW-TRENDS","scaffold":"Bradshaw's model predicts that as a river moves from source to mouth, width, depth, velocity, discharge and load size generally increase, while gradient and bed material size decrease. Moving from Westerham (upper course, closer to source) through Park Farm to Lullingstone (further downstream), the expected pattern is increasing width and discharge — this is the standard hypothesis for a downstream-change enquiry on the Darent.","reforge":{"stem":"According to Bradshaw's model, which variable should DECREASE between Westerham and Lullingstone Roman Villa?","options":{"A":"Cross-sectional area","B":"Discharge","C":"Gradient","D":"Channel width"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-02","spec":"GCSE-RVF","stem":"At each River Darent site, students measure width using a tape measure. What is the correct technique?","options":{"A":"Measure only the deepest channel, ignoring the banks","B":"Bank to bank at the waterline, with the tape taut","C":"Estimate the width visually without any equipment","D":"Measure the widest point of the entire valley, including the floodplain"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-MEASUREMENT-EQUIPMENT","scaffold":"Width is measured bank-to-bank at the point where dry land meets water. The tape must be held taut and must not touch the water's surface, since a sagging or wet tape would distort the reading and reduce reliability. This is one of the two starting measurements (with depth) for almost every river enquiry.","reforge":{"stem":"Why must the tape measure used for river width be kept taut and clear of the water surface?","options":{"A":"It has no effect on accuracy, only on speed","B":"It prevents the tape from being swept downstream","C":"It is a legal requirement under health and safety law","D":"A sagging tape gives an inaccurate width"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-03","spec":"GCSE-RVF","stem":"When measuring river depth across the channel at Park Farm, why should the metre rule be held with its flat side facing the banks (i.e. edge-on to the flow)?","options":{"A":"It makes the rule easier to read from the riverbank","B":"It prevents the rule from being seen by fish","C":"It disturbs the water level less at that point","D":"It has no effect on measurement accuracy"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-MEASUREMENT-EQUIPMENT","scaffold":"Placing the metre rule flat-side against the flow (rather than broad-side, which would dam the water slightly and raise the level around it) reduces its impact on the water height, giving a more accurate depth reading. Depth is measured at regular intervals across the width to build a full cross-sectional picture and calculate mean depth for the discharge formula.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is depth measured at several regular points across the river's width, rather than just once in the centre?","options":{"A":"One central reading misses how depth varies across","B":"It removes the need to measure width separately","C":"It is required only for rivers wider than 5 metres","D":"Depth never varies across a channel, so multiple readings check for equipment error only"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-04","spec":"GCSE-RVF","stem":"At a Darent site, students measure velocity using a float over a 10m stretch, timing it with a stopwatch at the left bank, centre, and right bank. Why take readings at three positions across the width?","options":{"A":"Velocity is identical everywhere in a channel so this simply checks for errors","B":"Three positions are needed to calculate river width","C":"It is only done to save time compared to using a flow meter","D":"Velocity is fastest mid-channel and slower at the banks"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-SAMPLING-TECHNIQUE","scaffold":"Friction with the bed and banks slows water near the edges, so velocity is generally highest in the centre/thalweg and lowest near the banks. Taking three readings (left, centre, right) and repeating each three times allows a representative mean velocity to be calculated — a single reading anywhere would misrepresent the whole cross-section.","reforge":{"stem":"A group's float times at the left bank, centre and right bank of a Darent site are 22s, 14s, 21s over 10m. What does this pattern suggest?","options":{"A":"The float was defective, since velocity should be identical everywhere","B":"Highest in the centre, lower at both banks","C":"The measurements must be discarded as anomalies","D":"The river is flowing upstream at the banks"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-05","spec":"GCSE-RVF","stem":"A group calculates cross-sectional area as 1.8 m² and mean velocity as 0.4 m/s at a Darent site. What is the discharge?","options":{"A":"0.72 m³/s","B":"2.20 m³/s","C":"1.44 m³/s","D":"7.20 m³/s"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-DISCHARGE-GEOMETRY","scaffold":"Discharge (m³/s) = cross-sectional area (m²) × velocity (m/s). Cross-sectional area = width × mean depth. Here: 1.8 × 0.4 = 0.72 m³/s. Always show both formulas and the substitution in an exam answer — method marks are available even if the final figure is wrong.","reforge":{"stem":"A river site has a width of 3.5m and mean depth of 0.6m. What is the cross-sectional area?","options":{"A":"4.1 m²","B":"0.6 m²","C":"2.1 m²","D":"1.05 m²"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-06","spec":"GCSE-RVF","stem":"Students measure the river's gradient at Westerham using a clinometer and two ranging poles set 10m apart. Why is gradient a useful measurement to link to Bradshaw's model?","options":{"A":"Gradient can only be measured at the river's source","B":"Gradient usually falls downstream, testing the model","C":"Gradient has no relationship to any other river variable","D":"Gradient always stays the same throughout a river's course"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-BRADSHAW-TRENDS","scaffold":"Bradshaw's model predicts gradient decreases from source to mouth as the river erodes vertically less and laterally more. On the Darent, comparing gradient at Westerham (steeper, upper course) against Lullingstone (gentler, lower course) directly tests this. Method: place ranging poles 0m and 10m apart, use the clinometer to measure the angle/height difference between them.","reforge":{"stem":"If the Darent's gradient is much steeper at Westerham than at Lullingstone Roman Villa, what would Bradshaw's model predict about velocity?","options":{"A":"Velocity and gradient have no relationship in Bradshaw's model","B":"Velocity must always be highest wherever gradient is steepest","C":"Velocity can only be measured where gradient is zero","D":"Efficiency downstream can offset the gentler gradient"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-07","spec":"GCSE-RVF","stem":"Which of these is a river-specific health and safety risk that should appear in a risk assessment for fieldwork on the Darent?","options":{"A":"Risk of sunburn only, since rivers have no other hazards","B":"There are no additional risks compared to classroom-based work","C":"Weil's disease, managed by covering wounds","D":"Risk of altitude sickness"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-FIELDWORK-SAFETY","scaffold":"River-specific risks: slippery rocks (sturdy footwear with grip), Weil's disease from contaminated water (wash hands, cover cuts, use anti-bacterial gel), weather (check forecast, sun protection), working in an unfamiliar place (work in groups, carry maps/phones, hi-vis). A good risk assessment names the specific hazard, who it affects, and precisely how it is managed — not just 'be careful'.","reforge":{"stem":"A risk assessment states: 'Hazard: slippery rocks. Risk: injury from slipping.' What is missing to complete it properly?","options":{"A":"How the risk is managed, such as sturdy footwear","B":"A description of what the rocks look like","C":"The exact GPS coordinates of every rock","D":"Nothing — hazard and risk are sufficient on their own"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-08","spec":"GCSE-RVF","stem":"A student wants to display the change in the Darent's channel shape from Westerham to Lullingstone. Which graph type is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"A pie chart showing proportions of the channel","B":"A choropleth map","C":"A rose diagram, since it uses compass direction","D":"A line graph showing channel shape"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-EVIDENCE-DISPLAY","scaffold":"A river cross-section is a special case of a line graph: although depth readings are not strictly continuous data, joining the plotted points shows the shape of the channel bed clearly — useful for comparing a narrow, deep upper-course channel (Westerham) against a wider, shallower or more uniform lower-course channel (Lullingstone).","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a scattergraph be more useful than a bar graph for showing the Darent's width against discharge across all sample sites?","options":{"A":"A scattergraph is always easier to draw than a bar graph","B":"A scattergraph shows the link between two variables","C":"A scattergraph removes the need to record discharge separately","D":"Bar graphs cannot use numerical data at all"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-09","spec":"GCSE-RVF","stem":"At one Darent site, velocity is unexpectedly much lower than at neighbouring sites with a similar gradient. What is the best way to treat this in the analysis?","options":{"A":"Name it as an anomaly and suggest a cause","B":"Assume the equipment is always correct and rewrite the hypothesis","C":"Ignore it since anomalies do not need to be discussed","D":"Delete the reading so it doesn't affect the graph"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-VALIDITY-EVALUATION","scaffold":"Anomalies should be named and explained, not hidden. Possible causes at a river site: debris or vegetation obstructing flow, a meander or pool slowing the current, human error in float timing, or a reading taken in an atypical spot. This is a very common 4-mark 'describe and explain an anomaly' exam question.","reforge":{"stem":"A 4-mark exam question shows a graph of the Darent's velocity with one clear anomaly. What must a full-marks answer include?","options":{"A":"A statement that the whole enquiry is invalid","B":"Only the numerical value of the anomaly","C":"How it differs from the trend, and why","D":"A new hypothesis unrelated to the anomaly"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-10","spec":"GCSE-RVF","stem":"After completing the River Darent enquiry, a student writes an evaluation. Which statement is a genuine evaluation point rather than a conclusion?","options":{"A":"'Bradshaw's model predicts width should increase downstream'","B":"'Reaction time may have skewed our float readings'","C":"'Our results matched the pattern predicted by Bradshaw's model'","D":"'Discharge increased from Westerham to Lullingstone, supporting our hypothesis'"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-VALIDITY-EVALUATION","scaffold":"B, C and D all restate findings or theory — that's conclusion territory. A critically examines the method itself and its limitation, which is what evaluation requires: was the equipment/method appropriate, how reliable is the data, what would you change next time (e.g. use a flow meter instead of a float to remove reaction-time error).","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these is the strongest evaluation point about sample size on the Darent enquiry?","options":{"A":"Sample size has no effect on the reliability of a conclusion","B":"The number of sites only matters for urban fieldwork, not river fieldwork","C":"Three sites is always enough for any river enquiry","D":"Three sites is too few to generalise the trend"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-RVF","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about measuring river discharge.","template":"Discharge is calculated using the formula: cross-sectional area × ___. Cross-sectional area itself is calculated as ___ × mean ___.","blanks":["velocity","width","depth"],"bank":["velocity","width","depth","gradient","time","length"],"tag":"MC-GG-RVF-DISCHARGE-GEOMETRY","scaffold":"Two-step calculation: (1) cross-sectional area (m²) = width (m) × mean depth (m); (2) discharge (m³/s) = cross-sectional area (m²) × velocity (m/s). Learn this chain — it's a guaranteed calculation question on river fieldwork.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-RVF","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Bradshaw's model applied to the River Darent.","template":"Moving downstream from Westerham to Lullingstone Roman Villa, Bradshaw's model predicts that channel width and ___ will increase, while ___ and the size of ___ material will decrease.","blanks":["discharge","gradient","bed"],"bank":["discharge","gradient","bed","velocity","depth","load"],"tag":"MC-GG-RVF-BRADSHAW-TRENDS","scaffold":"Bradshaw's model: width, depth, velocity, discharge and load quantity increase downstream; gradient, bed material size (load calibre) and friction decrease downstream. Using named sites (Westerham → Lullingstone) to test this model is exactly what an exam expects for a place-specific fieldwork answer.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-RVF","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about river velocity measurement technique.","template":"Using a float, students measure a set distance such as ___ metres, time how long the float takes with a ___, and repeat at each position three times to calculate a ___.","blanks":["10","stopwatch","mean"],"bank":["10","stopwatch","mean","clinometer","median","5"],"tag":"MC-GG-RVF-SAMPLING-TECHNIQUE","scaffold":"Standard float method: measure a known distance (commonly 10m), time the float's travel with a stopwatch, and repeat three times at each of the three cross-channel positions (left, centre, right) to calculate a reliable mean velocity for that site.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-11","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"How is river discharge calculated?","options":{"A":"Width ÷ gradient","B":"Area × velocity","C":"Rainfall ÷ basin area","D":"Velocity × lag time"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-DISCHARGE-GEOMETRY","scaffold":"Discharge is the volume of water passing a point each second. It is calculated as cross-sectional area multiplied by mean velocity, usually expressed in cubic metres per second.","reforge":{"stem":"A river has a cross-sectional area of 2 m² and mean velocity of 0.5 m/s. What is its discharge?","options":{"A":"0.25 m³/s","B":"2.5 m³/s","C":"4 m³/s","D":"1 m³/s"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-12","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Why does channel width usually increase downstream?","options":{"A":"The river always becomes steeper","B":"Sediment disappears from the channel","C":"Discharge usually increases","D":"The drainage basin becomes smaller"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-BRADSHAW-TRENDS","scaffold":"Tributaries add water and discharge generally increases downstream. Greater discharge and valley development allow the channel to become wider and deeper, although local geology and human activity can modify the pattern.","reforge":{"stem":"Which process helps explain a wider channel downstream?","options":{"A":"Tributaries add to discharge","B":"Less water enters the river","C":"The river loses all sediment","D":"The valley becomes narrower everywhere"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-13","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"What does a clinometer measure when used to investigate river gradient?","options":{"A":"River discharge","B":"The size of bedload","C":"The volume of rainfall","D":"The angle of slope"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-MEASUREMENT-EQUIPMENT","scaffold":"A clinometer measures the angle between a sight line and the horizontal. Repeated readings can be used to estimate the gradient between two points along the River Darent.","reforge":{"stem":"A student uses a clinometer between two ranging poles. What are they measuring?","options":{"A":"Channel depth","B":"Slope angle","C":"Sediment roundness","D":"Flood frequency"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-14","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Which hazard should be included in a risk assessment beside the River Darent?","options":{"A":"Slippery banks and deep water","B":"A change in population density","C":"A difference in rock type","D":"A lack of global trade"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-FIELDWORK-SAFETY","scaffold":"River fieldwork risks include slippery banks, deep or fast-flowing water, unstable ground and weather changes. A risk assessment should identify hazards and explain control measures such as staying away from the channel edge.","reforge":{"stem":"Which control would reduce risk during river fieldwork?","options":{"A":"Ignore the weather forecast","B":"Stand in the deepest flow","C":"Keep away from unstable banks","D":"Work alone at an unfamiliar site"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-15","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"What does Bradshaw's model predict about sediment downstream?","options":{"A":"It becomes larger and more angular","B":"It becomes smaller and rounder","C":"It disappears before reaching the middle course","D":"It changes into impermeable rock"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-SEDIMENT-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Bradshaw's model predicts that sediment becomes smaller and rounder downstream because repeated collisions cause attrition and smaller particles are transported more easily.","reforge":{"stem":"A pebble at a lower-course site is smoother than one near the source. Which process helps explain this?","options":{"A":"A shorter river long profile","B":"Less erosion at the source","C":"Glacial deposition in the channel","D":"Attrition during transport"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-16","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Which graph is most suitable for showing channel width at several sites along the River Darent?","options":{"A":"A pie chart","B":"A triangular graph","C":"A line graph","D":"A choropleth map"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-EVIDENCE-DISPLAY","scaffold":"A line graph can show how a continuous measurement changes between ordered sites along the river. Sites should be placed in downstream order and the axes should include units.","reforge":{"stem":"Which display best shows how Darent channel width changes from source to mouth?","options":{"A":"A line graph","B":"A pie chart","C":"A barometer","D":"A proportional symbol map"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-17","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Why take depth readings at regular intervals across a river channel?","options":{"A":"To make the channel steeper","B":"To remove all measurement error","C":"To measure air temperature","D":"Regular cross-section"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-SAMPLING-TECHNIQUE","scaffold":"Regular intervals provide a systematic cross-section of depth. The readings can be plotted to estimate cross-sectional area and help compare channel shape between sites.","reforge":{"stem":"A student measures depth every 0.5 m across a channel. What is the main advantage?","options":{"A":"It guarantees no anomalous reading","B":"Regular depth data","C":"It changes the river's flow","D":"It measures only the deepest point"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-18","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"What is a limitation of using a float to measure river velocity?","options":{"A":"It mainly measures surface speed","B":"It only works in dry channels","C":"It measures the river's discharge directly","D":"It cannot move with the current"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-SAMPLING-TECHNIQUE","scaffold":"A float travels at or near the surface, where velocity may be faster than the average through the whole channel. A flow meter can measure velocity at selected depths more directly.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a flow meter improve on a float method?","options":{"A":"It removes the need to measure distance","B":"It measures rainfall instead of water","C":"It can measure velocity below the surface","D":"It only records the fastest surface current"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-19","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"How can river cross-sectional area be estimated?","options":{"A":"Add velocity to channel gradient","B":"Multiply width by mean depth","C":"Divide sediment size by discharge","D":"Subtract wetted perimeter from width"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-DISCHARGE-GEOMETRY","scaffold":"A simple estimate multiplies channel width by mean depth, using several depth readings across the channel.","reforge":{"stem":"A channel is 4 m wide with mean depth 0.5 m. What is its cross-sectional area?","options":{"A":"8 square metres","B":"1.5 square metres","C":"0.125 square metres","D":"2 square metres"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-20","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"What is the wetted perimeter of a river channel?","options":{"A":"The straight-line width across the surface","B":"The distance from source to mouth","C":"The bed and banks touching water","D":"The total drainage-basin boundary"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-BRADSHAW-TRENDS","scaffold":"Wetted perimeter is the length of channel boundary in contact with flowing water across a cross-section.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a large wetted perimeter reduce velocity?","options":{"A":"More contact means more friction","B":"It removes all turbulence from the flow","C":"It makes the channel gradient steeper","D":"More water avoids the bed and banks"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-21","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"What does a larger hydraulic radius usually indicate?","options":{"A":"More channel contact for each unit of water","B":"A river without any measurable depth","C":"A lower discharge in every situation","D":"A more efficient channel shape"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-DISCHARGE-GEOMETRY","scaffold":"Hydraulic radius compares cross-sectional area with wetted perimeter; a larger value generally means proportionally less friction.","reforge":{"stem":"Which channel is likely to be most efficient?","options":{"A":"A shallow channel with rough exposed bed","B":"Deep channel, less relative friction","C":"A channel split around many boulders","D":"A dry channel with a wide floodplain"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-22","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Why can impermeable geology increase flood risk?","options":{"A":"Less infiltration produces more runoff","B":"It guarantees low drainage density","C":"It causes rainfall intensity to fall","D":"It stores all rainfall deep underground"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-FLOOD-PROCESSES","scaffold":"Impermeable rock limits infiltration, increasing overland flow and producing a faster river response.","reforge":{"stem":"Which geology is likely to produce the slowest storm response?","options":{"A":"Impermeable rock beneath urban paving","B":"Saturated clay with compacted soil","C":"Permeable chalk storage","D":"Bare rock on a steep valley side"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-23","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"How do saturated antecedent conditions affect fieldwork interpretation?","options":{"A":"They guarantee typical discharge for the season","B":"Recent rain may produce unusually high flow","C":"They prevent a float moving downstream","D":"Saturation makes channel width impossible to measure"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-FLOOD-PROCESSES","scaffold":"Measurements after prolonged rain may not represent normal conditions and should be compared with weather or gauge records.","reforge":{"stem":"What secondary data would best contextualise unusually high discharge?","options":{"A":"A national population pyramid","B":"A coastal geology photograph","C":"A retail land-use survey","D":"Recent rain and river levels"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-24","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Why can a circular drainage basin respond quickly to heavy rain?","options":{"A":"It always contains permeable limestone","B":"All slopes are necessarily gentle","C":"Runoff arrives near the outlet together","D":"It has no tributaries reaching the river"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-FLOOD-PROCESSES","scaffold":"Similar travel distances from many parts of a compact basin can synchronise tributary flows and raise peak discharge.","reforge":{"stem":"What field evidence helps assess basin shape and tributary pattern?","options":{"A":"Drainage-network topographic map","B":"A single pebble measured at one site","C":"A questionnaire about shopping habits","D":"A climate graph from another country"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-25","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Which is a useful qualitative method for assessing flood risk?","options":{"A":"Calculate discharge from width and velocity","B":"Measure depth at fixed intervals","C":"Record pebble length with callipers","D":"Annotate land use and bank condition"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-EVIDENCE-DISPLAY","scaffold":"Annotated photographs or field sketches can record vegetation, defences, buildings and land use that influence exposure and runoff.","reforge":{"stem":"What makes an annotated river photograph analytical?","options":{"A":"It is drawn without looking at the site","B":"Labels explain how features affect risk","C":"It includes only the date and weather","D":"It replaces every quantitative measurement"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-26","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Why use an Environment Agency flood-risk map?","options":{"A":"It provides wider secondary-risk context","B":"It shows exact future flood depths with certainty","C":"It removes the need for site observations","D":"It is primary data collected by the class"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-FLOOD-PROCESSES","scaffold":"Flood maps show modelled zones beyond the limited sites students can visit, but assumptions and dates should be considered.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one limitation of a flood-risk map?","options":{"A":"Maps cannot display areas or boundaries","B":"Flood risk never changes over time","C":"Modelled zones contain uncertainty","D":"Secondary data have no source date"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-27","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"How can ranging poles help measure river gradient?","options":{"A":"They calculate water chemistry automatically","B":"Fixed heights for angle measurement","C":"They record velocity below the water surface","D":"They sort sediment into size groups"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-MEASUREMENT-EQUIPMENT","scaffold":"Poles placed a measured distance apart provide consistent sighting points for a clinometer reading.","reforge":{"stem":"What reduces error in a clinometer gradient survey?","options":{"A":"Take only one hurried reading","B":"Estimate distance without a tape","C":"Hold poles at different unknown heights","D":"Use equal marked sighting heights"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-28","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Why use a roundness chart when studying river sediment?","options":{"A":"It measures the mineral composition exactly","B":"It weighs suspended sediment in the flow","C":"It standardises visual shape estimates","D":"It determines the age of each pebble"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-SEDIMENT-SAMPLING","scaffold":"A reference scale makes judgements of angularity or roundness more consistent between observers.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one limitation of a roundness chart?","options":{"A":"Some observer subjectivity","B":"It requires laboratory equipment that cannot be taken into the field","C":"It measures only river velocity","D":"It guarantees identical observer scores"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-29","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"How can selecting only easy-to-reach pebbles bias a sediment sample?","options":{"A":"It guarantees a random sample","B":"It increases the river's sediment load","C":"It makes every pebble equally rounded","D":"Convenience sample may be biased"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-SEDIMENT-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Convenience sampling may overrepresent deposits near one bank or exposed bar rather than the whole channel.","reforge":{"stem":"Which method gives each visible pebble a more equal selection chance?","options":{"A":"Choose only the largest stones","B":"Use random coordinates or numbers","C":"Collect stones beside the access path","D":"Select the smoothest pebble first"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-30","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Why repeat river measurements in different seasons?","options":{"A":"Flow and channel conditions vary over time","B":"Repeated visits remove every safety risk","C":"One season always represents annual conditions","D":"Season never affects river discharge"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-VALIDITY-EVALUATION","scaffold":"Seasonal rainfall, vegetation and antecedent conditions can change discharge, velocity and sediment exposure.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a single summer visit limit most?","options":{"A":"Ability to identify the river name","B":"Accuracy of the map's north arrow","C":"Year-round representativeness","D":"Whether measurements have units"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-31","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"Which control is most important before entering a river channel?","options":{"A":"Increase sample size before checking depth","B":"Assess depth, flow and safe access","C":"Wear ordinary shoes to feel the bed","D":"Work alone to reduce disturbance"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-FIELDWORK-SAFETY","scaffold":"A dynamic risk assessment should consider velocity, depth, slippery surfaces, weather and safe entry points.","reforge":{"stem":"What should students do if flow becomes faster after heavy rain?","options":{"A":"Continue because the plan cannot change","B":"Stand farther apart across the channel","C":"Remove buoyancy aids to move easily","D":"Stop in-channel work and reassess"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"},{"id":"GCSE-RVF-32","spec":"GCSE-RIVERFIELD","stem":"What would most improve the validity of a downstream-change investigation?","options":{"A":"Measure unrelated variables at random sites","B":"Choose sites only where parking is easy","C":"Sample comparable sites along the course","D":"Use different equipment at every location"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-RVF-VALIDITY-EVALUATION","scaffold":"Sites should represent meaningful downstream positions while controlling avoidable differences in method and obstruction.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a site immediately below a weir reduce validity?","options":{"A":"Weir alters flow and sediment","B":"The site is necessarily too far downstream","C":"Weirs have no influence on channel processes","D":"All river sites contain identical conditions"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-rvf"}]},"GCSE-GEO-URBFIELD":{"label":"Urban Fieldwork — Stratford, London","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-URF-01","spec":"GCSE-URF","stem":"A class investigates change across Old Stratford, Carpenters Estate, East Village and the Olympic Park. Which is the most appropriate hypothesis for this enquiry?","options":{"A":"Environmental quality improves nearer the Olympic Park","B":"Environmental quality is identical across all areas of Stratford","C":"Population density has no relationship to housing type in Stratford","D":"Traffic levels cannot be measured in Stratford"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URF-HYPOTHESIS-VALIDITY","scaffold":"A good urban hypothesis names a clear, testable, directional relationship. Stratford is ideal for this because it contains contrasting zones within walking distance: Old Stratford and Carpenters Estate (older, less-regenerated housing) versus East Village and the Olympic Park (post-2012 regeneration). A hypothesis like 'environmental quality improves with proximity to the regenerated Olympic Park' can be tested directly using an EQS at each location.","reforge":{"stem":"What would be an appropriate null hypothesis for the Stratford investigation above?","options":{"A":"The Olympic Park is not located in Stratford","B":"Stratford has no housing at all","C":"Quality does not improve nearer the Olympic Park","D":"Environmental quality always improves everywhere in London"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-02","spec":"GCSE-URF","stem":"Why is Stratford, London a strong location for an urban change enquiry linked to regeneration theory?","options":{"A":"It has no variation in building age or land use","B":"Sharply contrasting land use within a small area","C":"It has had no regeneration investment, so change cannot be measured","D":"It has no public transport links, making data collection easier"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URF-REGENERATION-CONCEPT","scaffold":"Regeneration is investment to reverse the decline of a run-down area. Stratford is unusually compressed for fieldwork purposes: Carpenters Estate (1960s social housing, some now vacant pending redevelopment) sits close to East Village (former 2012 Olympic Athletes' Village, now private and rented housing) and the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park (redeveloped green and leisure space). This lets students compare pre- and post-regeneration environments on a single day's fieldwork.","reforge":{"stem":"A student annotates a photograph of Carpenters Estate: 'older, low-rise blocks with visible signs of disrepair, contrasting with the new East Village development nearby.' What analytical skill is this annotation demonstrating?","options":{"A":"Secondary data analysis — since photographs cannot be primary data","B":"Quantitative analysis — calculating a numerical value from the photograph","C":"Statistical analysis — since it refers to a measurable value","D":"Qualitative analysis — annotation adds meaning"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-03","spec":"GCSE-URF","stem":"Students conduct an Environmental Quality Survey at Old Stratford, Carpenters Estate, East Village and the Olympic Park. Which feature would NOT typically appear on an EQS scoring sheet?","options":{"A":"Amount of litter","B":"Amount of green space and vegetation","C":"River discharge in m³/s","D":"Condition of pavements"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URF-EQS-DIMENSIONS","scaffold":"EQS scores urban features on a bipolar scale, typically -2 to +2: pavements, buildings, litter, traffic, green space, graffiti. River discharge is a river fieldwork measurement, not an urban one — a classic exam distractor testing whether students can tell urban and river methods apart. Scoring each feature at all four Stratford locations allows a direct site-to-site comparison.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might an EQS score for 'green space' be notably higher at the Olympic Park than at Carpenters Estate?","options":{"A":"The park was purpose-built with extensive green space","B":"Carpenters Estate has more parks than the Olympic Park","C":"Green space cannot be scored using an EQS","D":"EQS scores are unrelated to actual land use"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-04","spec":"GCSE-URF","stem":"A student conducts a pedestrian count at East Village and the Olympic Park at 9am, 11am, 1pm and 3pm using a tally chart. What is the main purpose of repeating the count at different times?","options":{"A":"It replaces the need for a risk assessment","B":"It removes the need to visit more than one location","C":"It has no purpose — one count at any time is equally valid","D":"Pedestrian flow varies, so several time slots are fairer"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URF-TEMPORAL-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Footfall changes with time of day — a leisure destination like the Olympic Park may peak differently to a commuter route near East Village. A single count only captures one moment, which may not be typical. Repeating counts at set times (9am, 11am, 1pm, 3pm) captures this variation and produces more reliable, comparable data across sites.","reforge":{"stem":"A pedestrian count at the Olympic Park is much higher on a Saturday afternoon than a count taken at Carpenters Estate on a Tuesday morning. Why is this comparison methodologically weak?","options":{"A":"The Olympic Park is not a valid fieldwork location","B":"Timing was not controlled, so it may explain the gap","C":"Tally charts cannot be used for pedestrian data","D":"Pedestrian counts cannot be compared between any two sites"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-05","spec":"GCSE-URF","stem":"Students use a questionnaire to ask residents near Carpenters Estate: 'The regeneration of the Olympic Park area has improved this neighbourhood.' Respondents choose strongly agree, agree, disagree or strongly disagree. What type of question is this?","options":{"A":"A statement rated on an opinion scale","B":"An open question with unlimited possible answers","C":"A closed numerical question","D":"A secondary data source"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URF-QUESTIONNAIRE-SCALES","scaffold":"Question types: closed (single word/number/limited list, e.g. 'How long have you lived here?'), statement/scale (agree–disagree scale, as here), open (any answer, e.g. 'What are your views on the regeneration?'). Statement questions produce data that is easy to quantify and compare, while open questions produce richer but harder-to-compare qualitative data.","reforge":{"stem":"'What are your views on the redevelopment of Carpenters Estate?' is an example of which question type, and what is its main limitation for data analysis?","options":{"A":"A secondary data question, since it is not answered by the respondent","B":"A statement question — responses are given as a numerical score","C":"An open question — rich, but hard to compare","D":"A closed question — responses are limited to yes or no"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-06","spec":"GCSE-URF","stem":"Which of these is a risk specifically worth including in a risk assessment for fieldwork around Stratford's Carpenters Estate and East Village?","options":{"A":"Risk of tidal flooding, since Stratford is on the coast","B":"Traffic risk, managed with hi-vis and safe crossing","C":"Risk of altitude sickness from elevation change","D":"Risk of encountering livestock"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URF-FIELDWORK-DESIGN","scaffold":"Urban fieldwork risks differ from river/rural ones: traffic and road crossings, getting lost in an unfamiliar area, contact with strangers during questionnaires, and general pollution exposure are the relevant hazards for a built-up site like Stratford. Livestock, tides and altitude sickness are risks associated with rural, coastal or upland fieldwork instead — another common distractor pattern in exam questions.","reforge":{"stem":"A student is completing questionnaires with the public near Stratford station. What precaution addresses the 'contact with strangers' risk most directly?","options":{"A":"Skipping the questionnaire method entirely","B":"Avoiding all human contact during the enquiry","C":"Completing questionnaires only inside a locked room","D":"Plan questions, work in groups, withdraw politely"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-07","spec":"GCSE-URF","stem":"A student wants to compare EQS scores for four Stratford sites (Old Stratford, Carpenters Estate, East Village, Olympic Park) on one chart. Which graph type is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"A scattergraph, since there is only one variable being measured","B":"A triangular graph, since there are four categories","C":"A bar graph, comparing one score across sites","D":"A choropleth map, since EQS data cannot be mapped"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URF-EVIDENCE-PRESENTATION","scaffold":"Bar graphs are ideal for comparing a single measured value (total EQS score) across distinct categories (the four named sites) — simple to construct and interpret, though they don't show relationships or explain causes. A scattergraph needs two variables; a triangular graph needs three percentage components; EQS scores actually can be mapped as proportional symbols, but a bar graph is the more direct comparison tool here.","reforge":{"stem":"If a student instead wanted to test whether EQS score is related to distance from the Olympic Park across many small sites, which graph would be more appropriate than a bar graph?","options":{"A":"A scattergraph of EQS score against distance","B":"A pie chart of each site's share of the total score","C":"A rose diagram","D":"A population pyramid"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-08","spec":"GCSE-URF","stem":"A student's Stratford enquiry finds Carpenters Estate has a surprisingly HIGH environmental quality score despite being the oldest housing studied. How should this anomaly be treated?","options":{"A":"Assume the survey method is entirely invalid and abandon the enquiry","B":"Ignore it, since anomalies are not relevant to urban enquiries","C":"Delete the result so the graph shows a clean trend","D":"Investigate a plausible cause, such as refurbishment"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URF-EVALUATION-ANOMALY","scaffold":"Treat unexpected urban results the same way as any anomaly: describe it, then suggest a specific, plausible cause tied to the actual location — e.g. a particular street within Carpenters Estate may have been recently maintained, the survey may have been completed by only one student (subjectivity), or the day's weather/traffic was unusually light. Specific, place-based explanations score far better than generic ones.","reforge":{"stem":"Which explanation for the Carpenters Estate anomaly above would be considered a strong, well-reasoned answer?","options":{"A":"The result should simply be relabelled as correct without explanation","B":"One student scored it, so group scoring would help","C":"Anomalies in urban data are always caused by equipment failure","D":"Carpenters Estate must not really exist in Stratford"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-09","spec":"GCSE-URF","stem":"A student evaluating their Stratford fieldwork writes: 'We only visited each site once, on a dry Tuesday afternoon in term time.' What is this evaluation point highlighting?","options":{"A":"A limit on reliability across days and weather","B":"A strength that guarantees the enquiry is fully reliable","C":"An irrelevant detail with no bearing on data quality","D":"A statement that belongs in the aims, not the evaluation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URF-EVALUATION-ANOMALY","scaffold":"This is a genuine, well-targeted evaluation point: single-visit fieldwork captures only one snapshot in time. Time of day, day of the week, weather and season can all affect footfall, traffic and even perceived environmental quality. A strong evaluation names the specific limitation and proposes a specific fix — e.g. repeat visits at a weekend, in different weather, or at a different time of year.","reforge":{"stem":"How could the Stratford urban enquiry's reliability be most directly improved?","options":{"A":"Reducing the number of questionnaire respondents","B":"Only visiting the Olympic Park and ignoring the other three sites","C":"Repeat at different times and days to test the pattern","D":"Removing the environmental quality survey entirely"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-10","spec":"GCSE-URF","stem":"Which theory or concept would most appropriately underpin a Stratford fieldwork enquiry into land use change since the 2012 Olympics?","options":{"A":"Bradshaw's model, since Stratford is a river source","B":"Urban regeneration and the sustainability of the legacy","C":"The demographic transition model, since it explains only population change over time","D":"Rostow's stages of economic growth, since it applies only to national economies"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URF-REGENERATION-CONCEPT","scaffold":"Every fieldwork enquiry needs an identifiable underpinning theory or concept. For an urban enquiry like Stratford, the relevant frameworks are urban land use change and sustainability of regeneration — directly relevant given the area's transformation from industrial/1960s estate land into the Olympic Park, East Village and ongoing Carpenters Estate redevelopment plans. Bradshaw's model is for rivers; Rostow's and the DTM apply at a national/international scale, not to a single urban regeneration site.","reforge":{"stem":"A student writes: 'Sustainability of urban regeneration' as their underpinning concept for Stratford. Which finding would directly relate to this concept?","options":{"A":"The number of respondents who refused a questionnaire","B":"The exact wind direction recorded on the day of fieldwork","C":"The distance between Old Stratford and Park Farm","D":"Whether the new housing stays affordable and green"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-URF","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Environmental Quality Surveys in Stratford.","template":"An EQS typically scores features such as pavements, buildings, litter, traffic and ___ space on a ___ scale, and the resulting data is ___.","blanks":["green","bipolar","quantitative"],"bank":["green","bipolar","quantitative","river","open","qualitative"],"tag":"MC-GG-URF-EQS-DIMENSIONS","scaffold":"EQS features: pavements, buildings, litter, traffic, green space, graffiti — each scored on a bipolar scale (e.g. -2 to +2). Despite being based on subjective judgement, the output is quantitative (numerical) data, which is why it can be compared and graphed across sites like Old Stratford, Carpenters Estate, East Village and the Olympic Park.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-URF","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about Stratford's urban regeneration.","template":"Stratford's ___ Estate represents older, pre-regeneration housing, while ___ Village was built as the Athletes' Village for the 2012 Olympics and the Queen Elizabeth Olympic ___ is the redeveloped green and leisure space.","blanks":["Carpenters","East","Park"],"bank":["Carpenters","East","Park","Old","West","Village"],"tag":"MC-GG-URF-REGENERATION-CONCEPT","scaffold":"The four named Stratford fieldwork locations: Old Stratford (established area), Carpenters Estate (1960s social housing, partly awaiting redevelopment), East Village (former Athletes' Village, now housing), Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park (redeveloped green/leisure space). This contrast makes Stratford ideal for testing a regeneration-based hypothesis.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-URF","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about question types in urban questionnaires.","template":"A ___ question limits answers to a single word or number, an ___ question allows any response, and a ___ question asks respondents to rate agreement on a scale.","blanks":["closed","open","statement"],"bank":["closed","open","statement","random","stratified","secondary"],"tag":"MC-GG-URF-QUESTIONNAIRE-SCALES","scaffold":"Closed: 'How long have you lived here?' Open: 'What are your views on the regeneration?' Statement: 'The regeneration has improved this neighbourhood — strongly agree to strongly disagree.' Closed and statement questions are quick to analyse and compare; open questions give richer detail but are harder to quantify.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-11","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"What is the purpose of an Environmental Quality Survey (EQS)?","options":{"A":"It measures national migration totals","B":"It scores environmental features","C":"It calculates river discharge","D":"It records only the age of buildings"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URF-EQS-DIMENSIONS","scaffold":"An EQS converts observations such as litter, noise, building condition and green space into scores. Using the same criteria at each Stratford site supports comparison, although scoring can remain subjective.","reforge":{"stem":"Why use the same EQS criteria at Old Stratford and the Olympic Park?","options":{"A":"To replace all observations with census data","B":"To make the sites identical","C":"To measure population change directly","D":"To compare environmental quality"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-12","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"A questionnaire asks respondents to choose strongly agree, agree, disagree or strongly disagree. What type of scale is this?","options":{"A":"A ratio scale","B":"A grid reference","C":"Ordinal scale","D":"A random sample"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URF-QUESTIONNAIRE-SCALES","scaffold":"These ordered response categories form a Likert or ordinal scale. The responses have a clear order, but the intervals between categories cannot be assumed to be equal.","reforge":{"stem":"Which scale is commonly used to record attitudes towards regeneration?","options":{"A":"Likert scale","B":"A nominal scale with no order","C":"A contour scale","D":"A time-series axis"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-13","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Why might stratified sampling be used in Stratford fieldwork?","options":{"A":"It selects only the easiest people to reach","B":"It guarantees that every answer is positive","C":"It removes the need for a clear hypothesis","D":"It represents important groups or zones"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URF-FIELDWORK-DESIGN","scaffold":"Stratified sampling divides the population into relevant groups or zones and samples from each. This can ensure that contrasting areas such as Old Stratford, Carpenters Estate and East Village are represented.","reforge":{"stem":"A class samples equal numbers of residents from four Stratford zones. Which aim does this support?","options":{"A":"Avoiding all comparison between zones","B":"Representing contrasting zones","C":"Sampling only visitors","D":"Making the questionnaire anonymous"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-14","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Which is a useful source of secondary data for comparing urban quality of life?","options":{"A":"Census or IMD data","B":"A fresh pedestrian count","C":"A field sketch made today","D":"A student's new EQS score"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URF-FIELDWORK-DESIGN","scaffold":"Census and Index of Multiple Deprivation data are secondary sources because they were collected by another organisation. They can provide wider context for primary observations in Stratford.","reforge":{"stem":"Which source was collected before the Stratford fieldwork by another organisation?","options":{"A":"Today's questionnaire","B":"A traffic count","C":"Census data","D":"A site photograph"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-15","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Why repeat pedestrian counts at different times of day?","options":{"A":"To make the street permanently busier","B":"To show time variation","C":"To remove all sampling limitations","D":"To prove that regeneration caused every movement"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URF-TEMPORAL-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Pedestrian flows vary with school, work, shopping and leisure patterns. Counts at different times reveal temporal variation and are more representative than one isolated observation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main benefit of counting pedestrians at 9am, 1pm and 3pm?","options":{"A":"It removes the need to record weather","B":"It guarantees the same number each time","C":"It measures building age directly","D":"It captures changes through the day"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-16","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"What makes a fieldwork hypothesis testable?","options":{"A":"It uses only opinions","B":"It cannot be disproved","C":"Measurable variables","D":"It avoids naming a location"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URF-HYPOTHESIS-VALIDITY","scaffold":"A testable hypothesis links variables that can be observed or measured, such as environmental quality and distance from a regeneration site. It should be precise enough to guide data collection.","reforge":{"stem":"Which hypothesis is most testable for Stratford fieldwork?","options":{"A":"Measurable EQS trend","B":"Stratford feels interesting","C":"Regeneration is always good","D":"The area has many changes"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-17","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"What is gentrification?","options":{"A":"The complete removal of public transport","B":"A fall in all urban land values","C":"The movement of every resident to a rural area","D":"Investment changes an area"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URF-REGENERATION-CONCEPT","scaffold":"Gentrification involves investment and wealthier residents changing a neighbourhood. It may improve buildings and services but can raise rents and displace lower-income residents.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change could indicate gentrification near a regeneration project?","options":{"A":"Falling investment and empty shops","B":"Rising investment and rents","C":"Lower rents for every resident","D":"The end of all population change"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-18","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"What should a strong evaluation of a fieldwork conclusion do?","options":{"A":"Compare evidence with the hypothesis","B":"List equipment without explaining its effects","C":"Ignore anomalies and sampling choices","D":"Repeat the hypothesis without evidence"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URF-HYPOTHESIS-VALIDITY","scaffold":"A strong evaluation returns to the hypothesis, uses the results and considers limitations such as sample size, timing, subjectivity and anomalies. It should explain how those issues affect confidence in the conclusion.","reforge":{"stem":"Which option best evaluates a Stratford conclusion?","options":{"A":"The hypothesis was correct because it sounded sensible","B":"Only the weather mattered","C":"Limited sites reduce confidence","D":"The sample was perfect because it had a graph"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-19","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Which method best measures residents' perceptions of quality of life?","options":{"A":"A river velocity float","B":"A structured questionnaire","C":"A geological cross-section","D":"A beach sediment ruler"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URF-QUESTIONNAIRE-QUALITY","scaffold":"Questionnaires can collect attitudes to safety, services, housing and environmental quality.","reforge":{"stem":"Why combine perception data with environmental measurements?","options":{"A":"Perceptions are always objectively correct","B":"Measurements reveal every personal experience","C":"Combining data guarantees agreement","D":"The sources capture different dimensions"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-20","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"What is a semantic differential scale?","options":{"A":"A tally of pedestrians passing a point","B":"A measure of sound in decibels","C":"A rating between opposite adjectives","D":"A random list of street addresses"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URF-QUESTIONNAIRE-SCALES","scaffold":"Respondents rate a place along a scale such as unsafe-safe or unattractive-attractive.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should opposite adjectives be clearly defined?","options":{"A":"It reduces inconsistent interpretation","B":"It ensures all respondents choose the middle","C":"It turns opinions into ratio data","D":"It removes the need for sampling"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-21","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"What might a high pedestrian count indicate in a shopping area?","options":{"A":"Environmental quality must be high","B":"River flood risk is increasing","C":"Every pedestrian lives locally","D":"The area has activity and footfall"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URF-URBAN-EVIDENCE","scaffold":"Pedestrian flow can indicate vitality or accessibility but does not alone reveal residents' quality of life.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should pedestrian counts be interpreted cautiously?","options":{"A":"A high count proves regeneration caused growth","B":"Purpose and timing affect the total","C":"They cannot be recorded quantitatively","D":"Counts are identical throughout the day"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-22","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"What does a sound-level meter add to an urban enquiry?","options":{"A":"Quantitative evidence of noise","B":"A subjective opinion of building design","C":"A direct measure of air pollution","D":"A count of different land uses"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URF-URBAN-EVIDENCE","scaffold":"Decibel readings provide comparable quantitative data, though traffic and temporary events affect them.","reforge":{"stem":"How should noise measurements be made comparable?","options":{"A":"Ignore unusual construction noise","B":"Measure for random unrecorded periods","C":"Use the same duration and position","D":"Hold the meter at different heights"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-23","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Why create a land-use map around each urban fieldwork site?","options":{"A":"It measures household income directly","B":"It helps explain observed spatial patterns","C":"It replaces every environmental survey","D":"It shows future planning decisions with certainty"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URF-EVIDENCE-PRESENTATION","scaffold":"Land use provides context for differences in noise, footfall, traffic, building condition and service access.","reforge":{"stem":"Which mapping category would include shops and offices?","options":{"A":"Agricultural land use","B":"Natural woodland succession","C":"River-channel morphology","D":"Commercial land use"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-24","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Why might deprivation data and an EQS show different patterns?","options":{"A":"Both measure exactly the same concept","B":"EQS includes every household income","C":"They measure different dimensions","D":"IMD records only visible litter"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URF-EQS-DIMENSIONS","scaffold":"A place can look physically improved while residents still experience low income, poor health or insecure housing.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the strongest response to conflicting EQS and IMD results?","options":{"A":"Explain that they measure different dimensions","B":"Delete the dataset that challenges the hypothesis","C":"Average unrelated scores into one number","D":"Assume the newest-looking area has no deprivation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-25","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Which Census variable can help assess housing conditions?","options":{"A":"Prevailing wind direction","B":"Daily river discharge","C":"Beach sediment roundness","D":"Tenure and overcrowding"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URF-URBAN-EVIDENCE","scaffold":"Census data on tenure, occupancy and household composition provide neighbourhood housing context.","reforge":{"stem":"Which Census variable helps investigate population ageing?","options":{"A":"Annual retail turnover","B":"Age-group structure","C":"Average building height","D":"Distance from the CBD"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-26","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"What does a high IMD rank for deprivation indicate?","options":{"A":"Several deprivation domains contribute","B":"Every street has poor environmental quality","C":"Crime is the only measured problem","D":"The area contains no wealthy households"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URF-EQS-DIMENSIONS","scaffold":"IMD combines weighted domains and describes area-level deprivation, not every individual resident.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should IMD not be used to label every resident as deprived?","options":{"A":"It contains no quantitative information","B":"It measures only rural settlements","C":"It summarises areas, not individuals","D":"It is collected by student fieldworkers"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-27","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Which questionnaire wording is least leading?","options":{"A":"Do you agree this excellent scheme improved everything?","B":"How satisfied are you with the scheme?","C":"Why has regeneration failed local people?","D":"Isn't the new park much safer now?"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URF-QUESTIONNAIRE-QUALITY","scaffold":"Neutral wording avoids signalling a preferred response and improves validity.","reforge":{"stem":"What is wrong with asking 'How bad is traffic here?'","options":{"A":"It contains no topic for respondents","B":"It protects respondent anonymity","C":"It can only produce numerical data","D":"It assumes traffic is bad"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-28","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Why record whether respondents are residents, workers or visitors?","options":{"A":"Only visitors can judge environmental quality","B":"The groups must receive identical opinions","C":"Perspectives may differ among users","D":"Residence status determines every answer"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URF-QUESTIONNAIRE-QUALITY","scaffold":"People who live, work or visit may experience services, housing and change differently.","reforge":{"stem":"Which sample best compares stakeholder perceptions?","options":{"A":"Include residents, workers and visitors","B":"Question only classmates at one site","C":"Select respondents who support regeneration","D":"Exclude people living in the area"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-29","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Why repeat an urban survey on weekdays and weekends?","options":{"A":"Repeated visits eliminate observer bias","B":"Land use becomes identical every day","C":"Weather cannot change between visits","D":"Activity patterns vary by day"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-URF-TEMPORAL-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Commuting, shopping and leisure flows vary through the week, affecting noise, traffic and footfall.","reforge":{"stem":"What should be recorded when comparing surveys on different days?","options":{"A":"The preferred conclusion beforehand","B":"Time, weather and unusual events","C":"Only the final average score","D":"Respondents' names and addresses"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-30","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"How can GIS improve presentation of EQS results?","options":{"A":"It maps scores to reveal clusters","B":"It proves environmental quality caused migration","C":"It removes spatial variation from the data","D":"It changes negative scores into positive ones"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-URF-EVIDENCE-PRESENTATION","scaffold":"Mapped scores can show gradients, hotspots and relationships with regeneration sites or land uses.","reforge":{"stem":"Which GIS layer could help explain high traffic scores?","options":{"A":"Rock types in the Scottish Highlands","B":"National infant mortality rates","C":"Major roads and junctions","D":"River depth measurements upstream"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-31","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"A regenerated site has high EQS but low resident satisfaction. What should students conclude?","options":{"A":"One dataset must have been recorded incorrectly","B":"Physical improvement may not meet social needs","C":"The resident sample should be ignored","D":"High EQS proves quality of life is high"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-URF-EVALUATION-ANOMALY","scaffold":"Quality of life is multidimensional, so clean design may coexist with affordability, displacement or service concerns.","reforge":{"stem":"What extra evidence would best investigate the disagreement?","options":{"A":"A second river velocity method","B":"A national climate graph","C":"A map of distant coastal geology","D":"Residents' open responses"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"},{"id":"GCSE-URF-32","spec":"GCSE-URBANFIELD","stem":"Why can fieldwork rarely prove regeneration caused every observed change?","options":{"A":"Urban places do not change over time","B":"Field data cannot contain measurements","C":"Other factors and baseline data matter","D":"Regeneration never affects quality of life"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-URF-HYPOTHESIS-VALIDITY","scaffold":"A before-after comparison, control area and wider evidence are needed to separate regeneration effects from broader trends.","reforge":{"stem":"What would strengthen a causal claim about regeneration?","options":{"A":"Before-after data plus a control","B":"Visit one regenerated site once","C":"Assume all change began on opening day","D":"Use only opinions from project managers"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-urf"}]},"GCSE-GEO-BIOSPHERE":{"label":"People and the Biosphere","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-BIO-01","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"What is the main factor determining the global distribution of biomes?","options":{"A":"Climate — temperature and rainfall set the growing season","B":"Biome type is determined only by soil pH","C":"The distance a country is from the equator has no effect on its biome","D":"Government policy decides which biome forms in a country"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Climate (temperature and precipitation) is the primary control on biome distribution: it determines the growing season length, which drives plant growth, which in turn determines biodiversity — the hotter and wetter a place is, the more plant species it supports, providing more food and habitats and so more animal species too. Local factors (altitude, soil/rock type, drainage, continentality, ocean currents) then create variation within that broad climatic pattern.","reforge":{"stem":"Two locations are at a similar latitude but one is far inland (continental) and one is on the coast. Why might their biomes differ?","options":{"A":"Inland and coastal areas always share an identical biome","B":"Ocean currents only affect biomes at the equator","C":"Inland areas heat and cool faster than the coast","D":"Latitude is the only factor that ever affects climate"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-02","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"A tropical rainforest and a hot desert are both located close to the equator. Why is their biodiversity so different?","options":{"A":"Deserts always have higher biodiversity than rainforests","B":"Constant warmth and rain support far more plant growth","C":"Biodiversity is unrelated to precipitation","D":"Latitude alone determines biodiversity regardless of rainfall"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Tropical rainforest: over 2000mm rainfall annually, hot and wet year-round, no dormant season — supports roughly 50% of the world's plant and animal species. Hot desert: below 250mm rainfall, extreme temperature swings — low biodiversity (cacti, yucca, scorpions, camels). Both sit at similar latitudes, proving precipitation, not latitude alone, is doing the work here.","reforge":{"stem":"The taiga and temperate forest are both found in the northern hemisphere, but the taiga has lower biodiversity. What is the main reason?","options":{"A":"The taiga is located closer to the equator","B":"Biodiversity is identical in all forest biomes","C":"The taiga receives more rainfall than the temperate forest","D":"A short growing season limits the taiga to conifers"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-03","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Which local factor explains why biome type can change dramatically over a short horizontal distance in a mountainous region?","options":{"A":"Latitude, since altitude and latitude are the same thing","B":"Continentality, which only applies to low-lying areas","C":"Altitude — cooler air changes the vegetation zones","D":"Ocean currents, since mountains are never affected by the sea"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-LOCAL-FACTORS","scaffold":"Local factors that modify biome distribution within the broader climatic pattern: altitude (temperature falls as height increases, compressing multiple biome-like zones onto one mountainside), rock/soil type (pH and mineral content affect which plants grow), drainage (soil moisture affects plant type), continentality, and ocean currents (warm/cold currents heat or cool nearby land).","reforge":{"stem":"A cold ocean current flows along a coastline that would otherwise be warm enough for tropical vegetation. What effect is this local factor likely to have?","options":{"A":"It cools the land, giving a cooler, drier biome","B":"It would increase biodiversity regardless of temperature","C":"Ocean currents have no measurable effect on nearby land climate","D":"It would make the coastline hotter than inland areas at the same latitude"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-04","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"In a tropical rainforest, vegetation is cleared. Explain the immediate impact on soil using the concept of interdependence.","options":{"A":"Soil quality automatically improves once vegetation is removed","B":"Soil nutrients increase because there is no longer any competition from plant roots","C":"Removing vegetation has no effect on soil since they are separate, unconnected systems","D":"No leaf litter is added, and the bare soil erodes and leaches"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-SOIL","scaffold":"Interdependence means biotic and abiotic components of a biome are interlinked, so a change in one affects the others. Soil (abiotic) depends on dead organic matter (biotic) for nutrients via decomposition. Removing vegetation cuts off that nutrient supply, exposes soil directly to sun and rain (increasing erosion), and reduces the biological weathering that builds new soil in the first place.","reforge":{"stem":"Following the same clearance, what is the most likely knock-on effect on local rainfall patterns?","options":{"A":"Soil erosion causes rainfall to increase","B":"Less transpiration means less moisture and rainfall","C":"Rainfall patterns are entirely unrelated to vegetation cover","D":"Rainfall would increase because there is less vegetation to intercept it"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-05","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Which is an example of a 'regulating' ecosystem service, as opposed to a 'provisioning' one?","options":{"A":"Waste decomposition","B":"Timber harvested for construction","C":"Fruit and nuts collected for food","D":"Fuel wood collected for cooking"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-SERVICES","scaffold":"The four ecosystem service categories: provisioning (goods — timber, food, water, medicine, fuel), regulating (climate, water quality, waste decomposition, soil quality, disease/pest control, air quality), cultural (health/spiritual benefits, recreation, employment, education), and supporting (soil formation, nutrient cycling). B, C and D are all tangible goods, so they are provisioning services.","reforge":{"stem":"A rainforest's role in absorbing CO2 through photosynthesis and helping maintain the balance of atmospheric gases is an example of which ecosystem service category?","options":{"A":"Cultural, since it benefits human wellbeing","B":"Provisioning, since gases are a tangible good","C":"Regulating — it controls a natural process","D":"None of these categories apply to gases"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-06","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"The Awá people of the Amazon rely on the rainforest for shelter, rope, food and fuel. Why are indigenous communities like the Awá especially vulnerable to commercial exploitation of the biosphere?","options":{"A":"Indigenous communities do not use any natural resources","B":"Their livelihoods depend directly on those resources","C":"Commercial exploitation always benefits indigenous communities financially","D":"The Awá import all of their food and materials from cities"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-INDIGENOUS","scaffold":"The Awá depend on the rainforest directly: branches and palm leaves for shelters (tapā í), palm fibres for rope and hammocks, wood for fires, and food from honey, fish, berries (açaí), tapir, deer and howler monkeys. Commercial exploitation (mining, agriculture, logging, HEP) removes exactly these resources, forcing communities to abandon traditional lifestyles when food sources disappear.","reforge":{"stem":"A mining company clears forest to build access roads near an indigenous community's territory. Which secondary impact is most likely to follow?","options":{"A":"No impact, since mining only affects the exact area being mined","B":"Improved biodiversity as a direct result of road building","C":"An automatic increase in the community's traditional food sources","D":"Toxic waste entering the river they drink from"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-07","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Why does the construction of a hydroelectric dam on a river affect ecosystems even far downstream?","options":{"A":"Dams only affect the exact location where they are built","B":"Dams increase the amount of sediment reaching downstream ecosystems","C":"The dam traps sediment and blocks fish migration","D":"HEP generates no environmental impact once construction is finished"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-EXPLOITATION","scaffold":"HEP impacts: forest clearance for the dam/reservoir, flooding of habitats, loss of food sources, a physical barrier to migratory fish (e.g. salmon), and sediment trapped behind the dam rather than replenishing the ecosystems and floodplains downstream. This is a good example of how one intervention creates a chain of effects across a whole river system, not just the dam site.","reforge":{"stem":"What best accounts for why cleared biomes are difficult to fully restore once exploited?","options":{"A":"Soil, plants and species take decades to re-establish","B":"Once cleared, biomes are legally prevented from ever regenerating","C":"Cleared land regenerates to its original state within a few months","D":"Ecosystem restoration has no relationship to how long the land was exploited"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-08","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How does deforestation directly affect the global carbon cycle?","options":{"A":"Deforestation has no effect on atmospheric CO2 levels","B":"Trees play no role in the carbon cycle","C":"Deforestation removes CO2 from the atmosphere more effectively than intact forest","D":"Fewer carbon sinks, and burning releases stored carbon"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-CARBON","scaffold":"Plants and soils act as carbon sinks — absorbing CO2 during photosynthesis, storing it in biomass, and (over long timescales) in soil and eventually fossil fuels. Human activity increases atmospheric CO2 two ways: burning biomass/fossil fuels releases stored carbon directly, and deforestation simply removes the sinks that would otherwise keep absorbing it — a double impact.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might felling trees for agriculture have a bigger long-term climate impact than the carbon released by the felling itself?","options":{"A":"Agricultural land absorbs more CO2 than forest","B":"A lost carbon sink no longer absorbs future emissions","C":"There is no long-term impact beyond the immediate release","D":"Felling trees permanently removes CO2 from the atmosphere"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-09","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Why is soil in a biome that has recently been deforested likely to become less fertile over time?","options":{"A":"Leaf litter stops, and nutrients are washed away","B":"Soil fertility is entirely unrelated to vegetation cover","C":"Deforestation adds nutrients to soil through increased sunlight exposure","D":"Soil fertility always increases once trees are removed"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-SOIL","scaffold":"The nutrient cycle depends on continuous input from decomposing organic matter. Remove the vegetation and that supply stops; the exposed soil surface is also more prone to leaching (nutrients washed down through the soil) and surface erosion (topsoil washed away entirely) since there's no canopy or root system to protect and hold it in place.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are tropical rainforest soils described as particularly vulnerable when vegetation is removed, even though rainforests have such high biodiversity?","options":{"A":"Rainforest soils contain no organic matter at all","B":"Rainforest soils are naturally the most fertile soils in the world","C":"Nutrients sit in the biomass, not the soil","D":"Removing rainforest vegetation has no effect on soil nutrients"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-10","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"According to Malthus's theory (1798), what would eventually happen if population growth outpaced food supply?","options":{"A":"Population growth would have no relationship to food supply","B":"A Malthusian catastrophe — famine, disease or war","C":"Food supply would automatically double whenever population doubled","D":"Technology would always increase food supply fast enough to prevent any problem"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-POPULATION-THEORY","scaffold":"Malthus (1798): population grows faster than food supply, so eventually there won't be enough food, triggering 'positive checks' (famine, disease, war) that raise the death rate, or 'preventative checks' that lower the birth rate — either way, this brings population back in line with resources. Malthus's original predictions proved wrong because he didn't foresee later agricultural technology, but neo-Malthusians argue his logic still applies given finite land and rising food prices today.","reforge":{"stem":"Neo-Malthusians point to modern famines as evidence supporting Malthus's original theory. What is their key argument?","options":{"A":"Famines have never occurred since 1798, disproving Malthus entirely","B":"Neo-Malthusians agree completely with Boserup's theory instead","C":"Technology has permanently solved the problem Malthus described, so neo-Malthusian concerns are baseless","D":"Fertile land is shrinking while population keeps growing"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-11","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How does Boserup's theory (1965) directly contradict Malthus's view of population and resources?","options":{"A":"Boserup agrees entirely with Malthus that population growth always outpaces resources","B":"Boserup argues population and food supply are completely unrelated","C":"Population pressure drives innovation, not famine","D":"Boserup predicts food supply will decline as population grows, just as Malthus did"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-POPULATION-THEORY","scaffold":"Boserup (1965) is the optimistic counter to Malthus's pessimism: as population grows, necessity drives innovation — new farming technology, more efficient use of resources, and a shift from non-renewable to renewable resources — increasing the food/resource supply to match demand, rather than being outpaced by it.","reforge":{"stem":"The Green Revolution of the 1960s–70s saw crop yields rise dramatically through new seed varieties, fertilisers and irrigation as global population grew. Which theory does this best support?","options":{"A":"Boserup — pressure stimulated innovation in food supply","B":"It disproves Boserup by showing technology cannot increase food supply","C":"Neither theory, since crop yields are unrelated to population","D":"Malthus's theory — it demonstrates a Malthusian catastrophe occurring exactly as predicted"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-12","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Why does increasing global wealth generally lead to greater pressure on the biosphere's resources?","options":{"A":"Wealthier countries always use fewer natural resources than poorer countries","B":"Wealth has no relationship to resource consumption","C":"Increased wealth automatically reduces a population's ecological footprint to zero","D":"Wealthier people consume more food, energy and goods"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DEMAND","scaffold":"Rising average wealth increases demand across the board: more spending on food, water (appliances like dishwashers), larger homes (more land), energy (production requires land for power stations/wind/solar farms), and consumer goods (more industrial production and waste). Combined with population growth reaching 8 billion in 2022 and rising urbanisation (55%), this compounds the pressure on biomes worldwide.","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination of trends explains the sharpest recent rise in global resource demand?","options":{"A":"A falling global population with static wealth levels","B":"Population growth, rising wealth and urbanisation","C":"Declining urbanisation with no change in wealth","D":"Resource demand is unrelated to any demographic or economic trend"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-BIO","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about factors affecting biome distribution.","template":"The main factor affecting biome distribution is ___, but local factors such as ___, rock and soil type, drainage and ___ also cause variation.","blanks":["climate","altitude","continentality"],"bank":["climate","altitude","continentality","population","currency","transport"],"tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Climate (temperature and precipitation) sets the broad global pattern of biomes; altitude, rock/soil type, drainage, continentality and ocean currents then create local variation within and across that pattern.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-BIO","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about ecosystem services.","template":"___ services are tangible products like timber and food. ___ services include climate control and waste decomposition. ___ services include recreation and education.","blanks":["Provisioning","Regulating","Cultural"],"bank":["Provisioning","Regulating","Cultural","Supporting","Extracting","Economic"],"tag":"MC-GG-BIO-SERVICES","scaffold":"The four ecosystem service categories: provisioning (goods — timber, food, water, medicine), regulating (climate, water/air quality, waste decomposition, pest control), cultural (health, recreation, employment, heritage, education), and supporting (soil formation, nutrient cycling).","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-BIO","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about deforestation and the carbon cycle.","template":"Plants and soils act as carbon ___, absorbing CO2 during ___. Deforestation removes this ability, while burning ___ releases stored carbon directly into the atmosphere.","blanks":["sinks","photosynthesis","biomass"],"bank":["sinks","photosynthesis","biomass","sources","respiration","minerals"],"tag":"MC-GG-BIO-CARBON","scaffold":"Plants absorb CO2 during photosynthesis, storing carbon in biomass and eventually soil — this makes vegetated biomes carbon sinks. Deforestation both removes this ongoing absorption and, if the cleared biomass is burned, releases the carbon that was already stored.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-BIO","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about population and resource theories.","template":"___ took a pessimistic view, arguing population growth would outpace food supply leading to a catastrophe. ___ took an optimistic view, arguing population growth stimulates ___ to increase food production.","blanks":["Malthus","Boserup","technology"],"bank":["Malthus","Boserup","technology","government","migration","trade"],"tag":"MC-GG-BIO-POPULATION-THEORY","scaffold":"Malthus (1798, pessimistic): population outpaces food, causing famine/disease/war. Boserup (1965, optimistic): population pressure drives technological innovation that raises food production to meet demand. Both theories focus on food but can be applied to other resources too.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-13","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"A student mixes up abiotic and biotic components of an ecosystem. What is the correct distinction?","options":{"A":"Abiotic factors are non-living; biotic ones are alive","B":"Abiotic components only exist in deserts","C":"Abiotic components are living organisms; biotic components are non-living factors","D":"They are two names for exactly the same thing"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"A simple memory hook: 'a-' at the start of abiotic means 'without' — abiotic factors have no life. Biotic factors are the living parts of an ecosystem (plants, animals, decomposers) that depend on and interact with those non-living conditions.","reforge":{"stem":"Soil type and sunlight exposure are examples of which type of ecosystem component?","options":{"A":"Neither — soil and sunlight are not considered ecosystem components","B":"Both abiotic and biotic simultaneously","C":"Abiotic components, since they are non-living environmental factors","D":"Biotic components, since they directly involve living organisms"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-14","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"In a food chain, what is the correct order and role of producers, consumers and decomposers?","options":{"A":"Consumers and producers are the same trophic level","B":"Producers photosynthesise, consumers eat, decomposers recycle","C":"Producers eat consumers, who then decompose to release energy","D":"Decomposers convert sunlight into glucose"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Energy flow: producers (plants) capture solar energy → consumers eat producers or other consumers → decomposers break down dead matter from any level, releasing nutrients back into the soil for producers to use again — this cycle is what nutrient cycling describes.","reforge":{"stem":"A dead animal is broken down by fungi and bacteria, releasing nutrients back into the soil. What role are the fungi and bacteria playing in this food chain?","options":{"A":"Primary consumers","B":"Producers","C":"Abiotic components","D":"Decomposers"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-15","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"What is altitudinal zonation?","options":{"A":"The process of carbon dioxide being stored long-term in soil or vegetation","B":"A theory about population growth and food supply","C":"Bands of vegetation at different mountain elevations","D":"The measurement of a mountain's total height above sea level"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-LOCAL-FACTORS","scaffold":"As altitude increases, temperature falls (roughly 6.5°C per 1000m) and conditions become harsher, so vegetation changes in visible bands up a mountainside — from forest at the base, through coniferous and alpine vegetation, to bare rock and snow near the summit.","reforge":{"stem":"Climbing a mountain, a hiker passes through forest, then coniferous vegetation, then bare rock near the summit, as temperature falls with height. What term describes this pattern?","options":{"A":"Altitudinal zonation","B":"Nutrient cycling","C":"Carbon sequestration","D":"The hydrological cycle"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-16","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How does carbon sequestration differ from simply 'not emitting greenhouse gases'?","options":{"A":"Carbon sequestration means releasing carbon dioxide into the atmosphere","B":"Carbon sequestration and emissions are the same process","C":"Only oceans, never forests, can sequester carbon","D":"Actively capturing and storing carbon already emitted"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-CARBON","scaffold":"A mature tropical rainforest is a major carbon sink because photosynthesis actively removes CO2 from the air and locks it into wood and soil for decades — this is why large-scale deforestation is doubly damaging: it releases stored carbon and removes future sequestration capacity.","reforge":{"stem":"A mature forest is cleared and burned, releasing stored carbon dioxide and removing the trees that once absorbed it. What has been lost from this landscape?","options":{"A":"A form of altitudinal zonation","B":"A carbon sink absorbing more than it releases","C":"An abiotic component with no relation to carbon","D":"A source of eutrophication"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-17","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"What causes eutrophication in an aquatic ecosystem, and why is it harmful?","options":{"A":"Fertiliser runoff feeds algae, which strip the oxygen","B":"Eutrophication only occurs in desert biomes","C":"Eutrophication is caused by a lack of nutrients in the water","D":"Eutrophication increases oxygen levels in water, benefiting all aquatic species"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-EXPLOITATION","scaffold":"Eutrophication is a knock-on chain reaction: nutrient input (e.g. nitrates/phosphates from fertiliser) → algal bloom → algae blocks light for other plants and eventually dies → decomposition of dead algae consumes dissolved oxygen → fish and other aquatic organisms suffocate.","reforge":{"stem":"Fertiliser runs off farmland into a nearby lake, causing a rapid increase in algae. What is the most likely eventual consequence for fish in the lake?","options":{"A":"Eutrophication has no effect on any organisms living in the water","B":"Oxygen levels in the lake will rise sharply, benefiting all species","C":"Decomposing algae strip oxygen and suffocate fish","D":"Fish populations will increase immediately with no negative effects"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-18","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Why are ecosystem services important when assessing the value of a biome?","options":{"A":"They describe only the number of species present","B":"Benefits ecosystems provide, such as food and storage","C":"They prove every ecosystem has the same economic value","D":"They refer only to tourism income from national parks"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-VALUATION","scaffold":"Ecosystem services are benefits people obtain from ecosystems. They include food and timber, water purification and flood regulation, recreation, nutrient cycling and soil formation.","reforge":{"stem":"A wetland is drained for construction and the town later experiences worse flooding. Which ecosystem service was reduced?","options":{"A":"Altitudinal zonation of vegetation","B":"International trade in timber","C":"Mineral extraction from bedrock","D":"Flood-water regulation and storage"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-19","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How does the precautionary principle apply to managing a threatened ecosystem?","options":{"A":"Wait until damage is irreversible before taking action","B":"Assume any development is sustainable if it creates jobs","C":"Act to prevent serious harm even without full evidence","D":"Ignore local communities because conservation is purely scientific"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-VALUATION","scaffold":"The precautionary principle supports prevention where potential environmental damage is serious or irreversible, even when evidence is uncertain. It means assessing risk, monitoring impacts and avoiding permanent damage.","reforge":{"stem":"A new road could fragment endangered-species habitat, but its full impact is uncertain. Which response best follows the precautionary principle?","options":{"A":"Assess impacts first, then avoid or reduce the damage","B":"Build immediately because the impact is not proven","C":"Ignore the species because roads never affect ecosystems","D":"Wait until the population collapses before monitoring it"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-20","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"A climate graph shows high temperatures and heavy rainfall in every month. Which biome is the best match?","options":{"A":"Tundra with frozen subsoil","B":"Temperate grassland","C":"Boreal forest","D":"Tropical rainforest"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-CLIMATE-GRAPH","scaffold":"A tropical rainforest climate is hot and wet throughout the year, allowing continuous plant growth and supporting high biodiversity. Climate graphs should be read using both temperature and precipitation, not just latitude.","reforge":{"stem":"Which climate pattern would best support a tropical rainforest biome?","options":{"A":"Low rainfall with a large daily temperature range","B":"Year-round heat and rainfall","C":"Cold winters with a brief summer thaw","D":"Warm summers followed by freezing winters"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-21","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Why can fewer sunshine hours reduce the productivity of a biome?","options":{"A":"Less light limits photosynthesis","B":"They permanently increase soil depth and drainage","C":"They make every plant species migrate immediately","D":"Less light always causes precipitation to stop"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Plants need light for photosynthesis. Fewer sunshine hours can shorten or weaken the growing season, reducing biomass production and the food available to consumers.","reforge":{"stem":"A high-latitude biome receives very little winter sunlight. What is the most direct effect on plants?","options":{"A":"Soil nutrients increase without decomposition","B":"Precipitation becomes impossible","C":"Photosynthesis and growth slow","D":"All vegetation becomes broad-leaved"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-22","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How can poor drainage alter a biome locally?","options":{"A":"It changes the latitude of the location","B":"Waterlogged soil favours tolerant plants","C":"It guarantees a longer period of sunshine","D":"It makes the underlying rock more resistant"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-LOCAL-FACTORS","scaffold":"Drainage is a local abiotic control. Waterlogged soils contain less oxygen, so species that tolerate saturated conditions can replace plants that need freely drained ground, even within the same regional climate.","reforge":{"stem":"Two nearby slopes share the same climate, but one has thin alkaline soil over limestone. Why might their vegetation differ?","options":{"A":"The slopes must be located in entirely different climate zones","B":"Altitude has no effect on plant growth","C":"Rainfall cannot reach limestone areas","D":"Local soil conditions select different plants"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-23","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How does forest vegetation help regulate water in the hydrological cycle?","options":{"A":"It prevents all evaporation from the land surface","B":"It converts groundwater directly into ocean currents","C":"Interception and roots slow runoff","D":"It stops precipitation reaching a drainage basin"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-SERVICES","scaffold":"Leaves intercept rainfall while roots encourage infiltration and store water. Forest vegetation also returns moisture to the atmosphere through transpiration, helping regulate flows through the hydrological cycle.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can removing forest increase the risk of rapid river flooding?","options":{"A":"Less interception increases surface runoff","B":"Deforestation makes all rainfall evaporate","C":"Tree removal lowers every river channel","D":"Bare soil stores more water than an established network of roots"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-24","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Which example best shows Indigenous use of a biosphere resource?","options":{"A":"An export firm clears forest for hardwood","B":"A global company strip-mines an entire region","C":"A national grid floods valleys for electricity","D":"A community gathers plants for medicine"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-INDIGENOUS","scaffold":"Indigenous and local communities may obtain food, medicine, building materials and fuel directly from their environment. This differs in scale and purpose from commercial exploitation for global mineral, timber, water or energy markets.","reforge":{"stem":"What most clearly distinguishes local subsistence use from commercial exploitation of a biome?","options":{"A":"Local use can never alter an ecosystem","B":"Resources mainly meet community needs","C":"Commercial activity uses no machinery","D":"Only commercial users collect fuel wood"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-25","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Why can industrialisation increase pressure on the biosphere?","options":{"A":"Industry raises energy and material demand","B":"Factories always use fewer materials than farms","C":"Industrialisation prevents urban growth and migration","D":"Manufacturing removes the need for water supplies"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-EXPLOITATION","scaffold":"Industrial growth requires energy, water, minerals and land, while also generating waste. Alongside population growth, urbanisation and rising affluence, this increases demand for the biosphere's goods and regulating services.","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination is most likely to raise national resource consumption?","options":{"A":"Falling population and less industrial output","B":"Lower incomes and reduced urbanisation","C":"Rising affluence and industrial growth","D":"Less manufacturing and lower energy demand"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-26","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"On a population projection graph, what does a widening gap between high and low projections show?","options":{"A":"Both projections predict identical population totals","B":"Uncertainty grows further into the future","C":"Population must stop changing after the base year","D":"The lower projection has the steeper growth rate"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-POPULATION-THEORY","scaffold":"Population projections use assumptions about future fertility, mortality and migration. As the time horizon increases, different assumptions compound, so the range between scenarios often becomes wider.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should a future population projection be treated as a scenario rather than a certain forecast?","options":{"A":"Population graphs cannot include any measured data","B":"Past population has no link to future change","C":"Every country follows the same demographic path","D":"Future fertility and mortality may change"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-27","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"A graph shows food supply rising faster after farmers adopt higher-yield crops. Which theory does this evidence support most directly?","options":{"A":"Malthus, because innovation lowers carrying capacity","B":"Malthus, because technology cannot affect supply","C":"Boserup, because pressure stimulates innovation","D":"Neither, because resources never change over time"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-POPULATION-THEORY","scaffold":"Boserup argued that population pressure can encourage technological change and agricultural intensification, increasing resource supply. Malthus expected population growth to outpace food supply unless checked by higher deaths or lower births.","reforge":{"stem":"A population repeatedly exceeds available food and is reduced by famine. Which interpretation fits Malthus most closely?","options":{"A":"A positive check restores balance","B":"Innovation always expands food supply first","C":"Resources are unrelated to population size","D":"Affluence removes every environmental limit"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-28","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Why does temperate deciduous forest have a seasonal growing cycle?","options":{"A":"The soil remains permanently frozen","B":"Rainfall is absent throughout summer","C":"Trees cannot photosynthesise in any season","D":"Seasonal temperature and daylight"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-ADAPTATION","scaffold":"Warm summers allow growth, while colder, darker winters lead many trees to lose their leaves.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do many temperate trees shed leaves before winter?","options":{"A":"Leaves become unnecessary in high rainfall","B":"It reduces water loss and frost damage","C":"Roots stop absorbing minerals forever","D":"It increases exposure to winter storms"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-29","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Why are extensive grasslands found where rainfall is moderate but seasonal?","options":{"A":"Water limits continuous tree cover","B":"Grass cannot survive dry periods","C":"Rainfall always supports dense tall forest","D":"Trees grow faster than grass in every climate"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Seasonal moisture can support grasses while drought and fire restrict dense forest development.","reforge":{"stem":"What change could convert some grassland toward scrub or woodland?","options":{"A":"A permanent reduction in precipitation","B":"Greater grazing pressure every year","C":"More moisture and less burning","D":"Longer periods of severe drought"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-30","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Which adaptation helps a desert plant conserve water?","options":{"A":"Broad thin leaves with many open stomata","B":"A waxy surface and reduced leaves","C":"Shallow roots confined to one small point","D":"Soft tissue exposed to midday heat"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-ADAPTATION","scaffold":"Waxy surfaces, spines and specialised roots reduce water loss or improve access to scarce rainfall.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can widely spreading shallow roots benefit a desert plant?","options":{"A":"They reach permanently frozen groundwater","B":"They increase transpiration during drought","C":"They prevent any competition for light","D":"They absorb brief surface rainfall quickly"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-31","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How does permafrost influence tundra vegetation?","options":{"A":"It creates deep freely drained soil","B":"It lengthens the growing season greatly","C":"It restricts drainage and root depth","D":"It allows large tree roots to penetrate deeply"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-LOCAL-FACTORS","scaffold":"Frozen subsoil limits rooting and can cause waterlogging during the short summer thaw.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are tundra plants commonly low-growing?","options":{"A":"Low growth reduces exposure to cold winds","B":"They compete beneath a dense forest canopy","C":"Permafrost supplies abundant deep nutrients","D":"Low plants receive no summer sunlight"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-32","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How can underlying rock alter vegetation within one climate zone?","options":{"A":"All rocks weather into identical soil","B":"Rock changes the latitude of a place","C":"Rock type determines global wind direction","D":"It influences soil depth, pH and nutrients"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-LOCAL-FACTORS","scaffold":"Parent material affects soil chemistry, texture and drainage, favouring different plant communities locally.","reforge":{"stem":"Which vegetation difference could result from acidic and alkaline soils?","options":{"A":"Soil pH affects only animal migration","B":"Soil-specific plant tolerance","C":"Both soils must support identical species","D":"Alkaline soil prevents all decomposition"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-33","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How does the biosphere help regulate atmospheric composition?","options":{"A":"Carbon storage and oxygen release","B":"Animals prevent gases mixing globally","C":"Plants release only carbon dioxide","D":"Soils remove all oxygen from the air"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-SERVICES","scaffold":"Vegetation absorbs carbon dioxide and releases oxygen, while ecosystems store carbon in biomass and soils.","reforge":{"stem":"What would reduce a forest's carbon-regulating service?","options":{"A":"Protection from repeated burning","B":"Natural regeneration of native trees","C":"Large-scale removal of living biomass","D":"Increasing soil organic-matter storage"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-34","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How do decomposers help maintain soil health?","options":{"A":"They remove every mineral from the soil","B":"They release nutrients from dead matter","C":"They prevent litter reaching the ground","D":"They stop plants taking up nutrients"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Decomposers break down organic material, returning nutrients to soil for reuse by producers.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a likely effect if cold conditions slow decomposition?","options":{"A":"Litter disappears more rapidly","B":"Soil respiration stops on every warm day","C":"Nutrients cycle instantly into plants","D":"Nutrients remain stored in litter longer"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-35","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Which is commercial exploitation of a water resource?","options":{"A":"A household collects rainwater for daily use","B":"A community uses a spring for drinking","C":"A company bottles groundwater for sale","D":"Wildlife drinks from a forest stream"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-EXPLOITATION","scaffold":"Commercial exploitation extracts ecosystem goods at scale for markets rather than primarily for local subsistence.","reforge":{"stem":"What question best evaluates commercial water extraction?","options":{"A":"Withdrawal compared with recharge","B":"Whether the company logo uses blue colours","C":"Whether all groundwater tastes identical","D":"Whether rain falls on another continent"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-36","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"How can rising affluence increase pressure on food resources?","options":{"A":"Wealth removes the need for agricultural land","B":"Higher incomes always reduce total consumption","C":"Affluence prevents food waste and imports","D":"Demand for meat and processed food may rise"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DEMAND","scaffold":"Richer diets often require more land, water and energy, especially where livestock and processed foods increase.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can meat-rich diets have large resource footprints?","options":{"A":"Livestock need no feed or water","B":"Feed conversion requires land and energy","C":"Meat production uses only urban land","D":"All livestock permanently improve biodiversity"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-37","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Why can rapid urbanisation increase water demand?","options":{"A":"Households and industry concentrate demand","B":"Urban residents collect no water at all","C":"Cities eliminate industrial and domestic use","D":"Piped systems always reduce total demand"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DEMAND","scaffold":"Growing cities require water for homes, services, sanitation, energy and manufacturing.","reforge":{"stem":"Which strategy addresses urban water demand most sustainably?","options":{"A":"Allow untreated sewage into water sources","B":"Extract groundwater faster than recharge","C":"Reduce leakage and promote efficient use","D":"Ignore seasonal supply variation"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-38","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"What is a Malthusian preventative check?","options":{"A":"Famine raising the death rate","B":"Later marriage reducing birth rates","C":"Disease following severe food shortage","D":"War increasing mortality rapidly"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-POPULATION-THEORY","scaffold":"Preventative checks lower birth rates; positive checks such as famine, disease and war raise death rates.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a positive check in Malthus's theory?","options":{"A":"Voluntary reduction in family size","B":"Technology raising crop yields","C":"Delayed marriage during hard times","D":"Famine increasing mortality"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-39","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"What is one environmental cost of Boserupian agricultural intensification?","options":{"A":"Innovation always reduces fertiliser use","B":"Higher yields require no water","C":"Inputs may pollute soil and water","D":"Intensification prevents soil erosion"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-POPULATION-THEORY","scaffold":"Technology can expand food supply but irrigation, fertilisers and pesticides may create environmental trade-offs.","reforge":{"stem":"What makes an evaluation of Boserup balanced?","options":{"A":"Compare higher yields with environmental costs","B":"Assume innovation has only benefits","C":"Ignore whether poorer farmers can afford inputs","D":"Treat carrying capacity as permanently fixed"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-40","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"Why do tundra and taiga form broad belts across high northern latitudes?","options":{"A":"Ocean currents have no regional influence","B":"Governments planted them in straight lines","C":"Every high-latitude soil has identical geology","D":"Large-scale climatic control"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Latitude shapes temperature, sunshine and growing-season length, producing broad biome patterns modified locally by other factors.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a mountain show several vegetation zones over a short distance?","options":{"A":"Rock type becomes identical with height","B":"Temperature decreases with altitude","C":"Latitude changes rapidly up the slope","D":"Drainage has no effect on vegetation"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-41","spec":"GCSE-BIO","stem":"A climate graph shows temperatures from 4°C to 19°C. What is the annual temperature range?","options":{"A":"15°C","B":"19°C","C":"4°C","D":"23°C"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-CLIMATE-GRAPH","scaffold":"Temperature range is the highest monthly mean minus the lowest: 19 - 4 = 15°C.","reforge":{"stem":"Monthly temperatures range from -6°C to 14°C. What is the annual range?","options":{"A":"8°C","B":"14°C","C":"20°C","D":"18°C"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-POPULATION-THEORY","stem":"An island can support 5,000 people indefinitely with its own water and food. Which term describes that limit?","options":{"A":"Its ecological footprint, measured in global hectares per person living there","B":"Its carrying capacity — the population the environment can sustain long term","C":"Its population density, calculated as the number of people per square kilometre","D":"Its demographic transition stage, based on the island's birth and death rates"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Carrying capacity is about supply meeting demand indefinitely. Exceeding it does not stop a population immediately; it degrades the resource base, so the ceiling itself falls. Technology and trade can raise the effective ceiling, which is exactly the disagreement between Malthus and Boserup.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do geographers argue that a country's carrying capacity is not a fixed number?","options":{"A":"Because carrying capacity depends only on the total land area available","B":"Because carrying capacity is set by international agreement between governments","C":"Because population size never changes enough to test the limit in practice","D":"Technology, trade and farming methods change how many people the land supports"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-POPULATION-THEORY","stem":"The Club of Rome's 'Limits to Growth' modelled resource use and concluded growth would eventually be constrained. Whose thinking does this most closely follow?","options":{"A":"Boserup, because it assumes innovation will always solve any resource shortage","B":"Neither, because it makes no claim at all about resources running short","C":"Malthus, because it argues finite resources ultimately limit population growth","D":"Rostow, because it describes five sequential stages of economic development"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Limits to Growth is neo-Malthusian: it accepts technology helps but argues exponential growth against finite resources still ends in constraint. Boserupian critics reply that the model underestimated substitution and efficiency gains. Being able to place a modern argument on that spectrum is what higher marks reward.","reforge":{"stem":"How would a Boserupian respond to a warning that the world will run out of food?","options":{"A":"By arguing that necessity drives innovation, so yields rise as pressure grows","B":"By arguing population growth will halt long before food supply is tested","C":"By pointing out that the food supply has never once increased at any point in history","D":"By agreeing that famine and disease are the only possible outcomes"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-SERVICES","stem":"A forest is valued by local people as a sacred site and by visitors for its scenery. Which category of ecosystem service is this?","options":{"A":"Provisioning services, because the forest supplies timber and food to people","B":"Regulating services, because the forest controls local climate and water flow","C":"Supporting services, because the forest cycles nutrients through its soils","D":"Cultural services — the non-material benefits people gain from an ecosystem"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The four categories are provisioning (goods taken out), regulating (processes controlled), supporting (the underpinning cycles) and cultural (spiritual, recreational and aesthetic value). Cultural services are the easiest to overlook in a cost-benefit case precisely because they are hardest to price, which is often the heart of a conflict over development.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are cultural ecosystem services often underweighted when a development scheme is assessed?","options":{"A":"Because cultural services benefit nobody living in the local area","B":"They are real but hard to give a monetary value, unlike timber or crops","C":"Because cultural services are legally excluded from all planning decisions","D":"Because they only apply to ecosystems that are entirely undisturbed"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-SERVICES","stem":"Nutrient cycling and soil formation are classed as 'supporting' services. What makes them different from the other three categories?","options":{"A":"They are the underlying processes that make the other services possible at all","B":"They benefit only plants and animals, and never provide any benefit to people","C":"They are the only ecosystem services that can be given a reliable market price","D":"They occur exclusively in tropical rainforest and in no other biome anywhere"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Supporting services sit underneath the others: without soil formation, nutrient cycling and photosynthesis there is no timber to provision, no carbon to regulate and no landscape to value culturally. That is why damage to soil is so serious — it removes the base the visible services depend on.","reforge":{"stem":"A scheme protects a wetland's ability to filter water and store floodwater. Which service is being protected?","options":{"A":"A cultural service, because people enjoy visiting the wetland area","B":"A provisioning service, because the wetland supplies fish to local people","C":"A regulating service, because a natural process is controlling water quality","D":"A supporting service, because the wetland is slowly forming new soil over time"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DISTRIBUTION","stem":"Net Primary Productivity is far higher in tropical rainforest than in tundra. What best explains the difference?","options":{"A":"Tundra plants are very much larger and therefore use up more of the available energy","B":"Rainforest has year-round warmth, light and water, so photosynthesis never stops","C":"Tundra receives more solar energy each year than the tropical rainforest does","D":"Productivity depends only on soil depth, which is greater in the rainforest"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"NPP measures how much energy plants fix beyond their own respiration. It needs warmth, light and water together. The equator supplies all three continuously, so growth is constant; the tundra has a growing season of weeks, low sun angle and frozen water for most of the year, so NPP is among the lowest on Earth.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a hot desert have low Net Primary Productivity despite receiving intense sunlight?","options":{"A":"Deserts receive too little sunlight for plants to photosynthesise at all","B":"Desert soils contain far too many nutrients for plants to tolerate them","C":"Desert plants have no adaptations at all that would allow them to survive the conditions","D":"Water is the limiting factor, so growth is restricted despite the energy available"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-VALUATION","stem":"A significant share of modern medicines derives from wild plant compounds. Why does this strengthen the case for conserving biodiversity?","options":{"A":"Because every plant species currently known has already been fully tested","B":"Because medicines can only ever be produced from plants growing in rainforest","C":"Undiscovered species may hold future value that is lost permanently if they go extinct","D":"Because conserving biodiversity guarantees that a cure will be found for any known disease"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"This is an option value argument: the worth is in keeping the possibility open. Extinction is irreversible, so a species lost before it is studied removes an option permanently. It pairs with the precautionary principle, and it is a useful line in any evaluation of whether a biome is 'worth' protecting.","reforge":{"stem":"Which argument for conservation does the phrase 'we do not yet know what we are losing' best express?","options":{"A":"The option value of biodiversity, since unknown future uses are lost forever","B":"That extinction is reversible if enough specimens are kept in a seed bank","C":"That ecosystems have value only where a direct use has been proven","D":"That biodiversity should only ever be conserved in the very poorest countries"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-CARBON","stem":"Oceans absorb a large share of the carbon dioxide humans emit. What is the main consequence of this for marine ecosystems?","options":{"A":"The oceans release all of the absorbed carbon back to the air within a year","B":"The oceans become steadily more alkaline, which dissolves marine sediments","C":"Absorbing carbon dioxide has no chemical effect on seawater whatsoever","D":"The oceans become more acidic, which weakens shells and coral skeletons"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Dissolved carbon dioxide forms carbonic acid, lowering ocean pH. Organisms building calcium carbonate structures — corals, molluscs, some plankton — must spend more energy to do so, and reefs weaken. The ocean sink therefore slows atmospheric warming while transferring the cost into the marine ecosystem.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the ocean described as a carbon sink?","options":{"A":"Because it emits more carbon dioxide each year than it takes in","B":"It absorbs and stores more carbon from the atmosphere than it releases","C":"Because carbon sinks to the seabed without ever dissolving in the water","D":"Because the ocean converts carbon dioxide directly into oxygen and rock"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-DISTRIBUTION","stem":"Tropical grassland (savanna) has scattered trees and thick grass rather than continuous forest. What maintains this?","options":{"A":"A marked dry season with fire and grazing, which stops forest closing over","B":"Year-round heavy rainfall that drowns tree seedlings before they can establish","C":"Permanently frozen ground preventing tree roots from penetrating the soil","D":"An absence of any grazing animals across the whole of the savanna region"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Savanna sits between rainforest and desert, with rainfall concentrated in one wet season. Grasses regrow from their bases after burning or grazing; tree seedlings mostly do not. Fire and large herbivores therefore hold the biome open, which is why suppressing fire can let scrub take over.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are grasses better suited than young trees to an environment with regular fire?","options":{"A":"Young trees grow back much faster than grasses do after a fire passes","B":"Grasses are entirely fireproof and cannot be burned by any wildfire","C":"Grasses regrow from their base and root system after the top is burned","D":"Fires in grassland biomes only ever burn the soil rather than the plants"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-ADAPTATION","stem":"Mediterranean shrubs often have small, tough, waxy leaves and deep roots. What are these adaptations to?","options":{"A":"Continuous heavy rainfall spread evenly throughout every month of the year","B":"Hot dry summers, so water loss is reduced and deep moisture can be reached","C":"Extremely low temperatures and a growing season lasting only a few weeks","D":"Permanently waterlogged ground with almost no oxygen available to the roots"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Adaptations answer the limiting factor. In a Mediterranean climate the constraint is summer drought, not cold or waterlogging, so plants reduce transpiration with small waxy leaves and reach stored water with deep roots. Matching the adaptation to the specific stress is what distinguishes a strong answer from a list.","reforge":{"stem":"Which adaptation would you expect in a plant growing where the limiting factor is a very short growing season?","options":{"A":"A thick waxy coating to prevent water escaping during a long dry summer","B":"Extremely deep roots reaching water many metres below the surface","C":"Very large thin leaves designed to maximise water loss from the plant","D":"Rapid flowering and seed production within a few weeks of the thaw"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-LOCAL-FACTORS","stem":"In the northern hemisphere, south-facing valley slopes often carry different vegetation from north-facing ones. What is this local factor called?","options":{"A":"Altitudinal zonation, where vegetation changes with height up a mountainside","B":"Latitude, which determines the total solar energy a whole region receives","C":"Aspect — the direction a slope faces, which changes the sunlight it receives","D":"Continentality, the effect of distance from the moderating influence of the sea"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Aspect changes the microclimate over a few hundred metres. A south-facing slope in the northern hemisphere receives more direct sun, so it is warmer and drier, with faster evaporation. That can support entirely different communities from a shaded, damper north-facing slope in the same valley under the same regional climate.","reforge":{"stem":"Two slopes in the same valley have the same rock, rainfall and altitude but different vegetation. What is the most likely cause?","options":{"A":"Aspect gives one slope more sunlight, making it warmer and drier","B":"One slope is in a completely different climate zone from the other","C":"Continentality affects one slope but has no effect on the other one","D":"They lie in different latitude bands despite being in one valley"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-INDIGENOUS","stem":"A national park is created and an Indigenous group is excluded from land it has managed for centuries. Why is this contested?","options":{"A":"Because national parks are always far less effective than having no protection at all","B":"Because Indigenous groups have never managed any land sustainably anywhere","C":"Because creating a national park requires the agreement of every single visitor","D":"Their management often maintained the biodiversity the park was created to protect"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"'Fortress conservation' assumes people and nature must be separated. Evidence often shows the opposite: long-standing practices such as rotational cultivation and controlled burning sustained the diversity now being protected. Excluding those communities can remove the management that produced the value, as well as raising a rights question.","reforge":{"stem":"What does evidence on Indigenous-managed land most often show about deforestation rates?","options":{"A":"Rates are always far higher than on comparable unprotected land nearby","B":"Rates are usually as low as or lower than in formally protected areas","C":"Deforestation never occurs on any land held by an Indigenous group","D":"Deforestation rates cannot be measured on Indigenous land using satellites"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"},{"id":"GCSE-BIO-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-BIO","tag":"MC-GG-BIO-EXPLOITATION","stem":"A fishery is managed with quotas set below the annual reproduction rate of the stock. Why is this described as sustainable use rather than preservation?","options":{"A":"The resource is still used, but only at a rate the stock can replace naturally","B":"Because the fishery is closed completely and no fishing at all is permitted","C":"Because sustainable use means taking resources without any limit being applied","D":"Because quotas guarantee that fish stocks will increase every year without fail"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Preservation means leaving a resource untouched; sustainable use means harvesting within the rate of natural replacement. Quotas below the reproduction rate let the stock persist indefinitely while still supporting an industry. The difficulty is enforcement and knowing the real reproduction rate, which is why stocks still collapse.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a fishing quota still fail to prevent a stock from collapsing?","options":{"A":"Because quotas are only ever applied to those fisheries that are already fully healthy","B":"Because setting any quota automatically causes the fish population to fall","C":"Illegal catches and overestimated stock sizes mean the real take exceeds the limit","D":"Because fish stocks are unaffected by the total quantity that is caught"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-bio"}]},"GCSE-GEO-FORESTS":{"label":"Forests Under Threat","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-FOR-01","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"In a tropical rainforest food web, the Zam tree relies on agouti to disperse its seeds, and leaf-cutter ants rely on Zam tree seedlings. If agouti are overhunted, what is the most likely knock-on effect?","options":{"A":"Fewer Zam trees means fewer ants, frogs and snakes","B":"There would be no effect on any other species in the food web","C":"The Zam tree population would increase because there is less competition for its seeds","D":"Leaf-cutter ant numbers would rise because they are unaffected by tree numbers"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-INTERDEPENDENCE","scaffold":"This is a classic keystone-species/interdependence chain question. Agouti disperse Zam tree seeds → fewer agouti means fewer seeds spread and fewer new trees germinate → less food for leaf-cutter ants → less food for tree frogs that eat the ants → less food for snakes that eat the frogs. A single change ripples through the whole food web because rainforest components are interdependent.","reforge":{"stem":"The Zam tree is pollinated by a specific butterfly species, and beetles lay eggs in that butterfly's faeces. If the Zam tree population declines, what happens to the beetle population?","options":{"A":"Beetles would switch permanently to a completely different food source with no population change","B":"Beetle numbers would increase due to more available habitat","C":"It declines too, since fewer flowers mean fewer butterflies","D":"Beetle numbers are entirely unrelated to tree or butterfly populations"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-02","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Which adaptation helps tropical rainforest trees such as those with buttress roots survive in the ecosystem?","options":{"A":"Buttress roots allow trees to store water during droughts","B":"Buttress roots anchor tall trees with shallow roots","C":"Buttress roots protect trees from cold winter temperatures","D":"Buttress roots help trees avoid competition for sunlight"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-ADAPTATION","scaffold":"Rainforest plant adaptations: waxy leaves with drip tips (shed water fast, preventing mould/leaf damage), buttress roots (extra above-ground support since soil nutrients and therefore roots stay shallow), lianas (vines climbing tall trees to reach sunlight), epiphytes (grow on trunks/branches, absorbing nutrients from air/rain/debris), and smooth trunks (reduce epiphyte colonisation).","reforge":{"stem":"A sloth has algae growing in its fur, and a toucan has an unusually large bill. What do these adaptations have in common?","options":{"A":"Both adaptations evolved specifically to cope with cold temperatures","B":"Both adaptations are found only in desert animals","C":"Neither adaptation provides any survival advantage","D":"Each helps the animal meet a rainforest challenge"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-03","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why is nutrient cycling in a tropical rainforest described as 'rapid', despite the soil itself being relatively infertile?","options":{"A":"Rainforest soils naturally contain the highest nutrient reserves of any biome","B":"Nutrient cycling in rainforests is actually the slowest of any biome","C":"Rapid uptake keeps nutrients in the living biomass","D":"Rainforest plants do not require nutrients from the soil"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-NUTRIENT","scaffold":"The heat and moisture of the rainforest accelerate decomposition (leaf litter breaks down fast) and plant growth (rapid nutrient uptake), so nutrients cycle quickly between biomass, litter and soil rather than accumulating in any one store. Because most nutrients end up locked in the standing biomass rather than the soil, the soil itself stays infertile — which is why cleared rainforest land quickly becomes unproductive for farming.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does clearing tropical rainforest for agriculture typically produce only a few years of good crop yields before the soil becomes unproductive?","options":{"A":"The nutrients were in the trees, and left with them","B":"Rainforest soil is naturally the most fertile soil type and never loses productivity","C":"Crop failure in cleared rainforest is caused entirely by lack of rainfall","D":"Nutrient levels in rainforest soil are unaffected by vegetation removal"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-04","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"In the taiga, salmon are described as a keystone species. Why does a decline in salmon numbers have a disproportionately large impact on the ecosystem?","options":{"A":"Salmon have no predators in the taiga ecosystem","B":"Wolves and bears do not rely on fish for any part of their diet","C":"Salmon numbers have no effect on any other taiga species","D":"Salmon are a key food source for bears and wolves"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-INTERDEPENDENCE","scaffold":"A keystone species has an outsized effect on its ecosystem relative to its numbers. Salmon migrate upstream and are a vital food source for bears and wolves — a barrier like a dam blocking migration, or disease affecting salmon, cuts that food supply and directly reduces predator populations. Scots Pine is the taiga's other named keystone species, supporting squirrels, voles, mice, insects, lichens and mosses through its bark and cones.","reforge":{"stem":"A dam is built on a river that salmon use to migrate upstream in the taiga. What is the most likely ecological consequence?","options":{"A":"Dams have no effect on any migratory species","B":"Salmon cannot reach spawning grounds, so predators lose food","C":"Salmon numbers would increase because dams provide additional habitat","D":"Wolf populations would be entirely unaffected since they do not rely on salmon"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-05","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why do coniferous trees in the taiga have a conical shape and needle-like leaves?","options":{"A":"A conical shape sheds snow and waxy needles cut water loss","B":"These features help the trees absorb more sunlight during long summer days","C":"The conical shape helps trees survive drought conditions in hot climates","D":"These features are unrelated to survival and are purely decorative"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-ADAPTATION","scaffold":"Taiga tree adaptations: conical shape (sheds snow, preventing branch damage), needle-shaped waxy leaves (reduce transpiration/water loss and resist frost damage), flexible branches (bend rather than break under snow weight), wide shallow roots (avoid frozen ground/permafrost while anchoring against strong winds), and seeds protected inside cones.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do many taiga animals such as the snowshoe hare change fur colour between summer (brown) and winter (white)?","options":{"A":"Only taiga plants change colour seasonally, not animals","B":"Fur colour change has no survival benefit and occurs randomly","C":"It gives seasonal camouflage against snow or bare ground","D":"It is purely a response to temperature with no camouflage function"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-06","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why does the taiga have lower Net Primary Productivity (NPP) than the tropical rainforest?","options":{"A":"The taiga receives more sunlight hours per year than the rainforest","B":"Cold winters and low light restrict growth and decomposition","C":"The taiga has a longer growing season than the rainforest","D":"NPP is identical across all forest biomes"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-NUTRIENT","scaffold":"NPP measures the rate of energy production by plants (photosynthesis minus respiration). The taiga's limiting factors: long cold winters, short 2–3 month growing season, low precipitation, slow chemical weathering, and nutrients stuck in a slowly-decomposing litter layer rather than the biomass. The rainforest has none of these limits, hence far higher NPP.","reforge":{"stem":"A 4-mark exam question asks: 'Explain why the taiga forest biome has very low levels of productivity.' Which answer chain would score full marks?","options":{"A":"The taiga is cold (1)","B":"Productivity is low because there are lots of trees (1)","C":"The taiga has high productivity, not low (1)","D":"Cold winters (1), low light (1), locked nutrients (1)"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-07","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Which countries experience the highest rates of tropical rainforest deforestation?","options":{"A":"Only countries with no rainforest at all","B":"Canada, Russia and Scandinavia","C":"Malaysia, Brazil, India and Indonesia","D":"The UK, France and Germany"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RATES","scaffold":"Malaysia, Brazil, India and Indonesia have the highest tropical deforestation rates. Note that deforestation is rarely caused by a single factor — settlement growth also drives agricultural clearance to feed the new population, so causes are usually linked together rather than isolated. Wildfires are a natural cause, but their frequency and severity have increased due to human-induced climate change.","reforge":{"stem":"A student explains deforestation in Indonesia purely as 'logging for timber'. Why is this an incomplete answer?","options":{"A":"Causes are linked — logging roads open land for farming","B":"Logging is the only cause of deforestation anywhere in the world","C":"Timber logging has no connection to any other human activity","D":"Settlement growth never contributes to deforestation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-08","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"How can drought act as an indirect threat to the tropical rainforest, beyond the direct loss of water?","options":{"A":"Droughts always increase rainforest biodiversity","B":"Drought conditions immediately restore rainforest nutrient levels","C":"Droughts have no effect on rainforest ecosystems beyond a temporary lack of water","D":"Dry litter kills decomposers, cutting soil fertility"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-CAUSES","scaffold":"Climate change is the most significant indirect threat, mainly through altered rainfall. The 2015/16 drought and resulting wildfires destroyed around 2.5 billion trees and plants. Drought effects cascade: dead decomposers → lower soil fertility → disrupted nutrient cycle; excess leaf loss → less food for herbivores → food web impacts; reduced evapotranspiration → potentially even less future rainfall; and during drought the rainforest can flip to emitting CO2 rather than absorbing it.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a severe rainforest drought make future droughts more likely in the same region?","options":{"A":"Fewer leaves always increase future rainfall","B":"Less transpiration recycles less moisture, lowering rainfall","C":"Droughts permanently increase soil fertility, preventing future droughts","D":"Droughts have no relationship to future rainfall patterns"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-09","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"How does acid rain damage taiga forests, and where does it originate?","options":{"A":"Sulphur and nitrogen oxides make rain that strips nutrients","B":"Acid rain is caused directly by logging activity within the taiga itself","C":"Acid rain only affects tropical biomes, not the taiga","D":"Acid rain has no measurable effect on coniferous trees"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-THREAT","scaffold":"Acid rain forms when SO2 and NOx (released by burning fossil fuels, often far from the taiga itself) react with atmospheric water, lowering rainfall pH below 5.7. Impacts: damaged needles reduce photosynthesis, depleted soil calcium weakens trees, and weakened trees become more vulnerable to pests and disease — an important point because the source of acid rain often needs tackling far outside the taiga region where the damage appears.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is tackling acid rain damage to the taiga particularly difficult compared with tackling a threat like logging?","options":{"A":"Acid rain is not linked to any human industrial activity","B":"Acid rain has an identical, easily local solution to logging","C":"Acid rain travels far, so the source lies outside the taiga","D":"Acid rain is generated entirely within the taiga by the trees themselves"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-10","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Between 2000 and 2013, how did the rate of taiga deforestation compare with deforestation in the Amazon over the same period?","options":{"A":"No deforestation occurred in the taiga during this period","B":"Taiga loss ran at one and a half times the Amazon rate","C":"Taiga deforestation was far lower than in the Amazon","D":"Taiga and Amazon deforestation rates were identical"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RATES","scaffold":"This statistic often surprises students, since the Amazon dominates media coverage of deforestation — but between 2000–2013, taiga loss (mainly in Canada and Russia) outpaced the Amazon by 1.5×. Main causes in the taiga: logging, mining, and oil/gas drilling, plus indirect impacts like air and water pollution from associated transport and road building.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might taiga deforestation receive less international media attention than Amazon deforestation, despite occurring at a higher rate?","options":{"A":"Taiga deforestation does not actually occur","B":"The Amazon has a lower deforestation rate, explaining the difference in coverage","C":"Media coverage is always proportional to the actual scale of an environmental issue","D":"Attention has centred on the Amazon, not the taiga"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-11","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What is the main aim of REDD (Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation)?","options":{"A":"To ban all logging activity worldwide immediately","B":"To fund the construction of new roads through protected forests","C":"It pays richer countries' money to keep forest standing","D":"To increase the rate of deforestation in developing countries"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-AGREEMENTS","scaffold":"REDD is a UN strategy funded by developed countries and TNCs to offset their carbon emissions, paying to protect forests instead. Strengths: tackles climate change, involves local communities. Weaknesses: no clear definition of what counts as 'forest', corruption and fraud, and unclear forest ownership. Compare with CITES (184 countries, restricts trade in endangered species/timber, but hard to monitor and enforce, with weak sanctions).","reforge":{"stem":"Why might REDD funding fail to achieve its intended protection of a forest area in practice?","options":{"A":"Corruption or unclear land ownership can misdirect the money","B":"REDD has no known weaknesses or implementation challenges","C":"REDD guarantees 100% success in every country that receives funding","D":"REDD funding always goes directly and transparently to local communities"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-12","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What is meant by 'sustainable forestry' as a management strategy for the taiga?","options":{"A":"Sustainable forestry only applies to tropical rainforests, not the taiga","B":"Forests are cleared as quickly as possible to maximise short-term profit","C":"All logging is banned permanently across the entire taiga","D":"Native species are replanted faster than trees are felled"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-SUSTAINABLE","scaffold":"Sustainable forestry balances the needs of current populations (industry, indigenous communities, wildlife) with future generations by ensuring replanting happens faster than logging removes trees, and natural forest isn't converted into monoculture plantations. The Boreal Forest Platform (industry, e.g. IKEA, working with NGOs like WWF) is a named example, alongside protected areas like Russia's proposed Dvinsky Reserve and Canada's Cape Breton Highlands National Park.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might indigenous communities and oil/gas companies have conflicting views on taiga management, even if both groups say they want to protect the environment?","options":{"A":"Oil and gas companies have no environmental impact on indigenous land","B":"Communities need intact land; companies want the resources","C":"Indigenous communities have no interest in how the taiga is managed","D":"Both groups always want exactly the same outcome with no disagreement"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-FOR","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about rainforest adaptations.","template":"Waxy leaves with drip tips allow rainwater to run off quickly, preventing ___ growth. ___ roots provide extra support for tall trees since below-ground roots are shallow. ___ are vines that climb trees to reach sunlight.","blanks":["mould","Buttress","Lianas"],"bank":["mould","Buttress","Lianas","fungus","Epiphytes","Canopy"],"tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-ADAPTATION","scaffold":"Key rainforest plant adaptations: waxy drip-tip leaves shed water fast (preventing mould and leaf damage), buttress roots give tall trees above-ground stability, and lianas use trees as scaffolding to reach the canopy's sunlight.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-FOR","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about keystone species in the taiga.","template":"___ are a keystone species that provide up to 50% of a wolf's diet, so a decline caused by disease or a ___ blocking migration reduces predator populations. ___ Pine is another taiga keystone species supporting squirrels, voles and insects.","blanks":["Salmon","dam","Scots"],"bank":["Salmon","dam","Scots","Trout","bridge","Silver"],"tag":"MC-GG-FOR-INTERDEPENDENCE","scaffold":"Salmon and Scots Pine are the taiga's two named keystone species. Salmon migration blocked by a dam cuts a major food source for bears and wolves; a decline in Scots Pine removes food and habitat for squirrels, voles, mice, insects, lichens and mosses.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-FOR","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about global forest protection strategies.","template":"___ is a UN strategy that provides financial incentives to protect forests and offset carbon emissions. ___ restricts international trade in endangered species and timber. The ___ approves wood sourced from sustainably managed forests.","blanks":["REDD","CITES","FSC"],"bank":["REDD","CITES","FSC","ITTA","UN","WWF"],"tag":"MC-GG-FOR-AGREEMENTS","scaffold":"REDD (Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation) funds forest protection via carbon credits. CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species) covers over 35,000 species across 184 countries. The FSC (Forest Stewardship Council) certifies sustainably sourced timber. ITTA restricts hardwood timber trade specifically.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-FOR","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about sustainable taiga management.","template":"Sustainable ___ involves replanting logged areas with native species faster than they are used, known as ___. The Boreal Forest ___ is an example of industry and NGOs working together to allow logging without losing biodiversity.","blanks":["forestry","afforestation","Platform"],"bank":["forestry","afforestation","Platform","mining","deforestation","Council"],"tag":"MC-GG-FOR-SUSTAINABLE","scaffold":"Sustainable forestry replants logged areas (afforestation) at a faster rate than logging removes trees, avoiding conversion of natural forest into plantations. The Boreal Forest Platform pairs industry (e.g. IKEA) with NGOs (e.g. WWF) to balance logging with biodiversity protection.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-13","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"A student confuses clear felling and selective logging. What is the correct distinction?","options":{"A":"Clear felling takes every tree; selective logging takes some","B":"Selective logging always removes more trees than clear felling","C":"Clear felling and selective logging are two names for the same process","D":"Clear felling only happens in the taiga, selective logging only in the tropics"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-LOGGING","scaffold":"Clear felling is fast and profitable but devastating — it destroys habitats completely and leaves soil exposed to erosion. Selective logging is slower and less profitable per trip but leaves the forest structure largely intact, making it a key part of sustainable forestry strategies.","reforge":{"stem":"A logging company removes every tree in a section of forest in one operation. What is this method called, and what is its main environmental drawback compared to selective logging?","options":{"A":"Sustainable forestry — it has no environmental drawbacks","B":"Afforestation — it involves planting new trees, not removing them","C":"Clear felling — it strips habitat and exposes the soil","D":"Selective logging — it always removes more trees than clear felling"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-14","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What are buttress roots, and why do tropical rainforest trees rely on them?","options":{"A":"An adaptation only found in taiga conifer trees to survive cold winters","B":"Wide above-ground roots that steady trees in thin soil","C":"Roots that grow underground to store water during droughts","D":"Woody vines that climb from the forest floor up into the canopy"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-ADAPTATION","scaffold":"Because rainforest soil is thin and nutrients cycle rapidly through the biomass rather than the ground, trees can't anchor deep roots the way temperate trees do — buttress roots spread wide above ground to give tall canopy trees the stability they need. Don't confuse them with lianas (climbing woody vines) or epiphytes (plants growing on other plants).","reforge":{"stem":"Why do tall trees in a tropical rainforest rely on wide, above-ground buttress roots rather than deep roots?","options":{"A":"Buttress roots allow trees to climb into the canopy like lianas","B":"Buttress roots are used to store water during droughts","C":"Buttress roots are an adaptation found only in taiga conifer trees","D":"Thin topsoil means deep roots cannot anchor a tall tree"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-15","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What is a drip-tip, and what problem does it solve for rainforest plants?","options":{"A":"A method used by loggers to identify which trees to fell","B":"A root adaptation that helps trees survive drought","C":"A tapered leaf tip that lets heavy rain run off","D":"An animal adaptation for hibernating through winter"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-ADAPTATION","scaffold":"Tropical rainforests receive extremely high rainfall — if water pooled on leaves it would encourage the growth of mould, fungi and bacteria in the hot, humid climate. The tapered drip-tip lets water shed quickly, keeping the leaf surface drier.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a leaf with a drip-tip be less likely to develop fungal growth in a tropical rainforest than a leaf without one?","options":{"A":"Its tapered tip sheds rain instead of letting it pool","B":"Drip-tips prevent all rainfall from reaching the leaf entirely","C":"Drip-tips are an adaptation for surviving cold winters","D":"Drip-tips have no effect on how water behaves on a leaf"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-16","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What is the difference between commercial farming and subsistence farming, and why does this distinction matter for deforestation?","options":{"A":"Commercial and subsistence farming are identical in scale and purpose","B":"Commercial farming only exists in the taiga, not the rainforest","C":"Subsistence farming always causes more deforestation than commercial farming","D":"Commercial farming sells for profit; subsistence feeds a family"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-CAUSES","scaffold":"Scale is the key exam distinction: commercial agriculture (like cattle ranching or plantations) is a major cause of large-scale tropical deforestation because it's profit-driven and land-hungry, whereas subsistence farming tends to clear much smaller areas to meet a family's own needs.","reforge":{"stem":"A farmer clears a small area of rainforest to grow just enough food to feed their own family. What type of farming is this, and how does its scale compare to cattle ranching?","options":{"A":"Commercial farming — on the same scale as cattle ranching","B":"Subsistence farming — it clears much smaller areas","C":"Plantation farming — always larger in scale than cattle ranching","D":"Subsistence farming always clears more land than commercial farming"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-17","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"How does a debt-for-nature swap work as a forest conservation strategy?","options":{"A":"Debt is cancelled in return for protecting forest","B":"A country sells its forests to pay off international debt","C":"It is a scheme where logging companies swap timber for cash payments","D":"It is another name for REDD+"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-AGREEMENTS","scaffold":"Debt-for-nature swaps give indebted countries a direct financial incentive to conserve forest rather than exploit it for short-term income — distinct from REDD, which is specifically a UN-run scheme funding emissions reduction from deforestation and forest degradation.","reforge":{"stem":"A wealthy country agrees to cancel part of a developing country's debt in exchange for that country protecting an area of rainforest. What is this arrangement called?","options":{"A":"Sustainable forestry plans","B":"CITES trade limits","C":"A debt-for-nature swap","D":"REDD+ carbon credits"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-18","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What is the correct definition of biodiversity, and how does deforestation affect it?","options":{"A":"Biodiversity increases automatically after any deforestation","B":"Biodiversity is the variety of life; deforestation cuts it","C":"Biodiversity refers only to the number of tree species, not animals","D":"Biodiversity is a measure of a forest's total biomass, unrelated to species variety"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-INTERDEPENDENCE","scaffold":"Biodiversity loss from deforestation is especially severe in the tropical rainforest, since so many species depend on very specific, complex interdependent relationships within that ecosystem — remove one habitat layer (like the canopy) and multiple dependent species can be affected at once.","reforge":{"stem":"An area of rainforest is cleared for cattle ranching, destroying habitat for thousands of species that lived there. What has been most directly lost as a result?","options":{"A":"Carbon sequestration capacity has increased as a result","B":"Nutrient cycling, which always increases after deforestation","C":"Nothing of ecological significance has been lost","D":"Biodiversity — the variety of life in that ecosystem"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-19","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What is leaching, and why does it make deforested rainforest soil less fertile?","options":{"A":"Leaching only occurs in the taiga, not the tropical rainforest","B":"Leaching increases soil fertility over time","C":"Rain washes minerals out of soil left bare by felling","D":"Leaching is the process of trees absorbing nutrients from the soil"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-NUTRIENT","scaffold":"In an intact rainforest, most nutrients are stored in the living biomass and cycled rapidly, not stored deep in the soil. Once trees are cleared, heavy tropical rainfall leaches remaining nutrients out of the exposed soil quickly, which is why deforested rainforest land is often unproductive within just a few years.","reforge":{"stem":"Shortly after a rainforest is cleared, heavy rainfall washes most of the remaining soil nutrients away. What is this process called, and why does it happen so quickly?","options":{"A":"Leaching — nutrients sat in the trees, not deep in the soil","B":"Nutrient cycling — a beneficial process that improves soil fertility after deforestation","C":"Leaching only occurs in the taiga, never in a rainforest","D":"Leaching increases soil fertility over time"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-20","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What is CITES, and what is its purpose?","options":{"A":"A private company that manages tropical plantations","B":"A United Nations scheme that pays countries to reduce deforestation","C":"An agreement specifically about reducing carbon emissions from factories","D":"CITES — an agreement restricting trade in endangered species"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-AGREEMENTS","scaffold":"CITES targets the trade side of species loss — regulating or banning international trade in endangered animals and plants (like ivory or rare timber) — which complements habitat-focused strategies like conservation areas and sustainable forestry.","reforge":{"stem":"An international agreement restricts the global trade of ivory to protect endangered elephants from exploitation. Which agreement is this most likely to be?","options":{"A":"Sustainable forestry certification","B":"CITES trade restrictions","C":"A debt-for-nature swap deal","D":"REDD+ carbon funding scheme"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-21","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why can selective logging be less damaging than clear-cutting, but still require careful management?","options":{"A":"It keeps some canopy, but roads still fragment the forest","B":"It removes every tree but leaves the soil untouched","C":"It prevents all carbon emissions from forestry","D":"It is the same as converting rainforest into a plantation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-LOGGING","scaffold":"Selective logging can retain more canopy, roots and biodiversity than clear-cutting if extraction is limited. However, roads fragment habitats, machinery damages remaining trees and repeated logging can degrade the forest, so monitoring matters.","reforge":{"stem":"Which practice would make selective logging more sustainable?","options":{"A":"Build as many access roads as possible","B":"Remove all mature trees in one operation","C":"Set limits, plan routes, and monitor and replant","D":"Burn the remaining vegetation after every harvest"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-22","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"How can agroforestry reduce pressure on tropical rainforests?","options":{"A":"It replaces all farming with mining","B":"It mixes trees with crops, keeping canopy and soil cover","C":"It requires farmers to remove every tree before planting","D":"It makes soils fertile without any nutrient cycling"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-SUSTAINABLE","scaffold":"Agroforestry integrates useful trees with crops or livestock. Trees can provide fruit, fuelwood, shade, habitat and carbon storage, while farmers earn income from several products.","reforge":{"stem":"A farmer grows cocoa beneath retained shade trees and harvests fruit and timber as well. Which benefit does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"A single-crop plantation with no biodiversity","B":"Mining as a conservation strategy","C":"Clear-cutting followed by permanent soil exposure","D":"Agroforestry spreading income and keeping habitat"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-23","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why are emergent trees found above the main rainforest canopy?","options":{"A":"They obtain nutrients from permanently frozen soil","B":"They avoid all exposure to wind and rain","C":"They grow towards stronger sunlight","D":"They survive because the forest has no competition"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-ADAPTATION","scaffold":"Rainforest vegetation is stratified because plants compete intensely for light. Emergent trees rise above the canopy, while shade-tolerant vegetation occupies lower layers where less sunlight penetrates.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do many rainforest lianas climb tree trunks?","options":{"A":"They reach light without thick trunks","B":"They need frozen ground for their roots","C":"They reduce rainfall reaching every canopy leaf","D":"They obtain energy by feeding on tree bark"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-24","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What does a complex tropical rainforest food web indicate?","options":{"A":"Each species feeds on only one other organism","B":"Decomposers have no role in nutrient cycling","C":"Energy returns from consumers to the Sun","D":"Species are strongly interdependent"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-INTERDEPENDENCE","scaffold":"A complex food web contains many feeding relationships. This can create resilience through alternative links, but the loss of an important or keystone species may still cause knock-on effects across several trophic levels.","reforge":{"stem":"A seed-dispersing animal disappears from a rainforest food web. What is a likely long-term consequence?","options":{"A":"All plants immediately become decomposers","B":"Dependent tree recruitment may decline","C":"The soil gains nutrients without any litter","D":"Every predator gains an alternative food source"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-25","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"A nutrient-cycle diagram has a large biomass store and rapid transfers from litter to soil. Which biome does it most likely represent?","options":{"A":"Tropical rainforest","B":"Taiga with a long frozen winter","C":"Tundra with widespread permafrost","D":"Cold desert with sparse vegetation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-NUTRIENT","scaffold":"Warm, wet rainforest conditions support rapid decomposition and plant uptake. Most nutrients are held in biomass, while the litter and soil stores remain relatively small because transfers between them are fast.","reforge":{"stem":"Which nutrient-cycle pattern is most typical of the taiga?","options":{"A":"Rapid decomposition throughout every season","B":"Most nutrients stored in lush broad-leaved biomass","C":"Slow transfers and a large litter store","D":"No litter store because needles decay instantly"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-26","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"How can hydroelectric development cause tropical deforestation?","options":{"A":"Reservoirs increase the area of intact forest","B":"Reservoirs flood forest and access roads fragment it","C":"Electricity generation removes demand for land","D":"Dams prevent any road construction or further settlement in the region"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-CAUSES","scaffold":"HEP schemes can clear forest directly for dams, reservoirs and transmission lines. New access roads may also open previously remote areas to logging, settlement, farming and mineral extraction.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can mineral extraction damage forest beyond the mine itself?","options":{"A":"Mine waste cannot enter rivers or soils","B":"Mining always increases canopy cover nearby","C":"Ore extraction requires no water or transport","D":"Roads open forest to further clearance"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-27","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What is one advantage of GIS when investigating forest loss?","options":{"A":"It removes cloud cover from all satellite images","B":"It proves the cause of every cleared patch","C":"It reveals spatial patterns over time","D":"It guarantees all mapped data are accurate"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RATES","scaffold":"GIS can layer and compare satellite images, roads, protected areas and land uses to reveal where forest loss is concentrated and how patterns change. Further evidence is still needed to establish causes.","reforge":{"stem":"A GIS map shows clearance clustered beside new roads. What is the safest conclusion?","options":{"A":"There is a spatial association","B":"Road building is the only possible cause","C":"The map proves all logging is illegal","D":"Forest loss is evenly distributed"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-28","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why can oil extraction threaten taiga biodiversity indirectly?","options":{"A":"Oil wells make winters warmer at every location","B":"Animals cannot migrate through any intact forest","C":"Extraction converts conifers into tropical species","D":"Pipelines and roads fragment habitats"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-THREAT","scaffold":"Energy development brings pipelines, roads and settlements as well as the extraction site. These divide habitats, disturb migration, increase spill risk and make remote forest more accessible to further exploitation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which impact is most likely where a pipeline crosses a taiga migration route?","options":{"A":"Every species gains a larger continuous habitat","B":"Movement and breeding may be disrupted","C":"Nutrient cycling immediately becomes tropical","D":"The route prevents all human access"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-29","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why do views conflict over creating a protected taiga wilderness?","options":{"A":"Conservation can restrict resource-based livelihoods","B":"All stakeholders value timber in the same way","C":"Protection has no effects on jobs, incomes, habitats or ecosystems","D":"National parks permit unlimited commercial logging"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Conservation groups and Indigenous communities may prioritise habitats and cultural value, while firms and some workers may emphasise timber, minerals, energy and employment. The conflict is about how benefits and costs are distributed.","reforge":{"stem":"Which stakeholder is most likely to oppose a ban on logging in a taiga town?","options":{"A":"A group restoring undisturbed wilderness","B":"Scientists monitoring rare migratory birds","C":"Workers dependent on the local sawmill","D":"Tourists seeking intact forest landscapes"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-30","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What is a possible limitation of REDD forest protection payments?","options":{"A":"REDD makes standing forest economically worthless","B":"Weak monitoring can allow continued clearance","C":"It applies only to renewable energy production","D":"It bans every local use of forest resources worldwide"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-AGREEMENTS","scaffold":"REDD can make forest conservation financially attractive by rewarding reduced emissions from deforestation. Its success depends on reliable baselines, monitoring, governance and fair distribution of payments; clearance may also shift elsewhere.","reforge":{"stem":"What would most improve confidence that a REDD scheme has reduced deforestation?","options":{"A":"Ignoring forest loss outside the project boundary","B":"Removing all satellite and field evidence","C":"Paying only after timber exports increase","D":"Independent monitoring against a baseline"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-31","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why do many taiga animals migrate seasonally?","options":{"A":"Summer food is absent from the biome","B":"Migration makes conifer trees grow faster","C":"Tracking food and milder conditions","D":"All taiga predators hibernate underground"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-ADAPTATION","scaffold":"Seasonal migration helps animals exploit summer productivity and escape extreme winter cold or food shortage.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change could disrupt a migratory taiga species?","options":{"A":"Roads fragmenting movement corridors","B":"Greater availability of seasonal food","C":"Continuous protected habitat along its route","D":"Reduced disturbance near breeding grounds"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-32","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"How do evergreen needles help conifers use the short taiga growing season?","options":{"A":"Needles fall before every period of sunlight","B":"Broad leaves develop under winter snow","C":"Trees stop photosynthesis throughout summer","D":"Leaves are ready when conditions improve"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-ADAPTATION","scaffold":"Retaining needles avoids the time and nutrients needed to regrow a full canopy each short summer.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a waxy needle useful in the taiga?","options":{"A":"It makes branches hold heavier snow","B":"It reduces water loss when water is frozen","C":"It absorbs nutrients directly from snow","D":"It increases the surface area exposed to wind"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-33","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why are many taiga podzol soils acidic and nutrient-poor?","options":{"A":"Slow decay and leaching remove nutrients","B":"Rapid decomposition releases abundant nutrients","C":"Warm conditions build a thick fertile topsoil","D":"Broad leaves neutralise all soil acids"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-NUTRIENT","scaffold":"Cold slows decomposition, acidic needles form litter and precipitation leaches minerals downward.","reforge":{"stem":"How does slow decomposition affect the taiga nutrient cycle?","options":{"A":"It moves all nutrients into biomass rapidly","B":"It prevents a litter layer forming","C":"Nutrients remain locked in litter","D":"It makes the soil identical to rainforest soil"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-34","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why might warmer winters increase pest damage in the taiga?","options":{"A":"Pests require permanent snow cover","B":"More pests survive to reproduce","C":"Cold winters cause every insect to reproduce","D":"Trees become immune to all disease"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-THREAT","scaffold":"Milder winters can reduce pest mortality and allow outbreaks that weaken or kill large areas of forest.","reforge":{"stem":"What can make pest outbreaks a climate feedback?","options":{"A":"Pests stop decomposition of fallen trees","B":"Insects permanently lower atmospheric carbon","C":"Damaged forests absorb more carbon dioxide","D":"Dead trees store less carbon and may burn"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-35","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"How can severe forest fires contribute to further climate change?","options":{"A":"Burning creates a permanent carbon sink","B":"Fire prevents any future soil respiration","C":"Stored carbon enters the atmosphere","D":"Ash removes all greenhouse gases"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-THREAT","scaffold":"Combustion releases biomass carbon and loss of trees reduces future sequestration; warming may also raise fire risk.","reforge":{"stem":"Which management reduces wildfire risk without removing the whole forest?","options":{"A":"Use controlled burns and firebreaks","B":"Allow fuel to accumulate beside settlements","C":"Ban all monitoring during dry periods","D":"Clear every tree across the biome"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-36","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why can acid precipitation damage forests far from its pollution source?","options":{"A":"Acid gases remain inside factory chimneys","B":"Rainfall cannot cross national borders","C":"Conifers neutralise every acidic compound","D":"Wind transports pollutants"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-THREAT","scaffold":"Sulphur and nitrogen compounds can travel long distances and acidify soils and water downwind.","reforge":{"stem":"Which response best addresses transboundary acid rain?","options":{"A":"Move monitoring stations nearer one tree","B":"International agreements to reduce emissions","C":"Increase sulphur content in power-station fuel","D":"Treat each damaged leaf after rainfall"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-37","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why can local fuel-wood demand cause forest degradation?","options":{"A":"Repeated cutting exceeds regrowth","B":"Gathering always increases tree density","C":"Fuel wood is obtained only from plantations","D":"Local use cannot affect biodiversity"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-CAUSES","scaffold":"Where households depend on wood or charcoal, repeated extraction can thin forest and reduce habitat if regeneration is slower.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action could reduce fuel-wood pressure?","options":{"A":"Prevent access to every alternative fuel","B":"Increase charcoal demand in nearby cities","C":"Provide efficient stoves and alternatives","D":"Remove community rights without consultation"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-38","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why can commercial cattle ranching cause more extensive clearance than subsistence farming?","options":{"A":"Commercial farms never use machinery","B":"Large-scale clearance for markets","C":"Cattle require dense intact canopy","D":"Subsistence plots always cover continents"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-CAUSES","scaffold":"Commercial ranching serves wider markets and often converts continuous forest into pasture at large scale.","reforge":{"stem":"What evidence would distinguish commercial from subsistence clearance?","options":{"A":"No roads connect the farm to buyers","B":"All crops are consumed by one household","C":"The cleared plot supplies only local food","D":"Large output is sold to distant markets"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-39","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"How can ecotourism support rainforest conservation?","options":{"A":"It requires complete removal of wildlife","B":"Tourism revenue automatically reaches everyone","C":"It earns income while maintaining forest value","D":"Visitor travel creates no emissions"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-SUSTAINABLE","scaffold":"Low-impact tourism can fund protection and alternative livelihoods, but leakage, disturbance and unequal benefits must be managed.","reforge":{"stem":"What would make an ecotourism project more locally beneficial?","options":{"A":"Local jobs and decision-making","B":"External firms retain all revenue","C":"Visitor numbers exceed ecological capacity","D":"Traditional access to land is prohibited"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-40","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"How can agroforestry reduce pressure on remaining rainforest?","options":{"A":"It separates crops from all trees","B":"It depends on permanent clear felling","C":"It guarantees no soil nutrients are lost","D":"Crops combined with tree cover"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-SUSTAINABLE","scaffold":"Agroforestry maintains trees alongside crops, providing products, shade, soil protection and habitat.","reforge":{"stem":"Which practice best represents agroforestry?","options":{"A":"Burn the whole plot after one harvest","B":"Grow cocoa beneath retained shade trees","C":"Remove roots before every rainy season","D":"Replace forest with a single paved surface"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-41","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What limits the effectiveness of CITES?","options":{"A":"Weak enforcement permits illegal trade","B":"It protects habitats without enforcement","C":"It applies only to fossil-fuel extraction","D":"It encourages unrestricted wildlife trade"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-AGREEMENTS","scaffold":"CITES regulates international trade in threatened species, but monitoring, enforcement and domestic markets affect success.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action would strengthen CITES implementation?","options":{"A":"Permit undocumented wildlife exports","B":"Remove species-identification training","C":"Improve customs monitoring and penalties","D":"Ignore online trade in protected species"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-42","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"What is forest-protection leakage?","options":{"A":"Trees absorb carbon outside a project","B":"Clearance shifts to another location","C":"Protected forests receive extra rainfall","D":"Payments remain within local communities"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-AGREEMENTS","scaffold":"Protection in one area may displace logging or agriculture elsewhere, reducing the net conservation gain.","reforge":{"stem":"How can monitoring detect leakage?","options":{"A":"Measure only trees inside one small plot","B":"Ignore land-use change beyond a boundary","C":"Record tourism numbers instead of clearance","D":"Compare wider regional forest change"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-43","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why can national-park creation conflict with Indigenous interests?","options":{"A":"Indigenous people never use forest resources","B":"National parks permit all industrial extraction","C":"Rules may restrict traditional land use","D":"Protection always recognises customary tenure"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Conservation imposed without participation can limit livelihoods and rights even when communities have managed forest sustainably.","reforge":{"stem":"Which approach is most likely to reduce this conflict?","options":{"A":"Co-manage the park with Indigenous communities","B":"Exclude residents from every decision","C":"Replace customary rights with logging licences","D":"Move park boundaries without consultation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-44","spec":"GCSE-FOR","stem":"Why is taiga logging important to pulp and paper industries?","options":{"A":"Taiga wood is used solely for fuel","B":"Only tropical hardwood can make paper","C":"Pulp production requires no tree harvesting","D":"Conifers provide abundant softwood fibre"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-THREAT","scaffold":"Large conifer forests supply straight, soft fibres suitable for timber, pulp and paper products.","reforge":{"stem":"Which practice makes softwood production more sustainable?","options":{"A":"Harvest faster than forest regrowth","B":"Replant and maintain mixed-age stands","C":"Clear protected river buffers","D":"Build roads through every intact area"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-INTERDEPENDENCE","stem":"Much of the rain falling on the western Amazon has already fallen once over the forest further east. What process makes this possible?","options":{"A":"Evapotranspiration returns water to the air, which then falls again inland","B":"Rainfall is carried westward entirely by rivers flowing across the basin floor","C":"The forest generates rain by directly cooling the air above its own canopy","D":"Ocean water is carried inland by the trees themselves through their root systems"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Trees draw water up and release it as vapour, so moisture is recycled several times as air moves inland rather than being lost after one rainfall. This is why clearance in one part of the basin can reduce rainfall hundreds of kilometres away, and why the forest is described as making its own climate.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can deforestation in eastern Amazonia reduce rainfall in regions far to the west?","options":{"A":"Because clearing forest changes the direction that the prevailing winds blow","B":"Because removing trees increases the total amount of water stored in rivers","C":"Fewer trees means less moisture recycled into the air travelling inland","D":"Because deforestation immediately raises the temperature of the nearby ocean"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-INTERDEPENDENCE","stem":"Scientists warn the Amazon may reach a 'tipping point' where forest shifts irreversibly towards savanna. What makes this a tipping point rather than gradual decline?","options":{"A":"It describes the exact year in which the very last rainforest tree will die","B":"Past a threshold, drying feeds back on itself and the change becomes self-sustaining","C":"It refers only to the point at which commercial logging finally becomes unprofitable to continue","D":"It means deforestation stops of its own accord once a set area is cleared"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Below the threshold, forest lost can regrow. Beyond it, reduced moisture recycling makes the remaining forest drier, which increases fire and dieback, which dries it further. The feedback drives the change without further clearance, so the system will not return even if deforestation stops.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a positive feedback loop in the context of forest loss?","options":{"A":"A management scheme that rewards landowners for not clearing their forest","B":"A change that produces a beneficial outcome for the forest ecosystem","C":"A process that always returns the forest to its original stable condition","D":"A change that triggers effects which push the same change further along"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-CAUSES","stem":"Satellite images show deforestation in the Amazon spreading outwards in a herringbone pattern from new roads. Why do roads have this effect?","options":{"A":"Road surfaces poison the surrounding soil so that trees can no longer grow there","B":"Roads are always built along the exact line of an existing natural forest clearing","C":"Roads open access, so loggers, ranchers and settlers clear land either side of them","D":"The construction of a road physically removes all of the forest in that whole region"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"A road is an enabler rather than a direct cause. Before it exists the forest is protected by inaccessibility; afterwards, logging, cattle ranching and small-scale settlement all become economic. Clearance therefore radiates from the route, which is why road-building programmes are treated as a major driver in their own right.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do conservation groups often oppose new roads through rainforest even when the road itself clears little forest?","options":{"A":"The access they create enables far more clearance than the road removes","B":"Because roads prevent researchers from reaching the forest to study it","C":"Because roads through forest are always more expensive than other routes","D":"Because road building is prohibited by international conservation treaties"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-CAUSES","stem":"A UK shopper buying processed food may contribute to rainforest clearance without ever visiting one. How?","options":{"A":"Every processed food product sold in the UK contains timber taken from rainforest","B":"Processed food is transported to the UK along roads built inside the rainforest","C":"UK supermarkets are legally required to source all produce from rainforest areas","D":"Palm oil and soy grown on cleared land end up in food and animal feed"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Demand acts at a distance through supply chains. Palm oil appears in a large share of packaged food and cosmetics, and soy is grown mainly as animal feed, both on land that was forest. That is what makes certification schemes and consumer pressure a genuine lever, and why the driver is global rather than local.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main limitation of relying on consumer choice to reduce rainforest clearance?","options":{"A":"Consumers have no influence at all on what supermarkets choose to stock","B":"Supply chains are complex and labelling often does not reveal the true origin","C":"Certified products are always more damaging than uncertified alternatives","D":"Rainforest products are simply never sold to consumers in any higher income country"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RF-ADAPTATION","stem":"Only around 2% of sunlight reaches the rainforest floor. Which adaptation is a direct response to this?","options":{"A":"Lianas that climb existing trunks to reach the light without building a trunk","B":"Buttress roots that provide stability for a very tall tree in shallow soil","C":"Thick waxy leaf coatings that reduce the total amount of water lost by the plant","D":"Drip tips that shed heavy rainfall quickly from the surface of the leaf"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Light is the scarce resource below the canopy, so adaptations compete for it. Lianas and epiphytes use other plants as scaffolding rather than investing years of growth in a trunk of their own. Drip tips and buttress roots are real adaptations, but they answer rainfall and shallow soil, not shade.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do epiphytes such as orchids grow on the branches of other rainforest trees?","options":{"A":"Because they cannot survive in warm conditions anywhere in the canopy","B":"Because they are parasites that take all their nutrients from the host tree","C":"It lifts them into the light without needing to grow their own tall trunk","D":"Because the forest floor receives far too much direct sunlight for them"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-NUTRIENT","stem":"Taiga forests accumulate a thick surface layer of undecomposed needles, unlike rainforest where litter disappears within weeks. Why?","options":{"A":"Taiga trees drop far more leaf litter each year than rainforest trees do","B":"Cold, acidic conditions slow decomposers, so litter builds up faster than it rots","C":"Rainforest soils have no decomposer organisms living within them at all","D":"Needles dropped by coniferous trees are chemically incapable of ever decomposing at all"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Decomposition is temperature and pH dependent. In the taiga the ground is cold or frozen for much of the year and needle litter is acidic, so bacteria and fungi work slowly and material piles up. In the rainforest constant warmth and moisture mean litter is broken down and taken up almost immediately.","reforge":{"stem":"In a Gersmehl nutrient cycle diagram for tropical rainforest, which store is largest?","options":{"A":"The soil store, because rainforest soils are exceptionally deep and fertile","B":"All three stores are exactly equal in size in a tropical rainforest system","C":"The litter store, because dead material accumulates on the forest floor","D":"The biomass store, because nutrients are held in the living vegetation"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-ADAPTATION","stem":"Taiga soils are often waterlogged in summer despite low precipitation. What explains this?","options":{"A":"Summer rainfall in the taiga is among the heaviest recorded anywhere on Earth","B":"Coniferous trees release large volumes of stored water into the soil each summer","C":"Frozen ground beneath blocks drainage, so meltwater sits near the surface","D":"The taiga has no rivers at all, so water cannot drain away from the region"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Precipitation is low, but so is evaporation, and permafrost or a frozen subsoil layer stops water percolating downwards. Meltwater therefore ponds in the active layer above, producing bogs and muskeg. This waterlogging, plus acidity, is why taiga soils are podzols with poor nutrient availability.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the taiga's low rate of evaporation matter for its soils?","options":{"A":"Little water is lost to the air, so modest precipitation still saturates it","B":"Because evaporation is the only process that ever removes any water from a soil","C":"Because low evaporation means the soil dries out completely each summer","D":"Because low evaporation increases the rate at which litter decomposes"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-NUTRIENT","stem":"After rainforest is cleared on a slope, heavy rain removes much of the topsoil within a few seasons. Why is the loss so rapid?","options":{"A":"Cleared soil becomes chemically unable to hold on to any water at all after felling","B":"Rainfall totals increase sharply in the years immediately following clearance","C":"Soil on a cleared slope is removed entirely by the machinery used in logging","D":"Tree roots and canopy no longer bind the soil or intercept the rain hitting it"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The canopy intercepts rainfall so drops do not strike bare soil at full force, and roots hold particles in place. Remove both and raindrop impact plus unchecked surface flow strip a thin, already nutrient-poor topsoil. Combined with leaching, this is why cleared rainforest land loses productivity within a few years.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does cleared rainforest land often support crops for only a few years?","options":{"A":"Because rainforest climates become too cold for crops after clearance","B":"Thin nutrients are quickly leached and eroded once the trees are gone","C":"Because crops planted on cleared land cannot germinate in tropical heat","D":"Because the land is always legally returned to forest after three years"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-STAKEHOLDERS","stem":"Studies find deforestation rates on land with recognised Indigenous title are often lower than on nearby unprotected land. What does this suggest about protection strategies?","options":{"A":"Legal recognition of existing land rights can be an effective conservation tool","B":"Only land that is entirely emptied of people can ever be protected successfully","C":"Indigenous communities make no use of forest resources of any kind at all","D":"Formal national parks are the single strategy that reduces deforestation rates"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Securing tenure gives communities both the legal standing and the incentive to exclude loggers and ranchers, and they are present year-round in a way distant enforcement agencies are not. It is cheaper than policing a park and avoids the displacement that makes fortress conservation contested.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can granting land title to forest communities be more effective than distant enforcement?","options":{"A":"Because land title removes all commercial interest in the forest permanently","B":"Because governments are legally barred from enforcing any forest protection","C":"Residents are present continuously and gain a direct stake in protecting it","D":"Because titled land is automatically excluded from all satellite monitoring"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-SUSTAINABLE","stem":"An FSC label certifies that timber came from a sustainably managed forest. What is the main weakness of relying on certification?","options":{"A":"Certified timber is always of noticeably lower quality than uncertified timber","B":"It works only where buyers will pay more, and much logging is illegal or uncertified","C":"Certification schemes are unable to check any forest more than once a decade","D":"Certification applies exclusively to timber that is grown in temperate coniferous forests"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Certification is a market mechanism, so it reaches only the part of the trade that sells to buyers who care and can pay. Illegal logging, domestic markets and fuelwood are largely outside it. It is a real improvement on nothing, but it cannot substitute for enforcement, which is the standard evaluation point.","reforge":{"stem":"Which strategy works by changing what buyers demand rather than by regulating producers?","options":{"A":"A national government banning logging within a designated protected area","B":"Rangers patrolling a national park to intercept illegal logging operations","C":"Satellite monitoring used to detect newly cleared areas of forest quickly","D":"Consumer certification schemes that label sustainably produced timber"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-RATES","stem":"Brazilian Amazon deforestation fell sharply after 2004, then rose again from 2019. What does this pattern best demonstrate?","options":{"A":"Deforestation rates are fixed and cannot be influenced by any human decision","B":"Satellite monitoring of forest loss became unavailable during those later years","C":"Enforcement and government policy strongly affect the rate of forest loss","D":"Deforestation rates depend only on the annual rainfall the region receives"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"The fall followed satellite monitoring, protected-area creation and enforcement against illegal clearance; the rise followed weakened enforcement and budget cuts. This matters for evaluation: it shows the driver is not simply population or poverty, and that policy change can reverse the trend in either direction quite quickly.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a country's deforestation trend more useful evidence than a single year's figure?","options":{"A":"A trend shows whether policy is working, rather than one year's conditions","B":"Because forest loss is identical in every year, making trends unnecessary","C":"Because a single year's deforestation figure can never be measured accurately","D":"Because trends are only ever calculated for countries with tropical forest"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"},{"id":"GCSE-FOR-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-FOR","tag":"MC-GG-FOR-TAIGA-THREAT","stem":"Thawing permafrost beneath the taiga releases methane. Why does this concern climate scientists particularly?","options":{"A":"The methane released is the only greenhouse gas present in the atmosphere at all","B":"Methane released from permafrost cools the atmosphere and masks the warming","C":"Methane released by thawing permafrost is entirely absorbed by taiga trees","D":"Methane is a potent greenhouse gas, so warming causes further warming"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Permafrost holds carbon frozen for millennia. Warming thaws it, decomposition releases methane and carbon dioxide, and those gases drive further warming, which thaws more ground. It is the same self-reinforcing structure as Amazon dieback, which is why both are treated as feedbacks rather than simple impacts.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are permafrost thaw and Amazon dieback both described as feedbacks rather than straightforward impacts?","options":{"A":"Because both processes reverse themselves automatically after a few years","B":"Each is an effect of warming that then causes further warming in turn","C":"Because neither has any measurable effect on the global climate system","D":"Because both are caused by deforestation rather than by any warming"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-for"}]},"GCSE-GEO-ENERGY":{"label":"Consuming Energy Resources","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-ENE-01","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Which of these correctly classifies gas, wind and biofuel by energy type?","options":{"A":"Gas non-renewable, wind renewable, biofuel recyclable","B":"All three are non-renewable","C":"All three are renewable","D":"Gas is renewable, wind is non-renewable, biofuel is finite"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-CLASSIFY","scaffold":"Non-renewable (finite, will run out): gas, oil, coal. Renewable (infinite, won't run out): hydroelectric, wave/tidal, wind, solar, geothermal. Recyclable (can be regrown or reused, e.g. biofuel and nuclear) sits as a distinct third category — don't lump it in with 'renewable' in an exam answer, since the distinction is often tested directly.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is nuclear energy classed as 'recyclable' rather than 'renewable' or 'non-renewable'?","options":{"A":"Nuclear energy cannot be classified using any of these three categories","B":"Nuclear energy is infinite and will never run out, making it fully renewable","C":"Uranium is finite, but the material can be reused","D":"Nuclear energy comes from an inexhaustible natural process like sunlight"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-02","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why can renewable energy sources still have negative environmental impacts, even though they don't emit greenhouse gases once operational?","options":{"A":"Renewable energy sources have zero environmental impact at every stage","B":"Building the turbines and panels still emits carbon","C":"Only non-renewable sources have any environmental impact whatsoever","D":"Renewable sources always improve biodiversity wherever they are built"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-HIDDEN-COST","scaffold":"Even 'clean' renewables carry costs: wind (visual/noise pollution, bird strikes, manufacturing emissions), solar (large land use, manufacturing emissions), HEP (flooding land, blocking fish migration, trapping sediment, deforestation in tropical areas), geothermal (emits sulphuric gases). A good exam answer avoids treating 'renewable' as automatically 'no impact'.","reforge":{"stem":"A hydroelectric dam produces no direct greenhouse gas emissions during operation. Which impact makes it still environmentally costly?","options":{"A":"HEP dams have no impact of any kind on the surrounding environment","B":"HEP dams always increase biodiversity in the surrounding area","C":"HEP dams eliminate flooding entirely with no other consequences","D":"Flooded land behind the dam blocks fish and sediment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-03","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why does the UK have large coal reserves that remain largely unused?","options":{"A":"UK coal reserves have all already been fully extracted","B":"The UK has no coal reserves at all","C":"Accessibility — the coal lies too deep to mine","D":"Accessibility has no bearing on whether a resource is used"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-RESERVES","scaffold":"Access to energy is shaped by: physical factors (geology determines whether coal/oil/gas formed; climate affects renewable efficiency — e.g. solar needs clear skies, wind needs steady but not excessive wind, HEP needs reliable precipitation), accessibility (depth underground or under oceans raises extraction cost), level of development (nuclear/geothermal need high technology and skilled staff), and politics (government investment decisions, and conflict disrupting supply, e.g. Russia's invasion of Ukraine affecting European gas).","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a developing country struggle to develop nuclear power even if it has suitable uranium deposits?","options":{"A":"It needs technology and skilled staff they may lack","B":"Nuclear power requires no specialist technology or training of any kind","C":"Uranium deposits alone guarantee a country can build nuclear power stations","D":"Developing countries always have more nuclear capacity than developed countries"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-04","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why do emerging economies such as India and China have much higher energy demand growth than developed economies?","options":{"A":"Energy demand is unrelated to a country's economic structure","B":"Developed economies always have the highest energy demand growth","C":"Emerging economies use no energy at all","D":"Manufacturing-led growth needs far more energy"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-DEMAND","scaffold":"Energy demand by economic structure: developing (LIC) countries — primary activities (agriculture), low energy demand; emerging (NEE) countries like India and China — secondary activities (manufacturing), high and rising energy demand; developed (HIC) countries — demand levelling off as more goods are imported rather than manufactured domestically. The USA is a notable exception with very high per-capita consumption (4.25% of world population, 16% of world energy use).","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a developed country's energy demand from industry decline even as its overall wealth and technology use increase?","options":{"A":"Wealth has no relationship whatsoever to energy demand","B":"Industry has moved abroad, so factory demand falls","C":"Developed countries use no energy for consumer technology","D":"Manufacturing energy demand always rises in developed countries"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-05","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Approximately what proportion of the world's primary energy currently comes from fossil fuels?","options":{"A":"84%","B":"11%","C":"50%","D":"25%"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-DEMAND","scaffold":"Global energy mix: fossil fuels 84%, renewables 11%, nuclear 4%. Despite growing renewable investment, the overwhelming majority of global energy still comes from fossil fuels — a key statistic for explaining why 'switching to renewables' is not a quick or simple fix. Around 2.4 billion people still rely on traditional fuel sources for cooking/heating, and roughly 920 million people (13% of the world) have no access to electricity at all.","reforge":{"stem":"Given that fossil fuels supply 84% of world energy and renewables only 11%, what does this suggest about the pace of the global energy transition?","options":{"A":"The energy transition is already complete worldwide","B":"Renewables have already overtaken fossil fuels as the primary global energy source","C":"Renewables would need a huge sustained rise in capacity","D":"Fossil fuel use has no bearing on how quickly renewables can expand"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-06","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Which factor caused global oil prices to rise sharply following Russia's invasion of Ukraine?","options":{"A":"Falling global demand for oil","B":"Political conflict disrupting supply","C":"An international agreement to permanently fix oil prices","D":"A sudden global increase in oil reserves"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-PRICE","scaffold":"Factors affecting oil supply and price: conflict (Russia/Ukraine raised prices due to supply concerns), diplomatic relations (Venezuela's 2010 threat to halt US sales), recession/boom (2008 financial crisis caused prices to crash), over/under-supply (Saudi Arabia increasing supply in 2013-14 to compete with Iran caused prices to fall; the 1979 Iranian Revolution cutting supply caused prices to rise), and customer preference shifts toward renewables reducing demand and therefore price.","reforge":{"stem":"In 2013-14, Saudi Arabia significantly increased oil production during a rivalry with Iran. What effect did this have on global oil prices, and why?","options":{"A":"Prices rose, because increased supply always raises prices","B":"Oil became a renewable resource as a direct result","C":"Prices were completely unaffected by the change in supply","D":"Prices fell, as extra supply outpaced demand"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-07","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"What is meant by 'unconventional' oil and gas sources such as tar sands and shale gas?","options":{"A":"They can only be found in the Arctic region","B":"They are sources that have never been used for energy production","C":"They need methods other than ordinary drilling","D":"They are extracted using exactly the same method as conventional oil wells"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-UNCONVENTIONAL","scaffold":"Tar sands (e.g. Athabasca, Canada) are bitumen-rich reservoirs treated to extract heavy crude oil, using huge quantities of water — almost 60% of Canada's oil production comes from tar sands. Shale gas is extracted via hydraulic fracturing ('fracking'): sand, chemicals and water pumped at high pressure to fracture rock and release trapped gas — over 40% of US dry natural gas is shale gas. Both avoid traditional oil wells/platforms/drilling.","reforge":{"stem":"Which environmental cost is specifically associated with fracking for shale gas, rather than tar sands extraction?","options":{"A":"Small earthquakes and groundwater contamination","B":"Higher rates of air pollution linked to bitumen processing","C":"Decline of caribou, lynx and wolverine populations from surface habitat destruction","D":"Higher greenhouse gas emissions than conventional oil extraction"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-08","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why is exploration for oil and gas in the Arctic increasing, despite the high costs involved?","options":{"A":"There is no economic incentive to explore the Arctic region","B":"Arctic extraction has become impossible due to permafrost melting","C":"The Arctic has no oil or gas reserves worth extracting","D":"Better technology and melting permafrost opened access"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-RESERVES","scaffold":"The Arctic supplies 10% of world oil and 25% of world gas, holding an estimated 25-30% of remaining reserves. Drivers of increased exploration: high oil/gas company profits fund expensive exploration, better technology (finding reserves faster, drilling in previously inaccessible cold/deep areas, liquefying gas for transport), and ironically, melting permafrost from global warming making reserves more accessible. Costs include oil spill risk, damage to fisheries and indigenous livelihoods, and infrastructure-driven permafrost melt.","reforge":{"stem":"How can Arctic oil and gas exploration create a feedback loop with climate change?","options":{"A":"Extracting oil and gas from the Arctic actively cools the region","B":"Melting permafrost opens reserves, adding to warming","C":"Arctic exploration has no relationship whatsoever to climate change","D":"Permafrost melt makes extraction completely impossible, halting all exploration"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-09","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"What does it mean for a country to have an 'energy gap', and how does this relate to energy security?","options":{"A":"Demand outstrips what the country can produce itself","B":"An energy gap means a country produces far more energy than it needs","C":"Energy gaps have no relationship to energy security","D":"Only developing countries can have an energy gap"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-SECURITY","scaffold":"Energy security requires an uninterrupted, affordable and accessible supply of energy. A country with an energy gap must import energy to make up the shortfall, making it dependent on other countries or a single source — reducing its security. Diversification (using a range of energy sources, including renewables and recyclables, rather than relying heavily on one) reduces this vulnerability.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does relying heavily on a single imported energy source make a country less energy secure than diversifying its energy mix?","options":{"A":"Relying on one source always guarantees a cheaper, more reliable supply","B":"Energy security depends only on a country's population size","C":"One disruption leaves no alternative supply","D":"Diversification has no effect on a country's vulnerability to supply disruption"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-10","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"According to the examiner's tip in the SaveMyExams guide, why is 'just switching to renewables' not a simple solution to reducing fossil fuel reliance?","options":{"A":"Renewable energy is always cheaper and more efficient than fossil fuels in every situation","B":"Renewables still cost more and need far more capacity","C":"There is no cost or efficiency difference between renewable and fossil fuel energy","D":"Renewable energy produces more electricity per unit than fossil fuels in all cases"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-HIDDEN-COST","scaffold":"A concrete example: it takes 233 wind turbines to produce the same energy as one average coal-fired power station. Renewables also face reliability issues (wind needs the right wind speed, solar needs sunshine) and higher costs, even as those costs fall over time. This is why energy strategy tends to focus on diversification and efficiency rather than a single rapid switch.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a government promote both renewable energy investment AND energy efficiency measures (like insulation grants) rather than renewables alone?","options":{"A":"Efficiency measures have no effect on total energy demand","B":"Governments only ever fund one energy strategy at a time","C":"Energy efficiency measures make renewable energy completely unnecessary","D":"Renewables cannot yet replace all fossil fuel capacity"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-11","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"What is meant by a person's 'ecological footprint', and why is it typically much higher in developed countries?","options":{"A":"Ecological footprint measures only a person's carbon dioxide emissions","B":"Ecological footprints are always lower in developed countries than developing ones","C":"Resource use in global hectares, higher where wealth is","D":"Ecological footprints are identical everywhere regardless of a country's wealth"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-FOOTPRINT","scaffold":"Ecological footprint (measured in global hectares, gha) captures total natural resource use, not just carbon: USA averages 15.5 gha per person versus Haiti's 0.7 gha. Carbon footprint specifically (measured in tonnes CO2e) shows a similar gap: global average 7 tonnes, USA 21 tonnes, Malawi just 0.2 tonnes. Both metrics rise with wealth, driven by more cars, more imported/food-mile-heavy goods, and more energy-using technology.","reforge":{"stem":"The USA's average carbon footprint (21 tonnes CO2e) is roughly 100 times Malawi's (0.2 tonnes CO2e). What is the main underlying cause of this gap?","options":{"A":"Greater wealth drives far higher consumption","B":"The USA has a smaller population than Malawi, explaining the difference","C":"Carbon footprint is unrelated to a country's wealth or consumption levels","D":"Malawi has access to more advanced technology than the USA"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-12","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why might increasing wealth in a country eventually lead to a REDUCTION in its carbon footprint, despite wealth usually increasing consumption?","options":{"A":"Wealth always increases carbon footprint with no possible reduction","B":"Poorer countries always have lower carbon footprints regardless of any other factor","C":"Carbon footprint has no relationship to a country's environmental policy","D":"Wealthier populations can afford efficient technology"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-FOOTPRINT","scaffold":"This nuance is often missed: rising wealth first increases consumption (more cars, food miles, technology) but can eventually reduce carbon footprint per unit of activity, because wealthier populations can afford efficient appliances, home insulation, solar panels/heat pumps, and electric/hybrid cars, and because education and pressure groups (Friends of the Earth, Greenpeace) raise environmental awareness once basic needs like food and shelter are already secured.","reforge":{"stem":"Which group is most likely to prioritise 'business as usual' (continued fossil fuel reliance) over rapid decarbonisation, according to typical stakeholder positions on energy?","options":{"A":"Climate scientists, over 95% of whom oppose any continued fossil fuel use","B":"Oil and gas TNCs protecting existing operations","C":"All stakeholder groups have identical views on energy policy","D":"Environmental organisations such as Greenpeace and WWF"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-ENE","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about classifying energy sources.","template":"___ energy sources such as gas, oil and coal are finite and will eventually run out. ___ energy sources such as wind and solar are infinite. ___ energy sources such as biofuel and nuclear can be reused or regrown.","blanks":["Non-renewable","Renewable","Recyclable"],"bank":["Non-renewable","Renewable","Recyclable","Sustainable","Fossil","Clean"],"tag":"MC-GG-ENE-CLASSIFY","scaffold":"Three distinct categories, not two: non-renewable (finite — gas, oil, coal), renewable (infinite — HEP, wave/tidal, wind, solar, geothermal), and recyclable (can be regrown/reused — biofuel, nuclear). Keep 'recyclable' separate from 'renewable' in exam answers.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-ENE","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about factors affecting access to energy.","template":"Physical factors such as ___ determine whether coal, oil and gas have formed. ___ affects the cost of extracting deeply buried resources. The level of a country's ___ affects whether it has the technology for nuclear or geothermal power.","blanks":["geology","Accessibility","development"],"bank":["geology","Accessibility","development","politics","climate","population"],"tag":"MC-GG-ENE-SECURITY","scaffold":"Access to energy depends on: physical factors (geology for fossil fuel formation, climate for renewable efficiency), accessibility (depth/location raising extraction cost — e.g. the UK's unmined deep coal), level of development (technology and skilled staff needed for nuclear/geothermal), and politics (investment decisions, conflict disrupting supply).","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-ENE","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about unconventional oil and gas.","template":"___ sands are bitumen-rich reservoirs found in Alberta, Canada, treated to extract heavy crude oil. ___ gas is extracted using hydraulic fracturing, known as ___.","blanks":["Tar","Shale","fracking"],"bank":["Tar","Shale","fracking","Coal","Oil","drilling"],"tag":"MC-GG-ENE-UNCONVENTIONAL","scaffold":"Tar sands (Athabasca, Canada) require huge water use to treat bitumen into crude oil. Shale gas is released by fracking — pumping sand, chemicals and water at high pressure to fracture rock. Both are called 'unconventional' because they skip traditional oil wells and drilling rigs.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-ENE","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about energy security.","template":"An energy ___ occurs when a country cannot meet its energy demand from domestic resources and must ___ energy instead, making it less energy ___.","blanks":["gap","import","secure"],"bank":["gap","import","secure","surplus","export","efficient"],"tag":"MC-GG-ENE-SECURITY","scaffold":"To be energy secure, a country needs an uninterrupted, affordable, accessible supply. An energy gap forces reliance on imports, and diversification (using a wide range of sources) is the main strategy for reducing this vulnerability.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-13","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"A student says 'renewable and recyclable energy mean the same thing.' What's the correct distinction?","options":{"A":"Renewable fuel replenishes; recyclable fuel is reused","B":"Both terms refer only to fossil fuels","C":"Renewable and recyclable are interchangeable terms with no meaningful difference","D":"Recyclable energy is infinite, renewable energy is finite"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-CLASSIFY","scaffold":"This three-way classification is commonly tested: non-renewable (finite — coal, oil, gas), renewable (infinite/rapidly replenished — wind, solar, tidal, wave, hydroelectric, geothermal), and recyclable (a limited resource that can be regrown or reprocessed — biofuel, nuclear). Don't collapse recyclable into renewable in an exam answer.","reforge":{"stem":"Uranium is a finite resource, yet nuclear energy is not classed as 'non-renewable' in the same way as coal or oil. Why?","options":{"A":"Nuclear energy produces no waste of any kind","B":"Uranium is actually an infinite resource, making nuclear fully renewable","C":"Nuclear material can be processed and partly reused","D":"Nuclear energy is identical to solar energy in classification"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-14","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"What is the difference between primary and secondary energy?","options":{"A":"They are the same thing measured in different units","B":"Primary is the raw fuel; secondary is electricity","C":"Only renewable sources produce secondary energy","D":"Primary energy is always electricity; secondary energy is always a raw fuel"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-CLASSIFY","scaffold":"Primary energy sources (coal, gas, wind, sunlight) are converted into secondary energy — usually electricity — at a power station or generation site, which is then distributed to homes and businesses via the grid.","reforge":{"stem":"A coal-fired power station burns coal (a primary energy source) to generate electricity. What is the electricity produced correctly classified as?","options":{"A":"Renewable energy, since it comes from a power station","B":"Primary energy, exactly the same as the coal itself","C":"Recyclable energy, since coal can be regrown","D":"Secondary energy, made from a primary source"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-15","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"A country has an energy surplus. What does this mean, and how does it differ from an energy deficit?","options":{"A":"An energy surplus and energy deficit describe exactly the same situation","B":"A surplus means a country has run out of energy resources entirely","C":"A surplus generates more than is needed; a deficit less","D":"A deficit means a country produces more energy than it can use"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-SECURITY","scaffold":"A country with a surplus can export energy or build reserves; a country in deficit (an 'energy gap') must import to meet demand, which — as with the earlier energy-gap question — tends to reduce its energy security.","reforge":{"stem":"A country generates 120 units of energy but its population only needs 100 units. What does this situation describe?","options":{"A":"An energy surplus — supply is greater than demand","B":"An energy deficit — supply is less than demand","C":"This situation cannot occur in reality","D":"An energy gap that must be filled through imports"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-16","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"How does fracking release natural gas, and what environmental concern is most specifically linked to it?","options":{"A":"Fracking involves burning coal to release trapped gas","B":"Fracking has no known environmental risks","C":"Fracking is a method of harnessing wind energy from underground caverns","D":"Pressurised liquid cracks shale rock to release gas"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-EXTRACTION-IMPACT","scaffold":"Fracking is a specific, named process (not a general term for 'gas extraction') — the fracturing of shale rock with high-pressure fluid injection is what creates the pathways for gas to escape, and it's this same high-pressure injection that has been linked to minor seismic activity and contamination risks.","reforge":{"stem":"High-pressure liquid is pumped underground to fracture shale rock and release trapped gas. What is this process called, and what risk has it been linked to?","options":{"A":"Geothermal extraction — linked to volcanic eruptions","B":"Fracking — linked to tremors and water contamination","C":"Tar sands extraction — linked to high water use only","D":"Open-cast mining — linked to surface habitat destruction only"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-17","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"How does geothermal energy generate electricity?","options":{"A":"Water heated by hot rock returns as steam for turbines","B":"It uses the movement of tides to turn turbines","C":"It relies on burning geological deposits of coal and gas","D":"Solar panels absorb heat from the sun and convert it directly into electricity"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-RENEWABLE-TECH","scaffold":"Geothermal energy depends on accessible underground heat, which is why it's most viable in tectonically active regions (e.g. Iceland, sitting on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge) — don't confuse it with hydroelectric (moving river/dam water) or tidal (moving sea water) energy, which use very different mechanisms.","reforge":{"stem":"Iceland generates significant geothermal energy due to its position on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. Why does this location make geothermal energy especially viable there?","options":{"A":"Geothermal energy relies on tidal movement, unrelated to tectonic activity","B":"Iceland's location has no bearing on geothermal energy production","C":"Magma close to the surface heats water into steam","D":"Iceland's geothermal energy comes from burning underground fossil fuel deposits"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-18","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"A student mixes up tidal energy and wave energy. What is the correct distinction?","options":{"A":"Tidal and wave energy are two names for exactly the same technology","B":"Tidal uses seabed turbines; wave uses floating buoys","C":"Wave energy relies on the gravitational pull of the moon, tidal energy does not","D":"Tidal energy is generated on the sea surface; wave energy is generated on the seabed"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-RENEWABLE-TECH","scaffold":"Both are marine renewables but exploit different forces: tidal power depends on the predictable rise and fall of tides (driven by gravitational pull), captured by seabed turbines; wave power depends on surface wave motion, captured by floating buoy-based turbines — a useful pairing for a 'compare and contrast' exam question.","reforge":{"stem":"A turbine on the seabed is turned by water as the tide rises and falls. What type of energy generation is this, and how does it differ from a buoy-mounted turbine turned by surface waves?","options":{"A":"Hydroelectric energy — associated only with river dams, not the sea","B":"Geothermal energy — powered by underground heat, not water movement","C":"Wave energy — identical in mechanism to tidal energy","D":"Tidal energy — tides are predictable, waves are not"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-19","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"What does decommissioning a power station involve, and why is it significant for nuclear power specifically?","options":{"A":"Decommissioning has no associated costs","B":"Decommissioning only applies to renewable energy infrastructure like wind farms","C":"Closing a plant and disposing of its waste safely","D":"Decommissioning means building a new power station from scratch"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-HIDDEN-COST","scaffold":"Decommissioning costs are a major hidden expense of nuclear power that's easy to overlook when comparing energy sources on 'cost per unit of electricity' alone — safely storing or disposing of radioactive waste can cost millions of pounds and take years, an issue not shared by renewable technologies like wind or solar.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can safely closing a nuclear power station take significantly longer and cost more than closing a coal-fired power station?","options":{"A":"Radioactive waste makes closure slow and costly","B":"Coal-fired power stations require more complex decommissioning than nuclear plants","C":"Closing any power station always takes exactly the same time and cost","D":"Nuclear power stations have no waste to dispose of at all"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-20","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why is energy security a concern for a country that imports most of its natural gas?","options":{"A":"Importing fuel removes all environmental impacts","B":"Imported gas is always cheaper and guaranteed","C":"Energy security means using only renewable electricity","D":"Prices, political disputes and transport disruption"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-SECURITY","scaffold":"Energy security means reliable, affordable access to energy. Dependence on imports can expose a country to price volatility, geopolitical conflict and disruption, so diversifying suppliers and sources can reduce vulnerability.","reforge":{"stem":"Which strategy would most improve a country's energy security?","options":{"A":"Depend on one overseas supplier","B":"Use a diverse mix with storage and good infrastructure","C":"Close all power stations before alternatives are available","D":"Increase dependence on a single fuel"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-21","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why can renewable energy create a challenge for balancing electricity supply and demand?","options":{"A":"Wind and solar vary, so backup or storage is needed","B":"Renewables always produce maximum output at exactly the same time","C":"Renewable sources cannot connect to a national grid","D":"Electricity demand never changes during the day"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-INTERMITTENCY","scaffold":"Wind and solar are intermittent: output varies with wind speed, daylight and cloud. Storage, interconnectors, flexible generation and demand management can help maintain a reliable grid.","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination could help manage periods when wind and solar output are low?","options":{"A":"Relying on one wind farm in one location","B":"Removing all electricity storage","C":"Battery storage, interconnectors and backup","D":"Using electricity only during daylight hours"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-22","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Which physical condition gives a country strong hydroelectric potential?","options":{"A":"Flat land with little surface water","B":"Steep relief and reliable rainfall","C":"Thick coal seams beneath every river","D":"Low winds and prolonged drought"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-RENEWABLE-TECH","scaffold":"HEP works best where reliable precipitation supplies river flow and steep relief creates a large head of water. Technology, investment and environmental acceptability also affect whether this physical potential is developed.","reforge":{"stem":"Which climate is most favourable for large-scale solar generation?","options":{"A":"Persistent cloud with very short daylight hours","B":"Reliable rainfall in steep valleys","C":"Frequent snowfall throughout the year","D":"High sunshine and limited cloud"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-23","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why can energy use per person remain low in a developing country?","options":{"A":"Primary industries always require more energy than manufacturing","B":"Traditional fuels are included as high grid demand","C":"Limited electricity access constrains consumption","D":"Low incomes guarantee abundant modern technology"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-DEMAND","scaffold":"Low incomes, limited grid infrastructure and reliance on traditional fuels can restrict access to modern energy. Economic structure matters too: industrial manufacturing generally uses more energy than subsistence primary activity.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might rapid industrialisation increase energy use per person?","options":{"A":"Manufacturing requires substantial power","B":"Industrial growth reduces transport activity","C":"Factories eliminate household electricity demand","D":"Economic development prevents grid expansion"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-24","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why can a global recession cause oil prices to fall?","options":{"A":"Recession turns oil into a renewable resource","B":"Governments must close every oil field","C":"Oil reserves become evenly distributed","D":"Lower economic activity reduces demand"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-PRICE","scaffold":"During recession, industrial output, freight and travel often decline, reducing demand for oil. If supply does not fall as quickly, oversupply puts downward pressure on price.","reforge":{"stem":"An oil-producing group cuts output while demand remains steady. What price effect is most likely?","options":{"A":"Prices must remain fixed by geology","B":"Prices may rise as supply tightens","C":"Oil consumption immediately becomes zero","D":"Prices fall because supply has increased"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-25","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"What is a likely economic trade-off when developing oil in an isolated region?","options":{"A":"Jobs and tax revenue, but high infrastructure costs","B":"Only environmental organisations and no other stakeholders receive revenue","C":"Isolation makes transport unnecessary","D":"There are no costs once oil is discovered"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-RESERVES","scaffold":"A remote conventional oil project may create employment, export income and tax revenue, but roads, pipelines, ports and worker settlements are expensive. A balanced judgement also considers spill risk and impacts on local ecosystems and livelihoods.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a government still approve expensive offshore drilling?","options":{"A":"Imported energy is always less secure than domestic oil","B":"Offshore drilling cannot cause an oil spill","C":"Expected revenue may exceed development costs","D":"Remote reserves require no specialist technology"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-26","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Which action is an example of energy conservation rather than energy efficiency?","options":{"A":"Replacing bulbs with lower-energy LEDs","B":"Turning unused lights off","C":"Improving loft insulation in a house","D":"Using a more efficient electric motor"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-CONSERVATION","scaffold":"Conservation reduces energy use through behaviour, such as switching off lights or travelling less. Efficiency delivers the same service using less energy, such as insulation, LEDs or more efficient engines.","reforge":{"stem":"Which household change is best described as energy efficiency?","options":{"A":"Heating fewer rooms during winter","B":"Choosing to walk instead of driving","C":"Switching the television off overnight","D":"Installing effective loft insulation"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-27","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"What is a major challenge when using hydrogen as a low-carbon transport fuel?","options":{"A":"Hydrogen cannot store or transfer any energy","B":"Fuel cells always release more carbon than coal","C":"Production and storage need energy and infrastructure","D":"Hydrogen is found in unlimited pure deposits ready for direct collection"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-HYDROGEN","scaffold":"Hydrogen can produce water at the point of use, but it must first be manufactured, compressed or liquefied, transported and stored. Its overall carbon footprint depends strongly on how the hydrogen is produced.","reforge":{"stem":"When would hydrogen offer the greatest carbon reduction?","options":{"A":"When low-carbon electricity produces it","B":"When storage leaks are ignored","C":"When produced entirely from unabated coal","D":"When vehicles also burn diesel continuously"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-28","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"A household's footprint falls from 10 to 7 global hectares. What is the percentage decrease?","options":{"A":"7%","B":"10%","C":"70% of the original footprint","D":"30%"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-FOOTPRINT","scaffold":"Percentage decrease = change divided by original value, multiplied by 100. The change is 3 global hectares, so 3 / 10 x 100 = 30%.","reforge":{"stem":"A carbon footprint drops from 8 tonnes to 6 tonnes. What is the percentage decrease?","options":{"A":"2%","B":"25%","C":"75% of the original footprint","D":"20%"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-29","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Which stakeholder is most likely to favour a rapid move away from fossil fuels?","options":{"A":"A climate-science organisation","B":"A government dependent on fossil-fuel taxation","C":"A consumer prioritising the lowest immediate price","D":"A TNC protecting newly developed oil reserves"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Stakeholder views reflect different priorities. Climate scientists and environmental groups are likely to emphasise emissions and long-term risk, while consumers, governments and energy TNCs may weigh affordability, employment, tax revenue and existing assets differently.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might an oil TNC support a slower energy transition?","options":{"A":"A rapid transition always raises oil demand","B":"Oil companies receive no revenue from extraction","C":"It has invested capital in fossil-fuel assets","D":"TNCs cannot invest in renewable technology"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-30","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"What distinguishes a conventional oil source from an unconventional one?","options":{"A":"Conventional oil is always renewable","B":"Standard-well extraction","C":"Unconventional oil contains no hydrocarbons","D":"Conventional reserves occur only on land"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-UNCONVENTIONAL","scaffold":"Conventional oil flows through reservoirs accessible by standard drilling; unconventional sources need methods such as mining, heating or fracturing.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are tar sands classed as unconventional?","options":{"A":"They are refined without using energy","B":"They form only beneath ocean trenches","C":"They can be replaced within one year","D":"Energy-intensive bitumen processing"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-31","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why can a country have large oil reserves but modest current production?","options":{"A":"Reserves are the same as annual output","B":"All reserves must be extracted immediately","C":"Costs and access may constrain extraction","D":"Production depends only on population size"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-RESERVES","scaffold":"Reserves estimate recoverable stocks, while production depends on investment, technology, infrastructure, policy and market price.","reforge":{"stem":"What could make previously known reserves economically viable?","options":{"A":"Higher prices or improved technology","B":"A permanent collapse in oil demand","C":"Removal of all transport infrastructure","D":"Lower geological knowledge of the field"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-32","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why can an economic boom raise oil prices?","options":{"A":"Booms eliminate manufacturing activity","B":"Economic growth prevents air travel","C":"Oil supply automatically doubles first","D":"Higher industrial and transport demand"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-PRICE","scaffold":"Higher output, freight and consumer travel increase demand; if supply responds slowly, prices can rise.","reforge":{"stem":"When might a boom not cause a large price rise?","options":{"A":"Demand rises while supply falls","B":"Supply expands enough to meet demand","C":"Refineries close during higher demand","D":"Conflict blocks major export routes"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-33","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"How can a diplomatic dispute affect an energy-importing country?","options":{"A":"A supplier may restrict exports","B":"It guarantees lower world prices","C":"It makes domestic geology change","D":"It turns imported gas into renewable energy"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-SECURITY","scaffold":"Political relations can affect contracts, pipelines, sanctions and export decisions, exposing import-dependent countries.","reforge":{"stem":"Which strategy reduces vulnerability to one supplier?","options":{"A":"Close all storage facilities","B":"Stop monitoring international markets","C":"Diverse suppliers and sources","D":"Depend on a single pipeline"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-34","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why can tar-sands extraction damage water quality?","options":{"A":"Bitumen naturally purifies nearby rivers","B":"Water use and polluted waste","C":"Extraction requires no chemicals or tailings","D":"Tar sands are collected only by wind power"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-EXTRACTION-IMPACT","scaffold":"Mining and processing disturb land, use large water volumes and create tailings that can contaminate ecosystems.","reforge":{"stem":"Which evidence best assesses downstream water impact?","options":{"A":"National population density","B":"Company employment totals alone","C":"A photograph of an unrelated wind farm","D":"Upstream-downstream water chemistry"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-35","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why can hydraulic fracturing cause small earthquakes?","options":{"A":"Gas combustion changes the plate boundary","B":"Shale becomes a moving tectonic plate","C":"Fluid injection can reactivate faults","D":"Fracking removes all pressure underground"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-EXTRACTION-IMPACT","scaffold":"High-pressure injection and wastewater disposal can alter stresses on existing faults and induce seismic events.","reforge":{"stem":"Which monitoring is most relevant near a fracking site?","options":{"A":"Water and seismic checks","B":"Coastal longshore drift","C":"Forest canopy height only","D":"Daily retail footfall"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-36","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"What is one social cost of a large HEP reservoir?","options":{"A":"Dams require no land beyond the river","B":"Electricity cannot reach urban areas","C":"Reservoirs preserve every cultural site","D":"Reservoir displacement"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Reservoir creation may displace settlements and livelihoods, alongside habitat and sediment impacts.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one benefit that can offset some HEP costs?","options":{"A":"Permanent elimination of river ecosystems","B":"Reliable low-carbon electricity generation","C":"Guaranteed equal compensation for residents","D":"No construction emissions or expense"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-37","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why can expanding biofuel crops threaten food security?","options":{"A":"Food-fuel competition for land and water","B":"Fuel crops always restore natural forest","C":"Biofuels use no agricultural inputs","D":"Biofuels can grow only on city rooftops"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-HIDDEN-COST","scaffold":"Competition for land, water and crops can raise food prices or encourage deforestation.","reforge":{"stem":"When is a biofuel most likely to have a lower land-use impact?","options":{"A":"When rainforest is cleared for plantations","B":"When food crops are diverted during shortage","C":"When made from genuine waste material","D":"When irrigation depletes local drinking water"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-38","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"What is the main grid challenge created by wind power?","options":{"A":"Turbines produce only fossil-fuel electricity","B":"Output varies with wind conditions","C":"Wind farms cannot connect to transmission lines","D":"Wind speed is constant across all seasons"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-INTERMITTENCY","scaffold":"Variable output requires storage, interconnection, flexible demand or backup generation to balance supply.","reforge":{"stem":"How can geographic spread make wind generation more reliable?","options":{"A":"Every region becomes calm simultaneously","B":"Turbines no longer need maintenance","C":"Longer cables create extra wind energy","D":"Geographic spread smooths output"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-39","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why can a very large solar farm create land-use conflict?","options":{"A":"Panels prevent any electricity generation","B":"Solar power requires underground coal seams","C":"It competes with farming or habitats","D":"Sunshine is unavailable outside cities"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"Utility-scale solar needs land and grid connections, creating trade-offs with food production, landscape and biodiversity.","reforge":{"stem":"Which design could reduce land-use conflict?","options":{"A":"Rooftops and brownfield land","B":"Clear protected habitat for new panels","C":"Remove panels from industrial roofs","D":"Build only on the most fertile farmland"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-40","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Which transport change is mainly an energy-conservation measure?","options":{"A":"Fit a more efficient engine to the same car","B":"Recover braking energy in an electric car","C":"Use lighter materials in vehicle design","D":"Replace a car journey with walking"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-CONSERVATION","scaffold":"Conservation reduces the amount of energy service demanded; efficiency delivers the service with less energy.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action is mainly transport energy efficiency?","options":{"A":"Cancel an unnecessary journey","B":"Lower energy use per kilometre","C":"Walk instead of taking a short car trip","D":"Share one journey instead of driving twice"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-41","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"Why is hydrogen difficult to store and transport?","options":{"A":"It has low density and needs compression","B":"It cannot release energy in a fuel cell","C":"It is a solid at room temperature","D":"It occurs in unlimited pure underground lakes"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-HYDROGEN","scaffold":"Hydrogen has low volumetric energy density, requiring high-pressure tanks, liquefaction or chemical carriers.","reforge":{"stem":"What additional factor determines whether hydrogen is low carbon?","options":{"A":"The colour of the storage tank","B":"The age of the delivery vehicle","C":"The production energy source","D":"The distance from the Equator"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-42","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"How does a diverse energy mix improve energy security?","options":{"A":"It guarantees every source has zero cost","B":"Disruption to one source has less impact","C":"Diversity removes the need for a power grid","D":"All fuels become domestically available"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-SECURITY","scaffold":"Multiple fuels, suppliers and technologies reduce dependence on any single vulnerable source.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one cost of maintaining a diverse energy mix?","options":{"A":"It makes storage technology unnecessary","B":"It always increases dependence on one supplier","C":"It prevents renewable generation expanding","D":"Investment in several systems"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-43","spec":"GCSE-ENE","stem":"A person's footprint falls from 12 to 9 tonnes CO2e. What is the absolute reduction?","options":{"A":"1 tonne CO2e","B":"2 tonnes CO2e","C":"3 tonnes CO2e","D":"4 tonnes CO2e"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-FOOTPRINT","scaffold":"Absolute change is the original value minus the new value: 12 - 9 = 3 tonnes CO2e.","reforge":{"stem":"Emissions fall from 20 to 15 tonnes CO2e. What is the percentage decrease?","options":{"A":"25%","B":"15%","C":"20%","D":"5%"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-CLASSIFY","stem":"A student lists nuclear power as a renewable energy source. Why is this wrong, and what is it correctly called?","options":{"A":"It is renewable, because a reactor can keep running for many decades","B":"It is a biofuel, because the fuel is processed before being used in a reactor","C":"It is a secondary energy source, because uranium must be refined before use","D":"It is non-renewable but low-carbon: uranium is a finite mined resource"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Two separate axes are being confused. Renewable versus non-renewable is about whether the resource replenishes; low-carbon versus high-carbon is about emissions at the point of generation. Uranium is mined and finite, so nuclear is non-renewable, yet it emits almost no carbon dioxide when generating, which is why it appears in decarbonisation plans.","reforge":{"stem":"Which pair of energy sources are both low-carbon at the point of generation but differ in renewability?","options":{"A":"Coal and natural gas, since both are fossil fuels formed deep underground over time","B":"Nuclear and wind, since uranium is finite while wind is continually replenished","C":"Oil and biofuel, since both are burned in order to release their energy","D":"Solar and tidal, since both depend entirely on predictable daily cycles"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-HIDDEN-COST","stem":"Nuclear power produces almost no carbon dioxide when generating. What is the strongest argument against expanding it?","options":{"A":"High build costs, long timescales and waste that stays hazardous for millennia","B":"Nuclear power stations cannot generate electricity during the hours of darkness","C":"Uranium is so abundant that its price would collapse and bankrupt the industry","D":"Nuclear plants release more carbon dioxide per unit than a coal-fired station"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"The case against nuclear is not usually about emissions. It is capital cost, construction times measured in decades, decommissioning liabilities and the unresolved problem of long-lived waste, plus public concern after Chernobyl and Fukushima. A good evaluation weighs those against the reliable, low-carbon output it provides.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is decommissioning a significant hidden cost of nuclear power?","options":{"A":"Decommissioning is completed within a year and costs almost nothing at all","B":"Because a decommissioned plant continues generating electricity for decades","C":"Dismantling and storing radioactive material costs billions over many decades","D":"Because decommissioning is paid for entirely by the electricity consumer abroad"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-SECURITY","stem":"The UK generated most of its electricity from coal in 1990; by the 2020s coal was near zero and wind was a major source. What best explains the shift?","options":{"A":"UK coal reserves were completely exhausted, so no coal remained to be burned","B":"Cheaper gas, then falling wind costs and carbon pricing, made coal uncompetitive","C":"Wind turbines were invented during this period and had not existed anywhere before 1990","D":"Electricity demand in the UK fell to almost zero, removing the need for coal"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Three forces combined: the dash for gas in the 1990s, a carbon price that made coal expensive to burn, and a steep fall in offshore wind costs. UK coal reserves were never exhausted, which is the common misconception — the coal is still there, but burning it is now both costly and politically untenable.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the UK still hold large coal reserves despite generating almost no electricity from coal?","options":{"A":"Because the remaining reserves are of a type that cannot be burned at all","B":"Because UK law requires that all coal reserves be left permanently in the ground","C":"Because no technology exists that is capable of extracting deep coal seams","D":"Extraction is uneconomic and burning it conflicts with emissions targets"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-PRICE","stem":"OPEC members agree to cut production, and world oil prices rise within days. What does this show about the oil market?","options":{"A":"Oil prices are fixed annually by agreement between importing countries","B":"Oil prices are determined solely by how much consumers choose to buy","C":"Coordinated supply decisions by major producers can move the global price","D":"Production levels have no relationship with the price a barrel of oil reaches"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Because demand for oil changes slowly in the short run, a modest cut in supply produces a large price move. A group controlling a substantial share of world output can therefore influence price deliberately. That is why importing countries treat concentrated production as an energy security risk, not just a price one.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a small change in oil supply often cause a large change in price?","options":{"A":"Demand changes little in the short term, so price must adjust instead","B":"Because oil can be substituted instantly by any other available fuel","C":"Because oil is traded only once a year, which exaggerates every change","D":"Because oil producers are legally required to raise prices after any cut"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-INTERMITTENCY","stem":"A grid with a high share of wind and solar invests heavily in battery and pumped-storage capacity. What problem is this solving?","options":{"A":"Storage is required because renewable electricity degrades if it is not used quickly","B":"Wind and solar produce too little total energy across a whole year to be useful","C":"Renewable generators produce direct current that the grid cannot ever accept","D":"Renewable output does not match demand hour by hour, so surplus must be stored"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Intermittency is a timing problem, not a quantity problem. Wind can exceed demand at 3am and fall away at the evening peak. Storage moves energy across hours, so surplus generation is captured rather than curtailed and released when demand rises, which is what allows a high renewable share without fossil backup.","reforge":{"stem":"How does pumped-storage hydroelectricity store surplus electricity?","options":{"A":"By converting surplus electricity directly into natural gas for later burning","B":"Surplus power pumps water uphill, which is released through turbines later","C":"By storing surplus electricity as heat in large insulated underground tanks","D":"By slowing the turbines so that electricity is held within the generator itself"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-CONSERVATION","stem":"Replacing a boiler with a more efficient model, and turning the heating down, both cut gas use. What is the difference between them?","options":{"A":"Efficiency gives the same service for less energy; conservation uses less service","B":"There is no meaningful difference, since both simply reduce total gas consumption","C":"Efficiency means using less heating, while conservation means buying new equipment","D":"Conservation applies only to businesses, while efficiency applies only to households"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Efficiency is technical: the same warmth for less gas. Conservation is behavioural: accepting less of the service. The distinction matters because efficiency gains can be partly cancelled when people use the cheaper service more, which is why policy usually pairs the two rather than relying on technology alone.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these is an energy conservation measure rather than an efficiency measure?","options":{"A":"Fitting LED bulbs that produce the same light using much less power","B":"Installing loft insulation so less heat escapes from the building","C":"Choosing to walk a short journey instead of driving the car","D":"Replacing an old fridge with a model that carries a higher efficiency rating"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-STAKEHOLDERS","stem":"A household cannot afford to heat its home adequately despite being connected to the gas grid. What is this called?","options":{"A":"An energy gap, meaning national demand exceeds the country's generating capacity","B":"Fuel poverty, where energy costs are too high a share of household income","C":"Energy insecurity, meaning the country depends on imported fuel from abroad","D":"An energy surplus, because more energy is produced than the household consumes"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Fuel poverty is about affordability at the household scale, not national supply. It is worse in poorly insulated homes, so it is as much a housing problem as an energy one. Distinguishing it from the energy gap and from energy security matters because each is measured differently and solved differently.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does improving home insulation help reduce fuel poverty more durably than a one-off payment?","options":{"A":"Because insulation raises the market value of the property being insulated","B":"Because a one-off payment is always larger than the cost of insulating a home","C":"Because insulation increases the total amount of energy the household uses","D":"It permanently lowers the energy needed, so bills fall every year afterwards"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-RENEWABLE-TECH","stem":"A solar panel in southern Spain generates considerably more electricity per year than an identical panel in Scotland. Why?","options":{"A":"Scotland is too far north for any sunlight to reach a solar panel at all","B":"Solar panels physically stop working whenever the air temperature falls","C":"Lower latitude means a higher sun angle and more hours of stronger sunlight","D":"Panels installed in Spain are always considerably larger than those in Scotland"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Insolation falls with latitude because sunlight strikes at a shallower angle and passes through more atmosphere, and day length varies more through the year. Cloud cover adds to the difference. Panels still generate usefully in Scotland, but the yield per panel is lower, which changes the economics rather than ruling it out.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can solar power still be worth installing in a cloudy high-latitude country?","options":{"A":"Panels still generate from diffuse daylight, and costs have fallen sharply","B":"Because solar panels generate their maximum possible output under thick cloud cover","C":"Because high-latitude countries receive more total sunlight each year","D":"Because solar panels operate independently of daylight altogether"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-HIDDEN-COST","stem":"Burning wood pellets is often counted as carbon neutral. Why do scientists dispute this?","options":{"A":"Burning wood pellets releases no carbon dioxide at all when they combust","B":"Wood pellets are always burned in exactly the same forest in which they were harvested","C":"Wood pellets are classified as a fossil fuel rather than as a form of biomass","D":"Carbon is released now, but regrowth takes decades, and transport adds emissions"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The accounting assumes a new tree absorbs what the burned one released. That is eventually true, but the release is immediate and the reabsorption takes decades the climate does not have. Pelleting, shipping and any land-use change add further emissions the neutral label omits.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the 'carbon payback period' for biomass energy?","options":{"A":"The number of years before a biomass plant repays its construction cost","B":"The time before regrowth reabsorbs the carbon released by burning it","C":"The period during which a biomass plant is exempt from any carbon tax","D":"The time taken to transport wood pellets from the forest to the power station"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-STAKEHOLDERS","stem":"A coalfield region loses thousands of mining jobs as the country decarbonises. What does the term 'just transition' mean here?","options":{"A":"That workers and regions bearing the cost of change are supported through it","B":"That every closing coal mine must be replaced by another mine somewhere else","C":"That decarbonisation should be delayed indefinitely to protect all mining jobs","D":"That the transition is judged fair because national emissions have fallen overall"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Decarbonisation spreads benefits nationally but concentrates costs on particular places and occupations. A just transition means retraining, investment and new industry in those regions, so the burden is not left with the communities least able to absorb it. It is the social dimension of an energy policy question.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the cost of an energy transition often described as unevenly distributed?","options":{"A":"Because energy transitions impose identical costs on every single region","B":"Because only national governments ever bear any cost of an energy transition","C":"Benefits are national while job losses fall on specific industrial regions","D":"Because the regions that lose jobs also receive the largest share of new investment"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-SECURITY","stem":"The UK is linked to France and Norway by undersea electricity interconnectors. How do these affect UK energy security?","options":{"A":"They remove the UK's need to generate any of its own electricity domestically","B":"They help balance supply, but add dependence on another country's generation","C":"They have no effect on security, since electricity cannot be traded across borders","D":"They guarantee that UK electricity prices will never rise under any circumstances"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Interconnectors cut both ways. They let the UK import when wind output is low and export a surplus, smoothing intermittency and lowering costs. They also mean a share of supply depends on decisions and conditions abroad, which is exactly the dependence that makes imported gas a security concern.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a diverse energy mix generally improve a country's energy security?","options":{"A":"Because a diverse mix always produces electricity more cheaply than one source","B":"Because using several sources removes the need for any electricity imports","C":"Because a diverse mix guarantees the country will become a net energy exporter","D":"Disruption to any one source affects only part of the total supply"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"},{"id":"GCSE-ENE-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-ENE","tag":"MC-GG-ENE-DEMAND","stem":"Smart meters and time-of-use tariffs encourage people to run appliances overnight. What grid problem does this address?","options":{"A":"It reduces the total quantity of electricity the country consumes each year","B":"It increases electricity demand during the evening peak when supply is tightest","C":"It shifts demand away from peak hours, so less spare capacity is needed","D":"It allows electricity to be stored inside the meter for use at a later time"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Grids must be built for the highest peak, not the average, so peak demand drives cost. Moving flexible loads such as washing and vehicle charging into low-demand hours flattens the curve, reduces the backup capacity needed and makes better use of overnight wind. It is demand management rather than energy saving.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is reducing peak demand valuable even if total annual consumption stays the same?","options":{"A":"Generating capacity is built for the peak, so a lower peak needs less capacity","B":"Because peak demand is the only electricity that households will actually pay for","C":"Because total annual consumption is not measured by electricity suppliers","D":"Because electricity generated at peak times is of a lower technical quality"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-ene"}]},"GCSE-GEO-DECISIONS":{"label":"Making Geographical Decisions","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-DEC-01","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"In Paper 3, students are given a resource booklet on a geographical issue and asked to justify which of several options should be chosen. What makes something a 'geographical issue' suitable for this task?","options":{"A":"A contested land-use decision with no single right answer","B":"It only ever involves a purely economic decision with no environmental or social dimension","C":"It is a simple factual question with one definitively correct answer","D":"It can only be about a hazard, never about development or resource use"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-ISSUE-FRAME","scaffold":"A geographical issue is a genuine dilemma — e.g. whether to build a road through a rainforest, where to site a new reservoir, or how a coastline should be managed — that draws together physical and human geography and forces a trade-off between competing priorities (environmental, economic, social). There is no 'correct' answer; the exam rewards a well-evidenced, justified choice, not a particular option.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these is best described as a geographical issue rather than a simple factual question?","options":{"A":"What the definition of GDP per capita is","B":"What the capital city of a named country is","C":"Hard engineering or managed retreat","D":"What year an earthquake happened"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-02","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"A student's answer to the decision-making question only lists advantages of the option they chose and ignores every source that suggests problems with it. Why will this lose marks?","options":{"A":"The mark scheme only rewards the final decision, not the reasoning","B":"Use evidence from across the booklet, including the counter-case","C":"Using only supporting evidence is the correct approach and should gain full marks","D":"Sources that don't support the chosen option should be actively contradicted or dismissed as wrong"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-EVIDENCE-BREADTH","scaffold":"This question is marked using a levels-based mark scheme. The top level requires a balanced, well-evidenced argument that draws on multiple sources across the booklet — including ones that seem to favour a rival option — and explains why, on balance, the chosen option is still the strongest. A one-sided answer that cherry-picks only supportive evidence is capped at a lower level, however confident or detailed it is.","reforge":{"stem":"What should a student do with a source in the resource booklet that provides evidence AGAINST the option they intend to choose?","options":{"A":"Only use sources that agree with the chosen option","B":"State that the source must be factually incorrect","C":"Ignore it completely so the argument looks stronger","D":"Name it, then say why the choice still wins"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-03","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Which three categories are commonly used to sort and weigh up evidence about a geographical issue?","options":{"A":"Primary, secondary and tertiary industry","B":"Push, pull and intervening obstacles","C":"Environmental, economic and social","D":"Weathering, erosion and deposition"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-ISSUE-FRAME","scaffold":"Sorting evidence into environmental (impact on ecosystems, pollution, landscape), economic (costs, jobs, investment, growth) and social (quality of life, health, community, displacement) impacts — sometimes alongside political factors like governance or international relations — is the standard method for organising a decision-making answer and comparing options fairly.","reforge":{"stem":"A source states that a proposed dam will displace 3,000 people from their homes. Which category of impact does this evidence belong to?","options":{"A":"Social impact","B":"Not categorisable","C":"Environmental impact","D":"Purely economic"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-04","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"A student's decision-making answer states 'Option B is best because it is cheaper.' Why is this an incomplete justification?","options":{"A":"The answer is already sufficient for full marks","B":"It should instead focus only on environmental factors and ignore cost entirely","C":"Cost is never a valid factor in a geographical decision","D":"It gives one reason and cites no evidence from the booklet"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-JUSTIFIED-CONCLUSION","scaffold":"'Justify' requires more than stating a reason — it requires specific evidence (e.g. 'Source C shows Option B costs £2 million less than Option A') integrated into a wider argument that weighs multiple factors against each other, not just the single cheapest one. A strong answer explains why cost matters more than the trade-offs it involves in this particular case.","reforge":{"stem":"Which version of a justification is strongest for a Making Geographical Decisions answer?","options":{"A":"'I prefer Option B'","B":"'Source C: Option B costs £2m less'","C":"'Option B is best because it is cheaper'","D":"'Option A and Option B are both fine'"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-05","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why does a strong decision-making answer often consider both short-term and long-term impacts of an option?","options":{"A":"A short-term gain can become a long-term problem","B":"Only short-term impacts are ever relevant to a geographical decision","C":"Long-term impacts cannot be predicted, so they should never be mentioned","D":"Time frame has no bearing on how strong a decision is"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-TIME-UNCERTAINTY","scaffold":"A classic pattern in these resource booklets: an option like slash-and-burn farming or unrestricted logging may bring fast short-term economic benefit but undermine long-term sustainability, while an option like sustainable forestry or managed retreat may cost more upfront but protect a resource or community for longer. Recognising this tension across timeframes is a marker of top-level analysis.","reforge":{"stem":"An option provides 500 jobs immediately but a source suggests the resource it exploits will be exhausted within 10 years. What does a strong answer do with this evidence?","options":{"A":"Only mentions the immediate job creation","B":"Assumes the resource will last forever regardless of the source","C":"Weighs the short-term gain against long-term sustainability","D":"Ignores the timeframe entirely"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-06","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"What does it mean to consider 'scale' when evaluating a geographical decision?","options":{"A":"Scale is irrelevant to justifying a geographical decision","B":"An option's impact can differ by local or national scale","C":"Scale only refers to the size of a map used in the resource booklet","D":"Scale means whether the decision affects one person or many people, with no other meaning"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SCALE-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"A proposed airport expansion, for instance, might bring national economic benefits (more trade, more jobs across the country) while creating local costs (noise, displacement, local air pollution) for residents nearby. Strong answers explicitly name which scale each piece of evidence operates at, since a decision that's positive nationally can still be negative locally, and both matter to a full evaluation.","reforge":{"stem":"A source shows a new mine will boost national GDP but increase water pollution in the immediate local river. What should a strong answer do with this?","options":{"A":"Assume local and national impacts are always the same","B":"Ignore scale since it isn't relevant to the mark scheme","C":"Only discuss the national benefit since GDP is more important","D":"Compare the national benefit against the local cost"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-07","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why might different stakeholders (e.g. a local government, an environmental NGO, and a mining company) interpret the same resource booklet evidence differently?","options":{"A":"Stakeholders always agree on the correct interpretation of geographical evidence","B":"Only government stakeholders' opinions are valid in a geographical decision","C":"Stakeholders have different priorities, so weigh the evidence","D":"Evidence has only one possible interpretation regardless of who is reading it"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SCALE-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"This is exactly why 'there is no single correct answer' — a mining company will prioritise the economic evidence (jobs, profit, GDP growth), an environmental NGO will prioritise ecological evidence (habitat loss, pollution, biodiversity), and a local government may prioritise social evidence (housing, services, community impact). A sophisticated answer can acknowledge multiple viewpoints even while reaching its own justified conclusion.","reforge":{"stem":"A geography student's essay considers how an environmental NGO and a logging company might each view the same deforestation evidence differently before reaching a personal conclusion. What skill is this demonstrating?","options":{"A":"Evaluating evidence from several stakeholder views","B":"Simply repeating the evidence without any interpretation","C":"Choosing a stakeholder at random and ignoring the others","D":"Avoiding a final decision altogether"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-08","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"What is the key difference between the command words 'describe', 'explain' and 'justify' as used in Making Geographical Decisions questions?","options":{"A":"All three command words require exactly the same type of answer","B":"'Describe' requires more evidence than 'justify'","C":"'Justify' only requires a one-word answer with no supporting evidence","D":"Justify defends a decision; explain only gives reasons"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-COMMAND-WORDS","scaffold":"Command words set the assessment objective being tested. 'Describe' = state features/patterns from the evidence (AO1/AO3). 'Explain' = give reasons or causes (AO1/AO2). 'Justify' = make and defend a choice, weighing evidence for and against, reaching a supported conclusion (AO3/AO4) — the command word used in the final, highest-mark decision-making question.","reforge":{"stem":"A question asks students to 'justify' which flood management option should be used. What must the answer include that a 'describe' question would not require?","options":{"A":"A list of facts with no argument or decision","B":"A clear decision defended with weighed evidence","C":"A description of the river's long profile","D":"Simply naming all three options with no further comment"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-09","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"What does the concept of 'sustainability' typically require a decision to balance?","options":{"A":"Meeting present needs without harming future ones","B":"Maximising short-term profit regardless of future consequences","C":"A decision that only considers the next twelve months","D":"Environmental protection only, with no consideration of economic or social factors"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-CRITERIA-TRADEOFF","scaffold":"Sustainability sits at the intersection of environmental, economic and social factors — often visualised as three overlapping circles, with a truly sustainable option sitting where all three overlap. Resource booklet options are often deliberately designed so that no single choice is perfectly sustainable, forcing students to justify which trade-off is most acceptable.","reforge":{"stem":"An option scores well on economic and social measures in a resource booklet, but a source shows it will damage a protected wetland. What must a strong answer do?","options":{"A":"State that environmental damage is never relevant to sustainability","B":"Assume the option is automatically unsustainable and cannot be chosen","C":"Compare the environmental cost against the benefits","D":"Ignore the environmental source since two other categories score well"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-10","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why should a decision-making answer reference specific sources by name (e.g. 'Source B shows...') rather than making general, unreferenced claims?","options":{"A":"Sources should only be referenced in the introduction, never in the main argument","B":"Named sources ground the answer in the evidence given","C":"Referencing sources is optional and has no effect on the mark awarded","D":"General claims with no source reference always earn higher marks"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-EVIDENCE-BREADTH","scaffold":"The whole point of the resource-booklet-based question is to test whether a student can select, interpret and apply the evidence they've been given — not just write generically about the topic from memory. Explicitly citing sources (Figure 3, Source D, the table in Source A) is what distinguishes a top-level, evidence-based justification from a generic, unsupported essay.","reforge":{"stem":"Two students write about the same geographical issue. One writes 'building the reservoir would harm the environment.' The other writes 'Source D shows the proposed reservoir would flood 40 hectares of ancient woodland, directly reducing local biodiversity.' Why does the second answer score more highly?","options":{"A":"It is shorter than the first answer","B":"It avoids naming any source material","C":"Both answers are equally strong","D":"It cites specific booklet evidence"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-11","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"A student spends their entire answer describing what each option involves, then writes 'Overall, I think Option A is best' with no further explanation in the final line. What is missing?","options":{"A":"The final line should introduce entirely new evidence not discussed anywhere else in the answer","B":"Nothing — describing each option in detail is sufficient for full marks","C":"A conclusion explaining why Option A outweighs the rest","D":"The student needs to describe the options in even more detail instead of reaching a conclusion"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-JUSTIFIED-CONCLUSION","scaffold":"A common mid-level answer describes all the options accurately but fails to build an argument towards a conclusion, or states a preference without justifying it. Top-level answers integrate evaluation throughout the answer (weighing each source against the others as they go) so the final decision reads as the logical outcome of the evidence already discussed, not an unexplained final-line opinion.","reforge":{"stem":"Which approach to structuring a Making Geographical Decisions answer is most likely to reach the top mark band?","options":{"A":"Weigh the evidence throughout, so the decision follows","B":"Only describing the option that will eventually be chosen","C":"Describing each option in full with no evaluation, then stating a preference in the last sentence","D":"Listing every source without connecting them to any argument"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-12","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why might a resource booklet deliberately include sources that conflict with each other, such as one report claiming a scheme will create jobs and another claiming it will cause net job losses?","options":{"A":"Contradictory evidence means no decision can be justified","B":"Conflicting sources indicate an error in the exam paper that should be ignored","C":"Students should only ever use the source that supports their chosen option","D":"It tests whether a student can weigh contradictory sources"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SOURCE-PROVENANCE","scaffold":"Real geographical issues rarely have clean, one-sided evidence — a mining company's own report predicting job creation may be less impartial than an independent economic study predicting losses. Evaluating the source itself (who produced it, what interest do they have, how was the data collected) alongside its content is a mark of the highest-level answers, and shows the skill of critically evaluating evidence, not just summarising it.","reforge":{"stem":"A resource booklet includes a report commissioned by the company proposing a new development, claiming it will benefit the local economy. What should a strong answer do with this source?","options":{"A":"Treat the source as entirely objective with no need for scrutiny","B":"Use it, but note the company's vested interest","C":"Ignore the source completely because it comes from a company","D":"Assume all sources in a resource booklet are equally unbiased regardless of who produced them"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-13","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why should a student rank the criteria before making a final geographical decision?","options":{"A":"Ranking shows which impacts matter most","B":"It means only the cheapest option can be selected","C":"It removes the need to use sources","D":"It guarantees every stakeholder will agree"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-CRITERIA-TRADEOFF","scaffold":"Ranking criteria makes priorities explicit. A decision may value environmental protection, jobs, cost, risk or fairness differently, but the answer should explain those priorities and apply them consistently to the evidence.","reforge":{"stem":"A student selects an option with lower environmental risk but higher cost. What should they explain?","options":{"A":"That the cheapest option must always be best","B":"That cost is never relevant to geography","C":"Why risk outweighed the extra cost","D":"Nothing beyond naming the chosen option"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-14","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"What is meant by a trade-off in a geographical decision?","options":{"A":"Finding an option with no disadvantages at all","B":"A gain in one area bought with a cost elsewhere","C":"Repeating the same evidence without evaluating it","D":"Ignoring social impacts in favour of economic growth"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-CRITERIA-TRADEOFF","scaffold":"A trade-off occurs when improving one outcome requires accepting a cost elsewhere, such as jobs versus habitat loss or flood protection versus landscape change. Strong answers identify the trade-off and justify why one side should carry more weight in the particular case.","reforge":{"stem":"A new airport creates jobs but increases noise for nearby residents. What is this an example of?","options":{"A":"A physical process rather than a human decision","B":"A decision with only positive impacts","C":"Evidence that noise has no geographical importance","D":"A trade-off between economic gain and social cost"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-FB-01","spec":"GCSE-DEC","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about geographical issues.","template":"A geographical issue involves a contested ___ about land or resource use, with no single ___ answer — geographers must weigh competing ___, economic and social priorities.","blanks":["decision","correct","environmental"],"bank":["decision","correct","environmental","random","factual","historical"],"tag":"MC-GG-DEC-ISSUE-FRAME","scaffold":"Geographical issues are genuine dilemmas that connect multiple topics and force a trade-off between environmental, economic and social priorities — there's no objectively right answer, only a better or worse justified one.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-FB-02","spec":"GCSE-DEC","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about using evidence in a justified decision.","template":"A top-level answer references specific ___ from the resource booklet, considers evidence both ___ and against the chosen option, and explains why the ___ outweigh the drawbacks.","blanks":["sources","for","advantages"],"bank":["sources","for","advantages","opinions","against","disadvantages"],"tag":"MC-GG-DEC-EVIDENCE-BREADTH","scaffold":"A one-sided answer that only lists supporting evidence is capped at a lower mark band — top answers acknowledge counter-evidence and explain why, on balance, their chosen option still wins.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-FB-03","spec":"GCSE-DEC","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about categorising evidence.","template":"Evidence in a resource booklet is often sorted into ___, economic and ___ categories, sometimes alongside ___ factors, to help compare how each option performs.","blanks":["environmental","social","political"],"bank":["environmental","social","political","physical","historical","biological"],"tag":"MC-GG-DEC-ISSUE-FRAME","scaffold":"Sorting evidence into environmental, economic and social (plus sometimes political) categories is the standard method for comparing options fairly across every relevant dimension of impact.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-FB-04","spec":"GCSE-DEC","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about command words.","template":"'Describe' asks what the evidence shows; 'explain' asks for ___; 'justify' asks for a ___ to be defended using ___ evidence, weighed for and against.","blanks":["reasons","decision","referenced"],"bank":["reasons","decision","referenced","opinions","guesses","random"],"tag":"MC-GG-DEC-COMMAND-WORDS","scaffold":"'Justify' is the command word used for the final, highest-mark decision-making question — it demands a defended choice built from weighed, referenced evidence, not just a stated preference.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-FB-05","spec":"GCSE-DEC","type":"fill_blank","stem":"Complete the sentence about evaluating sources.","template":"A strong answer considers who ___ a source and what ___ they might have, since this can affect the source's ___, alongside weighing what the evidence itself shows.","blanks":["produced","interest","reliability"],"bank":["produced","interest","reliability","read","budget","length"],"tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SOURCE-PROVENANCE","scaffold":"Evaluating who produced a source and their possible bias (e.g. a company's own report on its own proposed development) is a top-level skill — it goes beyond just summarising what evidence says to critically assessing how much weight it should carry.","specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-15","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"What makes an explanation based on a resource figure 'blended'?","options":{"A":"It uses two quotations without explaining either","B":"It copies every label and every statistic from the figure in sequence","C":"It replaces evidence with an unsupported opinion","D":"It links figure evidence with geographical knowledge"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-COMMAND-WORDS","scaffold":"Pearson describes strong short explanations as blended when they select relevant evidence from the figure and develop it using the student's own geographical knowledge or terminology.","reforge":{"stem":"A figure shows strong winds and severe soil erosion. Which response best develops the evidence?","options":{"A":"The figure contains arrows and labels","B":"Strong winds remove exposed topsoil, limiting nutrients for tree growth","C":"The figure proves wind is the only control on forest distribution and tree growth","D":"Soil erosion is important because the source says it is"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-16","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why is detailed description alone insufficient in an 'assess' answer?","options":{"A":"The impacts must be weighed and judged","B":"Assess questions require no resource evidence","C":"Only a list of technical terms receives credit","D":"A conclusion must repeat the question exactly"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-COMMAND-WORDS","scaffold":"Assessment goes beyond identifying or describing impacts. A strong answer weighs their significance using criteria such as severity, scale, duration, likelihood or reversibility and reaches a supported judgement.","reforge":{"stem":"Which sentence most clearly assesses an environmental impact?","options":{"A":"The reservoir covers part of the valley and the source marks the exact area flooded","B":"The source shows several affected species","C":"Habitat loss is most serious because it is long-term and difficult to reverse","D":"There are environmental, social and economic impacts"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-17","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"What does it mean to synthesise evidence from two figures?","options":{"A":"Describe the first figure and ignore the second","B":"Connect evidence to form one explanation","C":"Select whichever figure supports an initial opinion","D":"Copy one statistic from each without comparison"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Synthesis means bringing information together so that the sources jointly support a line of reasoning. Merely mentioning both figures separately does not explain the relationship between them.","reforge":{"stem":"Figure 2 shows plentiful geothermal resources and Figure 3 shows oil-dependent transport. Which conclusion best synthesises them?","options":{"A":"Only geothermal energy should be described","B":"The figures must contradict each other","C":"Transport demand is unrelated to the energy mix","D":"Different needs require a diverse energy mix"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-18","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why should a 12-mark decision answer explain why alternatives were rejected?","options":{"A":"Every option must receive exactly the same space","B":"Rejected options can never contain valid benefits","C":"Comparison strengthens the chosen priority","D":"The final choice is awarded without any reasoning"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"A convincing justification shows why the chosen option performs better against important criteria, while acknowledging genuine strengths and weaknesses in alternatives. Dismissing them without evidence weakens the comparison.","reforge":{"stem":"Which structure best supports a justified final decision?","options":{"A":"Choose, compare, reject, then conclude","B":"Describe one option and omit all sources","C":"List every figure without making a choice","D":"State a preference only in the final sentence"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-19","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"A question asks whether another country could copy an energy plan. What must the answer consider?","options":{"A":"Whether every country has identical physical resources","B":"Only whether the original plan was successful","C":"The colour scheme used in the resource booklet","D":"Whether its conditions make the plan transferable"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-TRANSFER-KNOWLEDGE","scaffold":"Transferability depends on physical conditions, finance, technology, governance, land availability and social priorities. A strategy can be adapted rather than copied exactly, for example using locally suitable renewable sources or vegetation.","reforge":{"stem":"A sunny low-income country cannot afford Iceland's geothermal technology. What is the strongest judgement?","options":{"A":"It must abandon every form of renewable energy","B":"Solar may be more transferable if finance is available","C":"Physical geography never affects energy choices","D":"The Icelandic plan can be copied without any financial or physical adaptation"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-20","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"How can renewable energy create impacts at contrasting geographical scales?","options":{"A":"It can cut global emissions but harm local habitats","B":"Its effects are always confined to one household","C":"Local environmental costs automatically cancel global benefits","D":"Scale refers only to the size of a map"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SCALE-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"A renewable project can contribute to lower emissions at national or global scale while creating local costs such as flooded habitat, landscape change or disrupted livelihoods. Assessment should weigh both scales.","reforge":{"stem":"A dam supplies low-carbon national electricity but floods one local valley. What concept helps evaluate this tension?","options":{"A":"Map projection","B":"Weather variability","C":"Geographical scale","D":"Population density alone"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-21","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why should the provenance of a resource-booklet source be considered?","options":{"A":"Every published source is equally reliable regardless of its author or purpose","B":"Its purpose and author may shape the evidence","C":"The newest source must always be accepted","D":"Provenance matters only for photographs"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SOURCE-PROVENANCE","scaffold":"The author, purpose, date and method can affect a source's reliability or perspective. A company prospectus and an environmental impact assessment may select different evidence even when discussing the same project.","reforge":{"stem":"A mining company predicts large job gains from its own proposal. What is the most careful response?","options":{"A":"Reject the claim because companies are always wrong","B":"Accept the figure because it is precise","C":"Ignore the source and use no employment evidence","D":"Use it cautiously and compare independent evidence"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-22","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"What best improves the quality of a Paper 3 extended conclusion?","options":{"A":"Repeating every advantage in the same order","B":"Adding unrelated facts remembered from another topic","C":"Judging by explicit criteria and evidence","D":"Choosing the option with the longest description"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-JUSTIFIED-CONCLUSION","scaffold":"A strong conclusion identifies the criteria that matter most - such as sustainability, cost, scale, time or impact - and uses evidence to explain why the chosen option outweighs alternatives. Precision is more valuable than length alone.","reforge":{"stem":"Which final sentence gives the strongest geographical judgement?","options":{"A":"Option B's long-term benefits outweigh its evidenced local costs","B":"Option B is best because I like it most","C":"All three options have environmental, social and economic advantages and disadvantages in different places","D":"The booklet contains many useful figures"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-23","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"What does AO4 mainly reward in a resource-booklet decision task?","options":{"A":"Recall of every case-study statistic","B":"Choosing the examiner's preferred option","C":"Writing the longest possible introduction","D":"Selecting and using source evidence"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-EVIDENCE-BREADTH","scaffold":"AO4 assesses skills in selecting, interpreting and applying information from the provided resources.","reforge":{"stem":"Which sentence demonstrates AO4 most clearly?","options":{"A":"Deforestation is an important issue","B":"Figure 4: doubled roadside clearance","C":"I remember learning about forests","D":"Option A sounds like the best choice"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-24","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"How should students use their own knowledge in the final decision?","options":{"A":"Apply relevant concepts to strengthen evidence","B":"Add any case study even if unrelated","C":"Replace all resource evidence with memorised facts","D":"Avoid subject terminology from taught topics"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-TRANSFER-KNOWLEDGE","scaffold":"Own knowledge should explain or evaluate source evidence using concepts such as carbon sinks, scale or sustainability.","reforge":{"stem":"Which use of own knowledge is most effective?","options":{"A":"Ignore every figure in the booklet","B":"Describe an unrelated earthquake case study","C":"Apply fragmentation to road evidence","D":"List definitions without applying them"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-25","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why should criteria be weighted before selecting an option?","options":{"A":"Every criterion must be equally important","B":"Priorities make the judgement transparent","C":"The cheapest option always receives priority","D":"Weighting removes all value judgements"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-CRITERIA-TRADEOFF","scaffold":"Explicit priorities such as cost, fairness, sustainability or urgency explain why some evidence carries more weight.","reforge":{"stem":"Which conclusion uses weighted criteria?","options":{"A":"Option C has several advantages","B":"All options are equally good","C":"I chose C before reading the sources","D":"Prioritise long-term water security"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-26","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why should impacts on local stakeholders be separated from national impacts?","options":{"A":"Local people never experience national policy","B":"National growth automatically benefits everyone","C":"Benefits and costs may be unevenly distributed","D":"Scale has no role in geographical decisions"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SCALE-STAKEHOLDERS","scaffold":"A scheme can raise national income while displacing or polluting a particular community.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a distributional judgement?","options":{"A":"National gains do not offset village losses","B":"The project has one cost and one benefit","C":"Every stakeholder will value the scheme equally","D":"Local and national scales are identical"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-27","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why consider both short- and long-term effects?","options":{"A":"Only immediate impacts can be evidenced","B":"Time scale removes uncertainty from evidence","C":"Long-term impacts are always more important","D":"Benefits may change or create later costs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-CRITERIA-TRADEOFF","scaffold":"Jobs may arise quickly while ecosystem loss or debt persists, so a decision should compare duration and timing.","reforge":{"stem":"Which option shows a time trade-off?","options":{"A":"A scheme has the same impact every year","B":"High initial cost; lasting savings","C":"A source contains no date","D":"A map uses two different scales"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-28","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why does reversibility matter when assessing an impact?","options":{"A":"Permanent losses reduce future choices","B":"Irreversible change can be ignored if cheap","C":"Reversible impacts are always harmless","D":"Every environmental effect is reversible"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-TIME-UNCERTAINTY","scaffold":"An impact such as species extinction or ancient habitat loss may deserve greater weight because it cannot readily be undone.","reforge":{"stem":"Which impact is least reversible?","options":{"A":"A short closure of one footpath","B":"Temporary construction noise","C":"Endemic-species extinction","D":"Seasonal traffic congestion"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-29","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"How should uncertainty in a source affect a decision?","options":{"A":"Treat the estimate as exact fact","B":"Acknowledge the range and manage risk","C":"Discard all projections automatically","D":"Select only the most favourable value"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-TIME-UNCERTAINTY","scaffold":"Uncertain evidence can still inform decisions when assumptions, ranges and consequences of error are considered.","reforge":{"stem":"A flood projection ranges from 20 to 45 hectares. What is the best response?","options":{"A":"Use 20 hectares without explaining why","B":"Ignore the source because it has a range","C":"State exactly 45 hectares will flood","D":"Test the option across the range"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-30","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"How can qualitative evidence contribute to a decision?","options":{"A":"It provides only exact numerical totals","B":"It is automatically less useful than data","C":"It reveals experiences and priorities","D":"It proves every stakeholder agrees"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Interviews and testimony provide insight into impacts that may not be captured by numerical measures.","reforge":{"stem":"How should qualitative and quantitative sources be combined?","options":{"A":"Statistics plus stakeholder testimony","B":"Convert every interview into national GDP","C":"Ignore views when numerical data exist","D":"Assume one quotation represents everyone"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-31","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"What should a student do when two credible sources conflict?","options":{"A":"Choose the source with the brighter graphic","B":"Avoid mentioning either in the answer","C":"Assume both claims must be false","D":"Compare methods, interests and evidence"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SOURCE-PROVENANCE","scaffold":"Conflicting evidence should be evaluated through provenance, methodology, scale and relevance rather than hidden.","reforge":{"stem":"Which response best handles conflicting job estimates?","options":{"A":"Use only the larger number","B":"Different assumptions explain the totals","C":"Average them without checking methods","D":"State that precise figures cannot be useful"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-32","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why does the date of a source matter?","options":{"A":"Conditions or technology changed","B":"The date changes the command word","C":"Old evidence is always incorrect","D":"All geographical conditions remain fixed"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-TIME-UNCERTAINTY","scaffold":"Data may become less representative after economic, environmental or policy change.","reforge":{"stem":"When might an older map remain useful?","options":{"A":"When new infrastructure is being located","B":"When current boundaries are required precisely","C":"Showing land-use change over time","D":"When recent population totals are needed"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-33","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"How can Paper 1 climate knowledge strengthen a Paper 3 decision?","options":{"A":"It replaces the need to discuss people","B":"It explains future hazard or resource pressures","C":"It proves every renewable project is sustainable","D":"It applies only to tectonic boundaries"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-TRANSFER-KNOWLEDGE","scaffold":"Synoptic thinking can connect climate change to forests, energy demand, water or hazards within the source issue.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a valid synoptic link?","options":{"A":"Plate margins determine household recycling","B":"CBD land value controls solar output","C":"River velocity measures political influence","D":"Deforestation weakens a carbon sink"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-34","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why must the final Paper 3 decision combine physical and human geography?","options":{"A":"The issue has no environmental dimension","B":"Economic evidence is always sufficient","C":"People and environments interact","D":"Physical processes determine every viewpoint"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-ISSUE-FRAME","scaffold":"Resource decisions involve physical opportunities or hazards alongside social, economic and political impacts.","reforge":{"stem":"Which pairing shows a people-environment interaction?","options":{"A":"Dam power versus habitat loss","B":"Two figures using different font sizes","C":"A population total and an unrelated date","D":"A compass direction and a page number"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-35","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"What must a student do in the final choice-of-three question?","options":{"A":"Recommend all options equally","B":"Describe the booklet without deciding","C":"Invent a fourth option instead","D":"Select one option and justify it"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-JUSTIFIED-CONCLUSION","scaffold":"Students choose one proposal and defend it using sustained evidence, own knowledge and comparison with alternatives.","reforge":{"stem":"Can a student gain high marks with any of the three options?","options":{"A":"Only the first option can reach the top level","B":"Yes, if the justification is well evidenced","C":"The shortest option is always preferred","D":"No, the mark scheme selects one correct policy"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-36","spec":"GCSE-DEC","stem":"Why should a conclusion avoid introducing a completely new unsupported argument?","options":{"A":"It should synthesise the case already made","B":"Conclusions should contain no judgement","C":"New claims automatically gain evaluation marks","D":"Evidence is needed only in the introduction"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-JUSTIFIED-CONCLUSION","scaffold":"The conclusion should draw together weighted evidence and criteria, not surprise the reader with an undeveloped claim.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the best role of a final paragraph?","options":{"A":"List every source without interpretation","B":"Introduce an unrelated case study","C":"Decision plus decisive evidence","D":"Repeat the question word for word"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-ISSUE-FRAME","stem":"A resource booklet offers three schemes, and a student argues none should go ahead. Is 'do nothing' a legitimate answer?","options":{"A":"No, because the question requires one of the three named options to be chosen","B":"Yes, if it is argued with evidence — but the named options must still be weighed","C":"No, because doing nothing can never have any consequences worth discussing","D":"Yes, but only if the student refuses to examine any of the three options offered"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Doing nothing is itself a decision with consequences, and recognising that shows real understanding. But the question sets the options, so a strong answer still evaluates each one and explains why inaction outperforms them, rather than using 'do nothing' to avoid engaging with the resources.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the 'do nothing' option still a decision with consequences?","options":{"A":"Because choosing to do nothing removes all risk from the situation entirely","B":"Because doing nothing always costs more money than any of the alternatives","C":"Because inaction is prohibited under UK planning law in every single case","D":"Because taking no action allows existing problems and trends to continue"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-CRITERIA-TRADEOFF","stem":"A student writes that the best option is a scheme costing far more than the budget in the booklet. What has been overlooked?","options":{"A":"Sustainability, which is the only criterion that ever matters in a decision","B":"Nothing, because the most desirable option is always the correct one to choose","C":"Feasibility — an option that cannot be delivered is not a real choice","D":"The command word, which asks only for a description of each of the options"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Desirability and feasibility are different tests. An option can be the best outcome on paper and still be undeliverable on the stated budget, timescale or technology. Strong answers check the option against the constraints given in the booklet, rather than choosing whatever sounds most ambitious.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the difference between an option being desirable and being feasible?","options":{"A":"Desirable means good outcomes; feasible means it can actually be done","B":"There is no difference; the two words mean the same thing in a decision","C":"Desirable options are cheap to build, while feasible ones are expensive","D":"Feasible options are always more sustainable than desirable ones in practice"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-CRITERIA-TRADEOFF","stem":"A cost-benefit table in a booklet gives construction costs in pounds but leaves the loss of a wetland unpriced. Why does this matter?","options":{"A":"It does not matter, since anything without a price has no real value at all","B":"Because wetlands are the only environmental feature that has any genuine value","C":"Because a cost-benefit table is not permitted to include any monetary figures","D":"Costs that cannot be priced are easily underweighted against the figures"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"What gets counted tends to get weighted. Construction cost is a hard number; habitat loss, community disruption and cultural value are not, so a table that prices one and not the other quietly favours the priced option. Naming that imbalance is a strong evaluative move in an assessment of the sources.","reforge":{"stem":"How should a student handle an important impact that cannot be given a monetary value?","options":{"A":"Leave it out of the answer entirely, since it cannot be compared fairly","B":"State the impact clearly and weigh it alongside the costs that are priced","C":"Assign it an invented monetary figure so it can be added to the table","D":"Assume its value is zero and rely only on the figures the booklet supplies"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-ISSUE-FRAME","stem":"One scheme cuts a city's emissions; another builds flood defences for the rising sea. What is the difference between these approaches?","options":{"A":"The first is mitigation of the cause; the second is adaptation to effects","B":"Both are mitigation, since each responds in some way to a changing climate","C":"The first is adaptation and the second is mitigation, which reduces the hazard","D":"Neither is a recognised approach to managing the risks that climate change brings"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Mitigation reduces the cause, so emissions; adaptation manages the consequences we can no longer avoid. Most real strategies need both, and an answer that identifies which type each option is can then argue about timescale: mitigation acts slowly and globally, adaptation quickly and locally.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do most climate strategies combine mitigation and adaptation rather than choosing one?","options":{"A":"Because adaptation on its own would reduce global emissions far faster","B":"Because mitigation removes the need for any adaptation within a few years","C":"Locked-in warming needs adapting to; emissions still need cutting","D":"Because international law requires every country to fund both approaches"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SCALE-STAKEHOLDERS","stem":"A reservoir would supply a city while flooding three villages. Which framing shows the strongest geographical understanding?","options":{"A":"The scheme is good because more people benefit than are harmed by it overall","B":"Benefits fall on the city while the costs fall on the villages","C":"The scheme should be judged only on the total volume of water it would supply","D":"The scheme is bad because any scheme that displaces residents is unacceptable"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Counting heads is not the same as weighing impacts. The city gains a moderate benefit spread across many people; the villagers lose their homes entirely. Identifying that costs and benefits fall on different groups, at different scales and with different severity, is what an 'assess' or 'justify' question rewards.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is 'more people benefit than are harmed' a weak justification on its own?","options":{"A":"Because the number of people affected can never actually be counted","B":"Because benefits and costs always fall on exactly the same group of people","C":"Because schemes that benefit a majority are always rejected by planners","D":"It ignores how severe each impact is and who has to bear the worst of it"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-CRITERIA-TRADEOFF","stem":"A student judges Option A on cost, Option B on environmental impact and Option C on public support. What is wrong with this?","options":{"A":"Nothing, because each option should be assessed on whichever criterion suits it","B":"Three separate criteria is always too many for a decision-making answer to use","C":"The options are not being compared, since each is judged on a different measure","D":"Cost should never be used as a criterion when judging a geographical scheme"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"A comparison needs a constant. Applying a different test to each option produces three unconnected verdicts and no basis for choosing between them, and it usually hides a preference already made. Set the criteria first, then run every option through all of them, including the ones it fails.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main advantage of setting decision criteria before examining the options?","options":{"A":"It reduces the risk of choosing first and justifying afterwards","B":"It removes the need to refer to the resource booklet sources at all","C":"It guarantees that the cheapest of the available options will be selected","D":"It means only one option needs to be examined in any detail at all"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-TIME-UNCERTAINTY","stem":"A flood wall protects one town but pushes floodwater downstream onto another. What is this an example of?","options":{"A":"An adaptation strategy that successfully reduces flood risk across the whole basin","B":"A trade-off that was fully anticipated and accepted by all parties in advance","C":"A failure of the scheme, since the wall did not protect the town it defended","D":"An unintended consequence, where a scheme creates a problem elsewhere"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Schemes act on systems, so relief in one place can transfer the problem rather than remove it. Naming that possibility, and asking whether the booklet's sources would have detected it, distinguishes a strong evaluation from one that accepts each option's stated benefits at face value.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does thinking about a river basin as a whole system help anticipate unintended consequences?","options":{"A":"Because a system approach guarantees no scheme will ever cause any harm","B":"Because a change at one point alters conditions further along it","C":"Because river basins are unaffected by anything humans build within them","D":"Because considering the whole basin removes the need to consult stakeholders"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-TIME-UNCERTAINTY","stem":"A student recommends an option and adds that its effects should be monitored and the scheme adjusted if needed. Does this strengthen the answer?","options":{"A":"Yes — it acknowledges uncertainty without avoiding a clear choice","B":"No, because a decision-making answer must never mention what happens afterwards","C":"No, because recommending monitoring shows the student is unsure of the decision","D":"Yes, because monitoring guarantees that the chosen scheme will succeed in future"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Examiners reward a decision that is committed but not overclaimed. Projections in a booklet carry uncertainty, and saying how you would check the outcome shows you understand that without retreating into 'it depends'. The essential thing is that a clear choice is still made first.","reforge":{"stem":"How should genuine uncertainty in a source affect a student's final decision?","options":{"A":"It should be ignored, since booklet sources are always fully reliable","B":"It should lead the student to avoid reaching any decision at all","C":"State it, make the decision, then qualify your confidence","D":"It should cause the student to reject every option the booklet offers"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-EVIDENCE-BREADTH","stem":"A booklet includes one resident's quotation opposing a scheme and a survey of 500 residents showing majority support. How should a student use these?","options":{"A":"Use only the quotation, because individual voices matter more than survey data","B":"Use both — the survey shows the pattern, the quotation what is at stake","C":"Discard both, since the quotation and the survey contradict one another directly","D":"Use only the survey, because a personal quotation has no place in a geography answer"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"Quantitative and qualitative evidence answer different questions. The survey establishes how widespread a view is; the quotation shows its depth and human meaning. Treating one individual as representative is the error, and so is dismissing lived experience as irrelevant because it is not numerical.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the risk of building an argument on a single resident's quotation?","options":{"A":"Quotations from residents are never included in a resource booklet","B":"A quotation can only be used if it agrees with the survey data given","C":"Qualitative evidence carries no credit in a decision-making answer","D":"One person's view may not represent the wider community's position"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-SYNTHESIS","stem":"Which sentence best demonstrates synthesis of two booklet sources rather than simple listing?","options":{"A":"Figure 2 shows rainfall data, and Figure 4 shows the population of the region","B":"Figure 2 is about rainfall. Figure 4 is about population. Both are relevant here","C":"Figure 4's population growth falls in the very area Figure 2 shows is drying","D":"Figure 2 and Figure 4 both contain useful information about the study region"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Synthesis makes one source do work on another. Naming both figures is not enough; the mark comes from the connection, here that demand is rising exactly where supply is falling. A useful test: if removing one figure would not change the sentence, no synthesis has happened.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the quickest test of whether a sentence genuinely synthesises two sources?","options":{"A":"Whether removing one source would change the point the sentence is making","B":"Whether the sentence is longer than the other sentences in the paragraph","C":"Whether both sources appear somewhere within the same paragraph of the answer","D":"Whether the sentence includes the figure numbers of both of the sources"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-JUSTIFIED-CONCLUSION","stem":"A student's conclusion states a choice and also explains the strongest objection to it and why it is outweighed. Why is this effective?","options":{"A":"It shows the student could not decide between the options that were offered","B":"It fills space in the conclusion when there is nothing further left to say","C":"It is not effective, because a conclusion should only restate points made earlier","D":"It shows the decision survives the best case against it"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"A justified decision is one that has been tested. Handling the strongest counter-argument, rather than the weakest, demonstrates the choice was reached by weighing evidence and not by preference. Conclusions that only restate advantages read as assertion, however confident they sound.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is it a weakness to address only the weakest argument against your chosen option?","options":{"A":"Because the weakest argument is always the one examiners expect to see","B":"It leaves the real objection unanswered, so the decision is untested","C":"Because every argument against an option carries exactly equal weight","D":"Because a conclusion should never mention any opposing argument at all"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"},{"id":"GCSE-DEC-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-DEC","tag":"MC-GG-DEC-COMMAND-WORDS","stem":"A question asks a student to 'justify' their chosen option. What does this specifically require beyond explaining it?","options":{"A":"Giving reasons the choice beats the alternatives, backed by evidence","B":"A longer answer, since 'justify' questions always carry more marks than others","C":"A detailed description of what each of the three options would actually involve","D":"An explanation of how the chosen scheme would be built and funded in practice"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Explain asks why something happens; justify asks why this choice rather than another. That comparative element is the difference, and it is why a justify answer must engage with the rejected options. Explaining your option thoroughly while ignoring the others leaves the comparison unmade.","reforge":{"stem":"What distinguishes an 'evaluate' question from an 'explain' question?","options":{"A":"Explain requires evidence, whereas evaluate is based only on personal opinion","B":"Evaluate asks for a longer answer, but the skill being tested is the same","C":"Evaluate asks for a judgement on strengths and weaknesses, not just reasons","D":"Explain asks for a judgement while evaluate asks only for a clear description"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-dec"}]},"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS":{"label":"Geographical Skills","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-01","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"When giving a grid reference, which part should be read first?","options":{"A":"The easting, then the northing","B":"The northing, then the easting","C":"The map scale, then the easting","D":"The contour interval, then the northing"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-GRID-REFERENCES","scaffold":"Grid references are read across then up: the easting is the first number and the northing is the second. A four-figure reference identifies a grid square; a six-figure reference locates a more exact point within it.","reforge":{"stem":"A student writes the grid reference 5142. What does the first pair of digits show?","options":{"A":"The distance travelled along a route","B":"The height of the location above sea level","C":"The easting across the map","D":"The northing, showing how far up the map the square is"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-02","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"What is the main purpose of a six-figure grid reference?","options":{"A":"To show the map's contour interval","B":"A precise point within the square","C":"To identify the direction of north","D":"To calculate the gradient of a slope"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-GRID-REFERENCES","scaffold":"A six-figure grid reference divides a grid square into smaller parts, allowing an exact feature or point to be located. The first three figures are eastings and the last three are northings.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a six-figure reference more precise than a four-figure reference?","options":{"A":"It uses a larger map scale in every case","B":"It measures distance along a road more accurately","C":"It includes the height of the land as well as its location","D":"It subdivides the square for a closer location"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-03","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"A map has a scale of 1:25,000. What ground distance does 4 cm represent?","options":{"A":"100 m","B":"250 m","C":"1 km","D":"750 m"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-SCALE","scaffold":"At 1:25,000, 1 cm represents 25,000 cm, or 250 m. Therefore 4 cm represents 4 × 250 m = 1,000 m = 1 km. Convert centimetres to metres or kilometres before giving the final answer.","reforge":{"stem":"On a 1:50,000 map, a footpath measures 2 cm. What is its real distance?","options":{"A":"1 km","B":"25 km","C":"100 m","D":"2 km"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-04","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"What is the most reliable way to measure a curved route on a map?","options":{"A":"Measure only the straight-line distance between its endpoints","B":"Use the largest contour value along the route","C":"Count the number of grid squares it crosses","D":"Mark each bend on paper, then use the scale"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-SCALE","scaffold":"A curved route can be broken into short straight sections. Mark each section on a strip of paper, pivoting at each change in direction, then compare the total with the map scale. A straight-line measurement underestimates a winding route.","reforge":{"stem":"Why would measuring only between the endpoints underestimate the length of a winding road?","options":{"A":"The scale changes at bends","B":"The route has bends","C":"Height is included","D":"Curves cross a contour"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-05","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"A grid bearing is measured from which starting direction?","options":{"A":"Grid north, clockwise through 360°","B":"The nearest contour line, clockwise through 180°","C":"Magnetic south, anticlockwise through 360°","D":"The direction of the steepest slope, through 90°"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-CONVENTIONS","scaffold":"A grid bearing is measured clockwise from grid north, which is 000°. East is 090°, south is 180° and west is 270°. Bearings are written as three figures, such as 280°.","reforge":{"stem":"A bearing of 180° from point A indicates which direction?","options":{"A":"Due west","B":"Due east","C":"South","D":"Due north"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-06","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"What do contour lines that are close together show?","options":{"A":"A flat area","B":"A steep slope","C":"A level river channel","D":"Closely spaced buildings"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CONTOUR-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Contour lines join points of equal height. When they are close together, the height changes rapidly over a short horizontal distance, showing a steep slope. Widely spaced contours indicate gentler slopes or flatter land.","reforge":{"stem":"Which contour pattern would be most suitable for locating a gentle slope?","options":{"A":"Concentric lines with no height values","B":"Very closely spaced contour lines","C":"A single line crossing the map","D":"Very widely spaced contour lines"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-07","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"What does a four-figure grid reference identify?","options":{"A":"An exact point such as a trigonometrical station","B":"The height of a hill above sea level","C":"A grid square containing the feature","D":"The bearing from one feature to another"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-GRID-REFERENCES","scaffold":"A four-figure grid reference names the square containing a feature. It is less precise than a six-figure reference, which estimates the feature's position within that square.","reforge":{"stem":"A feature lies in square 2748. Which reference gives its exact position within that square?","options":{"A":"A six-figure reference","B":"A four-figure reference only","C":"A contour interval","D":"A compass direction"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-08","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"Which type of data is most suitable for a line graph?","options":{"A":"Percentages that must total exactly 100%","B":"Named categories with no numerical order","C":"A list of land uses at separate sites","D":"Continuous data over time"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CORRELATION-DATA","scaffold":"Line graphs are useful for continuous numerical data and for showing change over time or distance. Both axes should be labelled and the scale should be chosen carefully because altered scales can mislead.","reforge":{"stem":"A class records river discharge every 500 metres downstream. Which graph would best show the pattern?","options":{"A":"A pie chart of the sampling sites","B":"A line graph with both variables","C":"A population pyramid for the river basin","D":"A pictogram using one symbol per site"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-09","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"Which feature distinguishes a histogram from a bar chart?","options":{"A":"A histogram displays continuous data with touching bars","B":"A histogram is only used for percentages","C":"A histogram always has a title but a bar chart does not","D":"A histogram uses categories while a bar chart uses continuous data"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CHART-SELECTION","scaffold":"Histograms show grouped continuous data, so the bars touch because there are no gaps between the class intervals. Bar charts usually show discrete categories, so separate bars have spaces between them.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should the bars of a histogram touch?","options":{"A":"The categories are separate","B":"It makes values equal","C":"Intervals have no gaps","D":"A ruler needs them to touch"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-10","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"A pie chart represents 25% of a data set. What angle should this sector have?","options":{"A":"45°","B":"90°","C":"120°","D":"180°"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-PERCENTAGE-CALCULATION","scaffold":"A full pie chart is 360°. Calculate 25 ÷ 100 × 360 = 90°. The sector angle is proportional to the percentage of the whole represented.","reforge":{"stem":"A land-use category represents 15% of a total. What is its pie-chart angle?","options":{"A":"36°","B":"45° (a different sector angle)","C":"15°","D":"54°"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-11","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"Which data would a rose diagram be most suitable for displaying?","options":{"A":"The population of towns by age group","B":"Annual rainfall totals in millimetres","C":"The frequency of wind directions at a site","D":"The percentage contribution of three soil types"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CHART-SELECTION","scaffold":"Rose diagrams use compass directions as axes and bars extending from the centre. They are suitable for directional data such as wind direction, noise direction or light levels.","reforge":{"stem":"A rose diagram for a coastal site has its longest bar pointing southwest. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Frequent southwest winds","B":"The southwest is the steepest direction on the map","C":"The site has its highest rainfall in the southwest","D":"Most buildings face southwest"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-12","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"What is a key limitation of a choropleth map?","options":{"A":"It cannot show any pattern across an area","B":"It can only display data from one location","C":"It requires every value to be shown as a separate photograph","D":"It can hide variation within zones"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-CONVENTIONS","scaffold":"Choropleth maps shade areas according to value ranges. They show broad spatial patterns clearly, but the boundary between shades can look like a sudden change, while differences within one shaded area are hidden.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a proportional-symbol map be better than a choropleth map for traffic counts taken at exact sites?","options":{"A":"It removes the need to record any data","B":"Exact-site symbols avoid zone boundaries","C":"It makes every traffic count appear identical","D":"It can only show qualitative information"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-13","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"A survey count rises from 40 to 50. What is the percentage increase?","options":{"A":"25%","B":"10% (the raw increase)","C":"20%","D":"40% of the final value"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-PERCENTAGE-CALCULATION","scaffold":"Percentage increase = (final value − original value) ÷ original value × 100. Here (50 − 40) ÷ 40 × 100 = 25%. The original value is the denominator, not the final value.","reforge":{"stem":"A river's discharge falls from 80 m³/s to 60 m³/s. What is the percentage decrease?","options":{"A":"33%","B":"20%","C":"25%","D":"75% (using the wrong base)"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-14","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"What is the mean of the four values 4, 6, 8 and 10?","options":{"A":"6","B":"7","C":"8","D":"28"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-PERCENTAGE-CALCULATION","scaffold":"Add the values and divide by the number of values: (4 + 6 + 8 + 10) ÷ 4 = 28 ÷ 4 = 7. The mean is sensitive to unusually high or low values.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the median of 3, 5, 6, 9 and 12?","options":{"A":"5","B":"9 (the upper middle value)","C":"7","D":"6"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-15","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"A data set contains the values 12, 17, 9 and 14. What is its range?","options":{"A":"5","B":"7","C":"8","D":"9 (the highest value)"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-SUMMARY-STATISTICS","scaffold":"Range is the highest value minus the lowest value. 17 − 9 = 8. It describes the spread using only the two extreme values.","reforge":{"stem":"A survey records 6, 10, 11, 15 and 18 responses at five sites. What is the range?","options":{"A":"12","B":"11","C":"10","D":"24 (highest minus lowest)"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-16","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"What does a positive correlation on a scatter graph show?","options":{"A":"One variable rises while the other falls","B":"The variables have no measurable relationship","C":"Every point has exactly the same value","D":"The two variables tend to increase together"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CORRELATION-DATA","scaffold":"Positive correlation means that higher values of one variable tend to be associated with higher values of the other. A best-fit line usually slopes from bottom left to top right; correlation alone does not prove causation.","reforge":{"stem":"A scatter graph shows that wider river channels generally have greater depth. What type of relationship is this?","options":{"A":"Negative correlation","B":"Positive correlation","C":"A proportional-symbol relationship","D":"No correlation"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-17","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"When asked to describe a photograph, what should a student mainly do?","options":{"A":"State only visible features","B":"Explain how each feature formed","C":"Judge the photographer's purpose","D":"Add unseen case-study facts"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-COMMUNICATE-EVIDENCE","scaffold":"Describe means saying what can be observed: physical features, human features, relief, vegetation, land use or settlement patterns. Explanation and interpretation may be required by a later command word, but should not replace accurate observation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which response is most clearly descriptive of a photograph of a settlement?","options":{"A":"The buildings follow a regeneration scheme","B":"The settlement has a high crime rate","C":"Visible buildings and road layout","D":"The area will become more expensive"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-18","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"Which feature should be included in a clear field sketch?","options":{"A":"A detailed artistic background unrelated to the enquiry","B":"Title, location, direction and labelled features","C":"A scale that is perfectly accurate for every feature","D":"Every visible object, even when it is irrelevant"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-COMMUNICATE-EVIDENCE","scaffold":"A useful field sketch is selective rather than artistic. It should have a frame, title, location or site number, compass direction, key features and clear labels. It can omit irrelevant detail and does not need an exact scale.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a field sketch be useful even when its scale is not accurate?","options":{"A":"It guarantees that every measurement is correct","B":"It records more precise distances than a map","C":"It replaces the need for a risk assessment","D":"It selects relevant enquiry features"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-19","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"What is most important when making a decision in an issue-evaluation question?","options":{"A":"Choose the cheapest option without considering other evidence","B":"Repeat the information from the booklet without taking a position","C":"Weigh evidence and justify a decision","D":"Select the option that appears first in the resource booklet"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-COMMUNICATE-EVIDENCE","scaffold":"Making geographical decisions tests interpretation, analysis and evaluation. A strong answer uses specific evidence from the resource material, adds relevant geographical knowledge, considers competing impacts and explains why the chosen option is preferred.","reforge":{"stem":"A resource booklet gives evidence for and against a new wind farm. Which approach best supports a high-level answer?","options":{"A":"Compare impacts before judging","B":"Describe every source but avoid reaching a conclusion","C":"Use only the evidence supporting the wind farm","D":"Choose the option with the most positive words in the booklet"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-20","spec":"GCSE-GEO-SKILLS","stem":"A map has a scale of 1:50,000. A route measures 2 cm on the map. What is the ground distance?","options":{"A":"500 m","B":"750 m","C":"2 km","D":"1 km"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-SCALE","scaffold":"At 1:50,000, 1 cm represents 50,000 cm, or 500 m. Therefore 2 cm represents 1,000 m, which is 1 km. Always convert the scale units before selecting the answer.","reforge":{"stem":"A straight section measures 3 cm on a 1:25,000 map. What distance does it represent on the ground?","options":{"A":"250 m","B":"750 m","C":"7.5 km","D":"1.25 km"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-21","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"On an OS map, what does the number 184 beside a small dot usually show?","options":{"A":"The exact elevation at that point","B":"The grid-square population","C":"The bearing to the nearest summit","D":"The annual rainfall in millimetres"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-RELIEF-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"A spot height marks the surveyed elevation of a precise point, normally in metres above mean sea level.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a triangulation pillar symbol identify?","options":{"A":"The centre of a settlement","B":"A public telephone beside a road","C":"A surveyed control point","D":"The start of a national trail"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-22","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"When contour lines cross a river valley, which way does their V-shape point?","options":{"A":"Toward the river mouth","B":"Upstream toward higher land","C":"Toward the lowest adjacent contour","D":"Across the steepest valley side"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CONTOUR-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Contours bend upstream where they cross a valley because elevation rises toward the river's source.","reforge":{"stem":"How can a valley be recognised from contours alone?","options":{"A":"Contour values fall toward the source","B":"Every contour forms a perfect circle","C":"The lines remain equally straight throughout","D":"Contour bends point toward higher ground"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-23","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"A path rises 120 m over 3 km. What is the average gradient in metres per kilometre?","options":{"A":"20 m per km","B":"60 m per km","C":"40 m per km","D":"360 m per km"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CONTOUR-INTERPRETATION","scaffold":"Divide vertical change by horizontal distance: 120 / 3 = 40 metres per kilometre.","reforge":{"stem":"A road climbs 150 m in 5 km. What is its average gradient?","options":{"A":"30 m per km","B":"15 m per km","C":"25 m per km","D":"75 m per km"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-24","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"Why can a printed map's stated scale become wrong after photocopy enlargement?","options":{"A":"Ground distances expand with the sheet","B":"North changes direction on the enlarged copy","C":"Contour heights double automatically","D":"Map length changes; ground length does not"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-SCALE","scaffold":"Enlarging a map changes distances measured on paper, so the representative fraction or scale bar must be adjusted.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the safest way to measure distance on a map that may have been resized?","options":{"A":"Count grid squares as one kilometre each","B":"Use a scale bar printed on the resized map","C":"Assume every photocopy is exactly full size","D":"Use the original ratio without checking"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-25","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"What should a student do when an unfamiliar OS map symbol appears?","options":{"A":"Consult the map legend","B":"Infer its meaning from its colour alone","C":"Assume it marks a tourist attraction","D":"Replace it with a contour value"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-CONVENTIONS","scaffold":"OS symbols are standardised, but the key is the authoritative guide; colour alone may represent several feature types.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the legend essential when interpreting a blue map symbol?","options":{"A":"Blue always means a navigable river","B":"Every blue symbol marks the coastline","C":"It distinguishes water features","D":"Map symbols change meaning with altitude"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-26","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"How is a topographic cross-section constructed?","options":{"A":"Plot land use against population density","B":"Plot heights along a transect","C":"Join every settlement with a straight line","D":"Copy contour lines without using height"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-RELIEF-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Where a transect crosses contours, transfer distance and elevation to axes and join the points smoothly.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a cross-section show especially well?","options":{"A":"Migration flows between countries","B":"Exact annual rainfall at each point","C":"The age of every building","D":"Changes in relief along a line"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-27","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"A percentage rises from 40% to 50%. What is the increase in percentage points?","options":{"A":"25 percentage points","B":"90 percentage points","C":"10 percentage points","D":"50 percentage points"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-PERCENTAGE-CALCULATION","scaffold":"Percentage-point change subtracts the two percentages: 50 - 40 = 10 points. This differs from the relative percentage increase.","reforge":{"stem":"A share rises from 20% to 25%. What is the percentage-point increase?","options":{"A":"5 percentage points","B":"2 percentage points","C":"4 percentage points","D":"25 percentage points"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-28","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"A district has 24,000 people in 12 km². What is its population density?","options":{"A":"288,000 people per km²","B":"12,000 people per km²","C":"24,000 people per km²","D":"2,000 people per km²"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-SUMMARY-STATISTICS","scaffold":"Population density equals population divided by area: 24,000 / 12 = 2,000 people per square kilometre.","reforge":{"stem":"A town has 45,000 people across 15 km². What is its density?","options":{"A":"30,000 people per km²","B":"3,000 people per km²","C":"45,000 people per km²","D":"675,000 people per km²"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-29","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"A map area ranges from 120 m to 360 m elevation. What is its relief?","options":{"A":"240 m","B":"120 m","C":"360 m","D":"480 m"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-RELIEF-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Relief is the difference between the highest and lowest elevations: 360 - 120 = 240 m.","reforge":{"stem":"The highest point is 510 m and the lowest is 170 m. What is the relief?","options":{"A":"170 m","B":"280 m","C":"340 m","D":"680 m"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-30","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"An index uses 100 as its base. What does a value of 125 mean?","options":{"A":"The value fell by 125%","B":"25% above the base","C":"The value increased by 125%","D":"The value is 25% of the base"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-PERCENTAGE-CALCULATION","scaffold":"An index of 100 represents the base; 125 is 25 index points, or 25%, higher.","reforge":{"stem":"A chart sets 2020 equal to 100 and shows 2025 at 80. How has the value changed?","options":{"A":"The base increased by 80%","B":"20% above the base","C":"80% below the base","D":"20% below the base"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-31","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"What is the mode of 2, 3, 3, 5, 7 and 8?","options":{"A":"2","B":"8","C":"3","D":"5"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-SUMMARY-STATISTICS","scaffold":"The mode is the value occurring most frequently, which is 3.","reforge":{"stem":"A land-use survey records R, C, R, I, R, C. What is the modal category?","options":{"A":"Residential","B":"Industrial","C":"Commercial","D":"There is no mode"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-32","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"What does a downward trend from left to right on a scatter graph indicate?","options":{"A":"A positive correlation","B":"No variables were measured","C":"A perfect causal relationship","D":"A negative correlation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CORRELATION-DATA","scaffold":"A negative correlation means one variable tends to decrease as the other increases.","reforge":{"stem":"Which pattern shows no clear correlation?","options":{"A":"Every point on one upward line","B":"Points scattered without a trend","C":"Points falling tightly left to right","D":"Points rising tightly left to right"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-33","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"What is a divided bar chart especially useful for showing?","options":{"A":"The composition of a whole","B":"The exact route between two places","C":"Relief along a cross-section","D":"Direction measured as a bearing"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CHART-SELECTION","scaffold":"A divided bar partitions a fixed total into proportional categories, making composition easy to compare.","reforge":{"stem":"What should the segments of a percentage divided bar total?","options":{"A":"The largest category only","B":"The number of survey sites","C":"100% of the whole","D":"The map's contour interval"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-34","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"Why might a radar graph be used to compare two places?","options":{"A":"It shows migration direction with arrows","B":"It compares several indicators together","C":"It maps exact locations using coordinates","D":"It calculates distance from a ratio scale"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CHART-SELECTION","scaffold":"Each spoke represents an indicator, so profiles for places or times can be compared across multiple dimensions.","reforge":{"stem":"What is essential for a fair radar-graph comparison?","options":{"A":"Join categories in a random order","B":"Give each place different indicator meanings","C":"Remove labels from every axis","D":"Consistent spoke scales"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-35","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","stem":"What does a proportional circle map communicate?","options":{"A":"Direction of flow using arrowheads","B":"Change over time using joined points","C":"Magnitude through symbol area","D":"Relief using contour spacing"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CHART-SELECTION","scaffold":"Circle sizes represent values at locations, allowing spatial differences in magnitude to be compared.","reforge":{"stem":"What must a proportional-symbol map include for interpretation?","options":{"A":"A size key linked to values","B":"A written conclusion inside each circle","C":"A climate projection to 2100","D":"Identical symbols for every magnitude"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-01","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-CONVENTIONS","stem":"A location is given as 51°N, 0°W. Which line does the 51 refer to?","options":{"A":"The height of the land at that point, measured in metres above sea level","B":"Longitude, measured in degrees east or west of the Greenwich meridian","C":"The map's grid north, measured clockwise from the top of the sheet edge","D":"Latitude, measured in degrees north or south of the Equator"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Latitude comes first and runs east to west, measuring distance north or south of the Equator. Longitude follows, running pole to pole and measuring east or west of Greenwich. The order matters: reversing them can place a location in the wrong hemisphere entirely.","reforge":{"stem":"Which line of longitude is defined as 0° and used as the reference for all others?","options":{"A":"The Equator, which divides the northern and southern hemispheres equally","B":"The Greenwich meridian, running through London from pole to pole","C":"The Tropic of Cancer, which lies at approximately 23.5 degrees north","D":"The International Date Line, which runs down the middle of the Pacific"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-02","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-CONVENTIONS","stem":"A student measures a bearing of 270° from a viewpoint. In which direction are they looking?","options":{"A":"West, since bearings run clockwise from north through 090, 180 and 270","B":"East, because bearings are always measured from the eastern edge of the map","C":"North, because 270 degrees is measured anticlockwise from the north line","D":"South, because 270 degrees is three quarters of the way round the compass"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Bearings are three figures measured clockwise from north. So 090 is east, 180 south, 270 west and 360 back to north. Writing 270° as west is the check: if a bearing answer does not match the rough compass direction you can see on the map, the protractor was aligned wrongly.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are bearings always written with three figures, such as 045° rather than 45°?","options":{"A":"Because three figures show the distance as well as the direction travelled","B":"Because a bearing can never be smaller than one hundred degrees in practice","C":"To avoid confusion, since a bearing runs from 000° all the way to 360°","D":"Because the third figure indicates which map sheet is being used at the time"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-03","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-SCALE","stem":"On a 1:25,000 OS map, a woodland covers roughly 6 full grid squares. What area is that on the ground?","options":{"A":"6 hectares, because one grid square covers exactly one hectare of ground","B":"6 square kilometres, since each grid square is one kilometre across","C":"600 square metres, since each grid square measures 100 m by 100 m","D":"6 square centimetres, which is the area measured directly on the map sheet"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"OS grid squares are 1 km by 1 km on the ground whatever the map scale, so each is 1 km². Counting squares therefore gives area directly. On a 1:25,000 sheet each square is 4 cm on paper; on 1:50,000 it is 2 cm, but the ground area it represents is unchanged.","reforge":{"stem":"How should you estimate the area of a lake that covers parts of several grid squares?","options":{"A":"Count every square the lake touches, however little of it lies inside","B":"Count only those grid squares that the lake fills completely and ignore the rest","C":"Measure the length of the lake and multiply that figure by the map's scale","D":"Count full squares, then combine the part-filled ones into approximate wholes"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-04","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-MAP-SCALE","stem":"Two villages are 3 km apart on the ground. How far apart will they be on a 1:50,000 map?","options":{"A":"1.5 cm, because the ground distance is halved when it is drawn on the map","B":"15 cm, since one kilometre is represented by five centimetres at this scale","C":"6 cm, since 1 cm represents 0.5 km, so 3 km is six centimetres on the map","D":"3 cm, because the scale means one centimetre always represents one kilometre"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"At 1:50,000, 1 cm on the map is 50,000 cm on the ground, which is 500 m or 0.5 km. Working backwards, 3 km needs 3 ÷ 0.5 = 6 cm. Converting the ratio into 'what one centimetre is worth' first makes both directions of the calculation straightforward.","reforge":{"stem":"On a 1:25,000 map, what ground distance does 1 cm represent?","options":{"A":"250 metres, since 25,000 cm is 250 m on the ground at this map scale","B":"25 metres, because the scale divides every measurement by one thousand","C":"2.5 kilometres, which is 25,000 divided by ten thousand centimetres","D":"25 kilometres, since the ratio gives the distance directly in kilometres"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-05","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CHART-SELECTION","stem":"A map shows lines joining all points of equal rainfall across a region. What type of map is this?","options":{"A":"A choropleth map, which shades administrative areas by their recorded value","B":"A proportional symbol map, where circle area represents the size of a value","C":"A flow line map, where arrow width shows the volume moving between places","D":"An isoline map, which joins points that share the same recorded value"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Isolines join equal values, so contours (height), isobars (pressure) and isohyets (rainfall) are all the same technique. They suit continuous data that varies smoothly across space, unlike a choropleth, which forces the data into administrative units and implies a sudden change at each boundary.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are contour lines a form of isoline?","options":{"A":"Because they are always drawn using a single unbroken brown line","B":"Because each line joins points that are all at the same height above sea level","C":"Because they show the steepness of a slope rather than any fixed value","D":"Because they can only ever be used on Ordnance Survey mapping within the whole UK"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-06","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CHART-SELECTION","stem":"A student wants to show where individual crimes occurred across a town, one mark per incident. Which technique fits best?","options":{"A":"A dot map, with one dot placed at the location of each recorded incident","B":"A pie chart showing the proportion of crimes falling into each separate category","C":"A line graph plotting the number of crimes recorded in each month of the year","D":"A radar graph comparing several different characteristics of two chosen places"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Technique follows the question. A dot map preserves location for individual events, showing clustering that an area-based choropleth would average away. A pie chart or line graph answers composition or change over time instead, which are different questions about the same data.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main drawback of a dot map when dots become very densely packed?","options":{"A":"The dots automatically change colour once they begin to overlap each other","B":"Dot maps simply cannot display any data that has been collected across a town","C":"Overlapping dots become impossible to count, so the density is underestimated","D":"Dense areas of the map are always shown with fewer dots than sparse ones"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-07","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-SUMMARY-STATISTICS","stem":"Six river depth readings are 22, 25, 28, 31, 35 and 40 cm. What is the median?","options":{"A":"28 cm, because it is the middle value once the readings are placed in order","B":"29.5 cm, the mean of the two middle values, 28 and 31, in the ordered list","C":"30.2 cm, which is the mean of all six of the depth readings taken together","D":"18 cm, which is the difference between the largest and smallest readings"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"With an even number of values there is no single middle reading, so the median is the mean of the two central ones: (28 + 31) ÷ 2 = 29.5. Taking one of them, usually the lower, is the standard error. Ordering the data first is essential and is where most marks are lost.","reforge":{"stem":"Five pebble measurements are 4, 6, 7, 9 and 40 mm. Why is the median a better summary than the mean here?","options":{"A":"Because the median is always larger than the mean in any data set","B":"Because the mean simply cannot be calculated when a data set contains five values","C":"Because the median uses every value while the mean discards the extremes","D":"The single very large value pulls the mean upward, but barely moves the median"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-08","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-SUMMARY-STATISTICS","stem":"A town's house prices are strongly skewed by a few very expensive properties. Which measure best represents a typical price?","options":{"A":"The mean, because it takes account of every single property in the town","B":"The range, because it shows the full spread from cheapest to most expensive","C":"The median, because it is not distorted by a small number of extreme values","D":"The mode, because the most frequently occurring price is always typical"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Skewed data is exactly where averages diverge. A handful of multi-million-pound houses lifts the mean above what almost anyone pays, while the median sits at the middle of the actual distribution. This is why published house price statistics quote medians, and it is a strong evaluation point in fieldwork.","reforge":{"stem":"What does it mean to say a data set is skewed?","options":{"A":"Values are bunched towards one end, with a tail of extremes at the other","B":"That the data was collected using an unreliable or biased method","C":"That the data contains exactly the same number of high and low values","D":"That the data cannot be displayed on any single form of graph or chart at all"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-09","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CORRELATION-DATA","stem":"A student investigates whether distance from a river affects house price. Which variable belongs on the x-axis?","options":{"A":"House price, because it is the variable the student is most interested in","B":"Whichever variable happens to produce the neatest looking line of best fit","C":"Neither, because a scatter graph does not distinguish between its two axes","D":"Distance from the river, since it is the independent variable being tested"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"The independent variable, the one you choose or cannot change, goes on the horizontal x-axis; the dependent variable, the one that responds, goes on the vertical y-axis. Here distance is fixed by the site and price responds to it, so distance is independent. Getting this the wrong way round is a common mark loss.","reforge":{"stem":"In an investigation into whether altitude affects temperature, which is the dependent variable?","options":{"A":"Altitude, because it is the factor the student is choosing to measure","B":"Temperature, because it is expected to respond to a change in altitude","C":"Neither variable is dependent in an investigation of this particular kind","D":"Both variables are dependent, since each one affects the other equally"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-10","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-CORRELATION-DATA","stem":"A line graph shows river discharge at 09:00 and at 11:00. A student needs an estimate for 10:00. What are they doing?","options":{"A":"Interpolating — reading an estimated value between two known plotted points","B":"Extrapolating, which means extending a trend beyond the plotted data range","C":"Calculating the mean of the entire data set shown across the whole graph","D":"Identifying an anomaly, since 10:00 does not appear among the plotted values"},"correct":"A","scaffold":"Interpolation reads between known points and is usually reliable, because the trend either side constrains it. Extrapolation continues a line beyond the data and is far riskier, since nothing guarantees the trend holds. Naming which one you have done, and how confident it makes you, earns credit.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is extrapolating from a graph riskier than interpolating within it?","options":{"A":"Because extrapolation can only ever be done using a ruler and pencil","B":"Because extrapolated values are always smaller than the plotted values","C":"There is no data beyond the plotted range to confirm the trend continues","D":"Because interpolation requires far more data points than extrapolation does"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-11","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-COMMUNICATE-EVIDENCE","stem":"A student writes 'cliff' beside a feature on a photograph, and elsewhere writes 'cliff retreating because waves undercut its base'. What is the difference?","options":{"A":"The first is an annotation and the second is a label, which is simply shorter","B":"The first names the feature (a label); the second explains it (an annotation)","C":"The second is incorrect, because photographs should only ever carry single words","D":"There is no difference, since labels and annotations mean exactly the same thing"},"correct":"B","scaffold":"A label identifies; an annotation explains or evaluates. Exam questions asking you to annotate are asking for geographical reasoning attached to the image, so single-word naming caps the marks available. The same distinction applies to field sketches and to diagrams in extended answers.","reforge":{"stem":"A question asks a student to annotate a photograph of a flooded town. Which response best meets that instruction?","options":{"A":"Writing the single word 'flooding' with an arrow pointing at the water","B":"Listing out the names of every single building that is visible in the photograph","C":"Describing the weather conditions on the day the photograph was taken","D":"Noting that houses on the floodplain are worst hit because the land is lowest"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"},{"id":"GCSE-SKILL-Y10-12","spec":"GCSE-SKILL","tag":"MC-GG-SKI-RELIEF-REPRESENTATION","stem":"On an OS map, contours are widely spaced near a hilltop but very close together lower down the slope. What shape is the slope?","options":{"A":"A uniform slope, because the contour interval stays the same all the way down","B":"Convex, because the slope is steepest at the very top and eases towards the base","C":"Concave, because it is gentle at the top and becomes steeper further down","D":"A vertical cliff, because contour lines appear at intervals along the whole slope"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Contour spacing is the gradient: wide means gentle, close means steep. Gentle at the top steepening downwards is a concave slope, which curves inwards like the inside of a bowl. A convex slope is the reverse, steep at the top and flattening out at the bottom.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a series of contour lines that are evenly spaced down a hillside indicate?","options":{"A":"That the slope has a constant gradient from the top to the bottom","B":"That the hillside contains a number of small hollows and hidden rises","C":"That the slope becomes progressively steeper towards the bottom of the hill","D":"That the map has been enlarged and its contour interval is no longer correct"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-geo-skills"}]}});
